var tafsirData_7 = {
  "1": "“Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad” is the opening of this chapter with letters whose precise meaning Allah has kept to Himself. This surah begins, like several others, with separated letters pronounced by name, not as a word. The Quran signals from its very first sound that its language is made of the same Arabic letters that the Arabs used in daily speech and poetry, yet its composition remains beyond their ability to imitate, even though they are masters of eloquence. Saying these letters and then immediately presenting a clear Book is a quiet challenge to those who called revelation ancient fables or claimed the Prophet had studied previous scriptures. If the Quran were merely learned, any poet could replicate it from the same letters, yet none could.\n\nClassical commentators note that Allah knows best the intended wisdom, while affirming that the letters are not meaningless. They function as an attention marker, like a sound that makes a listener pause. For the Meccan audience accustomed to measured poetry, hearing disjointed letters followed by divine address would arrest attention and declare that what follows is weighty. Similar openings appear before passages about the Quran being a guide, a light, or a decisive criterion, linking the mysterious letters to the miraculous nature of the message that follows.\n\nThe combination “Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad” is unique to this surah, while “Alif-Lam-Mim” opens the second chapter that began with certainty of the meeting, and “Alif-Lam-Ra” opens others. The addition of “Sad” here has been linked by some scholars to the name of the surah, Al-A‘raf, and to the emphasis on storytelling that will follow, including the stories of Adam, Noah, and others, though such associations remain reflective rather than definitive.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to force a fixed translation onto these letters as if they were an ordinary word with a dictionary meaning. The respectful method transmitted from the companions is to recite them as revealed, affirm that their exact intent is with Allah, and focus on the clear guidance that follows them. For the listener who seeks straight guidance and whose heart is meant to expand to Islam, the letters become a humble reminder that human knowledge has limits, that the One whose knowledge encompasses every leaf and grain retains knowledge of some matters entirely with Himself, and that the proper response is to listen attentively to the reminder that comes after, which offers the light by which the straight path is walked toward the Home of Peace.",
  "2": "“˹This is˺ a Book sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺— do not let anxiety into your heart regarding it—so with it you may warn ˹the disbelievers˺, and as a reminder to the believers” follows immediately after the mysterious letters, explaining their purpose. The Book indicated is this Quran, whose blessed arrival was celebrated at the close of the previous surah as a detailed, perfected Word in truth and justice. The phrase “sent down to you O Prophet,” or “unzila ilaika,” personalizes the address to Muhammad, the one earlier commanded to follow only what is revealed and to convey without seeking reward. The next instruction is tenderly protective: “do not let anxiety into your heart regarding it,” or “fa-la yakun fi sadrika harajun minhu.” The word “haraj” means tightness, constriction, distress, the same tightness described for those whose chest is made narrow as if climbing into the sky when left astray. For the Prophet, tightness could mean hesitation to warn because the disbelievers mock the verses, call them fables, or intimidate. Allah says, let your chest remain spacious as it was opened to Islam; do not narrow with anxiety.\n\nThe purpose of the Book is then given in two directions. First, “so with it you may warn the disbelievers,” or “li-tunthira bihi,” using it as a warner, just as earlier the Book was sent to warn the Mother of Cities and around it and to warn of the Day of Gathering. Second, “and as a reminder to the believers,” or “wa thikra lil-mu’mineen,” a remembrance that revives what the heart already knows by fitrah, the innate call to the Originator. The same Book thus functions differently according to the recipient: for the heedless it is a warning that saves from dreadful punishment for turning away, for the mindful it is a gentle reminder that strengthens the certainty of the meeting.\n\nThis verse links to the repeated assurances that the Prophet is not a keeper over people and that messengers are only bringers of good news and warners. His duty is clear conveyance with a spacious heart; the outcome belongs to the One who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing. A common misunderstanding is to think that anxiety about delivering truth indicates weakness of faith. This verse normalizes the human feeling of tightness when facing opposition while correcting it with divine reassurance. For believers today, the verse teaches to hold the Book with confidence, to use it both to warn with wisdom and to remind with kindness, trusting that the One who sent it down and who knows best where to place His message will support the open heart and not leave the sincere invoker constricted.",
  "3": "“Follow what has been sent down to you from your Lord, and do not take others as guardians besides Him. How seldom are you mindful!” is the command to the entire community that follows the consolation to the Prophet about the Book sent down to warn and remind. After telling the Prophet not to let anxiety tighten his chest regarding the Book, Allah turns to all who hear: “Follow what has been sent down to you from your Lord,” or “ittabi‘oo ma unzila ilaikum min Rabbikum.” The verb “ittabi‘oo” means to walk behind, to trace the steps of, to stay upon the path, echoing the repeated call to follow the straight path and the blessed Book so you may be shown mercy. The source is specified as “from your Lord,” Rabbakum, the same nurturing Lord who provides for all, originates from a single soul, and maintains everything. Following therefore is not following a tribal custom but following the Nurturer who knows what benefits His servants.\n\nThe second command guards that following from dilution: “and do not take others as guardians besides Him,” or “wa la tattabi‘oo min doonihi awliya’.” “Awliya” are protectors, allies, guardians taken as authorities to be obeyed alongside Allah. This mirrors earlier warnings not to seek a judge besides Allah when the detailed Book has come, not to seek a guardian other than the Originator who provides for all, and not to obey those who fabricate lies about lawful and forbidden in Allah’s name. Taking guardians besides Him includes following invented food taboos, treating elegant deception as proof, and dividing faith into sects.\n\nThe verse then remarks with gentle reproach: “How seldom are you mindful!” or “qaleelan ma tathakkaroon,” how little you remember. This mirrors earlier refrains about seldom giving thanks and seldom being mindful, showing that forgetfulness is the root that lets beautified misdeeds remain appealing. Mindfulness, or “tadhakkur,” means connecting reminder to action, remembering that the Day you will be gathered together is real and that the Home of Peace awaits the guided.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think following revelation restricts freedom. The verse presents the opposite: guardians besides Him lead away from His Way and scatter the traveler into diverging paths. True freedom stays with the One whose Word is perfected and whose knowledge encompasses every leaf. Another mistake is to treat remembrance as occasional. The Book was sent as a continuous reminder precisely because heedlessness is recurrent, so following must be renewed daily through establishing prayer, paying dues without waste, and speaking justly, keeping the heart open rather than tight.",
  "4": "“˹Imagine˺ how many societies We have destroyed! Our torment took them by surprise ˹while sleeping˺ at night or midday” follows the call to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians, showing what happens when that call is ignored. The previous surah concluded with promises that what you are promised will certainly come and you will have no escape, and after varied signs and warnings, the immediate reminder is that many before did not escape. The phrase “how many societies We have destroyed,” or “wa kam min qaryatin ahlaknaha,” shows the pattern is not rare. “Qaryah” means a town or society, a settled community with leaders, markets, and customs, as earlier “We placed in every society the most wicked to conspire.” Many such societies, from ‘Ad and Thamud to the flourishing peoples before Mecca who were more established in the land, were destroyed for persisting in denial after messengers warned and proof was made distinct.\n\nThe detail “Our torment took them by surprise while sleeping at night or midday,” or “fa-ja’aha ba’suna bayatan aw hum qa’iloona,” captures both the timing and the suddenness. “Bayatan” is night attack, when people sleep vulnerable after a day of activity; “qa’iloona” is midday rest, the siesta in heat when vigilance relaxes. In both cases, the punishment, or “ba’suna,” arrived “baghtatan,” suddenly, as earlier promised for the Hour that takes losers by surprise, and for those showered with everything they desired and became prideful and were seized instantly.\n\nThis timing teaches that security in worldly routine is not protection. Night sleep and midday rest are times of trust, when a person feels safest, yet if hearts have been hardened and misdeeds made appealing, safety is illusory. The earlier warning that torment can come from above or below and split into factions finds illustration here: a specific society’s end came from above or below in its appointed moment, visible history confirming the unseen promise.\n\nA common misconception is to think societies are destroyed only gradually. This verse shows destruction can be sudden at the very hour of ease. For the mindful reader, it revives the opening call to follow what was sent down. Following with a guarded, remembering heart turns heedlessness into preparedness, so midday rest and nighttime sleep remain gifts of the One who causes dawn to break and made night for rest, rather than moments when surprise overtakes the heedless whose deeds were beautified until the term arrived.",
  "5": "“Their only cry—when overwhelmed by Our torment—was, ‘We have indeed been wrongdoers’” captures the moment after the surprising torment just described seized a society at night or midday. The previous verse said how many societies were destroyed when Our punishment came as a sudden visit. Now we hear the human sound that followed. The phrase “Their only cry,” or “fa-ma kana da‘wahum,” means their call, their plea, their declaration, when the punishment overwhelmed was nothing but this: “We have indeed been wrongdoers,” or “inna kunna zalimeen.” The doubling “inna” indeed we were shows forced admission, like those detained before the Fire who beg to return or detained before their Lord who cry “Absolutely, by our Lord!”\n\nThe word “zalimeen,” wrongdoers, is the same character description used throughout the previous surah for fabricators of lies, those who forbid what Allah provided, and those who will never succeed. In this life they called revelation ancient fables and followed elegant deception; under surprise punishment they recognize the label they had denied. Their confession is true but late, paralleling the earlier warning that on the Day signs arrive, belief will not benefit those who did not believe earlier. The tormented society, like individuals bearing burdens on backs, finds all eloquent excuses vanished, leaving only self-incrimination.\n\nThe verse also connects to the covenant of justice that no society is destroyed while its people are unaware. They had been warned, signs were varied, messengers endured, the Book was sent down to warn and to remind, but they preferred guardians besides Him and made deeds appealing until the appointed term.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think a last-moment admission necessarily saves. The Quran distinguishes between timely repentance, which the Lord who took mercy upon Himself welcomes with forgiveness and straightening, and compelled confession under chastisement, which arrives after the door for “mending ways” has closed. For the living, the lesson is to make this cry voluntarily and early, before surprise, like those who say “peace be upon you” to returning believers and who are told their Lord has taken mercy. Saying now, we were among the heedless, seeking openness of chest to Islam, transforms the cry from a condemned epitaph into a living turning that the All-Hearing, All-Knowing answers before the societies of today taste what earlier societies tasted when they persisted in scattered ways.",
  "6": "“We will surely question those who received messengers and We will question the messengers ˹themselves˺” broadens the accounting from destroyed societies who cried we were wrongdoers to the universal questioning that awaits every generation. After warning how many towns were seized at night or midday and how their confession came too late, the Quran now describes the procedure that ensures precise justice at the final gathering. The phrase “We will surely question those who received messengers,” or “fa-la-nas’alanna allatheena ursila ilaihim,” means those to whom messengers were sent, every community given insights from their Lord, from the Mother of Cities and around it to peoples who received Moses and others, will be asked: did the message reach you, how did you respond, did you follow what was sent down or take other guardians, did you dismiss the poor believers or guard orphan wealth and full measure? No claim of “we were unaware of their teachings” will remain, because a blessed, detailed Book was sent to remove that excuse.\n\nThe second questioning balances the first: “And We will question the messengers themselves,” or “wa la-nas’alanna al-mursaleen.” Even the noble messengers, who were told they are not keepers and not maintainers, who said “I only follow what is revealed to me” and “my prayer and worship and life and death are for Allah,” will be asked did they convey faithfully, did they warn and remind as commanded, did they turn away from polytheists as instructed. This is not accusation against them but establishment of perfect testimony, honoring their role as witnesses and removing any doubt about delivery.\n\nThis double questioning guarantees the two prerequisites stated earlier: your Lord would never destroy a society while its people are unaware, and your Lord is never unaware of what they do. Everyone questioned will be given a full account with sure knowledge, because He was never absent, as the next verse announces. A common misunderstanding is to think messengers are exempt from review or that communities can claim they were never told. The verse shows both sender and receiver are examined, making the conclusive argument truly conclusive. For those alive today, it encourages taking followership seriously, seeking the detailed Word, embodying it before testimony is demanded, and remembering that a spontaneous good word of justice or a just weight today will be remembered in a questioning where the Most Subtle, All-Aware will not miss a leaf’s fall or a whisper’s elegance.",
  "7": "“Then We will give them a full account with sure knowledge—for We were never absent” completes the scene of universal questioning that began with the promise to question both those who received messengers and the messengers themselves. The previous verse said We will surely question both groups, establishing testimony. This verse explains what follows: “We will give them a full account,” or “fa-la-naqussanna alaihim bi-ilm,” We will narrate, recount in detail, upon them with knowledge. “Qass” means to relate step by step, story after story, without omitting. The recounting is “with sure knowledge,” or “bi-ilm,” with complete, certain knowledge that misses nothing, and “for We were never absent,” or “wa ma kunna gha’ibeen,” We were never elsewhere while they acted. The Lord who knows what is in land and sea, who knows not a leaf falls without writing it, who calls back souls by night and knows what is done by day, and who sends guardian angels who never neglect, is never absent from any moment.\n\nThe wording “We will give them a full account” implies the account will be so detailed it leaves no gaps for false oaths like “By Allah, we were never polytheists” to stand. Earlier it was said even those who swore falsely would be exposed when mouths are sealed and limbs testify. The full account provides the true narrative against which every claim will be measured, fulfilling the earlier law that messengers are only bringers of good news and warners and that your Lord would never destroy a society while its people remain unaware. Awareness having been delivered, the account now rehearses it with sureness.\n\nThis verse also balances the scale metaphor that immediately follows: the weighing on that Day will be just, heavy scales for the successful, light for those who doomed themselves. Recounting with sure knowledge ensures weighing is not guessed.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to imagine the questioning is like a human interrogation where the questioner learns something new. The phrase “We were never absent” corrects that: questioning is for establishing proof to the questioned, not for acquiring information. Another misunderstanding is to think meticulous narration means harshness. For those who invoked morning and evening seeking His Face, walked with a light among people, established prayer and guarded measure, the full account with knowledge will be a comforting validation, preserving every overlooked kindness. For the heedless who took faith as play, the same detail ensures no wrong is missed. Remembering that He is never absent encourages living as if watched, which is the essence of being mindful.",
  "8": "“The weighing on that Day will be just. As for those whose scale will be heavy ˹with good deeds˺, ˹only˺ they will be successful” describes the measure that follows the full account with sure knowledge. After saying We were never absent while deeds were done, the Quran now says the evaluation of those deeds will be by weighing, or “wazn.” The phrase “The weighing on that Day will be just,” or “wal-waznu yawma’ithin al-haqq,” means the weighing itself is truth, accurate, upright, like the weight that is to be fulfilled with justice earlier commanded for markets. No adding, no subtracting, no confusion between ranks according to deeds will occur, because your Lord is never unaware and is the Swiftest Reckoner.\n\nThe result is then stated: “As for those whose scale will be heavy with good deeds, only they will be successful,” or “fa-man thaqulat mawaazeenuhu fa-ula’ika hum al-muflihoon.” Heavy scales mean abundant goods on the balance, the tenfold multiplied deeds, the clothing of righteousness, the mindful following of the detailed Book. “Mawaazeen” can be the scales themselves or the weighted deeds; scholars say deeds will have weight, given form, so even sincere words will count. Success, or “falah,” is the same triumph described as absolute triumph for whoever is spared torment, and as the end that belongs to the guided who walk the straight path to the Home of Peace.\n\nThis image links to the previous surah’s contrast between play and amusement and the better Home for the mindful, showing that many scattered ways ultimately converge at one balance. Earlier the polytheists were told they will soon know who will fare best in the end; this is how that knowing arrives, by weight.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think weighing is physical like market weighing only. The Quran presents it as precise moral weighing that includes intentions, as earlier the heart’s openness versus its constriction as if climbing into the sky already measured inward states. Another mistake is to think a heavy scale requires extraordinary acts. Even if many deeds are small, the ten-to-one multiplication and Allah’s mercy mean persistence in establishing prayer, remembering morning and evening, eating with His Name, and shunning sin can weight the scale more than a single grand act mixed with shirk. The verse thus encourages steadily accumulating heavy goods while the chance to earn remains, before the Day when belief after signs will no longer benefit those who did no good through faith.",
  "9": "“But those whose scale is light, they have doomed themselves for wrongfully denying Our signs” gives the counterpart to the heavy scale, completing the balance of just weighing. The previous verse said the weighing on that Day will be just and the heavy will be successful. Now the light scale is described. The phrase “those whose scale is light,” or “wa man khaffat mawaazeenuhu,” depicts a balance where good deeds are few, weightless, or nullified, as earlier said had they associated others with Him their good deeds would have been wasted. “Thaqulat” and “khaffat” form a clear pair: heavy versus light, like the expansive chest opened to Islam versus the tight constricted chest. Those light in good are not merely unlucky; the verse says “they have doomed themselves,” or “fa-ula’ika allatheena khasiroo anfusahum,” they ruined, lost themselves, a phrase that repeatedly described deniers who called revelation fables, who demanded shares for idols, and who arrived alone leaving behind everything they were given. Self-dooming shows responsibility; the light scale is the result of their own earning, acquiring sin open and secret, following elegant deception, and persisting in turning others away.\n\nThe cause is named precisely: “for wrongfully denying Our signs,” or “bima kanu bi-ayatina yazlimoon.” “Yazlimoon” indicates continuous injustice, placing signs where they do not belong, denying insights that had come from their Lord, beautifying misdeeds, and insisting revelation never came. This connects to the opening call not to be anxious about the Book sent to warn and remind, and to the command to follow what was sent down rather than take other guardians. Those who denied the signs denied the very light that would have heavy-weighted their scale.\n\nThe verse does not say they are doomed because scales were miscalibrated; it says they wrongfully denied signs, tying weightlessness to denial. This matches the earlier groups who denied the meeting until the Hour surprised them and now bear burdens on backs; light scales and heavy burdens are two images of the same loss.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think a light scale means a person was inherently bad. The Quran shows the path to lightness involves choices: refusing to see with insights, leaning toward decorated lies because the Hereafter was denied, and waiting for angels or final signs while doing no good through faith. The lesson is to make the scale heavy while heaviness still matters, by adding multiplied goods, shunning association even in small forms, and remembering that the Lord who established you on earth and provided livelihood, yet is seldom thanked, will weigh with perfect truth on the Day He was never absent.",
  "10": "“We have indeed established you on earth and provided you with a means of livelihood. ˹Yet˺ you seldom give any thanks” shifts from the scene of weighing to the present bounty that should have made the scales heavy. After describing heavy success and light self-dooming for denying signs, the Quran reminds humanity of the baseline favor that makes denial inexcusable. The phrase “We have indeed established you on earth,” or “wa laqad makkannakum fil-ard,” means We set you firmly, gave you ability, authority, and a settled place to live, much as earlier He placed successors on earth and made night for rest and day for seeking, establishing people and providing means of livelihood. This establishment includes physical stability, homes on firm soil, tools to cultivate gardens and manage cattle fit for labour, all by the One who produces gardens trellised and untrellised and who sends down rain producing clustered grain.\n\nThe next phrase details sustenance: “and provided you with a means of livelihood,” or “wa ja‘alna lakum fiha ma‘ayish,” We made for you therein livings, plural, suggesting many forms: crops of different flavours, olives and pomegranates, meat slaughtered with His Name, trades measured with justice, clothing to cover nakedness and as adornment, and the very breath revived each morning after souls are called back by night. Every leaf and grain recorded is part of that provision system maintained by the Trustee of all things.\n\nThen comes gentle reproach: “Yet you seldom give any thanks,” or “qaleelan ma tashkuroon.” This echoes earlier refrains, “how seldom are you mindful” and “how seldom do they comprehend.” Gratitude, or “shukr,” means recognizing the Giver, mentioning His Name, using provision within explained limits, paying dues at harvest, giving full measure, and turning the face to the Originator. Little thanks means most people use the established earth and its livelihoods while attributing them to other guardians, idols, or chance, as those who made deeds appealing and followed whispering devils.\n\nThe verse prepares for the Adam story that follows, showing that establishment on earth began with creation, shaping, angelic prostration, and provision in Paradise, yet ungratefulness led to deception there as well. A common mistake is to treat provision as self-earned entitlement, forgetting the Establisher. Remembering establishment and provision daily, even in the simple act of eating what Allah provided without following Satan’s footsteps, builds the heavy scale that leads to success rather than to self-dooming.",
  "11": "“Surely We created you, then shaped you, then said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Adam,’ so they all did—but not Iblis, who refused to prostrate with the others” returns to the very beginning of human establishment after the reminder that you were established on earth and given livelihood yet seldom give thanks. The verse compresses the origin story into three stages: “We created you,” or “khalaqnakum,” initial creation from clay and from a single soul as earlier said; “then shaped you,” or “thumma sawwarnakum,” fashioning into proportion, giving hearing, sight, and hearts; and “then said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Adam,’” honouring the human in his full constitution. The command was to angels, high beings of light who obey without delay, to bow as a sign of honour and obedience to the Creator’s will, not worship of Adam, for prostration to any creature as a deity is forbidden and the angels knew the command was a test of their submission. Ibn Kathir explains that the prostration was an acknowledgment of Adam’s dignity and of the divine wisdom in creating him, a bow of the body in obedience to the One who commanded it.\n\nThe verse records the obedience of the angels in a single phrase: “so they all did,” or “fa-sajadoo,” the entire host prostrating at once, without hesitation or exception, their obedience as complete as their worship. Then comes the exception that the whole story turns upon: “but not Iblis, who refused to prostrate with the others,” or “illa Ibleesa lam yakun mina as-sajideen.” Iblis stood among the angels without being of them — the Quran will clarify that he was of the jinn, a creature of fire endowed with free will, who had attained a lofty station through long devotion — and when the command came, his hidden pride surfaced and he refused. Al-Sa’di notes that the verse sets the two responses side by side as the eternal lesson: the angels obeyed and remained, Iblis refused and fell, and the difference between them was not knowledge or rank but humility before the command.\n\nThe opening “We created you” addresses the people of Mecca and, through them, every reader, drawing each soul back to its own beginning in the same event. The reminder is that the dignity of humanity is a gift, the command to the angels a grace, and the refusal of Iblis the first act of arrogance in creation. For the listener, the verse is a summons to choose one’s side in the ancient division: to be among those who prostrate when God commands, or among those who, like Iblis, measure the command against their own pride and refuse. The story of Adam and Iblis is not ancient history but the present drama of every soul, and the prostration of the angels and the refusal of Iblis are the two possibilities that still lie before every human heart.",
  "12": "“What prevented you from prostrating when I commanded you? He replied, ‘I am better than him. You created me from fire and him from clay’” records the first interrogation in creation and the first rationalization of disobedience. God asked Iblis the question whose answer would expose the truth of his heart: “What prevented you from prostrating when I commanded you?” or “ma mana’aka alla tasjuda idh amartuka.” The question is not for information — God knew the answer — but for confession and for the record, so that the refusal and its cause would be spoken aloud and preserved for every generation. Ibn Kathir notes that the question carries within it the indictment: there was no barrier to prostration except Iblis’s own will, and the command that had been given to all was binding upon him too. The asking of the question is itself the first judgment, for the one who must explain his disobedience has already begun to stand in the dock.\n\nIblis’s answer is the first instance of that fatal logic by which every rebel since has justified his rebellion: “I am better than him. You created me from fire and him from clay,” or “ana khayrun minhu khalaqtani min narin wa khalaqtahu min teen.” The argument is a comparison of origins: fire, in Iblis’s reckoning, is nobler than clay, and therefore the creature of fire is nobler than the creature of clay. The commentators dissect the argument with care, and al-Hasan al-Basri’s observation has become proverbial: Iblis was the first to use analogical reasoning — qiyas — against a direct command of God, preferring his own comparison of fire and clay to the plain divine order to prostrate. The flaw is fatal on every level: the materials of creation carry no intrinsic nobility, for all are creatures of the same Creator; the true measure of honour is obedience, not substance; and the one who measures the command of God against his own opinion has already set himself up as a judge over the Judge. Al-Sa’di observes that Iblis’s words are the prototype of every claim of racial, social, or material superiority, for they reduce the worth of a soul to the grossness or fineness of its origin.\n\nThe verse thus exposes the root of all disobedience: not ignorance but arrogance, not inability but refusal, and the refusal is dressed in the garments of reason. Iblis did not deny the command; he denied its application to himself, on the ground that he was better than the one he was commanded to honour. For the listener, the verse is the Quran’s first and clearest warning against the pride that measures divine commands by personal prejudice, and its lesson is the one that runs through the entire story: the only valid response to the command of God is “we hear and we obey,” and the first word of every fall is “I am better.”",
  "13": "“Then get down from Paradise! It is not for you to be arrogant here. So get out! You are truly one of the disgraced” pronounces the sentence upon Iblis, and the commentators note the completeness of the fall. The command is threefold, and each clause deepens the punishment. “Get down from Paradise,” or “ihbit minha,” the expulsion from the heavenly assembly and the lofty station Iblis had attained; “It is not for you to be arrogant here,” or “fa ma yakoonu laka an tatakabbara feeha,” the statement of the principle that Paradise is the abode of humility and cannot contain the arrogant; and “So get out! You are truly one of the disgraced,” or “fakhruj innaka mina as-saghireen,” the command of expulsion sealed with the verdict of disgrace. Ibn Kathir explains that Iblis had been dwelling in the company of the angels through his worship, and that his arrogance in that sacred place was the sin that made his presence there impossible — the proud cannot dwell in the house of submission.\n\nThe verse isolates arrogance, “takabbur,” as the specific crime, and the commentators dwell on this. The word comes from the root kibr, greatness, and denotes the claim of a greatness that belongs only to God, the swelling of the self beyond its measure. Al-Sa’di observes that arrogance is the mother of sins, the first sin ever committed in creation and the root from which every later rebellion springs, for the arrogant soul refuses the very posture of submission that is the essence of worship. The verse thus teaches that Paradise is barred not to the sinner who repents but to the proud who will not bow, and that the way to the Garden lies through the gate of humility.\n\nThe word “saghireen,” the disgraced, carries its own lesson. Iblis had sought to exalt himself above Adam by the comparison of fire and clay, and the verdict upon him is the opposite of what he sought: not exaltation but humiliation, not honour but disgrace. The commentators note the perfect justice of the sentence: the one who claimed to be better was declared to be lower, and the pride that would not bow was made to fall. For the listener, the verse is the Quran’s declaration of the divine law that governs all pride: whoever exalts himself above the command of God is cast down by the very arrogance that raised him, and the station of honour with God is reached only through the prostration that Iblis refused.",
  "14": "“Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection” is the request Iblis makes immediately after his expulsion, and the commentators note what it reveals about the rebel. Iblis did not repent, did not ask for mercy, and did not reconsider his refusal; instead, he asked for time — “anzirni ila yawmi yub’athoon,” grant me respite until the day when they, the children of Adam, are raised. The request shows that Iblis had accepted his own ruin but had fixed his attention upon the very beings whose creation he had resented, resolving to spend the respite he sought in leading them astray. Ibn Kathir explains that the word “yub’athoon,” they are resurrected, already contains the target of his malice: he asked for life until the resurrection so that he might work against the children of Adam throughout their earthly existence, seeking to make them companions in his fall.\n\nThe request itself is a study in the psychology of evil. The proud soul, when it falls, does not turn toward God but turns against others; the ruin that Iblis could not escape he resolved to share. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse exposes the enmity that would define the rest of the story: from the moment of his expulsion, Iblis dedicated himself to the destruction of the human soul, not out of any benefit to himself but out of pure malice, the spite of the fallen who wishes company in his fall. The request for respite is thus the declaration of war, the first sentence of the long campaign that the Quran will describe in the verses that follow.\n\nThere is also a subtle truth in the timing. Iblis asked to be delayed until the day of resurrection, and the commentators note that this request itself was granted only in part, for the respite given was not until the resurrection but until “the appointed day,” a term earlier than the resurrection itself, as the next verse will show. The rebel who asked for the longest possible time was given only what the divine wisdom decreed, and the lesson is that even the requests of the enemy of God are answered only within the limits of the divine will. For the listener, the verse is the introduction to the enmity that every believer must face, and the reminder that the enemy’s time is limited, his campaign is known, and his end is written.",
  "15": "“You are delayed ˹until the appointed Day˺” is the curt divine reply to Iblis’s request for respite, and the commentators dwell on the precision of the answer. Iblis had asked to be delayed until the day of the resurrection of humanity, but God granted him a lesser term: “You are delayed,” or “innaka mina al-munzareen,” until “the appointed Day,” or “ila yawmi al-waqti al-ma’loom” — the day fixed and known to God alone, which the commentators explain is the day of the first blast, the hour when all living things perish, a term that precedes the resurrection itself. Ibn Kathir notes the difference with care: the respite granted to Iblis was not the full span he had requested but a limited period, its end known only to the One who decreed it, and the limitation is itself part of the divine wisdom, for the enemy of humanity is given time to test but not to reign, and his term is bounded by a day he cannot know.\n\nThe verse teaches several lessons at once. First, that the answer to even the most audacious request is given by the divine will, which grants what it wills and withholds what it wills, and which answered the enemy of God not with annihilation but with a measured respite, for the testing of humanity required that the tempter be given his term. Second, that the respite is itself a mercy to humanity wrapped in a test: the believer who knows that the enemy has a fixed and limited term fights with the confidence of one who knows the outcome, while the heedless soul is tested by the very delay. Al-Sa’di observes that the granting of the respite is one of the great proofs of divine wisdom, for it makes the struggle of this life meaningful — the enemy is real, the test is genuine, and the term of both is appointed.\n\nThe word “al-waqt al-ma’loom,” the appointed time, carries its own weight. It is the time that is “known” — known to God, hidden from all else — and it reminds every soul that the terms of all things, the enemy’s campaign and the believer’s own life, are fixed in a knowledge beyond human reach. For the listener, the verse is the assurance that the evil of Iblis is not eternal and not unbounded: it has an appointed end, its span is measured, and the believer’s struggle against it is a race toward a day that the Lord has already set.",
  "16": "“For leaving me to stray I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path” is Iblis’s declaration of war upon humanity, and the commentators unpack its every word. The opening, “For leaving me to stray,” or “fabi-ma aghwaytani,” is the rebel’s final blame — the accusation against God that would become the signature of every wrongdoer, for Iblis attributed his own ruin to the divine will rather than to his own pride, saying, in effect, “because You caused me to stray, I will cause them to stray.” Ibn Kathir notes the fallacy that the Quran exposes here and elsewhere: God did not cause Iblis to stray except as the consequence of his own refusal, and the rebel’s attempt to shift the blame is itself the proof of his corruption. The sentence teaches that the wrongdoer, when he falls, accuses his Lord, while the penitent, like Adam, accuses only himself.\n\nThe threat that follows is precise and terrifying: “I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path,” or “la-aq’udanna lahum sirataka al-mustaqeem.” The “sirat al-mustaqeem,” the Straight Path, is the very path of guidance that leads to God, and the verse reveals the essential fact about Iblis’s campaign: he does not build a rival path but ambushes the true one. The enemy’s work is not the creation of an alternative road but the obstruction of the right one — the doubt planted in the heart of the believer, the desire whispered into the soul of the obedient, the sin cast into the way of the one who walks toward God. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the most important truth in the verse: the believer is never more in danger than when he is on the right path, for the enemy has sworn to sit in ambush precisely there, and the one who thinks himself safe from temptation has forgotten where the enemy lies in wait.\n\nThe word “aq’udanna,” I will surely sit, conveys the patience and determination of the ambusher — the long watch, the waiting, the choosing of the moment. Ibn Kathir notes that the threat reveals the method of the enemy: not a single assault but a lifelong siege, the persistent sitting upon the path until the traveler grows weary or careless. For the listener, the verse is the Quran’s call to vigilance: the Straight Path is the way of safety and the place of danger at once, and the one who walks it must walk with open eyes, knowing that the ambush is real, the enemy is patient, and the only shield is the remembrance and help of the One who guides.",
  "17": "“I will approach them from their front, their back, their right, their left, and then You will find most of them ungrateful” completes Iblis’s vow and maps the full circumference of his assault. The verse describes an attack from four directions — “min bayni aydeehim wa min khalfihim wa ‘an aymanihim wa ‘an shama’ilihim,” from before them, behind them, on their right, and on their left — and the commentators explain the meaning of each direction. From the front: the corruption of the future, the doubt cast upon the Hereafter and the resurrection, the despair that sees no good ahead. From behind: the exploitation of the past, the regret over what has passed, the attachment to the world one has left behind. From the right: the assault upon the good, the tainting of deeds with pride and showing off, the corruption of the very worship that should save. From the left: the seduction into sin, the lure of the forbidden, the invitation to the wrong that lies open. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari record these meanings and note the single omission: Iblis did not say “from above,” for the mercy of God descends from above, and the enemy has no access to it, nor did he claim the direction from which the believer’s only true refuge comes.\n\nThe omission of “above” is itself a mercy, and the commentators dwell on it. The believer is surrounded on four sides by the enemy’s campaign, but the way upward remains open — the way of supplication, of turning to God, of raising the hands in prayer — and the enemy who can approach from every horizontal direction cannot cut off the vertical line that runs between the servant and the Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the comfort hidden within the threat: the siege is complete on every side but one, and the one open side is the side of salvation. The believer under assault has only to look up.\n\nThe verse closes with Iblis’s bitter prediction: “and then You will find most of them ungrateful,” or “thumma la tajidu aktharahum shakireen.” The enemy of humanity declares that the majority of the children of Adam will fall, and the Quran records the prediction as a warning and a challenge: the believer is summoned to be among the few, among the grateful whom the ambush cannot take. Ibn Kathir notes that the word “shakireen,” the grateful, is the opposite of the fallen, for the ungrateful heart is the heart that the enemy wins, while the grateful heart, the heart that remembers its Lord, is the heart that the siege cannot conquer. For the listener, the verse is the map of the enemy’s campaign and the key to its defeat: four directions are his, and one is ours — the upward turn of the grateful heart toward the Lord who alone can save.",
  "18": "“Get out of Paradise! You are disgraced and rejected! I will certainly fill up Hell with you and whoever follows you from among them all” pronounces the final sentence upon Iblis and his followers, and the commentators note the completeness of the decree. The command of expulsion is repeated with added force — “Get out of Paradise! You are disgraced and rejected!” or “ukhruj minha madh’uman madhooran” — the two words “madh’um” (disgraced) and “madhoor” (rejected) sealing his fate with humiliation and banishment together. Ibn Kathir explains that the repetition of the expulsion, after the earlier command in this same passage, marks the finality of the judgment: the door that had been opened for repentance was now closed by Iblis’s own persistence, and the rebel who had blamed his Lord and sworn war upon humanity was cast out beyond recall.\n\nThe verse then records the divine oath that follows: “I will certainly fill up Hell with you and whoever follows you from among them all,” or “la-amla’anna jahannama minka mimman tabi’aka minhum ajma’een.” The commentators dwell on the word “ajma’een,” all of them together, and on the promise it contains: Hell will be filled with Iblis and with all those who follow him, from every generation and every people. The verse is the Quran’s stark statement of the two camps and their two ends: the camp of the followers of Iblis, gathered with their leader into the Fire, and the camp of the grateful, who walked the path that the enemy ambushed and reached their Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is a warning whose purpose is mercy: God declares the end of the path of Iblis so that no one may walk it unwarned, and the filling of Hell is announced not as a threat to be delighted in but as a truth to be escaped.\n\nThe verse also fixes the meaning of “following Iblis” for every generation. The followers of Iblis are not those who make a formal pact with him but those who walk in his footsteps — the footsteps of arrogance, of blame, of the refusal to bow before the command of God. Ibn Kathir notes that the Quran elsewhere warns the believers not to follow the footsteps of Satan, and the warning here is the same: the path of the follower of Iblis is not a different road but the same road the first rebel walked, and its end is the same end he earned. For the listener, the verse is the closing of the account of Iblis’s fall: the command to leave, the verdict of disgrace, and the oath of the Fire — and with it, the summons to be among those who do not follow.",
  "19": "“O Adam! Live with your wife in Paradise and eat from wherever you please, but do not approach this tree, or else you will be wrongdoers” turns from the fall of Iblis to the settlement of Adam, and the commentators note the contrast the Quran draws between the two. Iblis had been expelled in disgrace; Adam was settled in Paradise in honour, with a wife and a Garden and the freedom to eat from wherever he pleased. The address — “O Adam! Live with your wife in Paradise,” or “ya Adamu uskun anta wa zawjuka al-jannah” — is the first command of ease, the establishment of the first human household in the first home, and the word “uskun” conveys dwelling, settling, the peace of a life established in security. Ibn Kathir explains that Hawwa was created for Adam, and the two were placed together in the Garden as the beginning of the human family, the first marriage and the first companionship.\n\nThe command is then qualified by the single boundary: “but do not approach this tree,” or “wa la taqraba hathihi ash-shajarah,” and the commentators note the precision of the wording. God did not say “do not eat from it” but “do not approach it,” teaching the first human pair the principle of distance from temptation — the guarding of the soul by keeping away from the thing that leads to sin. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the root of the Quranic principle of “sadd al-dhara’i,” the blocking of the paths that lead to wrongdoing: the soul is protected not only by avoiding the forbidden act but by avoiding its precincts, and the one who lingers near the boundary will soon cross it. The tree is not named, and the commentators note the wisdom of the omission: the point is not the tree but the command, the test of obedience in its purest form.\n\nThe warning that follows names the consequence: “or else you will be wrongdoers,” or “fa-takoona mina az-zalimeen.” The word “zulm,” wrongdoing, is the Quran’s term for self-harm, the injustice that a soul does to itself by transgressing its Lord’s limit, and the verse teaches that sin is, before anything else, a wrong against one’s own soul. Ibn Kathir notes that the warning is itself a mercy: the boundary was drawn, the consequence was named, and the pair were told in advance what the tree would cost, so that their fall, when it came, would be a matter of full knowledge and free choice. For the listener, the verse is the first lesson of the divine law: freedom within limits, blessing with a boundary, and the warning that the transgression of the limit is the wronging of the self.",
  "20": "“Then Satan tempted them in order to expose what was hidden of their nakedness. He said, ‘Your Lord has forbidden this tree to you only to prevent you from becoming angels or immortals’” records the first whisper of the enemy and the first seduction of humanity, and the commentators reconstruct the scene with care. The verse says that Satan “tempted them in order to expose what was hidden of their nakedness,” or “fa-waswasa lahuma ash-shaytanu li-yubdiya lahuma ma wooriya ‘an-huma min saw’atihima,” and the commentators explain that Adam and Hawwa were covered with a garment of light or with the covering of Paradise, and that the first effect of the sin would be the loss of that covering and the exposure of their private parts. Ibn Kathir notes the sequence the verse implies: the temptation came first, the whisper of the enemy, and the nakedness was the result, for the disobedience stripped from them the covering that obedience had maintained.\n\nThe whisper itself is recorded: “Your Lord has forbidden this tree to you only to prevent you from becoming angels or immortals,” or “ma nahakuma rabbukuma ‘an hathihi ash-shajarati illa an takoona malakayni aw takoona mina al-khalideen.” The commentators dissect the deception with precision, for this single sentence is the model of every lie the enemy has ever told. It took a true thing — the forbidden tree — and wrapped it in a false promise: that the prohibition was not for their protection but for their deprivation, that God had forbidden the tree only to keep them from the greatness of angels or the eternity of the immortals. The lie reversed the divine mercy into a divine envy, and it did so by appealing to the noblest of human aspirations — the longing for elevation and immortality — and redirecting them toward the forbidden. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the eternal method of Satan: he does not invite to the ugly but to the seemingly beautiful, dressing the sin in the garments of a blessing, and the first sin was born of a promise of angelhood.\n\nThe verse also contains a subtle warning in its language: Satan’s whisper was a “waswasah,” the repeated, low, insinuating suggestion that works by persistence rather than force, and the Quran will use the same word for every temptation that follows. Ibn Kathir notes that the enemy did not compel but whispered, and the fall of Adam and Hawwa was the result of a choice, not an overwhelming — the whisper found a hearing ear. For the listener, the verse is the first lesson in the psychology of temptation: the enemy lies, the lie flatters, and the flattered heart falls — and the only protection is the recognition that the prohibition of God is mercy, not deprivation.",
  "21": "“And he swore to them, ‘I am truly your sincere advisor’” follows the decorated promise that preceded it, and the commentators note the escalation of the deception. Having whispered that the tree would make them angels or immortals, Satan now sealed his lie with an oath — “wa qasamahuma,” he swore to them — and the oath was the strongest instrument of persuasion available: “I am truly your sincere advisor,” or “inni lakuma la-mina an-nasiheen.” Ibn Kathir explains the gravity of this: the enemy of God invoked the very language of friendship and sincerity, presenting himself as the well-wisher who desired only their good, and he swore to it, binding his lie with the most solemn of human assurances. The commentators note that this is the method of every deceiver: the lie is not enough until it is accompanied by the oath of sincerity, and the victim is most vulnerable when the deceiver protests his good intentions.\n\nThe word “nasiheen,” sincere advisors, is the key, and al-Sa’di observes the irony that the Quran preserves: the one who was cast out of Paradise for his enmity to Adam now presents himself as Adam’s advisor, and the enmity is dressed in the garments of counsel. The verse teaches that the most dangerous enemy is not the one who declares his hostility but the one who swears his friendship, and that the believer must test every voice that claims to advise him — even the voice that swears — against the command of God. The tree was forbidden, and no amount of sworn sincerity could make it permissible; the oath of the advisor did not change the fact of the prohibition.\n\nThe verse also marks the depth of the fall that followed. Adam and Hawwa, who had heard the command of God directly, listened to the oath of the one whom God had expelled, and the commentators note that this is the measure of the seduction: the direct word of the Lord was set aside for the sworn word of His enemy, and the pair who had every reason for certainty were drawn into doubt by a promise wrapped in an oath. Ibn Kathir observes that this is why the Quran repeatedly warns the believers against the oath-taking of deceivers, for the enemy of humanity has never ceased to swear his sincerity. For the listener, the verse is the warning that sincerity can be counterfeited and that the counterfeit is the enemy’s favourite tool — the believer’s protection is not the trust of every advisor but the anchoring of the heart in the command of God, against which no oath and no whisper can prevail.",
  "22": "“So he brought about their fall through deception. And when they tasted of the tree, their nakedness was exposed to them, and they began to cover themselves with the leaves of Paradise. Then their Lord called out to them, ‘Did I not forbid you from that tree and tell you that Satan is your sworn enemy?’” records the fall and its immediate aftermath, and the commentators dwell on each element. The verse states the cause with precision: “he brought about their fall through deception,” or “fa-dallahuma bi-ghuroor,” the word “dalla” conveying the gradual lowering, the drawing down step by step, and “ghuroor” the deception, the illusion that had been sold to them. Ibn Kathir notes the choice of words: they were not pushed but lowered, not forced but deceived, and the deception was the oath and the promise by which the enemy had made the forbidden appear as the gateway to the eternal.\n\nThe first consequence followed at once: “when they tasted of the tree, their nakedness was exposed to them,” or “badata lahuma saw’atuhuma,” and the covering that had shielded them fell away, so that they saw their own nakedness and “began to cover themselves with the leaves of Paradise,” or “wa tafiqa yakhsifani ‘alayhima min waraqi al-jannah.” The commentators observe the terrible irony of the scene: the pair who had been promised angelhood and immortality found themselves instead stripped and hiding, stitching the leaves of the Garden over the shame that the sin had revealed, and the tree that had promised elevation delivered only the knowledge of their own exposure. The leaves of Paradise, which they now grasped at in haste, were the first clothing of the fallen — the beginning of the long human labour of covering what sin uncovers.\n\nThen came the divine address, and the commentators note its tone: “Then their Lord called out to them, ‘Did I not forbid you from that tree and tell you that Satan is your sworn enemy?’” or “alam anh-kuma ‘an tilkuma ash-shajarati wa aqul lakuma inna ash-shaytana lakuma ‘aduwwun mubeen.” The question is not the anger of a tyrant but the gentle reproof of a Lord who reminds His servants of what they had been told — the prohibition and the warning of the enemy — so that they might recognize their fault without being crushed by it. Al-Sa’di observes that the question is itself a mercy, for it leads the pair to the acknowledgment that opens the door of repentance: they had been warned, and the warning had been true, and the enemy who had sworn sincerity had been, all along, the sworn enemy whose enmity had been declared. For the listener, the verse is the anatomy of every fall: the gradual lowering, the sudden exposure, the hasty covering, and the voice of the Lord calling the fallen back to the memory of the warning — the first step on the road of return.",
  "23": "“Our Lord! We have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will surely be among the losers” records the first prayer of repentance in human history, and the commentators present it as the model of every prayer that has followed. The words are the answer to the divine question of the previous verse, and they are the exact opposite of the words of Iblis: where Iblis blamed his Lord, Adam and Hawwa blamed themselves; where Iblis accused, they confessed. The prayer opens with the acknowledgment that is the heart of all repentance: “We have wronged ourselves,” or “zalamna anfusana,” the recognition that the sin was their own doing and the harm was to their own souls, for no sin harms God and no transgression touches His majesty, but every disobedience wounds the one who commits it. Ibn Kathir notes that this confession is the first step of every return: the soul that owns its wrong has begun to walk back.\n\nThe prayer then turns to the plea that only such a confession can make: “If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will surely be among the losers,” or “wa in lam taghfir lana wa tarhamna la-nakoonanna mina al-khasireen.” The two requests — forgiveness, “maghfirah,” the covering of the sin, and mercy, “rahmah,” the grace that restores — are joined, for the sinner needs both: the erasure of the past and the kindness for the future. And the prayer closes with the confession of dependence: without the forgiveness and mercy of God, they would be among the losers, the “khasireen,” those who lose their souls and their share of the eternal. Al-Sa’di observes the completeness of the prayer: it contains the confession of the wrong, the request for the two mercies, and the acknowledgment of total need, and it is for this reason that the commentators identify it as the very words that God taught Adam, and the model of the supplication of every repentant heart.\n\nThe verse also draws the contrast that the whole story has been building. Iblis, confronted with his sin, said “I am better than him”; Adam, confronted with his, said “we have wronged ourselves.” The one was cast out in his pride, and the other was restored in his humility, and the difference between the two sentences is the difference between damnation and salvation. Ibn Kathir notes that the Quran places the two responses side by side so that every soul may choose which sentence to speak, and that the prayer of Adam remains the open door for every sinner who will speak its words. For the listener, the verse is the Quran’s greatest teaching on repentance: the way back begins not with excuses but with confession, not with blame but with humility, and the words “we have wronged ourselves” are the words that every returning soul must learn to say.",
  "24": "“Descend as enemies to each other. You will find in the earth a residence and provision for an appointed time” records the decree of the descent, and the commentators note the mercy folded within the sentence. The command — “Descend,” or “ihbitoo” — addresses Adam, Hawwa, and the serpent-tempter together, as the commentators explain, and the words “as enemies to each other,” or “ba’dukum li-ba’din ‘aduww,” establish the permanent enmity between humanity and Satan, the war that would define the earthly existence. Ibn Kathir explains that the enmity is structural and enduring: Satan is the declared enemy of the children of Adam, and the children of Adam are commanded to treat him as their enemy, and this enmity is the framework of the test of life — the soul on earth is a soul at war, and its salvation lies in the direction of its allegiance.\n\nYet the decree is not a curse but a provision, and the commentators dwell on the words that follow: “You will find in the earth a residence and provision for an appointed time,” or “wa lakum fi al-ardi mustaqarrun wa mata’un ila heen.” The earth, which might have been imagined as the place of punishment, is described as a place of settlement — “mustaqarr” — and provision — “mata’” — for an appointed span — “heen.” Al-Sa’di observes that this is the divine wisdom of the descent: the earth was never a prison but a stage, the appointed dwelling for the appointed test, furnished with everything the traveler needs for the journey, and bounded by a term that leads not to nothingness but to the return. The descent of Adam was not the end of the story but the beginning of the mission for which humanity had been created — the stewardship of the earth, announced to the angels before the first human was formed.\n\nThe word “heen,” an appointed time, carries the same lesson as the earlier “appointed Day”: the earthly life is measured, its span fixed, and the traveler who knows the term of the journey lives differently from the one who imagines the road is endless. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse sets the entire human condition in a single sentence: the enmity that tests, the residence that shelters, the provision that sustains, and the term that limits — and the believer reads in it both the warning of the struggle and the comfort of the provision. For the listener, the verse is the charter of the earthly life: descend, struggle, be provided for, and journey toward the return, knowing that the enmity is real, the provision is sure, and the term is appointed.",
  "25": "“There you will live, there you will die, and from there you will be resurrected” states the complete cycle of the earthly existence, and the commentators note its precision. The verse gathers the whole of human life on earth into three clauses: “There you will live,” or “feeha tahyawn,” the span of life lived upon the earth; “there you will die,” or “wa feeha tamootoon,” the death that comes to every soul upon the earth; and “from there you will be resurrected,” or “wa minha tukhrajoon,” the resurrection that will bring every soul forth from the earth on the day of return. Ibn Kathir explains that the verse answers the question of what the descent meant: the earth is the place of living and dying, but not the final home, and the soul that is brought out of the earth at the resurrection is the same soul that was placed into it, so that the journey of the earth is a passage between two emergences — the emergence at birth and the emergence at the resurrection.\n\nThe verse also carries a profound reassurance. The descent from Paradise, which might have seemed an abandonment, is here revealed as a complete divine plan: the soul is placed on the earth for a purpose, and the purpose is not ended by death, for the earth itself will give back what it received, and the one who descended will ascend again at the resurrection. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer to see the earth as a place of sojourn and not of settlement — to live in it without being rooted in it, to die in it without being lost in it, and to await the resurrection from it as the traveler awaits the return home. The three clauses are the three stations of the journey, and the believer walks them with the certainty of the one who knows the road.\n\nThere is also a subtle lesson in the order of the three. Life, death, and resurrection are not three separate realities but one movement, and the Quran places them in a single sentence to teach that they are all the decree of the one God, who places the soul in the earth, takes it in death, and brings it forth at the resurrection. Ibn Kathir notes that this verse is one of the Quran’s many proofs of the Hereafter: the earth that receives the dead will give them up, and the one who created the soul from nothing will raise it from the earth at the appointed time. For the listener, the verse is the summary of the human condition and the ground of its hope: we live here, we die here, and from here we will be raised — and the wisdom of it all belongs to the One who decreed the descent.",
  "26": "“O children of Adam! We have provided for you clothing to cover your nakedness and as an adornment. But the best clothing is righteousness. This is one of Allah’s bounties, so perhaps you will be mindful” turns from the story of the first fall to the direct address of every generation, and the commentators note the tenderness of the shift. The address — “O children of Adam,” or “ya banee Adama” — speaks to all of humanity as the descendants of the first pair, drawing every listener into the lesson of the story that has just been told. The verse then states the gift: “We have provided for you clothing to cover your nakedness and as an adornment,” or “qad anzalna ‘alaykum libasan yuwaree saw’atikum wa reeshan.” Ibn Kathir explains the two functions of clothing named here: the covering, “libas,” that conceals the nakedness whose exposure followed the first sin, and the adornment, “reesh,” the beauty and dignity of fine clothing by which the human being is distinguished from the beasts. The verse thus traces the gift of clothing to the lesson of the fall: what the sin uncovered, the mercy of God has provided the means to cover.\n\nThe verse then states the hierarchy that governs the whole matter: “But the best clothing is righteousness,” or “wa libasu at-taqwa thalika khayr,” the garment of taqwa, the God-consciousness that clothes the soul in purity, is better than every garment of the body. The commentators dwell on this: the verse does not denigrate the clothing of the body but ranks the clothing of the soul above it, teaching that the true covering of the human being is not the cloth that hides the flesh but the taqwa that hides the faults. Al-Sa’di observes the wisdom of the verse for the age of the pilgrimage in particular, when some of the Arabs would circumambulate the Kaaba naked, claiming they would not worship in the clothes in which they had sinned — the Quran answers that the covering God wants is the covering of righteousness, and that bodily nakedness is not piety but the abandonment of the very gift that God provided.\n\nThe verse closes by naming the purpose of the whole provision: “This is one of Allah’s bounties, so perhaps you will be mindful,” or “min ayati Allahi la’allahum yadhakkaroon.” The clothing, the covering, and the adornment are signs of the divine bounty, and their purpose is remembrance — the mindfulness of the One who provided them. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse teaches the believer to receive every gift as a sign, and to let the garment on the body remind the soul of the garment it must seek. For the listener, the verse is the application of the story of Adam to the daily life of every soul: the body is clothed by the mercy of God, but the soul is clothed only by righteousness, and the best of the two garments is the one no eye can see.",
  "27": "“O children of Adam! Do not let Satan deceive you as he tempted your parents out of Paradise, stripping them of their clothing to expose their nakedness. Surely he and his tribe watch you from where you cannot see them. We have made the devils allies of those who disbelieve” closes the passage with the warning that gathers its whole lesson, and the commentators note its force. The address is repeated — “O children of Adam” — and the warning is drawn directly from the story that preceded it: “Do not let Satan deceive you as he tempted your parents out of Paradise,” or “la yaftinannakumu ash-shaytanu kama akhraja abawaykum mina al-jannah.” Ibn Kathir explains the logic: the enemy who deceived your first parents, who stripped them of their covering and drove them from the Garden, is the same enemy who lies in wait for you, and the warning is delivered not as a remote historical lesson but as a present and personal danger. The verb “yafitannakum” — let him tempt you, test you — makes the warning continuous: the temptation of the first pair is renewed in every generation, and every soul stands where Adam stood.\n\nThe verse then reveals the hidden nature of the enemy: “Surely he and his tribe watch you from where you cannot see them,” or “innahu yarakum huwa wa qabeeluhu min haythu la tarawnahum.” Satan and his tribe — the devils of his kind, the “qabeel” — see the children of Adam from a vantage that the children of Adam cannot see, and the commentators note the strategic implication: the enemy observes while remaining unobserved, and the believer who forgets the unseen watcher is the believer who falls. Al-Sa’di observes that this is why the believer must never feel safe from temptation: the enemy sees every weakness, watches every moment, and chooses the instant of the attack, and the only protection is the constant remembrance of the One who sees the enemy as He sees all.\n\nThe verse closes with the statement that explains the enemy’s power: “We have made the devils allies of those who disbelieve,” or “inna ja’alna ash-shayateena awliya’a lillatheena la yu’minoon.” The devils are the allies — the “awliya’” — of those who disbelieve, and the commentators explain the meaning: the alliance is not arbitrary but chosen, for the disbeliever, by turning from God, has made himself the companion of the devils, and the devils, by their nature, seek out those who will follow them. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse thus defines the two camps for every generation: the allies of the devils and the allies of God, and the believer is summoned to the second by the warning of the first. For the listener, the verse is the seal of the story of Adam and Iblis: the enemy is real, unseen, and allied to unbelief, and the only safety from his temptation is the remembrance of the One who warned — the Lord who stripped the first pair only to clothe every generation after them in His mercy.",
  "28": "“Whenever they commit a shameful deed, they say, ‘We found our forefathers doing it and Allah has commanded us to do it.’ Say, ‘No! Allah never commands what is shameful. How can you attribute to Allah what you do not know?’” unmasks the next trick after warning not to be deceived as parents were. The phrase “Whenever they commit a shameful deed,” or “wa itha fa‘aloo fahishatan,” uses “fahishah,” an enormity openly foul, like the pre-Islamic practice of circumambulating the Kaaba naked claiming purity, or other indecencies excused as tradition. Two excuses are quoted together: “We found our forefathers doing it,” appealing to ancestral custom, and “and Allah has commanded us to do it,” attributing the shame to divine instruction. Both mimic the earlier fabrications where divisions of cattle were claimed to be from Allah while being lies without knowledge.\n\nThe commanded reply is sharp and clear: “Say, ‘No! Allah never commands what is shameful,’” or “qul inna Allaha la ya’muru bil-fahsha’.” Allah’s commands were just recited in the code: not associating partners, honoring parents, not killing children for fear of poverty, not coming near indecencies open or secret, guarding orphan wealth, fulfilling measure and covenant, all guided to the straight path that is your Lord’s Path. Nothing in that path is fahishah. To claim He commands shame is to invert His Word which is perfected in truth and justice.\n\nThe question that follows exposes ignorance: “How can you attribute to Allah what you do not know?” or “a-taqooloona ala Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon.” This mirrors earlier challenges: “Do you have any knowledge that you can produce for us? You follow only assumption,” and “Tell me with knowledge if you are truthful.” Attributing a command to Allah without knowledge is the same as fabricating lies against Him, one of the greatest wrongs that will never succeed and that earns dreadful punishment for turning away.\n\nA common error is to think ancestral continuity sanctifies a practice. The Quran separates heritage from authority; heritage must be measured against the detailed Book. Another error is to think divine permission for adornment, or eating and drinking without waste, contradicts modesty. The next verses will show adornment during worship and lawful provision are gifts from Allah, while shame is not. The safeguard is sincerity, uprightness, and calling upon Him alone, distinguishing what He truly commanded from what devils make appear as commanded while watching from where you cannot see them.",
  "29": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘My Lord has commanded uprightness and dedication ˹to Him alone˺ in worship, calling upon Him with sincere devotion. Just as He first brought you into being, you will be brought to life again’” sets the true command against the false claim that Allah orders shame. After the polytheists said “We found our forefathers doing it and Allah commanded it,” and were told He never commands fahishah, the Prophet is instructed to state what the Lord actually commands. The opening, “My Lord has commanded uprightness,” or “qul amara Rabbi bil-qist,” uses “qist” meaning justice, balance, equity, like giving full measure and weighing with justice, speaking justly even regarding relatives, and judging fairly. Uprightness is the straight path without crookedness, the Deenan Qiyaman perfect way.\n\nThe next command is “and dedication to Him alone in worship,” or “wa aqimoo wujoohakum inda kulli masjid,” literally establish your faces at every place of worship, meaning direct the entire face and being toward Him at every prostration, in every mosque, at every prayer, as earlier “I have turned my face to the Originator” and “Establish prayer and be mindful of Him” indicated. The phrase “calling upon Him with sincere devotion,” or “wa id‘oohu mukhliseena lahu al-deen,” emphasizes “ikhlas,” purity, making religion sincerely His alone, without seeking other guardians.\n\nThe verse then reminds of power that makes both uprightness and resurrection coherent: “Just as He first brought you into being, you will be brought to life again,” or “kama bada’akum ta‘oodoon.” As He created you from clay, shaped you, originated you from a single soul, assigned a place to live and to be laid to rest, gave life to a dead heart with a light to walk among people, and promised you will live, die, and be resurrected from the earth, so He will bring you back. This reasoning was used earlier to answer “How can you be deluded” when He brings living from dead.\n\nA misunderstanding is to view worship as occasional ritual detached from justice. This verse joins them: Qist in dealings and ikhlas on the prayer mat are both commanded by the same Lord, showing that social uprightness and devotional sincerity are one command. For practice, it means dressing properly for worship rather than the pre-Islamic naked circumambulation, eating from lawful adornments without waste, and remembering that the One who first created in truth will recreate with truth on the Day the weighing will be just, when guidance that was offered is weighed and ranks assigned.",
  "30": "“He has guided some, while others are destined to stray. They have taken devils as their masters instead of Allah— thinking they are ˹rightly˺ guided” explains the division that results from the upright command just stated, linking back to the earlier principle that guidance belongs to Allah who opens chests to Islam and tightens others as if climbing into the sky. The verse says “He has guided some,” or “fareeqan hada,” a group whom He guided, and “while others are destined to stray,” or “wa fareeqan haqqa alaihim al-dalalah,” another group upon whom straying has become justly established. The wording “haqqa” indicates straying became their due after choices: they turned away when insights came, refused to believe at first, leaned toward elegant deception because they disbelieved in the Hereafter, and made deeds seem appealing.\n\nThe reason is then given: “They have taken devils as their masters instead of Allah—thinking they are rightly guided,” or “innahum ittakhathoo al-shayateena awliya’a min doon Allahi wa yahsaboona annahum muhtadoon.” This is the alliance warned about where devils become allies of those who disbelieve, and where wrongdoers are made allies of one another because of misdeeds. “Awliya” here are guardians, protectors, authorities obeyed, exactly the guardians the Book warned not to take besides Him. The tragedy is self-deception, or “yahsaboona,” they reckon, suppose, think they are guided, like those earlier who followed assumption and beautified killing of children thinking it was commanded.\n\nThis self-perception shows that feeling guided is not proof of guidance. The true proof is conformity to what was sent down, to the Book perfectly explained whose Word is perfected in truth and justice. The verse links to the repeated note that most people who follow assumption lie, and that elegant words of deception tilt hearts that are already disbelieving.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to conclude that if guidance and straying are by Allah’s will, human effort is irrelevant. The Quran pairs will with reason: those who took devils as masters are destined to stray because they chose that guardianship. Another mistake is to think sincerity alone guarantees guidance while following devils. The verse shows sincerity misdirected to devils still counts as straying. The safeguard is to test guardianship against the Lord’s command of qist and ikhlas and against the prophetic warning not to be deceived as parents were when the enemy swore he was a sincere advisor. Choosing alliance with the All-Hearing, All-Knowing over the watching unseen soldiers keeps one among the guided who will have the Home of Peace rather than among those who only think they are guided while wandering blindly in defiance.",
  "31": "“O Children of Adam! Dress properly whenever you are at worship. Eat and drink, but do not waste. Surely He does not like the wasteful” gives practical instructions for honoring the body and bounty that follow the warning about mistaking devils for guides. After saying He has guided some and others are destined to stray because they took devils as masters thinking they are rightly guided, the Quran addresses all descendants with counsel that restores the two coverings introduced earlier: clothing that covers nakedness and adornment, and righteousness that is the best clothing. The call “O Children of Adam” recalls that parents were first given beautiful provision in Paradise and later leaves to cover after tasting, establishing dress as part of human dignity.\n\nThe first command, “Dress properly whenever you are at worship,” or “khuthoo zeenatakum inda kulli masjid,” means take your adornment at every place of prostration, every prayer and every act of worship. “Zeenah” is beauty, adornment, clean and presentable clothing that reflects giving thanks for establish-ment and livelihood. This corrects the shameful deed where pagans circumambulated naked claiming Allah commanded it, and whose claim was answered that He never commands shame. Proper dress at worship shows uprightness, or “qist,” and dedication with sincerity, turning the face to the Originator who is never absent.\n\nThe second command balances nourishment: “Eat and drink, but do not waste,” or “wa kuloo wa ishrabou wa la tusrifoo.” The earlier permissions to eat clustered grain, gardens, and lawful cattle with His Name, and to pay dues at harvest but not waste, are condensed here. Eating and drinking are permitted as part of the means of livelihood seldom thanked; waste, or “israf,” is exceeding the bound by excess, neglect, or showing off, whether in food, clothing, or worship itself.\n\nThe seal, “Surely He does not like the wasteful,” or “innahu la yuhibbu al-musrifeen,” is the same phrase used for harvest waste. The Lord whose establishment and livelihood should evoke gratitude does not love excess that forgets gratitude. A common error is to view ascetic nakedness as piety or wasteful indulgence as gratitude. The verse corrects both toward the middle path of the straight way: dress with dignity for worship, enjoy lawful provision thankfully, and avoid waste, wearing both the adornment of fabric and the better clothing of mindfulness that remains aware of the gathering before Him.",
  "32": "“Who has forbidden the adornments and lawful provisions Allah has brought forth for His servants? Say, ‘They are for the enjoyment of the believers in this worldly life, but they will be exclusively theirs on the Day of Judgment. This is how We make Our revelations clear for people of knowledge’” confronts the invented taboos that made some adornment and food seem unlawful. The previous verse taught to dress properly for worship, eat and drink without waste, and the earlier catalogue exposed those who said cattle and crops are reserved and who divided shares for idols. Now the Quran asks a rhetorical question that expects no human legislator: “Who has forbidden the adornments and lawful provisions Allah has brought forth,” or “man harrama zeenata Allahi allati akhraja li-ibadihi wat-tayyibati min al-rizq.” “Zeenah” includes lawful clothing and beauty He sent down for covering and adornment, and “tayyibat min al-rizq” means good, pure, wholesome provisions, like gardens trellised and untrellised, olives and pomegranates, cattle fit for labour and small graziers. “Akhraja” emphasizes He brought them forth, not devils or associates.\n\nThe answer is implied: no one has authority to forbid what Allah made lawful without knowledge, just as the greatest wrong is to fabricate lies against Him about lawful and forbidden, as asked earlier “Has He forbidden the two males or the two females?” The verse then announces the distribution: “Say, They are for the enjoyment of the believers in this worldly life, but they will be exclusively theirs on the Day of Judgment,” or “qul hiya lillatheena amanoo fil-hayati al-dunya khalisatan yawma al-qiyamah.” In this world, disbelievers may also enjoy adornment and provision by mercy, but in the Hereafter delight is purified, exclusive, unmixed with fear or reckoning, like the Home of Peace versus Hell’s boiling drink.\n\nThe closing, “This is how We make Our revelations clear for people of knowledge,” or “kathalika nufassilu al-ayat li-qawmin ya‘lamoon,” repeats the pattern of varying signs for those who know, comprehend, remember. Knowledge here means learning that the detailed Book is judge, not elegant deception.\n\nA misunderstanding is to think piety requires rejecting beauty. The Quran shows adornment and tayyibat are brought forth for servants to use gratefully, within justice and without waste, distinguishing true prohibitions like open and secret indecencies and associating partners, which the next verse lists, from invented fabrications. The informed person therefore enjoys gratefully now and hopes for exclusive joy later where no provision is left behind as when they came alone.",
  "33": "“My Lord has only forbidden open and secret indecencies, sinfulness, unjust aggression, associating ˹others˺ with Allah ˹in worship˺—a practice He has never authorized—and attributing to Allah what you do not know” lists the true prohibitions that answer the question who has forbidden adornment and lawful provision. After affirming that adornments and wholesome provisions are brought forth for believers to enjoy in this world and exclusively in the next, the Quran clarifies what is actually forbidden, contrasting the short, clear divine list with the pagans’ long invented divisions.\n\nThe first category, “open and secret indecencies,” or “al-fawahisha ma zahara minha wa ma batana,” covers shameful acts visible and hidden, as earlier commanded not to come near indecencies openly or secretly and not to follow Satan’s footsteps that led to stripping cover. “Sinfulness,” or “al-ithm,” is every act of disobedience that earns punishment, earlier commanded to shun all sin open and secret. “Unjust aggression,” or “al-baghyu bi-ghairi al-haqq,” means transgression without right, like spilling sacred life without legal right, usurping orphan wealth, or giving short measure, all violations of uprightness, or qist.\n\nThe list then reaches the gravest two: “associating others with Allah—a practice He has never authorized,” or “an tushrikoo billahi ma lam yunazzil bihi sultana,” the same “sultan,” authoritative proof, mentioned when Abraham asked how to fear associate-gods without authorization. Shirk is greatest because it nullifies good deeds and makes the scale light. Finally, “and attributing to Allah what you do not know,” or “wa an taqooloo ala Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon,” captures the method behind invented taboos: speaking about lawful and unlawful without knowledge, like dividing wombs between males and females or claiming shameful deeds are commanded.\n\nThis catalogue mirrors the earlier code: do not associate partners, do not kill children, do not approach indecencies, do not kill the sacred soul, do not come near orphan wealth except to enhance, give full measure, speak justly, fulfill covenant, all paths whose straightness leads to mindfulness. A common mistake is to think religion forbids the beautiful while allowing these core wrongs. The verse reverses the picture: beauty and tayyibat are permitted for thanksgiving, while the listed indecencies, sins, aggressions, shirk, and speaking without knowledge are the true fences that the All-Hearing, All-Knowing never authorized to cross.",
  "34": "“For each community there is an appointed term. When their time arrives, they can neither delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it” places the newly listed true prohibitions within the framework of time that has governed this surah’s warnings. After cataloguing what the Lord actually forbids, from open indecencies to speaking without knowledge, the Quran reminds that persistently crossing those lines as a community has a limit. The phrase “For each community there is an appointed term,” or “wa li-kulli ummatin ajal,” uses “ummah,” a community bound by shared life and belief, and “ajal,” a fixed term, like the appointed term earlier described for living, dying, and being resurrected from earth, and like the term human and jinn allies admitted they had reached. Just as individuals have a place to live and a place to be laid to rest, communities collectively have a span within which warnings are varied, signs are made distinct, and proof is made conclusive.\n\nThe precision is then stressed: “When their time arrives, they can neither delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it,” or “fa-itha ja’a ajaluhum la yasta’khiroona sa‘atan wa la yastaqdimoon.” Hour, or “sa‘ah,” here means the briefest moment. Human cleverness, large numbers, and elegant conspiracy, like the greatest wicked in every society who plot without perceiving they plot against themselves, cannot move the appointed hour by a heartbeat. This matches the law that your Lord is swift in punishment but also all-forgiving, and that He is never unaware of deeds.\n\nThis verse links directly to earlier warnings that torment took societies at night or midday suddenly, that their only cry was we were wrongdoers too late, and that what you are promised will certainly come with no escape. It also balances the delay given to Iblis until the appointed Day, showing his respite is itself bounded by an ajal.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat community terms as distant and irrelevant to personal conduct. The Quran shows collective terms are built from individual choices to follow what was sent down or to take other guardians, to eat the tayyibat thankfully or to forbid them without knowledge. Contemporary communities that beautify shameful deeds and attribute lies to Allah accumulate toward their term exactly as past communities did. Remembering the unmovable appointment encourages hastening toward the best clothing of righteousness, sincere worship, and gratitude for provision before the hour that can neither be delayed nor hastened renders belief after it useless for those who did not believe earlier.",
  "35": "“O children of Adam! When messengers from among yourselves come to you reciting My revelations—whoever shuns evil and mends their ways, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve” returns to the universal call after establishing that each community has an unmovable appointed term. The previous verse said when the time arrives it cannot be delayed or advanced; this verse explains the purpose of the time given. The call “O children of Adam,” as earlier with clothing and not letting Satan deceive, gathers all descendants without distinction, echoing the origin from a single soul. “When messengers from among yourselves come to you reciting My revelations,” or “ya’tiyannakum rusulun minkum yaqussoona alaikum ayati,” means messengers sharing your nature, language, and concerns, as earlier it was said messengers from among you proclaiming revelations and warning of the meeting. Their task is “qass,” recounting signs with sure knowledge, the same varied signs made clear for people of knowledge.\n\nThe condition for success after hearing is then stated: “whoever shuns evil and mends their ways,” or “fa-man ittaqa wa aslaha.” Like earlier “whoever believes and does good, there will be no fear for them,” and “whoever commits evil ignorantly then repents and mends,” this pairs guarding, or “taqwa,” shunning what the true prohibitions listed, with “islah,” repairing, making good after fault. It is not perfection without lapse but guarding and patching.\n\nThe promise is luminous: “there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve,” or “fa-la khawfun alaihim wa la hum yahzanoon,” repeated throughout the Book for the heavy-scaled, the guided, and the entrusted who never disbelieve. No fear of the appointed term’s sudden torment, no fear of the gathering before the True Master, and no grief for worldly play left behind, because the Home of Peace awaits.\n\nThe verse contrasts the plight of those who do not guard and mend, whose term ends with the cry we were wrongdoers and whose testimony will be they were disbelievers. A common mistake is to think messengers came only for ancient societies. The Quran frames their coming as recurring until the term, with the final messenger warning the Mother of Cities and around it with the blessed Book so no one can say we were unaware. The practical invitation is to respond while the term still allows: shun the listed indecencies and associations, mend what was approached near of sin open and secret, and hold fast to the straight path so fear and grief are replaced by the security earlier promised to those whose faith is not tarnished.",
  "36": "“But those who receive Our revelations with denial and arrogance will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever” gives the counterpart to the promise that whoever shuns evil and mends will have no fear nor grief. The previous verse opened hope for children of Adam when messengers come reciting revelations; this verse states the outcome for the opposite response. The phrase “receive Our revelations with denial and arrogance,” or “allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina wa-istakbaroo anha,” joins two attitudes already described at length: denying, or “taktheeb,” calling signs fables, attributing adornment laws to Allah without knowledge, and arrogantly deeming oneself above surrender, like Iblis who said I am better and like those whose hearts were hardened and veils cast.\n\nThe sentence uses intensified arrogance, “istakbaroo,” to consider oneself too great to submit, refusing to prostrate to the command, refusing to eat what Allah’s Name is mentioned upon, refusing to approach worship properly adorned. The recompense is “will be the residents of the Fire,” or “ula’ika as-haabu al-Nar,” companions, inhabitants who stay, abiding as earlier the alliance of jinn and humans said the Fire is your home to stay forever, and “they will be there forever,” or “hum fiha khalidoon,” lingering, not a passing visit. This matches the warning that belief will not benefit after your Lord’s major signs arrive for those who did not believe earlier or did no good through faith.\n\nThe verse also echoes the just weighing: light scales doom themselves for wrongfully denying signs, while heavy scales belong to the successful. Residency in Fire is the light-scale outcome made vivid.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think denial is limited to verbal rejection. The Quran’s pairing shows denial plus arrogance includes lived rejection, like taking devils as masters thinking they are rightly guided, forbidding adornments Allah brought forth, and committing shameful deeds while claiming forefathers and Allah commanded them. The alternative was earlier offered: follow what was sent down from your Lord and do not take other guardians; how seldom you are mindful. The door remains open to shun and mend before the term arrives that can neither be delayed nor advanced, before the day the account with sure knowledge leaves confession as the only word: we were disbelievers. Choosing humble reception now places one among those whose scales are heavy rather than among those whose arrogance lights the Fire’s companionship.",
  "37": "“Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His revelations? They will receive what is destined for them, until Our messenger-angels arrive to take their souls, asking them, ‘Where are those false gods you used to invoke besides Allah?’ They will cry, ‘They have failed us,’ and they will confess against themselves that they were indeed disbelievers” intensifies the verdict on denial with arrogance by naming it the greatest wrong and then depicting its boundary at death. The question “Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His revelations?” echoes the repeated refrain about the worst injustice, earlier tied to claiming false prophecy and attributing taboos without knowledge. Fabrication, or “iftara,” includes saying Allah commanded shame, dividing provision for idols unfairly, and claiming adornments are forbidden. Denial, or “taktheeb,” includes calling the blessed, detailed Book fables.\n\nThe phrase “They will receive what is destined for them,” or “ula’ika yanaluhum naseebuhum min al-kitab,” means their share written in the clear record that none can change will reach them exactly as decreed until the appointed term for their community arrives. The portion may be provision in this world, respite, or the recorded deeds, but it continues only until the threshold: “until Our messenger-angels arrive to take their souls,” or “hatta itha ja’at-hum rusuluna yatawaffawnahum,” the same angels earlier described as never neglecting, who take without delay and who will stretch hands to the fabricators saying give up your souls today you will be rewarded with disgrace.\n\nThe angels’ question exposes the lie: “Where are those false gods you used to invoke besides Allah?” or “aina ma kuntum tad‘oona min dooni Allah.” The question is the same as posed on the Day all are gathered, “Where are your partners you used to claim?” The expected rescue never appears. The answer is stripped: “They have failed us,” or “dalloo anna,” they have strayed from us, they led us astray and then vanished, as earlier said fabricated gods will fail and claims will stray. The closing, “and they will confess against themselves that they were indeed disbelievers,” or “wa shahidoo ala anfusihim annahum kanu kafireen,” mirrors the gathering where all testify against themselves that worldly life deluded them, confirming the earlier pattern that denial born of arrogance ends in forced confession at the moment scale weight is sealed.\n\nA common self-deception is to trust in naseeb, allotted share, without preparing for its end. The verse warns the share ends when the takers arrive, asking the unanswerable question that reveals who truly owns guidance and rescue.",
  "38": "“Enter the Fire along with the evil groups of jinn and humans that preceded you. Whenever a group enters Hell, it will curse the preceding one until they are all gathered inside, the followers will say about their leaders, ‘Our Lord! They have misled us, so multiply their torment in the Fire.’ He will answer, ‘It has already been multiplied for all, but you do not know’” describes the social agony that follows the solitary testimony at death, when fabricated lies meet their destination. The previous verse showed soul-takers asking where false gods are, and the dying confessing they failed us. Now the command is collective: “Enter the Fire along with the evil groups of jinn and humans that preceded you,” or “qala udkhuloo fin ar an qablakum min al-jinn wal-ins.” “Umam” means communities, groups that shared a way, like the “umam amthalukum” earlier for animal communities, but here grouped by misguidance: devilish humans and jinn who whispered, most wicked who conspired in every town, wrongdoers made allies of one another.\n\nInside, the social bond that beautified alliance shatters: “Whenever a group enters Hell, it will curse the preceding one,” or “kullama dakhalat ummatun la‘anat ukhtaha.” “La‘anat” means to curse, to ask for distance from mercy. Those who once thought they were rightly guided by taking devils as masters now see guidance as misguidance and love as enmity. The scene continues “until they are all gathered inside,” or “hatta itha iddarakoo fiha jamee‘an,” until they are piled together, followers and followed all caught in the same Fire whose filling with you and your followers was promised to Iblis.\n\nThe followers plead: “Our Lord! They have misled us, so multiply their torment in the Fire,” or “Rabbana ha’ula’i adalloona fa-atihim athaban di‘fan.” “Di‘fan” means doubled, multiplied. The answer is sobering: “It has already been multiplied for all, but you do not know,” or “li-kullin di‘fun wa lakin la ta‘lamoon.” Each group’s torment is already multiplied, followers for obeying, leaders for misleading, as wrongdoers plotted only against themselves yet also earned burdens for misguiding others.\n\nA common misunderstanding is that blame can be shifted to leaders as sincere excuse. The Quran shows leaders and followers both receive multiplied recompense because alliance was chosen when devils were taken as masters thinking they were guided. The next verse shows leaders retort “you were no better than us, taste what you committed,” sealing mutual responsibility before the Just weighing that never wrongs.",
  "39": "“Then the leaders will say to their followers, ‘You were no better than us! So taste the torment for what you used to commit’” continues the exchange inside the Fire after followers asked for doubled punishment for leaders who misled them and were told all already have doubled portions but you do not know. The previous scene showed groups cursing predecessors as they entered. Now the leaders answer. The phrase “You were no better than us,” or “fa-ma kana lakum alaina min fadl,” means you were not superior to us, you were not favored over us by guidance or innocence. This rebuts the followers’ implied claim that they were victims without choice. Leaders point out that followers willingly accepted elegant deception, followed assumptions, forbade adornments Allah brought forth, and said Allah commanded shame, while guidance had been sent down and insight had come with sure knowledge. Both sides turned away from the reminder, took devils as masters, and thought they were rightly guided.\n\nThe command “So taste the torment for what you used to commit,” or “fa-thooqoo al-athaba bima kuntum taksiboon,” uses “thooqoo,” taste, as earlier the punishment for disbelief was told to be tasted and as dwellers of Hell will taste boiling drink and flame cover. “Bima kuntum taksiboon” ties directly to ranks according to deeds and earning sin open and secret. Taste implies immediate, personal experience that leaves no doubt, just as exposure of nakedness was immediate when the tree was tasted.\n\nThis exchange dismantles the comforting illusion that wrong can be outsourced to charismatic figures, whether the greatest wicked in a town who conspired, or human and jinn whisperers who beautified killing children and dividing livestock. Alliance of wrongdoers made allies of one another becomes mutual accusation inside the Fire, completing the pattern where ties are broken and claims stray on the Day each arrives alone leaving behind what was given.\n\nA common self-deception is to wait for a leader to take responsibility before deciding. The Quran shows each soul will be told to taste for its own earning; following is itself an earning. The contrast is the Home of Peace where bitterness is removed from hearts and rivers flow beneath, awarded to those who chose to follow what was sent down from their Lord and not take other guardians, walking with a light among people rather than with a leader into cursing. The lesson is to examine allegiances now, before the question “Where are your gods?” becomes unanswerable.",
  "40": "“Surely those who receive our revelations with denial and arrogance, the gates of heaven will not be opened for them, nor will they enter Paradise until a camel passes through the eye of a needle. This is how We reward the wicked” intensifies the torment after the mutual cursing of leaders and followers, sealing the barrier between those who were heavy with belief and those who were light through denying signs. The opening repeats the formula “those who receive Our revelations with denial and arrogance,” or “allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina wa-istakbaroo anha,” the same pair that leads to abiding in Fire. The first consequence is given: “the gates of heaven will not be opened for them,” or “la tufattahu lahum abwabu al-sama’.” This has been understood in two united ways: their deeds and prayers do not ascend as good deeds via the gates through which provision is sent down, and at death their souls do not ascend through the gates as souls of believers do, but are cast down, as reported in the hadith of the soul’s ascent where angels do not open for a disbelieving soul. In both senses, ascent is blocked.\n\nThe second consequence uses the most graphic impossibility in everyday imagery: “nor will they enter Paradise until a camel passes through the eye of a needle,” or “hatta yalija al-jamalu fi sammi al-khiyat.” The camel is among the largest animals the Arabs knew, the needle’s eye among the smallest openings. The image says entrance is impossible by the ordinary norms of measure and justice that the Quran earlier commanded to keep and of which Allah perfects truth and justice. Some recitations read “jummal” as thick rope, sharpening the image of the impossible thread, but the message is the same: denial and arrogance make the scale light beyond remedy after the opportunity to shun evil and mend has passed.\n\nThe verse closes, “This is how We reward the wicked,” or “wa kathalika najzi al-mujrimeen,” those who cut themselves off, the same who were called wicked when the way was made distinct and whose reward is dreadful punishment for turning away.\n\nA common mistaken hope is that afterlife entry may be achieved through lineage, intercession without permission, or delayed profession when signs arrive. The verse shows the gate is conditioned on reception of revelations with submission and humility. For the living, it urges receiving revelations with attentive response, establishing prayer, dressing properly for worship, eating without waste, and guarding orphan wealth, thereby keeping deeds that ascend through open gates toward the Home of Peace rather than being rejected at the gate.",
  "41": "“Hell will be their bed; flames will be their cover. This is how We reward the wrongdoers” completes the description of the wicked’s abode after the impossible camel-and-needle barrier, giving a tactile image of entrapment after the gates of heaven are shut. The previous verse said those who received revelations with denial and arrogance will not have gates opened nor enter Paradise until an impossibility occurs. Now the Quran describes where they do reside. The phrase “Hell will be their bed,” or “lahum min Jahannama mihad,” uses “mihad,” a prepared bed, a mattress laid out beneath. But this is not restful night made for stillness; it is a bed that is punishment, where the surface itself is torment. The next phrase, “flames will be their cover,” or “wa min fawqihim ghawash,” means coverings, canopies, overlays from above, flames that blanket and press. Beneath is Hell, above is fire, enveloping from both sides as earlier torment could come from above or below and as societies were seized at night or midday while sleeping.\n\nThe image of bed and cover echoes the earlier gifts of establishment on earth with livelihood and clothing to cover nakedness and as adornment, where the best clothing is righteousness. Those who refused gratitude for establishment and rejected the best clothing, taking devils as masters thinking they are guided and committing shameful deeds while attributing them to Allah, find the opposite furnishing: a bed and cover that expose rather than adorn, that scorch rather than shelter.\n\nThe verse seals with the same formula: “This is how We reward the wrongdoers,” or “wa kathalika najzi al-zalimeen,” the unjust who placed things where they do not belong, who associated partners, forbade tayyibat, and attributed lies. It parallels the reward for the wicked in the previous verse, showing that “mujrim” and “zalim” converge on the same denial with arrogance that lightened the scale.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to imagine punishment as distant abstraction. The bed-and-cover image brings it close to daily experience of sleeping and dressing, reminding that the respite of night for rest and the mercy of clothing were signs that could have led to thankfulness. Choosing instead to follow elegant deception makes the once-merciful night and covering become agents of justice. The contrast with the believers’ reward, rivers beneath them with bitterness removed from hearts and the announcement that this Paradise is awarded for what they used to do, makes the urgency tangible: follow what was sent down from your Lord now, before the appointed term that can neither be delayed nor advanced arrives.",
  "42": "“As for those who believe and do good—We never require of any soul more than what it can afford—it is they who will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever” presents the counterpart to the bed of Hell and cover of flames, following the same pairing logic that contrasted heavy and light scales. The verse opens with the beloved Quranic pair “those who believe and do good,” or “allatheena amanoo wa ‘amiloo al-salihat,” belief, or “iman,” as trust in Allah alone without tarnishing faith with shirk, and good deeds, or “salihat,” as repairing and mending, like shunning evil, establishing prayer, guarding orphan wealth, giving full measure, speaking justly, and following the blessed Book. This echoes earlier promises: whoever believes and does good, there will be no fear for them nor will they grieve, and whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded tenfold.\n\nThe parenthesis, “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford,” or “la nukallifu nafsan illa wus‘aha,” is a merciful insertion that reassures. The same principle appeared after commands to fulfil measure and speak justly. No soul is burdened beyond capacity; the heavy scale does not demand angelic perfection but sincere, consistent effort within ability to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians. This prevents despair that the straight path is unattainable.\n\nThe recompense is then announced: “they will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever,” or “ula’ika as-haabu al-Jannah hum fiha khalidoon.” “As-haab,” companions, suggests intimacy and ownership, as earlier as-haab al-Nar for the wicked. The dwelling is the Home of Peace promised to those mindful, where bitterness is removed, rivers flow beneath, and satisfaction is exclusive beyond this world’s mixed enjoyment.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think Paradise requires perfection without error. The verse, by pairing belief and good with the capacity principle, shows that sincere, mindful effort, even with repentance after ignorance that mends, suffices with Allah’s mercy, which is infinite and outweighs punishment that is swift only for the persistent wicked. Another mistake imagines good deeds are worthless if small; the tenfold multiplication and the merciful standard ensure that steady small goods, like covering with proper adornment for worship, eating wholesome provision without waste, and wearing righteousness, accumulate toward eternal residence before the term arrives.",
  "43": "“We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts. Rivers will flow under their feet. And they will say, ‘Praise be to Allah for guiding us to this. We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us. The messengers of our Lord had certainly come with the truth’” describes the interior and exterior bliss of those residents of Paradise whose faith and good deeds, within capacity, made the scale heavy. The previous verse promised those believers eternal companionship with Paradise; now the experience of entry is unfolded.\n\nThe first mercy is inward: “We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts,” or “wa naza‘na ma fi sudoorihim min ghill.” “Ghill” means hidden ill will, rancour, envy, lingering grudge, the residue that even sincere believers might have carried from disputes in worldly life or from being divided by sects. The One who knows what breasts conceal and who encompasses every leaf and grain extracts it with subtlety, or “lutf,” leaving hearts spacious as they were opened to Islam, not tight as if climbing into the sky. This purified heart makes peace complete, as Paradise is the Home of Peace.\n\nThe outward panorama follows: “Rivers will flow under their feet,” or “tajri min tahtihim al-anhar,” beneath elevated dwellings, an image of effortless abundance contrasting with the desert’s search for water and with the affliction of thirst. The provision is continuous, without need to earn, unlike earthly livelihood seldom thanked.\n\nTheir own speech completes the scene: “Praise be to Allah for guiding us to this. We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us,” or “alhamdulillahi allathee hadana li-hatha wa ma kunna li-nahtadiya lawla an hadana Allah.” “Alhamdulillah” as first words in Paradise echo the opening of the previous surah’s praise for the Lord of all worlds and the closing praise after wrongdoers were uprooted. The acknowledgment that guidance itself was a gift matches the earlier principle that He has guided some while others are left to stray after taking devils as masters thinking they were guided. Here the guided confess the source.\n\nThe final witness seals proof: “The messengers of our Lord had certainly come with the truth,” or “laqad ja’at rusulu Rabbina bil-haqq,” confirming that the blessed, detailed Book and all earlier warnings were true. Then it will be announced, “This is Paradise awarded to you for what you used to do,” acknowledging both divine guidance and human deeds weighed tenfold, neither canceling the other, merging mercy and justice in the perfected Word.",
  "44": "“The residents of Paradise will call out to the residents of the Fire, ‘We have certainly found our Lord’s promise to be true. Have you too found your Lord’s promise to be true?’ They will reply, ‘Yes, we have!’ Then a caller will announce to both, ‘May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers’” dramatizes the moment of final verification that follows the removal of bitterness and the rivers flowing beneath. The previous verse showed the guided praising Allah for guidance after entry. Now communication stretches across the barrier between the two homes. The phrase “The residents of Paradise will call out to the residents of the Fire,” or “wa nada as-haabu al-Jannati as-haaba al-Nar,” shows proximity yet absolute separation, as seen earlier when groups entered Hell cursing predecessors and followers. The question is framed as shared discovery: “We have certainly found our Lord’s promise to be true. Have you too found your Lord’s promise to be true?” This mirrors the earlier dynamic of promise given to both sides: what you are promised will certainly come and you will have no escape, the gathering about which there is no doubt, the better Home for the mindful, the dreadful punishment for the wicked. On that Day, each finds the promise realized exactly as described by the Most Subtle, All-Aware, never absent.\n\nThe answer is unanimous and unwillingly sincere: “They will reply, ‘Yes, we have!’” or “qaloo na‘am.” Earlier they confessed “they have failed us” when asked where false gods are, and all confessed they were disbelievers; now they admit the promise’s truth with no decorated speech left to incline the heart.\n\nThen a formal announcement seals the divide: “Then a caller will announce to both, ‘May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers,’” or “fa-aththana mu’aththinun bainahum an la‘natu Allahi ala al-zalimeen.” “La‘nah” means expulsion from mercy, a curse that corresponds to “We have made the devils allies of those who disbelieve” and to the gates of heaven not being opened. The announcer identifies the wrongdoers further in the next verse as those who hindered from Allah’s Way, made it appear crooked, and disbelieved in the Hereafter.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think promises are metaphorical. This dialogue shows they are factual and will be acknowledged by both sides. For the living, it urges treating the Lord’s promise as already verified in advance, as the people of knowledge do when revelations are made clear, choosing the adornment of righteousness and the Book sent down to follow while the invitation still replaces condemnation.",
  "45": "“those who hindered ˹others˺ from Allah’s Way, strived to make it ˹appear˺ crooked, and disbelieved in the Hereafter” identifies the wrongdoers upon whom the caller’s condemnation just announced will rest, continuing the conversation between residents of Paradise and residents of the Fire. The previous verse had the announcer say “May Allah’s condemnation be upon the wrongdoers.” This verse explains who those wrongdoers are with three traits that have been displayed throughout both surahs. First, “those who hindered others from Allah’s Way,” or “allatheena yasuddoona an sabili Allah,” as earlier those who turned others away from the Prophet and distance themselves, who said do not listen to this Quran, and who vigorously whispered with elegant deception to incline hearts. Hindering the Way is not limited to physical blocking; it includes mocking the Book so listeners doubt, making faith appear as play, and beautifying killing children as piety.\n\nSecond, “strived to make it appear crooked,” or “wa yabghoonaha iwaja,” sought to make the straight path seem bent, twisted, flawed. The straight path was described as “your Lord’s Path perfectly straight” and “My Path perfectly straight so follow it and do not follow other ways for they will divide you.” The hinderers work to make that straight path look crooked by presenting adornments brought forth for servants as if forbidden, by presenting shameless deeds as if commanded, and by presenting devils as if they were sincere advisors who swore they are. They invert the values, calling truth ancient fables and decorated falsehood wisdom.\n\nThird, “and disbelieved in the Hereafter,” or “wa hum bil-akhirati kafiroon,” the root of inclination toward elegant deception because, as earlier, hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter are receptive to it, pleased with it, and persistent in it. Disbelief in the Hereafter makes hindered, crooked-making attractive since the meeting that gives life weight is denied.\n\nThe sequence reveals a progression: disbelief in the meeting dulls the scale, beautified hindering hardens the heart, and making the straight seem crooked leads followers away until the appointed term arrives unmoved. A common mistake is to think hindering is only done by powerful tyrants. The Quran shows it is also done by anyone who spreads decorated lies that make the path look crooked, confirming that alliance of wrongdoers spreads. The next verse shows a barrier between the destinations, with people on the heights recognizing both sides, hoping to join Paradise while praying not to be joined with the wrongdoers, illustrating that awareness of these three traits is itself guidance toward not being among them.",
  "46": "“There will be a barrier between Paradise and Hell. And on the heights ˹of that barrier˺ will be people who will recognize ˹the residents of˺ both by their appearance. They will call out to the residents of Paradise, ‘Peace be upon you!’ They will have not yet entered Paradise, but eagerly hope to” depicts the striking middle place that follows the mutual call between the two homes and the caller’s condemnation upon wrongdoers who hindered and made the Way crooked while disbelieving in the Hereafter. The phrase “There will be a barrier between Paradise and Hell,” or “wa bainahuma hijab,” describes a partition, a veil that separates the two ultimate destinations so their conditions do not mix, just as in this world the Book separates the way of the wicked made distinct from the straight path. Upon the heights of that barrier, or “wa ala al-a‘raf rijal,” are people on the elevations, the “a‘raf,” plural of heights, from which they can see both sides. The Quran says they will recognize residents of both by their appearance, or “siymah,” marks. Believers’ faces shine with light given to walk among people and the ablution of prayer, while deniers are marked by dust and constriction.\n\nTheir greeting is telling: “They will call out to the residents of Paradise, ‘Peace be upon you!’” or “nadoo as-haaba al-Jannati an salamun alaikum.” “Salam” is the same greeting the believers in Our revelations are told to receive, “Peace be upon you! Your Lord has taken upon Himself to be Merciful.” Those on the heights therefore speak as well-wishers of the blessed, already inclined toward mercy, yet “They will have not yet entered Paradise, but eagerly hope to,” or “lam yadkhulooha wa hum yatma‘oon.” Hope, or “tama‘,” means longing expectation with good anticipation, reflecting that their scales may be balanced, with good and bad equal, or with uncertain weight, awaiting divine mercy that the next verses show will indeed admit them after acknowledging the humble believers the arrogant had scorned.\n\nClassical scholars differ on their precise identity, some saying they are people whose good and evil equalize, others that they are eminent just witnesses, but all agree they illustrate the theme of rank according to deeds and of the Word perfected in truth and justice where not even a leaf falls unknown.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to imagine the barrier means indifference. The scene shows active recognition, greeting, and hope, teaching that after death identities remain and recognition persists, as earlier the dead given a light walk among people recognizable by their illumination. For the living, the barrier encourages striving to be among those greeted with peace due to following what was sent down, not being deceived as parents were when cover was stripped, so the hope of the heights becomes entry into the Home of Peace where bitterness is removed.",
  "47": "“When their eyes will turn towards the residents of Hell, they will pray, ‘Our Lord! Do not join us with the wrongdoing people’” continues the scene on the heights that began with the barrier between Paradise and Hell. The previous verse said those on the heights recognize both groups by appearance and call to the residents of Paradise with “Peace be upon you” while eagerly hoping to enter. Now the gaze shifts. The Arabic says “wa itha surifat absaruhum tilqaa as-haab al-nar,” when their eyes are turned toward the companions of the Fire. The verb “surifat” suggests the eyes are turned, perhaps unwillingly or by divine direction, not that they seek to look at punishment. The sight is sobering: they see the same wrongdoers just described as hinderers who made the straight seem crooked, whose scales were light, whose bed is Hell with flames as cover. Immediately the people of the heights, who themselves have not yet entered Paradise and are still in a place of hope and suspense, make a supplication that reveals their awareness: “Our Lord! Do not join us with the wrongdoing people,” or “Rabbana la taj‘alna ma‘a al-qawmi al-zalimeen.” They ask not to be placed with the unjust, the associators, the deniers described earlier.\n\nThis prayer mirrors the earlier prayer of the righteous after being saved from the Fire, and it teaches that even those near Paradise remain humble, fearful of being counted among the wrongdoers. The phrase “wrongdoing people,” or “al-qawmi al-zalimeen,” connects to every earlier use: those who fabricated lies, divided faith, denied signs with arrogance, and took adornment as forbidden while permitting shame. The Heights people recognize that the only thing separating them from that company is mercy, not a claim of perfection.\n\nThe verse shows that sight on that Day is not neutral. Looking at Paradise brings greeting of peace; looking at Hell produces urgent seeking of refuge. The turning of the eyes becomes a lesson for the living: keep your gaze toward what you hope to join, and when you see the fate of arrogance and heedlessness, renew the same prayer now, before eyes are turned by compulsion. Classical commentators note that this supplication was preserved for believers to use in this world whenever they witness corruption, reminding them to ask Allah to keep them distinct from the unjust while they still can choose to follow what was sent down and not take other guardians.",
  "48": "“Those on the heights will call out to some ˹tyrants in the Fire˺, who they will recognize by their appearance, saying, ‘Your large numbers and arrogance are of no use ˹today˺!’” deepens the conversation across the barrier. The previous verse showed those on the heights praying when their eyes turned to Hell, asking not to be joined with wrongdoers. Now they actively address a specific group inside the Fire, recognized by marks that have become unmistakable. The Arabic says “wa nada as-haabu al-a‘rafi rijalan ya‘rifoonahum bi-seemahum,” they will call men they know by their marks, their faces, their bearing. In this context, the recognized figures are tyrannical leaders whose large followings and high status in worldly life were their boast, the same chiefs who earlier said of poor believers “Has Allah favoured these” and who forbade what Allah provided.\n\nTheir rebuke is concise: “Your large numbers and arrogance are of no use today!” or “ma aghna ankum jam‘ukum wa ma kuntum tastakbiroon.” “Jam‘ukum,” your gathering, your amassing, includes wealth, tribal numbers, armies, and crowds that once seemed to guarantee security. “Ma kuntum tastakbiroon,” what you used to be arrogant about, includes pride over lineage, refusal to bow like Iblis, rejection of the messenger as a liar or fool, and dismissing poor believers as unworthy. Today, on the weighing when gates of heaven do not open for the arrogant, none of that accumulation helps, just as earlier it was said those fabricated gods will fail them and their large numbers cursed each other inside the Fire.\n\nThe verse mirrors earlier worldly boasts. In Mecca, chiefs mocked the small circle of believers and trusted their multitude; here the small circle on the heights questions the multitude in the Fire. It also echoes the question to the Heights people about the humble believers they swore would never be shown mercy, about to be answered with entry to Paradise.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think that status and numbers guarantee success before Allah. This verse shows the opposite: what counts is not jam‘ but taqwa, the righteous clothing that is best, the heavy scale built from belief and good within capacity. For the living, the Heights’ call teaches to value humility over haughtiness, to follow the detailed Book rather than the consensus of the powerful, and to remember that appearance which seems impressive may on that Day be the very mark of loss, while the humble faces mocked may be the ones told “Enter Paradise, no fear for you.”",
  "49": "“Are these ˹humble believers˺ the ones you swore would never be shown Allah’s mercy?’ ˹Finally, those on the heights will be told:˺ ‘Enter Paradise! There will be no fear for you, nor will you grieve’” brings the Heights episode to its merciful climax after the prayer not to be joined with wrongdoers and the rebuke to tyrants whose numbers and arrogance proved useless. The Heights people now turn to address the inhabitants of the Fire about the very believers those tyrants had scorned in worldly life. The question, “Are these humble believers the ones you swore would never be shown Allah’s mercy?” or “a-ha’ula’i allatheena aqsumtum la yanaluhum Allahu bi-rahmah,” quotes the oaths of the disbelieving elite who, as earlier said, asked “Has Allah favoured these poor believers out of all of us?” and who swore the weak would never attain divine favor. Their oaths were solemn, public, and confident, asserting that mercy is distributed by worldly standards.\n\nThe verb “aqsumtum” shows they swore by Allah Himself that He would withhold mercy from those they deemed lowly, the same poor who invoked their Lord morning and evening seeking His Face. The Quran now exposes the vanity of such oaths. Mercy is not granted by tribal council; it is the Lord who took mercy upon Himself and whose mercy is always close to the good-doers. The worldly verdict is reversed: those sworn to be excluded are the ones now about to be welcomed.\n\nThe final announcement resolves the suspense of the Heights: “Enter Paradise! There will be no fear for you, nor will you grieve,” or “udkhuloo al-jannata la khawfun alaikum wa la antum tahzanoon.” This is the same promise given to those who shun evil and mend ways, who believe and do good within capacity, and whose scales are heavy. Addressed here to both the humble believers and, according to most commentators, to the people of the Heights themselves after their recognition and supplication, it declares that hope mixed with fear has turned into secure entry.\n\nThe verse teaches that swearing others out of mercy is a perilous act of presumption. The Heights’ question humbles the arrogant by making their own former oath the evidence against them, while merciful entry humiliates no humble believer but honors them. For listeners, the lesson is to refrain from declaring who is beyond mercy, to keep to the straight path of humility, to wear adornment and enjoy tayyibat without extravagance, and to trust that the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who never was absent will admit whom He wills to the Home of Peace where bitterness is removed and rivers flow beneath.",
  "50": "“The residents of the Fire will then cry out to the residents of Paradise, ‘Aid us with some water or any provision Allah has granted you.’ They will reply, ‘Allah has forbidden both to the disbelievers’” presents the desperate reversal of provision after the Heights have been told to enter Paradise without fear or grief. The previous verses showed the tyrants’ numbers and arrogance proved useless and the humble believers admitted to mercy. Now those in the Fire, whose bed is Hell and cover is flames, make a plea across the barrier that once separated them by choice. The cry, “Aid us with some water or any provision Allah has granted you,” or “afeedoo alaina min al-ma’i aw mimma razaqakum Allah,” is humble and specific. After a life where they forbade adornments Allah brought forth, divided crops for idols, and took faith as play, they now beg for the simplest tayyibat they had restricted in others and for any rizq that in Paradise flows as rivers beneath. The word “afeedoo” means pour, give abundantly, as if any amount would relieve scorching thirst.\n\nThe reply is firm and explanatory: “Allah has forbidden both to the disbelievers,” or “inna Allaha harramahuma ala al-kafireen.” The same verb “harrama” used for livestock taboos and for the true prohibitions of indecencies and shirk is now used for the final prohibition: water and provision of Paradise are forbidden to those who rejected the giver in the world. The prohibition is not arbitrary; it follows the life they chose when they were told “Eat and drink but do not waste” and instead they wasted gratitude, spread corruption after the land had been set in order, and called upon other guardians. The next verse defines those disbelievers as those who took faith as amusement and were deluded by worldly life, so today We will forget them as they forgot the meeting of this Day.\n\nThe exchange mirrors the earlier plea of the wretched detained before the Fire begging water, and the worldly situation where the grateful were denied by the powerful. Roles are reversed with perfect justice, weighed on scales that do not wrong. A common misunderstanding is to think that pleas at that stage can still change the barrier. The barrier, or “hijab,” is fixed after the appointed term that cannot be delayed or advanced, when belief after signs no longer benefits. For the living, the verse urges to give water and provision now while giving still builds the heavy scale, to remember gratitude for the One who sends winds ushering mercy and rain to a lifeless land, and to not treat faith as play, lest a day come when water itself is forbidden.",
  "51": "“those who took this faith ˹of Islam˺ as mere amusement and play and were deluded by ˹their˺ worldly life.’ ˹Allah will say,˺ ‘Today We will ignore them just as they ignored the coming of this Day of theirs and for rejecting Our revelations’” explains the reason for the prohibition of water and provision just announced to the disbelievers. After the Fire’s residents beg for water and are told Allah has forbidden both to disbelievers, this verse defines those disbelievers not by a single error but by a pattern of treating the serious as trivial. The phrase “took this faith as mere amusement and play,” or “allatheena ittakhadhoo deenahum lahwan wa la‘iban,” echoes earlier descriptions of those who take faith as play and amusement and are deluded by worldly life, and earlier that worldly life itself is play and amusement for the heedless. Here the faith itself was turned into entertainment, a subject for mocking revelations, debating with elegant deception, and varying stories to call the Quran ancient fables, while the real Home of Peace was treated as a joke.\n\nThe second trait, “were deluded by their worldly life,” or “wa gharrathum al-hayatu al-dunya,” shows the world’s nearer life tricked them, beautifying deeds and making accumulation seem meaningful, as earlier each people’s deeds were made appealing. The delusion closed their eyes to the scales that will be heavy or light and to the barrier now fixed between the two homes.\n\nThe divine response is then stated: “Today We will ignore them just as they ignored the coming of this Day of theirs and for rejecting Our revelations,” or “fa-alyawma nansahum kama nasoo liqaa yawmihim hatha wa ma kanu bi-ayatina yajhadoon.” “Nansahum” means We will forget them, not out of divine forgetfulness, since We were never absent and never unaware, but as recompense: We will leave them in their punishment as they left remembrance, ignoring them as they ignored the meeting whose knowledge would have made them mindful. The Day they ignored has arrived; the revelations they rejected now testify.\n\nA common misconception is that religion is a light cultural amusement. The verse shows treating the decisive Way as amusement is itself the hinge of loss, leading to being forgotten on the Day when being remembered is the only hope. The remedy is to treat the Book explained with knowledge as a serious guide and mercy, to follow what was sent down, to dress for worship, eat with gratitude without waste, and to keep the appointment in sight while the term still allows.",
  "52": "“We have certainly brought them a Book which We explained with knowledge—a guide and mercy for those who believe” follows the declaration that those who took faith as play were ignored as they ignored the meeting. The verse steps back to name the ignored gift itself. The pronoun “them” refers to humanity already addressed as children of Adam and as those who received messengers and who await fulfilment. The statement, “We have certainly brought them a Book,” or “wa laqad ji’nahum bi-kitab,” emphasizes arrival and divine agency. This is the same blessed Book sent down to warn and remind, the Book sent down from your Lord to follow and not take other guardians, whose Word is perfected in truth and justice and cannot be changed. The next phrase, “which We explained with knowledge,” or “fassalnahu ala ilmin,” mirrors earlier refrains “We explained with knowledge—a guide and mercy” and “We explained with knowledge for those who believe.” The explanation is not random variation but variation built upon knowledge, precise, detailed, covering worship, morality, livelihood, and the unseen, as earlier “We have left nothing out of the Record.”\n\nIts function is then stated: “a guide and mercy for those who believe,” or “hudan wa rahmah li-qawmin yu’minoonoon.” Guide, or “huda,” is the light that lets the dead-hearted walk among people and the straight path made clear to the mindful; mercy, or “rahmah,” is the closeness promised as “Allah’s mercy is always close to the good-doers” and the mercy taken upon Himself that welcomes the repentant who mend ways. For those who believe, the same Book that is a warning to the arrogant becomes guidance that expands the chest and mercy that pours like rain on a lifeless land.\n\nThis verse prepares for the next question, “Do they only await the fulfilment of its warning?” If the Book has already been brought and explained with knowledge, why wait for its threat to become sight? The waiting is portrayed as heedlessness, the same delay Iblis exploited lying in wait on the Straight Path.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to think the Book’s detail is too difficult or too distant. The verse says it is explained with knowledge precisely to be a guide, not a puzzle, and its mercy is for the believing, not for the one who insists on associating others and beautifying shame. For the sincere, the way to receive its mercy is the same as opening the heart to Islam: follow what was sent down, call upon Him humbly and secretly, avoid corruption after order, and remember the best clothing of righteousness as the lens through which the Book’s lessons become clear.",
  "53": "“Do they only await the fulfilment ˹of its warning˺? The Day it will be fulfilled, those who ignored it before will say, ‘The messengers of our Lord certainly came with the truth. Are there any intercessors who can plead on our behalf? Or can we be sent back so we may do ˹good,˺ unlike what we used to do?’ They will have certainly ruined themselves, and whatever ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them” shows the end of waiting once the Book explained with knowledge has been brought. The opening question, “Do they only await the fulfilment of its warning?” or “hal yanzuroona illa ta’weelahu,” asks what the deniers expect if not the very fulfilment, or “ta’weel,” the ultimate outcome the Book warned about, the gathering, the weighing, the Fire whose residents beg for water and are refused. The same expectation appeared earlier as “Do they only await the fulfilment?” and as “Are they awaiting the coming of angels or your Lord or some signs,” when belief after signs would no longer benefit.\n\nThe scene of that Day is then quoted. Those who ignored it before, the same who took faith as play and were deluded by worldly life, will say, “The messengers of our Lord certainly came with the truth,” or “la-qad ja’at rusulu Rabbina bil-haqq,” the exact confession made by those in Paradise who said we would never have been guided if Allah had not guided us and the messengers came with truth, but here said too late. Then the plea bursts: “Are there any intercessors who can plead on our behalf? Or can we be sent back so we may do good, unlike what we used to do?” The first request recalls the begging of those in the Fire for water and the earlier detained before the Fire begging to be returned, promising to be believers. The second request is the same as “If only we could be sent back we would never deny signs.”\n\nThe verdict closes: “They will have certainly ruined themselves, and whatever gods they fabricated will fail them,” or “qad khasiroo anfusahum wa dalla anhum ma kanu yaftaroon.” Self-ruin, or “khasiroo anfusahum,” describes light scales who doomed themselves, and failing gods echoes the moment polytheists will say “They have failed us” when the soul-takers ask where false gods are.\n\nA common self-deception is to postpone response until warning becomes sight. The verse shows sight after waiting is a confession without benefit. The time to answer the guide-and-mercy Book is while intercession is still sought correctly through sincere devotion in this world, before the fulfilment leaves only self-ruin and vanished fabrications.",
  "54": "“Indeed your Lord is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne. He makes the day and night overlap in rapid succession. He created the sun, the moon, and the stars—all subjected by His command. The creation and the command belong to Him ˹alone˺. Blessed is Allah—Lord of all worlds!” returns to the grandeur of Lordship after the warning about awaiting fulfilment. The previous verses showed the Book brought with knowledge as guide and mercy and the ruin of those who waited until fulfilment to confess. Now the foundation for that Book’s authority is restated: your Lord is Allah, not a guardian besides Him, not a devil taken as master, the same Lord called Self-Sufficient, Full of Mercy, Lord of everything.\n\nThe first proof is creation: “Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne,” or “khalaqa al-samawati wal-arda fi sittati ayyam thumma istawa ala al-arsh.” Six Days indicates deliberate, wise staging, not need for time, showing the All-Wise who measures every matter. “Established Himself on the Throne” affirms His majesty and highness above creation, in a manner befitting Him without likening to creatures and without emptying it of meaning, as passed down from the earliest scholars who said the meaning is known while the modality is not asked about.\n\nThe next proof is governance: “He makes the day and night overlap in rapid succession,” or “yughshi al-laila al-nahara yatlubuhu hatheetha,” He covers night with day, each chasing the other swiftly, the same overlap praised when He causes dawn to break and makes night for rest. “He created the sun, the moon, and the stars—all subjected by His command,” or “wa al-shamsa wa al-qamara wa al-nujoomu musakhkharatin bi-amrihi,” the celestial guides earlier said to travel with precision and to guide through darkness, all subservient, not lords.\n\nThe principle is then distilled: “The creation and the command belong to Him alone,” or “ala lahu al-khalqu wal-amr.” Creation, or “khalq,” is bringing into being; command, or “amr,” is directing, legislating, governing. Both belong to the same Owner, joining Lordship and Law, so the prohibition list that forbad indecencies, shirk, and lying about Allah is not an extra burden but the command of the Creator. The exclamation “Blessed is Allah—Lord of all worlds!” or “tabaraka Allahu Rabbu al-alameen,” praises abundance of blessing that flows from that unified ownership, the same blessedness of the Book which taught to follow and be mindful so you may receive mercy. A common error is to split creation from command, accepting the Creator while rejecting His law. The verse unites them, calling to humble, secret supplication that the next verses enjoin.",
  "55": "“Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly. Surely He does not like the transgressors” follows the majestic affirmation that creation and command belong to Allah alone, blessed Lord of all worlds, who makes night and day overlap and subjects sun, moon, and stars. The previous verse established who your Lord is; this verse teaches how to turn to Him. The command “Call upon your Lord,” or “ud‘oo Rabbakum,” includes both supplication for needs and worship that calls with action, like dressing for worship and eating with thanks. The manner is defined twice: “humbly and secretly,” or “tadarru‘an wa khufyatan.” “Tadarru‘” means lowliness, showing need, softness, humility of heart and voice, as opposed to the arrogance for which the Heaven’s gates do not open. “Khufyah” means secretly, quietly, private, not for show. The pair matches the earlier description of those who invoke morning and evening seeking His Face, and contrasts with the noisy boasting of those whose numbers and arrogance proved useless.\n\nThe reason given is moral: “Surely He does not like the transgressors,” or “innahu la yuhibbu al-mu‘tadeen.” Transgression, or “i‘tida,” means crossing bounds, like spreading corruption after order, wasting provision, associating partners, or committing indecencies openly or secretly. Calling upon Him arrogantly, loudly to be seen, or while persisting in transgression, contradicts the humility the call requires. Earlier prohibitions listed indecencies, sin, aggression without right, shirk, and speaking about Allah without knowledge as true transgressions that block the straight path.\n\nThis verse also corrects a misunderstanding about secret supplication. Some imagine that hidden devotion is less rewarded than public display. The Quran praises secret humility precisely because it is sincere, the same sincerity, or “ikhlas,” commanded as “calling upon Him with sincere devotion” after uprightness. Humble secret prayer is the antidote to the four-sided ambush that aims to make most ungrateful, because gratitude grows in private remembrance.\n\nFor daily life, it encourages regular, quiet, needful calling, like the winds that softly usher mercy before heavy rain, rather than loud, theatrical claims. The next verse continues, “Do not spread corruption after order and call upon Him with hope and fear,” showing that the humble secret caller is also the one who keeps the earth set in order and whose hope-filled fear keeps mercy close to the good-doers.",
  "56": "“Do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order. And call upon Him with hope and fear. Indeed, Allah’s mercy is always close to the good-doers” balances the previous call for humble secret supplication with social and emotional dimensions. The first command, “Do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order,” or “wa la tufsidoo fil-ardi ba‘da islahiha,” addresses the earth that was earlier said to have been created in six Days and established on the Throne, where day and night overlap by gentle command. “Islah” means setting in order, rectifying, making sound, as the Creator did by providing livelihood, sending winds with mercy, and guiding through the detailed Book. Corruption, or “fasad,” undoes that order through indecencies open and secret, shirk, unjust aggression, forbidding tayyibat, and beautifying child killing, the exact deeds the fabricators spread. The prohibition shows that worship and social responsibility are inseparable.\n\nThe second command completes inward orientation: “And call upon Him with hope and fear,” or “wa id‘oohu khawfan wa tama‘a.” After teaching to call humbly and secretly, now the heart’s two wings are named. Fear, or “khawf,” remembers the Fire whose residents beg for water and are refused, the Fire whose bed and cover are prepared for the arrogant, and the term that cannot be delayed. Hope, or “tama‘,” remembers the Home of Peace, the removal of bitterness, and the promise that scales heavy will be successful. Together they keep supplication alive, as earlier the winds usher mercy with both promise of rain and need for rain. Calling with only fear despairs; with only hope grows careless; with both, the heart stays mindful.\n\nThe promise that follows is tender: “Indeed, Allah’s mercy is always close to the good-doers,” or “inna rahmata Allahi qareebun min al-muhsineen.” Good-doers, or “muhsineen,” are those who excel, who worship as if seeing Him, who give full measure, guard orphan wealth, and shun sin, the same people near whom mercy is close even while the Fire’s prohibited water is far from transgressors. The statement links back to “He does not like transgressors” just before; not liking transgression does not mean distant; mercy’s closeness to the good is the counterpart.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to treat hope and fear as contradictions. The verse shows they are companions that keep corruption from spreading and keep private humility from becoming hidden despair, ensuring the land remains ordered and provision travels from winds to fruit as the next verses describe.",
  "57": "“He is the One Who sends the winds ushering in His mercy. When they bear heavy clouds, We drive them to a lifeless land and then cause rain to fall, producing every type of fruit. Similarly, We will bring the dead to life, so perhaps you will be mindful” returns to creation’s mercy as proof of resurrection, linking the Book’s warning and the command to call with hope and fear to a visible natural mercy. The opening, “He is the One Who sends the winds ushering in His mercy,” or “wa huwa allathee yursilu al-riyaha bushran baina yadai rahmatihi,” describes winds, or “riyah,” sent as bearers of glad tidings before rain, the mercy, or “rahmah,” that is rain itself. The same winds earlier praised as designs of the Almighty, All-Knowing now become heralds, much as messengers are sent to warn before the Day.\n\nThe sequence is then detailed: “When they bear heavy clouds, We drive them to a lifeless land and then cause rain to fall, producing every type of fruit,” or “hatta itha aqallat sahaban thiqalan suqnahu li-baladin mayyitin fa-anzalna bihi al-ma’a fa-akhrajna bihi min kulli al-thamarat.” The clouds are heavy with water, driven by command, not chance, to a dead land where soil seemed hopeless, as earlier fertile and infertile lands were contrasted and as earlier seeds sprouted by the Splitter. The result is every type of fruit, the same diversity seen in gardens trellised and untrellised, olives and pomegranates similar yet dissimilar, a sign for the believing and a provision seldom thanked.\n\nThe spiritual parallel is then drawn: “Similarly, We will bring the dead to life, so perhaps you will be mindful,” or “kathalika nukhriju al-mawta la‘allakum tathakkaroon.” The bringing forth of fruit from dead land is the exact likeness of bringing forth humans from the earth where they live, die, and will be resurrected. As the earlier rain produced green stalks with clustered grain calling to look at fruit’s ripening, so this rain-like resurrection calls to be mindful, or “tathakkaroon,” remembering that the Lord whose creation and command are His will also fulfil the promise awaited by the doubters.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to separate nature’s mercy from the final mercy. The verse joins them: the winds’ mercy proves the greater mercy of raising the dead and weighing justly is possible. For the mindful, every wind before rain and every fruit after becomes a reminder to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, keeping the land from corruption and the heart open to the mercy always close to the good-doers.",
  "58": "“The fertile land produces abundantly by the Will of its Lord, whereas the infertile land hardly produces anything. This is how We vary ˹Our˺ lessons to those who are thankful” uses the agriculture just described to illustrate receptive and unreceptive hearts, directly after promising that the dead will be raised like fruit from dead land. The previous verse showed winds bearing heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit as a likeness of resurrection. Now two kinds of land are contrasted. The fertile land, or “al-baladu al-tayyib,” the good, pure soil, “produces abundantly by the Will of its Lord,” or “yakhruju nabatuhu bi-ithni Rabbihi,” its plants emerge easily, fully, beautifully, with Allah’s leave, matching the believer’s heart that receives the Book explained with knowledge and responds as the attentive do, whose chest is opened to Islam. The infertile land, or “al-khabeeth,” the bad, salty, hard soil, “hardly produces anything,” or “la yakhruju illa nakidan,” only with difficulty, stunted, bitter, and scanty, matching the heart that disbelieves in the Hereafter, takes devils as masters thinking itself guided, and remains tight as if climbing into the sky.\n\nThe same water descends on both, just as the same blessed Book is sent to all, but outcome differs by capacity prepared internally and by willingness to be thankful. The verse then states its method: “This is how We vary Our lessons to those who are thankful,” or “kathalika nusarrifu al-ayat li-qawmin yashkuroon.” “Nusarrifu” means We turn, vary, repeat lessons from different angles, as earlier We varied lessons for those who know, who comprehend, who remember, and who believe. Here the audience is “those who are thankful,” or “yashkuroon,” because thankfulness is the fertile soil. Gratitude recognizes the Sender behind the wind and rain, attributes provision to the One who provides for all, and guards measure, covenant, and dress, producing abundant good that tenfold multiplies on the scale.\n\nA common mistake is to think the difference in harvest proves water is lacking. The verse shows water, like revelation, is ample; the soil’s readiness matters. Another error is to think thankfulness is only words. The fertile metaphor shows thankfulness is fruition, putting lessons into practice as abundant produce, not withholding. For daily cultivation, it encourages softening the heart with remembrance, seeking humble secret supplication with hope and fear, and not spreading corruption after order, keeping the inner land fertile so that when the mercy that is close to the good-doers arrives, it produces the righteousness that is the best clothing and the weight that makes the scale heavy.",
  "59": "“Indeed, We sent Noah to his people. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous Day’” begins the series of prophetic stories that illustrate fertile and infertile response after the parable of good and bad land. The previous verses used winds and soils to explain how the same rain produces different harvests in thankful versus unthankful ground. Now the same truth is shown in human history, starting with the first major messenger after Adam’s descent. The opening, “Indeed, We sent Noah to his people,” or “laqad arsalna Noohan ila qawmihi,” places Noah, or “Nuh,” as a sent messenger like the later Hud, Salih, Lot, and Shu‘ayb, confirming that the Lord would never destroy a community while its people are unaware. Ibn Kathir explains that Noah was the first messenger sent to a people who had fallen into idolatry, and that his mission is the template of every mission that follows: a single man, from among his own people, calling them to the worship of God alone.\n\nNoah’s call is then quoted: “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu.” This is the core of every prophetic message, the call to worship the One and to abandon every other object of worship. The commentators note the two parts of the sentence — the command to worship Allah and the declaration that there is no god besides Him — for they are the affirmation and the negation of pure monotheism, the very creed that the Quran was revealed to establish. The second part of Noah’s message is then stated: “I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous Day,” or “inni akhafu ‘alaykum ‘athaba yawmin ‘azeem.” The motive of the warning is fear born of love, not hatred: Noah feared for his people the punishment of the Day of Judgment, and the fear that drove his call was the fear of a man who wished his people to be saved. Al-Sa’di observes that prophetic warning is mercy, and that the one who warns of the fire does so to save from it, not to delight in it.\n\nThe phrasing connects directly to the present audience. The polytheists of Mecca were told the same thing: worship Allah alone, and fear the tremendous Day. Their chiefs’ reply in the next verse mirrors Mecca’s chiefs who called Muhammad a fool and a liar, and the Quran places the two rejections side by side so that the Prophet might know that the rejection of his message was not new but the repetition of an ancient pattern. Ibn Kathir notes that the stories of the prophets in this surah are arranged to comfort the Prophet and to warn his opponents: the same call, the same refusal, and the same outcome have occurred before, and the sunnah of God does not change. For the listener, the verse is the first note of the great theme of this passage: the message is one, the messengers are many, and the response of the people — belief or rejection — is the harvest of the seed that the call plants.",
  "60": "“But the chiefs of his people said, ‘We surely see that you are clearly misguided’” records the first rejection of Noah, and the commentators note the identity of the rejecters. The word “mala’,” the chiefs, the notables, the council of the community, is the Quran’s consistent term for those who lead the rejection of the prophets, and the verse teaches that the opposition to truth is led, in every age, by those whose authority and interest the truth threatens. Ibn Kathir explains that Noah’s people were idol-worshippers, and that their chiefs — the wealthy, the powerful, the guardians of the temples — saw in Noah’s call a challenge to the order from which they benefited, and so they answered his call with the accusation that the Quran records: “We surely see that you are clearly misguided,” or “inna la-naraka fi dalalin mubeen.”\n\nThe accusation is itself a study in the psychology of rejection. The chiefs did not answer Noah’s argument — they could not, for the argument was unanswerable — and so they attacked the man instead, declaring that he was in clear error. The word “dalal,” misguidance, is the very word they would hurl at every prophet after, and the commentators note the irony: the ones who worshipped stones and wood accused the worshipper of the One of being astray, and the word “mubeen,” clear, open, was applied to the error of the one who was, in fact, the only guided man among them. Al-Sa’di observes that this reversal is the constant feature of the confrontation between truth and falsehood: the false accuses the true of exactly the condition that the false itself suffers, and the accusation is the measure of the accuser, not the accused.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer about the loneliness of the caller to truth. Noah stood alone against the assembled chiefs, and his reward for the warning was the accusation of madness and error, and yet he did not waver, for the caller’s strength is not the number of his supporters but the truth of his message. Ibn Kathir notes that the Quran preserves this rejection not as a record of failure but as a stage in the story of every prophet, and that the Prophet Muhammad was being shown, through Noah, that the accusation of the chiefs of Mecca was the accusation that every chief had made against every prophet. For the listener, the verse is the first lesson of the prophetic stories: the truth is always opposed first by the powerful, the accusation is always the same, and the caller to God must expect, and endure, the verdict of “misguided” that falsehood pronounces upon truth.",
  "61": "“He replied, ‘O my people! I am not misguided! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds’” records Noah’s answer to the accusation of his chiefs, and the commentators note its dignity and precision. Noah did not descend to abuse, did not return the accusation, and did not despair; he answered with the two facts that constituted his entire defense: “I am not misguided,” or “laysa bee dalalah,” the denial of the charge, and “But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,” or “walakinnee rasoolun min rabbi al-‘alameen,” the affirmation of his mission. Ibn Kathir explains that the answer is the model of the prophetic response to accusation: the messenger does not defend his person but declares his office, for the truth of the message is the proof of the messenger, and the one who is sent by the Lord of all worlds cannot be the misguided one his enemies allege.\n\nThe title “Lord of all worlds,” or “rabb al-‘alameen,” is itself the argument, and the commentators dwell on it. The chiefs of Noah’s people worshipped many gods, each god a lord of a portion — a lord of the sea, a lord of the crops, a lord of the tribe — and Noah’s declaration of the “Lord of all worlds” swept them all away with a single phrase: the true Lord is not a lord among lords but the Lord of everything, and the one who calls to Him is not dividing the worship but restoring it to its single object. Al-Sa’di observes that the phrase contains the whole of Noah’s message: the universality of God’s lordship is the refutation of every polytheism, and the messenger of the Lord of all worlds speaks with an authority that no tribal god and no tribal chief can match.\n\nThe verse also reveals the patience of Noah’s method. He began, as he had begun his first call, with “O my people,” or “ya qawmi,” the address of kinship, for even in the face of their accusation he did not sever the bond of brotherhood but claimed it, speaking to them as one of them, bound to their good and anxious for their salvation. Ibn Kathir notes that this is the hallmark of the prophets: the rejection of their people never turned their love into hatred, and the accusation of madness never silenced their call. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the caller under attack: answer the accusation with the mission, the falsehood with the truth, and the hostility with the unbroken address of “O my people” — for the messenger of the Lord of all worlds has nothing to fear and everything to give.",
  "62": "“conveying to you my Lord’s messages and giving you sincere advice. And I know from Allah what you do not know” continues Noah’s self-description, and the commentators unfold the two duties it names. The first is the duty of delivery: “conveying to you my Lord’s messages,” or “uballighukum risalati rabbee,” the transmission of the revelation exactly as it was received, without addition, subtraction, or distortion. Ibn Kathir explains that this is the essence of the prophetic office: the messenger is a bearer, not an author, and his fidelity is to the message, not to the expectations of his audience. The second duty is the duty of counsel: “and giving you sincere advice,” or “wa ansahu lakum,” the “nasihah” that is the willing of good for another, the wholehearted concern for the welfare of the people that drove every warning Noah uttered. Al-Sa’di observes that the pairing of the two duties is the complete definition of the prophet: he conveys what he was given and he advises what he conveys, and the one who truly advises does not flatter but warns.\n\nThe verse closes with the phrase that explains the source of Noah’s confidence: “And I know from Allah what you do not know,” or “wa a‘lamu mina Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon.” Noah knew, from the revelation, the realities that his people did not know — the reality of the Hereafter, the certainty of the reckoning, the consequence of the rejection — and his knowledge was not a private opinion but a revelation from God. Ibn Kathir notes that this sentence is the answer to every accusation of the chiefs: the one who calls them to God knows what they do not know, and the accusation of “misguided” falls back upon the accusers, who are the ones walking in ignorance of the realities that the messenger has seen.\n\nThe verse also contains a lesson for every believer about the nature of sincere counsel. True advice is not the telling of what the hearer wishes to hear but the telling of what the hearer needs to know, and the sincere advisor is often, at first, the most unwelcome of voices. Al-Sa’di observes that Noah’s “sincere advice” was met with the accusation of madness, and that this has been the lot of every truthful advisor since — the truth is bitter to the taste that is accustomed to the sweet of flattery. For the listener, the verse is the portrait of the messenger and the call: the bearer of the message, the giver of the counsel, and the knower of what his people do not know — the three marks of every prophet and of every sincere caller who follows in their path.",
  "63": "“Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own kind, so that he may warn you, so that you may become mindful, and so that you may be shown mercy?” records Noah’s reply to the deeper objection of his people, and the commentators note the argument it answers. The chiefs of Noah’s people had not only accused him of error but had expressed their astonishment that God should send a mere man — a human being like themselves — as His messenger, and the Quran records the same objection in every prophetic story: the people found it incredible that revelation should descend upon one of their own. Noah’s answer is the divine answer: “Do you find it astonishing,” or “a-wa ‘ajibtum,” that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through a man of your own kind? Ibn Kathir explains that the astonishment of the people was the astonishment of pride — they imagined that a messenger must be an angel or a being of a higher order — and the Quran refutes it with the logic of mercy: the messenger is sent from among the people precisely so that he may be understood, so that his example may be followed, and so that no barrier of unfamiliarity may stand between the message and its recipients.\n\nThe verse then names the three purposes of the message: “so that he may warn you, so that you may become mindful, and so that you may be shown mercy,” or “li-yunthirakum wa li-tattaqoo wa la‘allakum turhamoon.” The warning, the mindfulness, and the mercy are the three stages of the message’s work: the warning awakens the heedless, the awakening produces the mindfulness of God, and the mindfulness draws down the mercy. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse thus answers the objection of astonishment with the purpose of love: the sending of a human messenger is not a strange thing but a mercy, for the reminder that comes through a man of one’s own kind is the reminder that the heart can receive and the life can follow.\n\nThe verse also teaches the principle that runs through all the prophetic stories: the messengers are human, and their humanity is the proof of the message, not its flaw. Ibn Kathir notes that the objection of “one of our own kind” is the objection of every age that refuses to be taught by its equals, and that the Quran’s answer is constant: the worth of the messenger is not in his substance but in his message, and the human messenger is the mercy of God made accessible. For the listener, the verse is the correction of every pride that would reject truth because of its bearer: the reminder comes through a man so that men may be warned, become mindful, and be shown mercy — and the one who is astonished at the mercy of God has only his own pride to blame.",
  "64": "“But they rejected him, so We saved him and those with him in the Ark, and drowned those who denied Our signs. They were certainly a blind people” closes the account of Noah in this surah with the verdict of the flood, and the commentators fill in the story that the verse compresses. The verse states the rejection, the salvation, and the drowning in three clauses: “But they rejected him,” or “fa-kaththaboohu,” the people persisted in their denial through the long centuries of Noah’s call; “so We saved him and those with him in the Ark,” or “fa-anjaynahu wallatheena ma‘ahu fi al-fulk,” the rescue of Noah and the believers aboard the great ship he had built by the divine command; and “drowned those who denied Our signs,” or “wa aghraqna allatheena kaththaboo bi-ayatina,” the flood that swept away the deniers. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari relate the fuller narrative: Noah called his people for nine hundred and fifty years, and only a small band believed; God commanded him to build the Ark, and the ridicule of his people accompanied the building; then the fountains of the earth burst and the sky poured down, and the flood covered the land, and every denier perished in the waters while the Ark carried the believers to safety.\n\nThe verse closes with the verdict that explains the whole: “They were certainly a blind people,” or “innahum kanoo qawman ‘ameen.” The word “‘ameen” — blind — is the key, and the commentators note that it refers not to the blindness of the eyes but the blindness of the heart, the loss of the inner sight that perceives the truth. The people of Noah had seen his signs and heard his warning for centuries, and yet they had walked in the blindness of their own denial, and the flood came upon a people whose hearts had already drowned. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the connection between the inner and the outer: the soul that goes blind to the truth walks toward the destruction that it cannot see, and the flood was the harvest of a blindness that no light had cured.\n\nThe verse also functions as a comfort and a warning for the Prophet’s own time. The denial of Noah’s people was total, and yet Noah and the believers were saved, while the deniers were drowned — and the same promise and the same warning attend the message of every prophet. Ibn Kathir notes that the story of Noah is told here in its essentials, while the fuller account of the Ark and the flood is given in Surah Hud, and that the purpose in this surah is the pattern: the call, the rejection, the salvation of the few, and the destruction of the many, as the constant law of the divine dealing with the deniers. For the listener, the verse is the first complete instance of the pattern that the rest of the passage will repeat: the flood is the measure of the fate of every people that rejects its warner, and the Ark is the measure of the salvation of every soul that believes.",
  "65": "“And to the people of ‘Ad We sent their brother Hud. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. Will you not then become mindful?’” moves from Noah to the second of the destroyed nations, and the commentators introduce the people of ‘Ad and their prophet. The ‘Ad were an ancient Arab people of great strength and stature, who dwelt in the region of al-Ahqaf, the sand dunes of southern Arabia, and who had been blessed with power, wealth, and prosperity after the time of Noah. To them God sent “their brother Hud,” or “akhahum Hoodan,” and the commentators note the word “their brother”: Hud was one of them, a man of their own lineage and tongue, and his kinship with them is mentioned so that no one might say the warning came from a stranger. Ibn Kathir explains that the ‘Ad are the first of the Arab peoples whose destruction the Quran records in this sequence, and that their story follows Noah’s as the second instance of the single pattern of call and rejection.\n\nHud’s call is identical in substance to Noah’s: “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu.” The commentators note the deliberate repetition: the message of every prophet is one, and the call that Noah delivered to his people is the call that Hud delivered to his, for the truth does not change with the generations. The addition that distinguishes Hud’s call is the closing question: “Will you not then become mindful?” or “afala tattaqoon,” will you not then fear God, will you not then take heed. Al-Sa’di observes that the question is the Quran’s characteristic appeal to the intellect: after the declaration of the oneness of God, the listener is asked whether he will not now become mindful, and the question leaves the answer to the conscience that hears it.\n\nThe verse also establishes the particular sin of the ‘Ad, which the following verses will develop: they were a people of arrogance and oppression, who had taken their strength as a license for tyranny, and the call of Hud was directed first against the arrogance that their power had bred. Ibn Kathir notes that the ‘Ad were said to be giants of great bodily strength, and that their power had made them tyrants who said, as the Quran records, “Who is mightier than us in strength?” — and the call of Hud to the worship of God alone was, at the same time, a call to the humility that their power had destroyed. For the listener, the verse is the second sounding of the single call: the worship of the One, the fear of the One, and the question that every people must answer — will you not become mindful?",
  "66": "“The disbelieving chiefs of his people responded, ‘We surely see you as a fool, and we certainly think you are a liar’” records the answer of the ‘Ad to their prophet, and the commentators note the two accusations it joins. The chiefs of ‘Ad — the “mala’,” the notables, those who led the people and profited from their order — answered Hud with a double charge: “We surely see you as a fool,” or “inna la-naraka fi safahah,” and “we certainly think you are a liar,” or “wa inna la-nazunnuka mina al-kathibeen.” Ibn Kathir explains the two accusations: the first attacked his intellect, declaring that the man who called them to abandon their gods was a fool, a man of weak and childish mind; the second attacked his honesty, declaring that his claim to prophecy was a lie. The two together were the complete dismissal — the message was the product of a fool, and the messenger was a liar, and so there was nothing in either that deserved a hearing.\n\nThe commentators note the psychology that the verse exposes. The accusation of folly and falsehood is the refuge of the one who cannot answer the argument, and the chiefs of ‘Ad, who could not refute the call to worship the One, refuted the caller instead, transferring their own refusal into a verdict upon his mind and his honesty. Al-Sa’di observes the irony that runs through the Quran’s account: the people who worshipped stones of their own carving accused the worshipper of the One of folly, and the people whose whole religion was a fabrication accused the messenger of lying — the accusation is the mirror in which the accuser appears. The verse also echoes the accusation of Noah’s people, and the repetition teaches that the rejection of truth takes the same form in every age: first the charge of folly, then the charge of falsehood, and then, when the argument remains unanswered, the threat.\n\nThe word “nazunnu,” we think, we suppose, is itself significant, and the commentators dwell on it. The chiefs did not claim to know that Hud was a liar; they supposed it, for they had no proof of the charge, and their accusation rested on nothing but the arrogance that refused to admit the truth of his message. Ibn Kathir notes that the word betrays the groundlessness of the accusation: the one who must “suppose” his opponent a liar has already conceded that he has no argument. For the listener, the verse is the second instance of the pattern of rejection, and the lesson that the accusation of the truth-teller is always the confession of the accuser — the charge of folly and falsehood is the last word of a falsehood that has nothing else to say.",
  "67": "“Hud replied, ‘O my people! I am no fool! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds’” records Hud’s answer, and the commentators note its identity with the answer of Noah and its dignity in the face of abuse. The accusation had been twofold — folly and falsehood — and Hud answered the first directly: “I am no fool,” or “laysa bee safahah,” denying the charge of weak intellect, and then affirmed the truth of his office: “But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,” or “walakinnee rasoolun min rabbi al-‘alameen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the answer is the same answer that Noah gave to his people, for the prophets answer the accusation of folly with the declaration of mission, and the declaration of mission with the title of the Lord who sent them: the one who is sent by the Lord of all worlds cannot be the fool his enemies allege, and the truth of the message is the refutation of every charge against the messenger.\n\nThe title “Lord of all worlds” carries the same weight here as in the story of Noah, and the commentators connect the two. The ‘Ad, like the people of Noah, had divided the lordship of the universe among many gods, and Hud’s declaration of the single Lord of all the worlds was the refutation of their entire pantheon in a phrase. Al-Sa’di observes that the repetition of the title in the mouths of the prophets is the Quran’s way of teaching the unity of the message: every prophet came with the same Lord and the same call, and the polytheism of the ‘Ad was no different in substance from the polytheism of the people of Noah, or from the polytheism of Mecca itself.\n\nThe verse also preserves the address of kinship, “O my people,” or “ya qawmi,” and the commentators note its constancy. Hud, abused by his chiefs and accused of folly, still addressed his people with the word of brotherhood, for the bond of the prophet to his people is not severed by their abuse, and the love that warns is not extinguished by the hatred that refuses. Ibn Kathir notes that this is the mark of the prophetic character throughout the Quran: the messenger answers the insult with the truth, the accusation with the mission, and the hostility with the unbroken address of kinship. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the caller under abuse: the fool is not the one who speaks the truth but the one who refuses it, and the messenger of the Lord of all worlds walks in a dignity that no accusation can touch.",
  "68": "“conveying to you my Lord’s messages. And I am your sincere advisor” continues Hud’s self-description, and the commentators note the two claims it makes. The first is the claim of fidelity: “conveying to you my Lord’s messages,” or “uballighukum risalati rabbee,” the delivery of the revelation without alteration, the messenger’s guarantee that what he speaks is the message of his Lord and not his own invention. The second is the claim of sincerity: “And I am your sincere advisor,” or “wa ana lakum nasihun ameen,” the “nasih” whose counsel is pure of self-interest, and the word “ameen” — trustworthy, faithful — sealing the claim with the assurance of honesty. Ibn Kathir explains that the two claims are the answer to the two accusations: against the charge of falsehood, the claim of faithful delivery; against the charge of folly, the claim of sincere counsel, for the sincere advisor is no fool, and the fool is the one who refuses the advice that would save him.\n\nThe word “nasih” is rich with meaning, and the commentators unfold it. The “nasihah” of the prophet is the willing of good for the people, the concern for their welfare that treats their salvation as his own, and the sincerity of the advisor is measured by the truth of his advice, not by its pleasantness. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the distinction between the flatterer and the advisor: the flatterer tells the people what they wish to hear and is loved for it, while the advisor tells them what they need to hear and is hated for it, and the hatred of the people is no measure of the advisor’s sincerity. Hud, who warned his people of destruction and was called a fool for it, was the truest advisor they ever had, and their rejection of his counsel was the measure of their loss.\n\nThe verse also contains a subtle self-portrait of the prophet that runs through the whole surah. The messenger is, in every story, the bearer and the advisor — the one who conveys the message of his Lord and the one who counsels his people with sincerity — and the two functions are inseparable, for the delivery of the message without the sincerity of counsel is mere recitation, and the sincerity of counsel without the delivery of the message is mere opinion. Ibn Kathir notes that this is why the prophets always declare both, and why the believer who calls others to God must combine both. For the listener, the verse is the definition of the true caller: faithful to the message and sincere in the counsel, and the one who is both has fulfilled the office of the prophet in his own measure.",
  "69": "“Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own kind, so that he may warn you? Remember that He made you successors after Noah’s people and increased you greatly in stature. So remember Allah’s favours, so that you may be successful” records Hud’s answer to the deeper objection of his people and his appeal to their history, and the commentators unfold its argument. The objection of the ‘Ad was the same as that of Noah’s people: astonishment that a mere man should be sent as a messenger. Hud’s answer is the same answer: “Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own kind, so that he may warn you?” or “a-wa ‘ajibtum an ja’akum thikrun min rabbikum ‘ala rajulin minkum li-yunthirakum.” Ibn Kathir explains that the astonishment is refuted by the mercy it ignores: the messenger is sent from among the people precisely so that the warning may reach them in a language and a form they can receive, and the humanity of the messenger is not the flaw of the message but its accessibility.\n\nThe verse then turns to the specific history of the ‘Ad: “Remember that He made you successors after Noah’s people and increased you greatly in stature,” or “wathkuroo idh ja‘alakum khulafa’a min ba‘di qawmi Noohin wa zadakum fi al-khalqi bastah.” The ‘Ad were the heirs of the earth after the flood that destroyed the people of Noah, and God had increased them in stature and strength, making them a people of great bodies and great power. The commentators note the force of the reminder: the very strength in which the ‘Ad boasted was a gift from the God they refused to worship, and the inheritance of the earth after the flood was a trust from the One whose messenger they now rejected. Al-Sa’di observes that the reminder of the favours is the prophet’s appeal to gratitude: the people who remember what God has given them will not deny the One who gave it, and the denial of the messenger is the fruit of a forgotten favour.\n\nThe verse closes with the command that draws the lesson: “So remember Allah’s favours, so that you may be successful,” or “fathkuroo ala’a Allahi la‘allakum tuflihoon.” The remembrance of the favours is the condition of the success, “falah,” the flourishing that the Quran promises to the grateful, and the commentators note the logic: the one who remembers the favours of God is led by the memory to the worship of God, and the worship of God is the path of success in both worlds. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is a complete sermon in miniature — the refutation of the objection, the reminder of the favour, and the call to the remembrance that leads to success. For the listener, the verse is the lesson that the strength and stature of a people are gifts to be remembered, not titles to be boasted, and that the people who forget the Giver of their strength will soon be reminded of it by its removal.",
  "70": "“They said, ‘Have you come to us so that we would worship Allah alone and abandon what our fathers used to worship? Then bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truthful’” records the defiance of the ‘Ad, and the commentators note the two things it reveals. The first is the true objection of the people, now spoken plainly: “Have you come to us so that we would worship Allah alone and abandon what our fathers used to worship?” or “a-ji’tana li-na‘buda Allaha wahdahu wa nathara ma kana ya‘budu aba’una.” The ‘Ad confessed, in their defiance, that the thing they could not accept was the worship of God alone and the abandonment of the gods of their ancestors. Ibn Kathir explains that this is the root of every rejection: the worship of the One demands the surrender of the inherited, the abandonment of the customs of the fathers, and the people who have made tradition their god will not give it up for the truth. The word “wahdahu,” alone, is the offence — the exclusivity of the divine claim that leaves no room for the gods of the tribe.\n\nThe second is the challenge that follows: “Then bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truthful,” or “fa’tina bi-ma ta‘iduna in kunta mina as-sadiqeen.” The people, unable to answer the argument, demanded the punishment instead, daring the prophet to bring down the destruction he had warned of, as if the delay of the punishment were the proof of the lie. The commentators note the recklessness of the challenge: the people called down the very judgment they did not believe in, and the Quran records their demand as the prelude to their destruction, for the punishment they challenged came upon them, and their defiance became the witness against them. Al-Sa’di observes that the demand for the punishment is the last word of the obstinate: the heart that has closed itself to the truth can no longer hear the warning, and so it mocks the very mercy that calls to it, and the mockery becomes the final seal upon its fate.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the price of the truth. Hud’s call demanded the abandonment of the fathers’ gods, and the demand cost him the hatred of his people, for the truth is never popular where it disturbs the inheritance. Ibn Kathir notes that the Quran preserves the defiance of the ‘Ad so that the Prophet might know that the defiance of Mecca was not new: the worship of the fathers, the rejection of the One, and the challenge of the punishment are the three notes of every rejection, and the prophet who hears them is in the company of every prophet who heard them before. For the listener, the verse is the warning that the inherited can be the enemy of the true, and that the one who will not abandon the gods of his fathers will one day meet the punishment he once dared.",
  "71": "“He said, ‘You will certainly be subjected to your Lord’s torment and wrath. Do you dispute with me regarding the names which you have made up—you and your fathers—for which Allah has sent no authority? Then wait! I too am waiting with you’” records Hud’s final answer, and the commentators note its structure. The first clause is the confirmation of the warning: “You will certainly be subjected to your Lord’s torment and wrath,” or “qad waqa‘a ‘alaykum min rabbikum rijsun wa ghadab,” the punishment and the anger that would descend upon them, now declared as certain, for the defiance of the people had sealed the decree. Ibn Kathir explains that the word “waqa‘a,” has fallen, is in the past tense in the Arabic, expressing the certainty of the event as if it had already occurred, for what God decrees is as certain as what has already happened.\n\nThe second clause is the argument that demolishes the people’s position: “Do you dispute with me regarding the names which you have made up—you and your fathers—for which Allah has sent no authority?” or “a-tujadilunani fi asma’in sammaytumooha antum wa aba’ukum ma nazzala Allahu biha min sultan.” The gods of the ‘Ad were, Hud declared, nothing but names — names invented by themselves and their fathers, labels without reality, for which God had sent down no authority, no proof, no warrant. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the most devastating of the Quran’s descriptions of idolatry: the false gods are not beings but names, and the worship of them is the worship of a sound, the bowing before a label that a people’s own imagination has invented. The word “sultan,” authority, is the criterion: the true religion is the one that God has authorized through revelation, and the religion of the fathers that rests on nothing but the fathers is a dispute over names.\n\nThe verse closes with the challenge that answers their challenge: “Then wait! I too am waiting with you,” or “fantaziroo inni ma‘akum mina al-muntazireen.” The people had dared Hud to bring the punishment, and Hud answered the dare with the patience of certainty: wait, and see, for I am waiting with you, and the wait will not be long. Ibn Kathir notes the dignity of the answer: the prophet does not hasten the punishment and does not fear the threat, but commits the outcome to the One who has decreed it, and the waiting of the prophet is the waiting of the one who knows the end. For the listener, the verse is the final word of every truthful warner: the false gods are names without authority, the punishment is certain, and the prophet and his people wait together — the one in the certainty of the promise, and the other in the heedlessness that the promise will soon overtake.",
  "72": "“So We saved him and those with him by Our mercy and uprooted those who denied Our signs. They were not believers” closes the account of Hud with the verdict of the ‘Ad, and the commentators describe the destruction that the verse compresses. The verse states the salvation and the destruction in two clauses: “We saved him and those with him by Our mercy,” or “fa-anjaynahu wallatheena ma‘ahu bi-rahmatin minna,” the rescue of Hud and the believers through the divine mercy; and “uprooted those who denied Our signs,” or “wa qata‘na dabira allatheena kaththaboo bi-ayatina,” the cutting off of the deniers at the root, the complete eradication of the people who had rejected the signs. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari relate the manner of the destruction: a wind, the wind that the Quran elsewhere describes, was sent against the ‘Ad for seven nights and eight days, a howling, barren gale that lifted the people and cast them down like uprooted palm trunks, until nothing remained of them but their empty dwellings. The word “dabira,” the root, the last remnant, conveys the totality of the ruin: the ‘Ad were not merely punished but extinguished, cut off so completely that no trace of their nation survived.\n\nThe verse closes with the verdict that explains the severity: “They were not believers,” or “wa ma kanoo mu’mineen.” The ‘Ad had been given their messenger, their warning, and their chance, and they had met it all with defiance and mockery, and the verse records that they were not believers — the final statement of the state that made their destruction just. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the second instance of the pattern of the surah: the messenger calls, the people reject, the few are saved by mercy, and the many are cut off for their denial, and the verse teaches that the destruction of a people is never arbitrary but always the harvest of their own rejection.\n\nThe verse also carries the comfort and the warning that attend every such account. The salvation of Hud and the believers “by Our mercy” assures the Prophet and his followers that the believers are never lost, however few they are, and however mighty the enemies that surround them. Ibn Kathir notes that the story of Hud, like the story of Noah, is told to the Prophet as a mirror: the same defiance, the same threat, and the same outcome await the deniers of every age, and the believers of every age are saved, as Hud and his companions were, by the mercy that never fails the faithful. For the listener, the verse is the seal of the second story: the wind of ‘Ad is the measure of the end of every people that denies its warner, and the mercy that saved Hud is the measure of the salvation of every soul that believes.",
  "73": "“And to the people of Thamud We sent their brother Salih. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. A clear proof has come to you from your Lord: this she-camel of Allah is a sign for you. So leave her to graze on Allah’s earth and do not harm her, or else a painful punishment will seize you’” introduces the third of the destroyed nations and their prophet, and the commentators set the scene. The Thamud were the successors of the ‘Ad, an Arab people who dwelt in the rocky valley of al-Hijr in northwestern Arabia, and who had inherited the earth after the destruction of their predecessors, carving their dwellings out of the mountains and building a civilization of remarkable skill and prosperity. To them God sent “their brother Salih,” or “akhahum Salihan,” a man of their own people, and the commentators note the same kinship mentioned for Hud: the messenger is always one of the people, sent to call them back from within, not to condemn them from without. Ibn Kathir explains that the Thamud, like the ‘Ad, had fallen into idolatry after the favour of God, and Salih was raised to restore them to the worship of the One.\n\nSalih’s call opens with the message that all the prophets brought: “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu.” Then the call takes its distinctive turn, for Salih came with a specific proof: “A clear proof has come to you from your Lord: this she-camel of Allah is a sign for you,” or “qad ja’atkum bayyinatum min rabbikum hathihi naqatu Allahi lakum ayah.” The she-camel, the “naqat Allah,” was the miraculous sign that the Thamud had themselves demanded, and the commentators describe its origin: the people had challenged Salih to bring forth a camel from the rock, and God answered by splitting the rock and bringing out a great she-camel, a sign of His power and a test of their obedience. Al-Sa’di observes that the sign was at once a proof and a trial — the proof of the truth of Salih’s message, and the trial of the people’s willingness to obey the command that came with it.\n\nThe command that accompanied the sign is then stated: “So leave her to graze on Allah’s earth and do not harm her, or else a painful punishment will seize you,” or “fatharooha ta’kul fi ardi Allahi wa la tamassooha bi-soo’in fa-ya’khuthakum ‘athabun aleem.” The she-camel was to be left free to graze and drink, and the people were forbidden to harm her, and the command was the test: the people who honoured the sign of God would prove their obedience, and the people who harmed it would bring upon themselves the punishment that the verse names. Ibn Kathir notes that the she-camel was given a day to drink and the people a day, the water of the valley being divided between them, and that this arrangement itself was a further test of their forbearance. For the listener, the verse is the introduction of the third story and the third sign: the camel of Salih, like the Ark of Noah and the wind of Hud, is the measure by which a people’s obedience is weighed.",
  "74": "“Remember when He made you successors after ‘Ad and established you in the land—and you build palaces in its plains and carve homes out of the mountains. So remember Allah’s favours, and do not go about spreading corruption in the land” records Salih’s appeal to the history and the prosperity of his people, and the commentators unfold its argument. Salih reminded the Thamud of what they had been given: “He made you successors after ‘Ad,” or “ja‘alakum khulafa’a min ba‘di ‘Ad,” the inheritance of the earth after the destruction of their predecessors; “and established you in the land,” or “wa bawa’akum fi al-ardi,” the settlement and security that God had granted them; “and you build palaces in its plains and carve homes out of the mountains,” or “tattakhithoona min suhooliha qusooran wa tanhitoona al-jibal buyootan,” the prosperity that their skill had achieved — palaces in the lowlands and dwellings hewn from the rock of the mountains. Ibn Kathir explains that the Thamud were famous for their craftsmanship, and that their mountain-carved homes were the wonder of their age, a sign of the favour that God had poured upon them.\n\nThe verse then draws the lesson from the favour: “So remember Allah’s favours, and do not go about spreading corruption in the land,” or “fathkuroo ala’a Allahi wa la ta‘thaw fi al-ardi mufsideen.” The remembrance of the favours is the remedy for the arrogance that the favours had bred, and the prohibition of corruption is the command that the favours implied, for the people who have been established in the land are the people who must not corrupt it. Al-Sa’di observes the logic of the verse: the favour of God is not a license but a trust, and the people who forget the Giver of their prosperity and use it for corruption have inverted the very purpose of the gift. The word “ta‘thaw” — to go about corrupting, to spread disorder — is the Quran’s term for the misuse of the earth and the oppression of its people, and the verse ties the prosperity of the Thamud to the obligation of justice that their prosperity carried.\n\nThe verse also teaches the general lesson that runs through all the prophetic stories: the appeal to history is an appeal to gratitude, and the gratitude that remembers the favour is the foundation of the obedience that preserves it. Ibn Kathir notes that Salih’s reminder of the ‘Ad was a warning wrapped in a favour: the Thamud had inherited the earth from a people who had been destroyed for their ingratitude, and the inheritance was itself the warning, for the people who repeated the sin of the ‘Ad would meet the fate of the ‘Ad. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of prosperity: the palaces and the carved homes are gifts to be remembered, and the people who forget the Giver and corrupt the land will find their prosperity turned into a witness against them.",
  "75": "“The arrogant chiefs of his people asked the lowly who believed among them, ‘Are you certain that Salih has been sent by his Lord?’ They replied, ‘We certainly believe in what he has been sent with’” records the social dimension of the Thamud’s rejection, and the commentators note its significance. The verse reveals that the division over Salih was not only theological but social: the “arrogant chiefs,” or “al-mala’ allatheena istakbaroo,” the proud notables of the community, addressed the “lowly who believed,” or “allatheena istud‘ifoo,” the weak and the poor who had accepted the message, and demanded of them: “Are you certain that Salih has been sent by his Lord?” Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the believers among the Thamud were drawn from the common people, while the chiefs were the wealthy and the powerful, and the question of the chiefs was not a request for information but a sneer, a way of mocking the faith of the weak by demanding that they justify their certainty before their betters.\n\nThe answer of the believers is recorded with dignity: “We certainly believe in what he has been sent with,” or “inna bima ursila bihi mu’minoon.” The weak did not waver, did not apologize, and did not seek the approval of the powerful; they declared their belief in the message with the certainty that the chiefs lacked, and the commentators note the reversal the verse preserves: the powerful questioned and doubted, while the weak answered and believed, for the truth of the message does not follow the ranks of the world. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the constant feature of the prophetic histories: the first believers are often the poor and the weak, because their hearts are free of the pride that wealth and power breed, while the last to believe, and the first to oppose, are the powerful, whose privilege the truth threatens.\n\nThe verse also teaches the eternal truth about the confrontation between faith and power. The chiefs of Thamud did not argue with Salih’s message; they sneered at his followers, as if the poverty of the believers were the refutation of the belief. Ibn Kathir notes that the Quran preserves this sneer so that every generation may recognize it: the mockery of the weak believer by the powerful disbeliever is the oldest of the world’s weapons, and the answer of the weak — “we certainly believe” — is the oldest of the world’s victories, for the certainty of the believer is the one thing that the power of the disbeliever cannot touch. For the listener, the verse is the portrait of the two camps in every age: the proud who question and the humble who believe, and the reminder that the ranks of this world are reversed in the world to come.",
  "76": "“The arrogant said, ‘We surely reject what you believe in’” records the final rupture after the exchange, and the commentators note the brevity of the sentence and the totality of the rejection. The chiefs, having heard the believers declare their certainty, answered with a declaration of their own: “We surely reject what you believe in,” or “inna billatheee amantum bihi kafiroon.” The word “kafiroon” — rejecters, disbelievers — is the mirror of the believers’ “mu’minoon,” and the verse places the two declarations face to face: the weak said “we believe,” and the powerful said “we reject,” and the community was split by a line that no argument would cross again. Ibn Kathir explains that this is the moment of separation in every prophetic story, the point at which the people divide into the two camps that will meet different ends, and the verse records it with the starkness that the moment deserves.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the word “kafiroon” and its root. The root “kafara” means to cover, to conceal, and the rejecters are those who cover the truth that has become clear to them, burying it under their pride and their interests. Al-Sa’di observes that the rejection of the chiefs was not the ignorance of those who had not heard but the concealment of those who had heard and refused, and that the word captures the voluntary nature of their unbelief: they knew the message of Salih and chose to cover it, and the covering was their own act and their own ruin. The verse thus teaches that the division between the believers and the rejecters is not a division of knowledge but of will, for the truth had reached them all, and only the response differed.\n\nThe verse also functions as a comfort to the believers of every age. The rejection of the chiefs was total, and the believers were few and weak, and yet the Quran records that the few and the weak spoke the word of certainty while the powerful spoke the word of rejection, and that the outcome of the two words would be decided by the One who hears them both. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is placed here to show the Prophet that the division he faced in Mecca was the division that every prophet had faced, and that the certainty of the weak believers is, in the divine scale, the seed of the victory that the powerful cannot prevent. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two declarations: the one that believes and the one that rejects, and the certainty that the two camps are already, in the unseen, on their two roads.",
  "77": "“Then they killed the she-camel—defying their Lord’s command—and challenged Salih, ‘Bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truly a messenger’” records the crime of the Thamud, and the commentators describe the deed and its perpetrators. The verse states the act with the word that condemns it: “Then they killed the she-camel,” or “fa-‘aqaroo an-naqata,” the hamstringing of the camel, the slaying of the sign of God, “defying their Lord’s command,” or “wa ‘ataw ‘an amri rabbihim,” in deliberate defiance of the order they had been given. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari relate the fuller story: the killing was not the act of the whole people but of a band of their most wretched — the commentators name Qudar ibn Salif and his accomplices — who conspired against the camel and slew it, and the verse attributes the deed to the people as a whole because they consented to it, for the silence of the community was its participation. The commentators note that the slaying of the camel was the culmination of the people’s test: the sign that had been given to prove the message was met with the violence that rejected it.\n\nThe verse then records the challenge that followed the crime: “and challenged Salih, ‘Bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truly a messenger,’” or “wa qaloo ya Salihu i’tina bima ta‘iduna in kunta mina al-mursaleen.” The slayers, having killed the sign, dared the prophet to bring down the punishment, and the dare is the same that the ‘Ad had flung at Hud: the demand for the threatened destruction, as if the delay of the punishment were the proof of the lie. Al-Sa’di observes the recklessness that the verse exposes: the people had crossed the final line, the killing of the sign of God, and instead of fearing the consequence they mocked it, and the mockery became the seal upon their fate. The word “al-mursaleen,” the messengers, in their challenge is itself the irony: they called Salih a messenger in the very sentence in which they defied him, and the title they used in mockery was the truth they would not accept.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the gravity of violence against the signs of God. The camel was a creature, but it was also a sign, and the killing of it was the killing of the message it bore, the final act of a people who had chosen defiance over obedience. Ibn Kathir notes that the crime of the Thamud is preserved as a warning: the one who harms the signs of God has declared war upon the One who sent them, and the punishment of such a declaration follows with a certainty that no delay can shake. For the listener, the verse is the climax of the third story: the sign killed, the prophet challenged, and the people standing on the edge of the destruction they had dared.",
  "78": "“Then an overwhelming earthquake struck them, and they fell lifeless in their homes” describes the destruction of the Thamud, and the commentators relate its manner and its timing. The punishment came after the killing of the camel and the challenge to Salih, and the verse names its form: “an overwhelming earthquake,” or “ar-rajfah,” a violent shaking, a convulsion of the earth, combined in the fuller accounts with a thunderous blast from the sky, the “sayhah” that the Quran mentions elsewhere, so that the Thamud were seized from above and below at once. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari describe the scene: the earthquake struck them, the blast overtook them, and “they fell lifeless in their homes,” or “fa-asbahoo fi darihim jathimeen,” the word “jathimeen” conveying the image of bodies fallen and still, kneeling upon their knees in death, every one of them struck down in the very dwellings they had carved from the rock. The commentators note the irony that the Quran preserves: the people who had boasted of their mountain-carved homes perished within them, and the dwellings that had been their pride became their graves.\n\nThe verse is the fulfillment of the warning that Salih had delivered, and the commentators note the precision of the sequence. The Thamud had killed the camel and dared the punishment, and the punishment came, and the delay between the crime and the judgment — three days, as the fuller accounts relate, during which Salih warned them again — was the final mercy, the last span in which they might have repented. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the manner of the divine judgment: it is not hasty but certain, and the delay is the mercy that precedes the decree, for the punishment of God comes only after every door of return has been shown and shut.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two homes. The Thamud built their homes in the mountains and thought themselves secure, and the security of stone collapsed before the decree of God, for no wall and no mountain can shelter the soul that has defied its Lord. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is one of the Quran’s many witnesses to the fragility of worldly security: the people who trust in their palaces and their carved homes are trusting in what cannot save them, and the only security that endures is the security of the one who shelters in the mercy of God. For the listener, the verse is the verdict of the third story: the earthquake of Thamud is the measure of the end of every people that kills the signs of God, and the lifeless bodies in the carved homes are the witness that no strength of stone withstands the decree.",
  "79": "“So he turned away from them, saying, ‘O my people! Surely I conveyed to you my Lord’s message and gave you sincere advice, but you do not like sincere advisors’” records Salih’s farewell to his destroyed people, and the commentators note its grief and its vindication. The verse describes Salih turning away from the ruins of his people, “tawalla ‘anhum,” departing from the valley of the dead, and his departing words are the words of the messenger whose duty has been fulfilled: “Surely I conveyed to you my Lord’s message and gave you sincere advice,” or “laqad ablaghtukum risalata rabbee wa nasahtu lakum.” The two claims — the delivery of the message and the giving of the counsel — are the summary of the prophetic office, and Salih spoke them over the ruins as the testimony that he had withheld nothing and failed in nothing. Ibn Kathir explains that the farewell of the prophet is not an accusation but a vindication: the destruction of the people was not for lack of warning, and the messenger could depart with the peace of the one who had done his whole duty.\n\nThe verse closes with the sentence that gathers the tragedy: “but you do not like sincere advisors,” or “walakin la tuhibboona an-nasiheen.” The people had not been deprived of counsel; they had hated it, and the hatred of the advisor was the measure of their ruin. Al-Sa’di observes the profound truth in the sentence: the sincere advisor is the one who warns, and the people who love their ease more than their salvation will always hate the warning, and the hatred of the advisor is the hatred of the truth itself. The verse thus teaches that the destruction of a people is not the failure of the message but the failure of the love, for the message had been delivered and the counsel had been given, and the only thing the people had refused was the sincerity that sought their good.\n\nThe farewell of Salih also echoes the farewell of every prophet, and the commentators connect it to the Prophet Muhammad’s own situation. The messenger who departs from a people that would not be saved speaks the same words in every age: I conveyed, I advised, and you would not hear. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is a comfort to the caller who grieves over the rejection of his people: the duty is the delivery and the counsel, and the outcome belongs to God, and the messenger who has delivered and advised has fulfilled the trust, whatever the response. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the messenger’s farewell: the message is the trust, the counsel is the love, and the people who reject the advisor have rejected not the messenger but their own salvation.",
  "80": "“And remember when Lot scolded the men of his people, saying, ‘Do you commit a shameful deed that no one in the world has ever done before?’” introduces the fourth of the prophets in the sequence, and the commentators set the scene of his mission. Lot, or Lut, was the nephew of Abraham, who had settled with him in the land and had been sent to the people of Sodom and its neighbouring cities, a people sunk in a vice that the Quran names without ambiguity. The verse opens with the word “scolded,” or “qala li-qawmihi,” for Lot’s address was a rebuke, the words of a prophet confronting his people with the deed that had made them infamous: “Do you commit a shameful deed that no one in the world has ever done before?” or “a-ta’toona al-fahishata ma sabaqakum biha min ahadin mina al-‘alameen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the “fahishah,” the shameful deed, was the practice for which the people of Lot have been remembered ever since — the men approaching men with lust, abandoning the women whom God had created for them — and that the verse declares it a deed without precedent, an innovation of depravity that no nation before them had practiced.\n\nThe commentators note the structure of the rebuke. Lot did not begin with a general call to monotheism, as the other prophets had, but with the specific sin that had consumed his people, for the first duty of the prophet is to confront the evil of his time, and the evil of Sodom was a sin so gross that it demanded the most direct of addresses. Al-Sa’di observes that the question — “do you commit this deed?” — is a rebuke that seeks the answer of the conscience, and that the phrase “no one in the world has ever done before” is the measure of the deed’s enormity: the people of Lot had invented a corruption that had no precedent among the nations, and their originality in evil was the mark of their fall. The verse thus teaches that the prophet confronts the sin of his people without euphemism and without delay, and that the naming of the sin is the first step of its healing.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the natural order that the sin violated. The Quran elsewhere states that the people of Lot approached men with lust “instead of women,” and the commentators explain that the deed was a violation of the order that God had established in creation, the turning of the natural inclination from its appointed object to its opposite. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse’s emphasis on the deed’s lack of precedent underscores this: the sin was not merely a lapse but an inversion, a corruption so total that it had no model in the history of the nations. For the listener, the verse is the opening of the fourth story and the warning it carries: the prophet of God confronts the shameful deed, and the people who persist in a corruption without precedent will meet a punishment that, as the story will show, is itself without parallel.",
  "81": "“You lust after men instead of women! You are certainly transgressors” spells out the shameful deed with the clarity that the Quran’s moral language always brings, and the commentators note the directness of the indictment. The verse removes the euphemism that the previous verse had kept at the level of “a shameful deed” and names the sin precisely: “You lust after men instead of women,” or “innakum la-ta’toona ar-rijala shahwatan min dooni an-nisa’,” the approaching of men with desire in place of women, the inversion of the order that God had appointed. Ibn Kathir explains that the verse is the Quran’s clearest statement of the sin of the people of Lot, and that the clarity is itself part of the warning, for the sin that is named can be avoided, while the sin that is veiled in euphemism is the sin that festers in the dark.\n\nThe verse closes with the verdict that the sin deserves: “You are certainly transgressors,” or “bal antum qawmun musrifoon.” The word “musrifoon” — transgressors, those who exceed the bounds — is the Quran’s term for the crossing of the limits that God has set, and the commentators dwell on its precision. The sin of the people of Lot was not merely an error but an excess, a passing beyond the boundary of the natural and the permitted into a corruption that no limit could contain, and the word “musrifoon” marks the crossing. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the complete Quranic view of the deed: it is an inversion of the created order, an excess beyond the bounds of the permissible, and a transgression that corrupts both the individual and the society, and the clarity of the language is the clarity of the moral law that forbids it.\n\nThe verse also serves a larger purpose in the surah’s argument. The sequence of the prophets is a sequence of sins and their corrections: the idolatry of Noah’s people, the arrogance of ‘Ad, the defiance of Thamud, and now the sexual corruption of the people of Lot, and the Quran confronts each with the same directness. Ibn Kathir notes that the surah thus presents the full range of human corruption and the full range of prophetic confrontation, teaching that the messenger of God does not flatter any age’s sins but names them, and that the naming of the sin is the mercy that offers the way out. For the listener, the verse is the clear statement of the deed that destroyed the cities of the plain, and the warning that the transgression of the limits of God, in whatever form, carries the same verdict that fell upon its first practitioners.",
  "82": "“But his people’s only response was to say, ‘Expel them from your land! They are a people who seek to be pure!’” records the answer of the people of Lot to his call, and the commentators note the tragic irony it preserves. Lot had confronted his people with their sin and called them to purity, and their only answer was the decision to expel him: “Expel them from your land!” or “akhrijuhum min qaryatikum,” the command to drive Lot and his followers out of the city, “They are a people who seek to be pure!” or “innahum unasun yatatahharoon.” Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the people of Lot, unable to answer the argument and unwilling to abandon the sin, resolved to rid themselves of the voice that condemned them, and their words — “they seek to be pure” — were spoken in mockery, as if the purity of Lot and his followers were itself the offense for which they must be cast out. The commentators note the reversal that the verse preserves: the people whose sin was the depth of impurity mocked the purity of the prophet, and the desire for purity became, in their mouths, a crime.\n\nThe word “yatatahharoon,” they seek to be pure, is the key, and the commentators dwell on it. The root “tahara” means purity, cleanliness, the removal of filth, and the verse records that the people of Lot used the very word of purity as their accusation, sneering at the believers as those who would cleanse themselves of the filth in which the city lived. Al-Sa’di observes the irony that has become proverbial: the corrupt mock the pure for their purity, and the city that has sunk into its sin finds the presence of the righteous intolerable, for the mere existence of the pure is a rebuke to the impure. The verse thus teaches that the people of falsehood do not merely disagree with the people of truth but cannot bear their presence, and the expulsion of the prophets is the oldest of the world’s answers to the truth.\n\nThe verse also reveals the isolation of the caller in a corrupt society. Lot and his followers were a minority whose very presence was resented, and the answer to their call was not debate but expulsion, for the society that has chosen its sin cannot tolerate the voice that condemns it. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is placed here to show the Prophet Muhammad that the expulsion of the believers was the fate of the believers before him, and that the mockery of the pure is the oldest of the world’s mockeries. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the price of purity: the pure are hated by the impure, the truthful are expelled by the false, and the believer who seeks to be pure must expect, and endure, the accusation that his purity is the offense.",
  "83": "“So We saved him and his family except his wife, who was one of the doomed” gives the divine verdict upon the cities of Lot, and the commentators note the precise boundary of the salvation. The verse states the rescue and its single exception: “We saved him and his family,” or “fa-anjaynahu wa ahlahu,” the deliverance of Lot and his household from the destruction; “except his wife, who was one of the doomed,” or “illa imra’atahu kanat mina al-ghabireen,” the wife who was not saved but remained behind among those who were destroyed. Ibn Kathir explains the reason the wife was excepted: she had been a believer in the religion of her people, or had sympathized with them in their sin, and had conspired against Lot by signaling to the men of the city when guests arrived at his house, and her heart was with the doomed though her body dwelt in the house of the prophet. The verse thus teaches the lesson that the Quran repeats: salvation is not a matter of kinship but of faith, and the wife of a prophet perished with the people of sin while the household of the prophet was saved, for the bond of blood does not cross the boundary between the saved and the doomed.\n\nThe verse compresses the fuller story that the Quran tells elsewhere: the angels who came to Abraham and then to Lot, the men of the city who demanded the guests, the command to Lot to leave by night without looking back, and the punishment that fell upon the cities at dawn. Here the surah states only the outcome, and the commentators note that the compression serves the pattern of the passage: the call, the rejection, and the verdict, with the details reserved for the fuller accounts. Al-Sa’di observes that the exception of the wife is the detail the surah retains because it carries the greatest lesson — the lesson that no one is saved by association, and that the heart, not the household, determines the destiny.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the comfort of the divine salvation. Lot and his family were few, surrounded by a corrupt city, and yet God saved them and destroyed the city, and the verse assures the believers of every age that the righteous are never abandoned to the fate of the wicked, however few they are and however mighty the wickedness around them. Ibn Kathir notes that the salvation of Lot is the fourth instance of the pattern: the Ark saved Noah, the mercy saved Hud, the warning saved Salih, and now the departure saved Lot, and the believers of every age are saved, as their brothers were, by the hand that never fails the faithful. For the listener, the verse is the verdict of the fourth story: the cities destroyed, the prophet saved, and the wife left behind — the eternal lesson that salvation follows the heart, not the blood.",
  "84": "“We poured upon them a rain of brimstone. See what was the end of the wicked!” describes the destruction of the cities of Lot, and the commentators relate its manner and its meaning. The punishment came as a “rain,” or “matarna ‘alayhim mataran,” but a rain of destruction: the commentators explain that the cities of the plain were overturned, lifted up and cast down, and that a shower of stones — baked clay, marked stones, brimstone — fell upon them, so that the people perished under the very sky that had once rained mercy upon the land. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari describe the scene: the cities were turned upside down, and the stones rained upon the fleeing people until not one of them escaped, and the land that had been the seat of their sin became the monument of their punishment. The word “matar,” rain, is itself the irony the verse preserves: the same word that the Quran uses for the blessed rain that revives the earth is used for the shower that destroyed the wicked, so that the listener is made to feel the reversal of the mercy into the wrath.\n\nThe verse closes with the question that turns the story into a lesson: “See what was the end of the wicked!” or “fanzur kayfa kana ‘aqibatu al-mujrimeen.” The word “mujrimeen,” the wicked, the criminals, is the verdict upon the people of Lot, and the question invites the listener to look, to reflect, to consider the end of the path of wickedness. Al-Sa’di observes that the question is the Quran’s method of teaching through history: the destruction of the cities is not a tale to be told but a lesson to be learned, and the one who “sees” the end of the wicked in the story is the one who is warned against the wickedness in his own soul. The word “‘aqibah,” the end, the final outcome, is the key: the Quran teaches that the measure of a deed is its end, and the end of the wicked, however long delayed, is the ruin that the story records.\n\nThe verse also carries the warning that the punishment of the people of Lot was not a relic of the past but a law of the present. The rain of stones that fell upon the cities is the standing witness that the corruption that defies the limits of God meets, in the end, a destruction that no power can avert, and the question “see what was the end of the wicked” is addressed to every generation that considers the same sins. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse, like the accounts of Noah, ‘Ad, and Thamud, is told to the Prophet as a warning to the deniers of Mecca and a comfort to the believers, and that the end of the wicked is the single lesson that the four stories together teach. For the listener, the verse is the seal of the fourth story: the rain of brimstone is the end of the wicked, and the question that follows it is the summons to see, and to be warned.",
  "85": "“And to the people of Midian We sent their brother Shu‘ayb. He said, ‘O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. A clear proof has come to you from your Lord. So give full measure and weight, do not defraud people of what is theirs, and do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set right. That is best for you, if you are believers’” introduces the fifth and last of the prophets in the sequence, and the commentators set the scene of his mission. The people of Midian, or Madyan, were an Arab people of the northwestern region, a commercial nation whose life was built on trade, and their sin, unlike the idolatry of Noah’s people or the vice of the people of Lot, was the sin of the marketplace: the defrauding of the measure and the weight, the cheating of the buyer, and the corruption of the economy through dishonesty. To them God sent “their brother Shu‘ayb,” or “akhahum Shu‘ayban,” and the commentators note the title of Shu‘ayb in the tradition: he is called the “orator of the prophets,” the “khatib al-anbiya’,” for the eloquence of his address, which the Quran preserves at length. Ibn Kathir explains that Shu‘ayb was sent to a people whose corruption was economic, and that his message joined the call to monotheism with the command of justice in the scales, for the worship of the One and the honesty of the market are two sides of a single faith.\n\nShu‘ayb’s call opens with the message that all the prophets brought: “O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him,” or “ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu,” and then turns to the specific command of his people: “So give full measure and weight, do not defraud people of what is theirs, and do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set right,” or “awfoo al-kayla wal-meezana wa la tabkhasoo an-nasa ashya’ahum wa la tufsidoo fi al-ardi ba‘da islahiha.” The commentators unfold the three commands: the full measure and weight, the “kayl” and the “meezan,” the honesty of the scales that is the foundation of all commerce; the prohibition of defrauding, the “bakhs,” the shortchanging of the buyer and the stealing of value; and the prohibition of corruption after the land has been set right, the “fasad” that undoes the order that God has established. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is one of the Quran’s great statements of economic justice, and that the sin of Midian — the cheating of the scales — is the sin of every society that builds its wealth on the defrauding of its own people.\n\nThe verse closes with the appeal that seals the command: “That is best for you, if you are believers,” or “thalikum khayrun lakum in kuntum mu’mineen.” The honesty of the scales is not a burden but a good, and the command of justice is for the benefit of those who practice it, for the society that trades honestly is the society that prospers, while the society that defrauds is the society that decays. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse ties the economic command to the faith that it tests: the honest measure is the proof of the believer, and the defrauding of the scales is the mark of the one whose faith has not reached his hands. For the listener, the verse is the opening of the fifth story and the lesson it carries: the worship of God is not complete without the justice of the scales, and the people who cheat their buyers have already cheated their own souls.",
  "86": "“And do not lie in ambush on every road—threatening and hindering those who believe in Allah from His path and seeking to make it crooked. And remember when you were few, and He multiplied you. And see what was the end of the corruptors!” records the further commands of Shu‘ayb and the reminder that accompanied them, and the commentators explain the sin that the verse condemns. The people of Midian did not only defraud in the market; they also “lay in ambush on every road,” or “wa la taq‘udoo bi-kulli siratin too‘idoon,” the banditry and intimidation by which they waylaid travelers, and the verse specifies the particular target: “threatening and hindering those who believe in Allah from His path and seeking to make it crooked,” or “wa tasuddoona ‘an sabeeli Allahi man amana bihi wa tabghoonaha ‘iwajan.” Ibn Kathir explains that the men of Midian would sit upon the roads, threatening the travelers and the believers, exacting tolls and instilling fear, and seeking to turn the path of God into a crooked way, so that the honest and the faithful were blocked from their journeys and their worship. The commentators note that the verse thus joins the economic sin of the previous verse to the sin of violence and obstruction, for the corruption of the people of Midian had spread from the marketplace to the highways.\n\nThe verse then turns to the reminder that underlies every command: “And remember when you were few, and He multiplied you,” or “wathkuroo idh kuntum qaleelan fa-kaththarakum.” The people of Midian had been a small tribe, and God had multiplied them into a numerous and prosperous nation, and the reminder of the multiplication is the appeal to the gratitude that their prosperity had erased. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse follows the method of the prophets: the command of justice is grounded in the memory of the favour, for the people who remember that their numbers and their wealth are the gift of God will not use them to oppress, and the gratitude of the heart is the root of the justice of the hand. The reminder also carries a warning within it, for the God who multiplied a few into many can return the many to few, and the people who forget the multiplication will be reminded of it by its reversal.\n\nThe verse closes with the question that turns the reminder into a warning: “And see what was the end of the corruptors!” or “fanzuroo kayfa kana ‘aqibatu al-mufsideen.” The word “mufsideen,” the corruptors, is the verdict upon the people of Midian, and the question invites them to look at the end of the corruptors before them — the people of Noah, of ‘Ad, of Thamud, of Lot — whose corruption had met its ruin, and to recognize that their own corruption was walking the same road. Ibn Kathir notes that the question is the Quran’s constant method: the warning is delivered through the example of the past, so that the people of the present may see their own end in the end of their predecessors. For the listener, the verse is the lesson that the corruption of the road and the scale is the corruption of the soul, and that the end of the corruptors, in every age, is the ruin that the question summons them to see.",
  "87": "“If some of you do believe in what I have been sent with while others do not, then be patient until Allah judges between us. He is the best of judges” records Shu‘ayb’s answer to the division of his people, and the commentators note the patience and the confidence it teaches. The people of Midian had split over Shu‘ayb’s message, some believing and some rejecting, and the believers were being pressured and threatened by the majority, so that the community was in the grip of a conflict that no human authority could settle. Shu‘ayb’s answer was to commit the matter to its only true judge: “then be patient until Allah judges between us,” or “fasbiroo hatta yahkuma Allahu baynana,” the command to wait in patience for the judgment of God, and the closing declaration: “He is the best of judges,” or “wa huwa khayru al-hakimeen.” Ibn Kathir explains that the verse is the prophet’s response to the division and the pressure: he did not call for rebellion, did not despair, and did not take the judgment into his own hands, but committed the case to the Judge whose verdict is always just and whose timing is always wise.\n\nThe word “sabr,” patience, is the key, and the commentators dwell on it. The patience commanded here is not the patience of the idle but the patience of the steadfast, the endurance of the believer who continues in his obedience while the judgment is awaited, and the verse teaches that the believer under pressure has no weapon greater than this patience, for the conflict between truth and falsehood is not settled by human haste but by divine judgment. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is addressed to both parties — to the believers, that they may endure the pressure without despair, and, by implication, to the rejecters, that they may know that the delay of the punishment is not the proof of their innocence but the patience of the Judge. The judgment of God, the verse declares, is certain, and the patience of the believer is the waiting for a verdict that cannot fail.\n\nThe verse also carries a lesson for every community divided over the truth. Shu‘ayb did not pretend the division did not exist, and he did not seek to paper it over with false harmony; he acknowledged it and committed it to the judgment of God, teaching that the truth is not decided by the numbers of its supporters but by the verdict of the Judge of all. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is a model for the believer in every age of division: to hold to the truth, to endure the pressure, and to await the judgment of the One whose verdict is final. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the patient trust that the whole surah has been teaching: the conflict is real, the judge is just, and the believer who waits in patience has already chosen the winning side.",
  "88": "“The arrogant chiefs of his people threatened, ‘O Shu‘ayb! We will certainly expel you and the believers with you from our town, unless you return to our faith.’ He replied, ‘Even if we hate it?’” records the threat of the Midian chiefs and Shu‘ayb’s answer, and the commentators note the structure of the exchange. The “mala’,” the arrogant chiefs, now moved from pressure to open threat: “We will certainly expel you and the believers with you from our town, unless you return to our faith,” or “la-nukhrijannaka ya Shu‘aybu wallatheena amanoo ma‘aka min qaryatina aw la-ta‘oodunna fi millatina.” Ibn Kathir explains the threat: the chiefs gave Shu‘ayb and the believers the choice between expulsion and apostasy, demanding that the prophet and his followers return to the religion of their fathers or be driven from the land, and the demand reveals the arrogance that the verse names — the chiefs regarded the town as “ours,” the faith as “ours,” and the presence of the believers within it as a favour to be withdrawn. The threat is the oldest weapon of the powerful against the truthful: the choice between the faith and the homeland.\n\nShu‘ayb’s answer is one of the most memorable in the Quran: “He replied, ‘Even if we hate it?’” or “qala a-wa law kunna kariheen.” The question demolishes the threat with a single stroke: you demand that we return to your faith, but would we return to it even if we hate it, even if we know it to be false? The implication is unanswerable: the faith to which they were being summoned was a faith they loathed, and no threat could make the loathed beloved, for the heart cannot be compelled to love what it knows to be false. Al-Sa’di observes the force of the question: Shu‘ayb exposed the absurdity of the demand, for the return to the faith of the fathers would be a return to what the believers detested, and the compulsion of the body cannot compel the heart. The question is the declaration of the believer’s freedom: the faith is not a garment to be changed under threat, and the truth that the heart has found cannot be exchanged for the falsehood that the tyrant commands.\n\nThe verse also teaches the dignity of the believer under threat. Shu‘ayb did not plead for the right to stay, did not bargain for a lesser punishment, and did not flinch from the demand; he answered the threat with a question that placed the absurdity of the demand in the open. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is a model for every believer who faces the choice between the faith and the homeland: the answer of Shu‘ayb is the answer of every sincere soul — even if we hate it, we will not return, for the homeland of the soul is the truth, and no expulsion from the town can expel the believer from his Lord. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the price and the dignity of faith: the powerful may threaten the town, but the heart that hates the falsehood cannot be forced to love it.",
  "89": "“We would surely be fabricating a lie against Allah if we were to return to your faith after Allah has saved us from it. It is not for us to return to it unless Allah, our Lord, wills. Our Lord encompasses everything in knowledge. In Allah we have put our trust. Our Lord! Judge between us and our people in truth, for You are the best of those who judge” records Shu‘ayb’s full reply, and the commentators present it as a complete statement of the believer’s position. The first sentence states the reason of the refusal: “We would surely be fabricating a lie against Allah if we were to return to your faith after Allah has saved us from it,” or “qadi iftarayna ‘ala Allahi kathiban in ‘udna fi millatikum ba‘da idh najjana Allahu minha.” Ibn Kathir explains the argument: the faith of the fathers had been shown to be false, and God had delivered Shu‘ayb and the believers from it, and to return to it now would be to fabricate a lie against God, to re-embrace what they knew to be false, and the believer cannot speak a lie against his Lord. The sentence teaches that apostasy from the truth is not merely a change of opinion but a falsehood against God, and the one who knows the truth and returns to falsehood has forged a lie in the face of his Lord.\n\nThe second sentence qualifies the refusal with the submission that the believer never forgets: “It is not for us to return to it unless Allah, our Lord, wills,” or “wa ma yakoonu lana an na‘ooda feeha illa an yasha’a Allahu rabbuna.” The commentators note the depth of this: even in the act of refusing, Shu‘ayb acknowledged that the final disposition of all things is the will of God, and that no human resolution stands except by His permission, so that the believer’s “no” is spoken within the circle of the divine will. The sentence is followed by the statement of trust and knowledge: “Our Lord encompasses everything in knowledge. In Allah we have put our trust,” or “wasi‘a rabbuna kulla shay’in ‘ilman ‘ala Allahi tawakkalna,” the confession that God’s knowledge surrounds all, and the declaration of the trust that follows from it. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse gathers the whole theology of the believer under threat: the refusal of the false, the submission to the will, the trust in the knowledge, and, in the closing prayer, the appeal to the judgment of God.\n\nThe verse closes with the prayer that commits the outcome to its only true Judge: “Our Lord! Judge between us and our people in truth, for You are the best of those who judge,” or “rabbana iftah baynana wa bayna qawmina bil-haqqi wa anta khayru al-fatiheen.” The word “iftah” — open, judge, decide — is the plea for the divine verdict, and the prayer asks that the separation between the believers and their people be made in truth, by the One whose judgment is the best. Ibn Kathir notes that the prayer is the prophet’s final recourse: having delivered the message and refused the falsehood, he commits the division to the judgment of God, and the prayer is answered, as the following verses show, in the destruction of the people and the salvation of the believers. For the listener, the verse is the complete answer of the believer to the demand of apostasy: the lie refused, the will submitted to, the trust declared, and the judgment committed to the best of judges.",
  "90": "“The disbelieving chiefs of his people threatened, ‘If you follow Shu‘ayb, you will surely be losers’” records the final pressure of the chiefs upon their people, and the commentators note its method and its irony. The chiefs, having failed to move Shu‘ayb, turned to the people with a threat of their own: “If you follow Shu‘ayb, you will surely be losers,” or “la-ini ittaba‘tum Shu‘ayban innakum ithan la-khasiroon.” Ibn Kathir explains the threat: the chiefs warned the people that the following of Shu‘ayb would mean the loss of their commerce, their prosperity, and their standing, for the honest measure and the abandoned banditry that Shu‘ayb commanded would cut into the profits that the people had built their lives upon, and the chiefs painted the path of obedience as the path of loss. The word “khasiroon,” the losers, is the key, and the commentators note the irony that the verse will expose: the chiefs called the followers of the truth “losers,” and the verdict of the story will reverse the label, for the true losers were the chiefs themselves, who lost their souls and their lives for the sake of the profits they would not surrender.\n\nThe verse reveals the economic root of the rejection, and the commentators dwell on it. The people of Midian were a commercial nation, and the call of Shu‘ayb threatened the dishonest gains that had become the foundation of their wealth, so that the rejection of the prophet was, at bottom, the defense of the profit. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the lesson of the story of Midian for every age: the truth is often rejected not because it is believed false but because it is costly, and the people who have made their peace with dishonest gain will call the call to honesty a loss. The word “khasiroon” thus carries its full weight: the chiefs measured gain and loss by the scales of the market, and they declared the honest losers, while the divine accounting, which the story will reveal, declared the honest the only winners.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the nature of the pressure that truth always faces. The threat of the chiefs was not the threat of violence alone but the threat of loss — the warning that the following of the truth would cost the follower his livelihood and his place — and the Quran preserves the threat so that every believer may recognize it, for the same warning is whispered in every age to those who hesitate at the threshold of obedience. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is placed here to complete the portrait of the rejection of Shu‘ayb: the pressure of the powerful, the threat of loss, and the fear that the threat instilled — and to prepare the verdict that the following verses pronounce. For the listener, the verse is the warning that the world will call the truth a loss, and the assurance that the scales of the world are not the scales of God, for the ones the chiefs called losers are the ones the Lord will call the saved.",
  "91": "“Then an overwhelming earthquake struck them and they fell lifeless in their homes” records the destruction of the people of Midian, and the commentators note the identity of the punishment with that of Thamud and its meaning. The verse uses the same words that described the destruction of Thamud: “an overwhelming earthquake,” or “ar-rajfah,” the violent shaking of the earth, and “they fell lifeless in their homes,” or “fa-asbahoo fi darihim jathimeen,” the bodies fallen still in the dwellings where they had schemed. Ibn Kathir explains the identity of the two punishments: the people of Midian, like the people of Thamud, were seized by the earthquake, and the fuller accounts add the blast from the sky, so that the two peoples who had shared the sin of corruption shared the manner of its punishment. The commentators note the justice of the symmetry: the people who had corrupted the land were destroyed by the land itself, the earth that they had defrauded and defiled rising against them in a single convulsion.\n\nThe verse completes the pattern of the surah’s five stories, and the commentators note its cumulative weight. Noah’s people were drowned, ‘Ad were destroyed by the wind, Thamud by the earthquake and the blast, the people of Lot by the overturned cities and the rain of stones, and now Midian by the earthquake — five peoples, five sins, five punishments, and one law running through them all. Al-Sa’di observes that the repetition of the stories is itself the teaching: the sunnah of God does not change, and the people who repeat the sins of the destroyed will meet the destruction of the destroyed, for the divine justice is as constant as the divine mercy. The verse thus serves as the fifth witness to the law that the surah has been establishing, and the listener is summoned to read the five stories as one story, and the one story as the warning of his own age.\n\nThe verse also carries the comfort and the warning that attend every such account. The destruction of Midian was the answer to the prayer of Shu‘ayb — “judge between us and our people in truth” — and the commentators note that the prayer of the prophet was answered in the ruin of the corruptors and the salvation of the believers, so that the verse assures the believers of every age that the judgment of God, when it comes, comes in truth. Ibn Kathir notes that the story of Shu‘ayb is the last of the five, and that its lesson is the lesson of them all: the corruptors fell lifeless in their homes, and the believers were saved, and the separation that the prophets prayed for was made in the open. For the listener, the verse is the verdict of the fifth story: the earthquake of Midian is the end of the corruptors, and the homes that sheltered their schemes became the graves that hold their dust.",
  "92": "“Those who rejected Shu‘ayb were wiped out as if they had never lived there. Those who rejected Shu‘ayb were the true losers” pronounces the final verdict upon Midian, and the commentators note the completeness of the ruin and the reversal of the label. The verse states the destruction with a phrase of total erasure: “Those who rejected Shu‘ayb were wiped out as if they had never lived there,” or “allatheena kaththaboo Shu‘ayban ka-an lam yaghnaw feeha,” the rejecters so completely removed that it was as if they had never dwelt in the land at all. Ibn Kathir explains the image: the cities of Midian, once teeming with the commerce and the schemes of their people, became empty and silent, and the passerby who saw them could not tell that a people had ever lived there, for the destruction had erased even the memory of their dwelling. The word “yaghnaw” — to dwell, to live comfortably, to thrive — is the measure of the loss: the people who had thrived in the land were removed from it as if their thriving had been a dream.\n\nThe verse closes with the sentence that reverses the threat of the chiefs: “Those who rejected Shu‘ayb were the true losers,” or “allatheena kaththaboo Shu‘ayban kanoo humu al-khasireen.” The chiefs had threatened the people, saying “if you follow Shu‘ayb, you will surely be losers,” and the verdict of the verse answers the threat with its exact reverse: the losers were not the followers of Shu‘ayb but the rejecters, and the word “khasiroon” that the chiefs had flung at the believers was returned upon their own heads. Al-Sa’di observes the divine irony that the verse preserves: the ones who measured loss by the scales of the market discovered, at the end, that the only loss that matters is the loss of the soul, and the ones they had called losers were the ones who had gained everything. The verse thus teaches the believer the true meaning of loss: the loss of the Hereafter is the only loss, and the people who gained the world and lost their souls are the “true losers” that the verse names.\n\nThe verse also seals the lesson of the five stories with its final word. The surah has traced the call and the rejection through five peoples, and the verdict upon each is the same: the rejecters were the losers, and the believers were the saved. Ibn Kathir notes that the repetition of the verdict — “the true losers” — is the Quran’s way of fixing the lesson in the heart: the scales of the world are not the scales of God, and the one who is called a loser by the world may be, in the accounting that matters, the only winner. For the listener, the verse is the conclusion of the first half of the surah’s prophetic history: the five stories have taught the one law, and the one law is the warning and the promise that the remaining story of Moses will unfold.",
  "93": "“He turned away from them, saying, ‘O my people! Indeed, I have delivered to you the messages of my Lord and given you sincere advice. So how can I grieve over a people who disbelieve?’” records the farewell of Shu‘ayb, and the commentators note its dignity and its lesson. Like Salih before him, Shu‘ayb turned away from his destroyed people, and his departing words are the words of the messenger whose duty has been completed: “Indeed, I have delivered to you the messages of my Lord and given you sincere advice,” or “laqad ablaghtukum risalati rabbee wa nasahtu lakum.” Ibn Kathir explains the farewell: the prophet departed with the testimony that he had delivered the message and given the counsel, and that nothing had been withheld and nothing had been neglected, so that the destruction of the people was not the failure of the messenger but the fruit of their own rejection. The farewell of Shu‘ayb echoes the farewell of Salih, and the repetition teaches that the messenger’s account is the same in every story: the delivery made, the counsel given, and the people left to the choice they had made.\n\nThe verse closes with the question that gathers the lesson of the farewell: “So how can I grieve over a people who disbelieve?” or “fa-kayfa asa ‘ala qawmin kafireen.” The question is not coldness but wisdom: the prophet who had done his whole duty could not grieve indefinitely over a people who had chosen their own ruin, and the question teaches the believer the limit of sorrow. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is a comfort to every caller who has grieved over the rejection of his people: the grief of the messenger is noble, but it must not become the despair that consumes, for the people who disbelieve after the message has been delivered have chosen their end, and the messenger who has delivered is free of their fate. The word “kafireen,” the disbelievers, is the final verdict, and the question “how can I grieve” is the release of the prophet from a sorrow that the people’s own choice had made fruitless.\n\nThe verse also completes the pattern of the prophetic farewells in the surah, and the commentators note its place. Salih had said “you do not like sincere advisors,” and Shu‘ayb said “how can I grieve over a people who disbelieve,” and the two farewells together teach the full lesson of the messenger’s end: the duty fulfilled, the counsel given, and the sorrow laid down, for the messenger of God walks away from the ruins with the peace of the one who has delivered, and the judgment of the people is left to the One who alone judges. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse prepares the transition to the story of Moses, the greatest of the prophetic histories in this surah, and that the five shorter stories have been the introduction to it. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the messenger’s peace: the delivery is the trust, the counsel is the love, and the grief over the rejecters is laid down at the feet of the One who will judge them — for the messenger has delivered, and his account is clear.",
  "94": "“Whenever We sent a prophet to a society, We afflicted its ˹disbelieving˺ people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled” extracts the general divine law behind the five stories just recounted from Noah to Shu‘ayb. The previous episodes each showed a people afflicted with a final seizing after rejecting their brother’s sincere advice. This verse steps back to explain the earlier, gentler phase that preceded final terms. The phrase “Whenever We sent a prophet to a society,” or “wa ma arsalna fi qaryatin min nabiyyin,” shows this is a recurring pattern, not an isolated reaction, linked to the principle that your Lord would never destroy a society while its people are unaware. The initial affliction, “We afflicted its disbelieving people with suffering and adversity,” or “akhathna ahlaha bil-ba’sa’i wa al-darra’i,” uses two terms already seen: “ba’sa,” severe hardship like famine, economic loss, defeat, and “darra,” distress like illness, bodily pain. These are the same sufferings that earlier were meant to make people humble, or “yata darra‘oon,” become low, suppliant, calling humbly and secretly.\n\nThe purpose is then stated with the same gentleness: “so perhaps they would be humbled,” or “la‘allahum yaddarra‘oon.” The particle “la‘alla” perhaps shows hope from mercy always close to the good-doers, the same mercy that sends winds ushering rain to lifeless land and that took mercy upon Himself to welcome the repentant who mend. Humbled calling, or “tadarru‘,” is the opposite of arrogance for which gates of heaven do not open.\n\nThis verse links to the later cycle where adversity is changed to prosperity until they flourish and then argue “our forefathers too had been visited by adversity and prosperity,” so We seized them by surprise while unaware. The first phase is therefore a merciful alarm, like day and night overlapping to remind of the Creator’s command.\n\nA common error is to view suffering as mere abandonment. The Quran shows even hardship is ordered, varied as lessons for those who are thankful, aiming to soften the hard soil so it might produce abundantly before the final weighing. For the Meccan audience who had enjoyed livelihood yet seldom gave thanks and who experienced drought before prosperity, the law was immediate. For any reader, the instruction remains to answer early humbling with humble secret calls, not with the hard-chested tightening that makes punishment come at night or midday while asleep or at play.",
  "95": "“Then We changed their adversity to prosperity until they flourished and argued ˹falsely˺, ‘Our forefathers ˹too˺ had been visited by adversity and prosperity.’ So We seized them by surprise, while they were unaware” continues the divine law after the first phase of suffering meant to humble. The previous verse said societies were afflicted with ba’sa and darra so perhaps they would become humbled. This verse describes the second phase when humbling did not occur. The phrase “Then We changed their adversity to prosperity,” or “thumma baddalna makana al-sayyi’ati al-hasanata,” means the evil, hardship, was replaced with good, ease, abundance. The word change is deliberate: the same hand that created and commands can swap states, as winds rotate and seasons turn. “Until they flourished,” or “hatta afaw,” indicates growth, increase in numbers, wealth, and produce, the same flourishing Midian enjoyed from few to many and ’Ad in stature, but without thankfulness the fertile land produces differently than the thankful.\n\nTheir interpretation was then twisted: “they argued falsely, ‘Our forefathers too had been visited by adversity and prosperity,’” or “qaloo qad massa aba’ana al-darra’u wa al-sarra’u.” “Massa” touch, meaning our state is just a natural cycle that touched ancestors too, not a sign from the All-Wise. This is assumption and beautified deception, the same reasoning that made the chiefs call Hud a fool and the Midian chiefs call followers losers. By naturalizing the sign, they neutralized its humbling purpose and made their deeds appealing again.\n\nThe consequence, “So We seized them by surprise, while they were unaware,” or “fa-akhathnahum baghtatan wa hum la yash‘uroon,” repeats the baghtah, suddenness, that took societies at night or midday, the Hour that takes losers by surprise with burdens on backs, and the Hour that when it comes belief no longer benefits. “While they were unaware,” or “la yash‘uroon,” shows heedlessness persisted despite varied lessons.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to attribute prosperity to approval and adversity to chance. The verse shows both are tests varied for the thankful, requiring humble secret calling with hope and fear after hardship and grateful stewardship after ease. The next verses declare had they been faithful and mindful We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth, but disbelief led to seizure for what they earned, proving that the rotation from adversity to prosperity is meant to lead to mindfulness, not to the argument that history merely cycles without a Judge.",
  "96": "“Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth. But they disbelieved, so We seized them for what they used to commit” states the alternate history that could have been for every destroyed society from Noah to Midian, revealing the full generosity behind the affliction-prosperity cycle. The condition, “Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful,” or “wa law anna ahla al-qura amanoo wa ittaqaw,” pairs belief, or “iman,” without tarnish, with taqwa, mindful guarding like the best clothing, the same pair that makes scales heavy and that earlier made the fertile land produce abundantly by the Lord’s leave. The promised result, “We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth,” or “la-fatahna alaihim barakatin mina al-sama’i wal-ardi,” uses “fatahna,” opened, pour, as rain once poured as mercy producing every fruit and as winds were driven to lifeless land. “Barakat,” blessings, means growth, continuity, increase that remains, unlike the fleeting play of deluding worldly life. From heaven, timely rain and protection; from earth, gardens trellised and untrellised, palms, olives, pomegranates, and cattle fit for labour.\n\nThe actual history is then given: “But they disbelieved, so We seized them for what they used to commit,” or “wa lakin kathaboo fa-akhathnahum bima kanu yaksiboon.” “Kathaboo” denied signs and messengers who had delivered and given sincere advice, and “bima kanu yaksiboon” for what they used to earn, linking to ranks according to deeds and earning sin open and secret.\n\nThis counterfactual shows that seizure was not inevitable but chosen by response. The same wind that can usher mercy can be left to bring barrenness when thankfulness is replaced by “our forefathers also had adversity and prosperity,” the assumption that neutralizes signs.\n\nA common error is to think divine blessings are random favor to the powerful. The verse teaches blessings are constrained to faith and mindfulness, which turn hard soil into fertile by thankfulness that varies lessons abundantly, while disbelief, even amid palaces carved into mountains, leads to being as if never there. The next three verses then ask, did societies feel secure that Our punishment would not come by night while asleep, by day while at play, or that they are safe from Allah’s planning, none feel secure except losers, urging the thankful to keep scales heavy before the term arrives.",
  "97": "“Did the people of those societies feel secure that Our punishment would not come upon them by night while they were asleep?” is the first of three probing questions that follow the counterfactual about blessings from heaven and earth. The previous verse said had those peoples been faithful and mindful We would have overwhelmed them with blessings, but they disbelieved and were seized. This question turns to present listeners who remain. The phrase “Did the people of those societies feel secure,” or “a-fa-amina ahlu al-qura,” asks whether they felt safe, guarded, free from fear, as earlier societies felt when they took faith as play and were deluded. The specific scenario, “that Our punishment would not come upon them by night while they were asleep,” or “an ya’tiyahum ba’suna bayatan wa hum na’imoon,” recalls exactly how overwhelming punishments were said to come: “Our torment took them at night or midday,” and Thamud and Midian were taken where they fell lifeless in their homes while sleeping. Night sleep is a time of trust, as night was made for rest and for the soul to be called back, a mercy seldom thanked. Feeling secure in that trust while persisting in denial, arrogance, defrauding measure, and ambushing roads mistakes mercy for immunity.\n\nThe question is rhetorical, expecting the answer “They should not feel secure,” and it links to the broader warning that every community has an appointed term that when it comes cannot be delayed or advanced, and that the fulfilment awaited by deniers, when it arrives, leaves only self-ruin and vanished fabrications.\n\nA common self-deception is to think punishment, if it comes, will give warning time to react. Night surprise while asleep shows no time is given after the door closes, just as belief after the sun rises from the west would not benefit. The next verses ask the same about coming by day while at play and about security from Allah’s planning, tightening the circle. For the living, the answer is to take the night itself as a reminder: each sleep is a minor death where souls are called back; waking is a revival to complete an appointed term, an opportunity to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear rather than to sleep securely while persisting in corruption after order, before the night’s mercy becomes night’s seizure.",
  "98": "“Or did they feel secure that Our punishment would not come upon them by day while they were at play?” continues the series of security questions after asking whether societies felt secure from punishment by night while asleep. The previous question painted nighttime vulnerability; this one paints daytime distraction. The phrase “by day while they were at play,” or “duhan wa hum yal‘aboon,” uses “duha,” forenoon, the bright part of day when markets are busy, caravans move, and people are absorbed in activity, and “yal‘aboon,” at play, engaged in amusements that treat serious faith as mere play, as earlier those who took faith as amusement and play were ignored as they ignored the meeting. The same root described worldly life as play and amusement and the midday “qa’iloona” rest that could also be seized.\n\nThe question, “Or did they feel secure,” or “awa amina,” again expects no; no people whose messengers conveyed and advised should feel secure while continuing to deny signs with arrogance, forbid what Allah permitted, or spread corruption after order. Daytime play is just as exposed as nighttime sleep. The wind that brings mercy and then produces every fruit can be commanded to bring shaking by day; the sun and moon subjected by His command mark time without protecting transgressors when the term arrives.\n\nThis pairing, night asleep and day at play, covers the full 24-hour cycle of human activity already described as “to Him belongs whatever exists in day and night” and “He knows what you do by day and calls back souls by night.” No hour is outside the creation and command that belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds. Feeling secure in either hour mistakes the provision of livelihood and the establishment on earth for entitlement, the hardly-thanks noted after establishment.\n\nA common illusion today is to think busy daytime achievement equals safety, that commerce at play can outpace accountability. The Quran says the same market whose measure must be given justly can be the scene of seizure if measure is defrauded and roads are ambushed, and the same plain where palaces are built can shake. The next verse asks the ultimate question, did they feel secure from Allah’s planning, none feels secure except losers, showing that security from punishment while persisting in taking devils as masters thinking they are guided is itself the mark of loss before the final barrier.",
  "99": "“Did they feel secure against Allah’s planning? None would feel secure from Allah’s planning except the losers” condenses the two previous night-and-day questions into their theological root and gives the verdict. After asking whether peoples felt secure from punishment by night while asleep or by day while at play, the Quran now names the deeper security they assumed: “against Allah’s planning,” or “makra Allah.” “Makr” here means subtle, intricate planning, the encompassing, all-aware arrangement that can shift adversity to prosperity, then seize by surprise while unaware, as with societies that flourished after hardship and said forefathers also saw cycles. It is the planning that made every prophet’s enemies devilish humans and jinn whispering elegant deception so hearts that disbelieve in the Hereafter incline, and that made the wicked allies of one another because of misdeeds, yet allowed the winds to usher mercy before rain that also proves resurrection. Allah’s planning is not deceit as human makr can be; it is the perfected, unseen governance that brings every matter to its mustaqarr, its set place.\n\nThe answer is stark: “None would feel secure from Allah’s planning except the losers,” or “fa-la ya’manu makra Allahi illa al-qawmu al-khasiroon.” “Khasiroon” are the losers earlier defined as those who denied the meeting until the Hour surprised them with burdens on backs, whose scales are light and who doom themselves, who were wiped as if never there. To feel secure while taking faith as play, hindering the Way, and making it seem crooked, is itself the sign of loss, as earlier said none feel secure from Allah’s planning except losers.\n\nThis verse balances the earlier security question about Iblis’s delayed end and the best judge’s patience. Security from planning while persisting in corruption after order mistakes patience for approval, a mistake the next verse warns successors not to make: is it not clear to those who take over the land after its former residents that if We will We can punish them too and seal hearts so they will not hear?\n\nA common misunderstanding is to read “fear Allah’s planning” as despair. The Quran joins it with the opposite virtue from earlier, calling humbly with hope and fear and knowing mercy is close to good-doers. Feeling secure while spreading corruption is loser’s logic; fearing the subtle plan while doing good is the fertile soil’s wisdom that remembers favours after succession, varies lessons thankfully, and stays mindful before the day when fulfilment leaves only self-ruin and strayed fabrications.",
  "100": "“Is it not clear to those who take over the land after ˹the destruction of˺ its former residents that—if We will—We can punish them ˹too˺ for their sins and seal their hearts so they will not hear ˹the truth˺?” turns from the destroyed to their successors, making the land itself a lesson, after warning that only losers feel secure from Allah’s planning. The previous episodes ended with societies seized at night or midday and the question whether successors feel secure; this verse answers by making succession the proof. The phrase “Is it not clear to those who take over the land after its former residents,” or “awa lam yahdi lillatheena yarithoona al-arda min ba‘di ahliha,” means has guidance, or “huda,” not reached, not become plain, to the heirs who inherit, or “yarithoona,” the earth after the destroyed? They see empty palaces, carved homes where the fallen became lifeless, markets where measure was cheated now silent, just as Midian and Thamud and ’Ad and Noah’s people left traces. Succession, like being made successors after Noah and after ’Ad, should teach, as fertile land remembers, that the same establishment can be the next term.\n\nThe threat is twofold: “if We will, We can punish them too for their sins,” or “an law nasha’u asabnahum bi-thunoobihim,” punishment for sins, like forebears’ seizure by earthquake, flood, or brimstone, and “and seal their hearts so they will not hear the truth,” or “wa natba‘u ala quloobihim fa-hum la yasma‘oon.” Sealing, or “tab‘,” is the consequence of refusing to believe at first, as earlier We turned hearts and eyes as they refused to believe at first, leaving them to wander, and as earlier gates of heaven do not open for the arrogant. A sealed heart cannot hear the reminder though it comes through one of your own, though the Book is a guide and mercy, though winds herald rain.\n\nThis verse links creation-and-command to succession-and-seal. The same Lord who established on the Throne and subjects sun and moon controls inheritance of land and openness of hearts. The fertile-infertile metaphor appears again: inheritors can be thankful and produce abundantly, or remain infertile, hardly producing, until punishment comes by night or day.\n\nA common error among successors is to think new possession equals new immunity, that because we built anew we will not be shaken. The verse says succession is not immunity but test, as earlier increase from few to many was a test. Is it not clear, then, to look at the ruins, to give just measure, not to lie in ambush, to call humbly and secretly, before the seal makes the varied lessons no longer heard.",
  "101": "“We have narrated to you ˹O Prophet˺ some of the stories of those societies. Surely, their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but still they would not believe in what they had already denied. This is how Allah seals the hearts of the disbelievers” summarizes the five narratives from Noah to Shu‘ayb and gives their theological conclusion, after asking whether successors are guided by the ruins. The opening, “We have narrated to you some of the stories of those societies,” or “tilka al-qura naqussu alaika min anba’iha,” shows the stories, or “anba’,” tidings, are selection, not exhaustive, told to the Prophet who was told not to let anxiety tighten his chest regarding the Book. The stories are the empirical “see the end” command fulfilled through revelation when travel is not possible, allowing the blind heart to see with sure knowledge though We were never absent.\n\nThe next sentence explains their stubbornness: “Surely, their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but still they would not believe in what they had already denied,” or “wa laqad ja’athum rusuluhum bil-bayyinati fa-ma kanoo li-yu’minoo bima kathaboo min qabl.” Their denial was not for lack of bayyinah; Salih had the she-camel, Shu‘ayb had just measure, Lot had scolding, Hud had reminder of stature, Noah had Ark, yet they would not believe in what they had already denied, meaning their earlier refusal hardened into inability, like the infertile land that hardly produces. The phrase connects to the previous sealing threat: after refusing at first, hearts and eyes are turned, leaving them to wander.\n\nThe verdict then states the law: “This is how Allah seals the hearts of the disbelievers,” or “kathalika yatba‘u Allahu ala quloobi al-kafireen.” Sealing is not arbitrary but the consequence of persistent denial with arrogance for which gates do not open, taking devils as masters thinking they are guided, and making misdeeds appealing. It is the same sealing mentioned for successors who will not hear if they follow forefathers’ cycle argument.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to view sealing as preventing belief despite desire. The verse shows sequence: clear proofs came, they refused what they had already denied, then sealing followed. The next verses will note We did not find most of them true to covenant and most were rebellious, and then will send Moses to Pharaoh as a new, larger-scale example of the same pattern, inviting successors not to repeat the hard-soil that, despite winds heralding mercy, produced only the rain of punishment.",
  "102": "“We did not find most of them true to their covenant. Rather, We found most of them truly rebellious” gives the divine assessment after summarizing the societies whose stories were narrated and whose hearts were sealed like the disbelievers. The previous verse said messengers came with clear proofs but they would not believe in what they had already denied. This verse explains why that refusal was deep, not momentary. The phrase “We did not find most of them true to their covenant,” or “wa ma wajadna li-aktharihim min ahdin,” refers to the primordial covenant taken from children of Adam, the pledge to worship Allah alone and to follow His command, and to the specific covenants each community made with its messenger when warned, like promising not to harm the she-camel or not to defraud measure. “Ahd” means binding promise. Not finding them true to it means their practice did not match their pledge, as earlier they took faith as play, hindered the Way, and attributed to Allah what they do not know.\n\nThe counterpart, “Rather, We found most of them truly rebellious,” or “wa in wajadna aktharahum la-fasiqeen,” uses “fasiqeen,” those who step out, break out, as earlier “those who deny Our signs will be afflicted for rebelliousness.” Rebellion here is persisting in corruption after order, like lying in ambush on every road and striving to make the path seem crooked, like expelling the chaste, like defrauding property.\n\nThis diagnosis links to the fertile-barren parable: most soils proved infertile, hardly producing, despite winds and rains varied for the thankful. It also links to the security questions: most felt secure from Allah’s planning as losers do, instead of calling humbly with hope and fear. The statement “most of them” shows the prophetic pattern where saving is by mercy for a few and uprooting for the denying majority, yet each community’s term still showcases mercy close to good-doers.\n\nA common error is to think covenant is only historical. The Quran shows covenant is renewed with each reminder through one of your own, each time the Book explained with knowledge arrives as guide and mercy. Breaking it by following elegant deception makes hearts sealed, as earlier We turned hearts as they refused first, ensuring they will not hear. The text therefore prepares for Moses and Pharaoh, where the clash between covenant-keeping and rebellion becomes an empire-wide manifestation, and invites the successor who inherits land after its former residents to be among the few who remain true to covenant, giving just measure and not spreading corruption, before the appointed term that cannot be delayed arrives.",
  "103": "“Then after them We sent Moses with Our signs to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they wrongfully rejected them. See then what was the end of the corruptors!” opens the greatest of the prophetic histories in this surah, and the commentators note its place in the sequence. The five shorter stories — Noah, Hud, Salih, Lot, and Shu‘ayb — have established the pattern of call and rejection, and now the Quran turns to Moses, the prophet whose story would fill the remainder of the passage, for the confrontation between Moses and Pharaoh is the fullest and most detailed instance of the pattern that the shorter stories introduced. Ibn Kathir explains that Moses was sent after the earlier prophets with “Our signs,” or “bi-ayatina,” the miracles and proofs that God had given him — the staff, the shining hand, and the signs that would be shown in Egypt — and that he was sent specifically to “Pharaoh and his chiefs,” or “Fir‘awna wa mala’ihi,” the ruler of Egypt and the ruling class that surrounded him, for the tyranny of Egypt was a tyranny of the state, and the message of Moses was addressed first to its head.\n\nThe verse then states the response in a single clause: “but they wrongfully rejected them,” or “fa-zalamoo biha,” the wronging of the signs, the rejection of the proofs in defiance of the truth they had been shown. The word “zalamoo” — they wronged — is the key, and the commentators note that the rejection of the signs is here named as a wrong, for the one who sees the clear proof and denies it has wronged not only the messenger but his own soul, and the wronging of the signs is the wronging of the self. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse thus prepares the verdict that the story will pronounce: the wronging of the signs is the sin that the destruction of Pharaoh and his chiefs will recompense, and the story of Moses is the story of that recompense in its fullest form.\n\nThe verse closes with the question that frames the whole account: “See then what was the end of the corruptors!” or “fanzur kayfa kana ‘aqibatu al-mufsideen.” The word “mufsideen,” the corruptors, is applied to Pharaoh and his chiefs, and the question invites the listener to watch, through the unfolding story, the end of the corruptors — the drowning in the sea, the ruin of the tyranny, the inheritance of the earth by the oppressed. Ibn Kathir notes that the question is the Quran’s method of opening a story with its lesson: the listener knows from the first verse what the end will be, and the story that follows is the demonstration of how the corruptors reached it. For the listener, the verse is the threshold of the story of Moses: the signs sent, the signs wronged, and the end of the corruptors declared before the story begins, so that every detail that follows is read in the light of the verdict that is already known.",
  "104": "“And Moses said, ‘O Pharaoh! I am truly a messenger from the Lord of all worlds’” records the first words of Moses to the tyrant, and the commentators note their courage and their content. Moses stood before Pharaoh — the man who claimed divinity for himself, who ruled the greatest power of the age, and who held the life of every subject in his hand — and addressed him with a title and a claim that struck at the root of his tyranny: “O Pharaoh! I am truly a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,” or “ya Fir‘awnu inni rasoolun min rabbi al-‘alameen.” Ibn Kathir explains the audacity of the address: the man who had been raised in Pharaoh’s own palace and had fled Egypt as a fugitive now returned to declare himself the messenger of a Lord greater than Pharaoh, and the title “Lord of all worlds” was the direct refutation of the claim of Pharaoh, who had said to his people, as the Quran records, “I am your lord, the most high.” The message of Moses was not merely a call to worship but a challenge to the throne, for the Lord of all worlds has no rival, and the one who claims lordship for himself is the enemy of the claim.\n\nThe commentators note the completeness of the declaration. Moses did not open with a request or a negotiation but with the statement of his mission, and the statement left no room for compromise: he was the messenger, and the Lord who sent him was the Lord of all, and Pharaoh was being informed, not consulted. Al-Sa’di observes that this is the manner of the prophets before the tyrants: the message is delivered in its fullness from the first word, for the truth is not a matter of bargaining, and the messenger of God does not trim his message to please the powerful. The address “O Pharaoh” itself is a lesson, for Moses spoke to the tyrant by name, without the flattery and the titles that the court required, and the omission of the customary honorifics was itself the declaration that before the Lord of all worlds, the tyrant is but a man.\n\nThe verse also establishes the framework of the entire confrontation that follows. The story of Moses and Pharaoh is the story of two claims to lordship — the claim of the tyrant and the claim of the God who sent His messenger — and every sign that Moses brings, every plague that descends, and every miracle that unfolds is a judgment between the two claims. Ibn Kathir notes that the declaration “I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds” is the thesis of the whole story, and that the verses that follow are the proof of the thesis, until the sea closes over the tyrant and the claim of the Lord of all worlds is vindicated in the open. For the listener, the verse is the opening of the greatest confrontation in the Quran: the messenger and the tyrant, the Lord of all worlds and the lord of Egypt, and the declaration that begins the judgment between them.",
  "105": "“obliged to say nothing about Allah except the truth. Indeed, I have come to you with clear proof from your Lord. So let the Children of Israel go with me” completes Moses’ opening address, and the commentators note the three claims it makes. The first is the claim of fidelity: “obliged to say nothing about Allah except the truth,” or “haqeequn ‘ala an la aqoola ‘ala Allahi illa al-haqq,” the messenger’s self-binding to the truth, the declaration that he would speak nothing of God but what is true. Ibn Kathir explains that the word “haqeequn” — truly, it is incumbent upon me — is the messenger’s oath of fidelity, the guarantee that his message is not his own invention but the truth of God, and the claim is the answer to the accusation, which the next verses record, that Moses was a liar or a magician. The second claim is the claim of proof: “Indeed, I have come to you with clear proof from your Lord,” or “qad ji’tukum bi-bayyinatin min rabbikum,” the announcement that the message was not a bare assertion but carried the clear signs that would demonstrate it — the staff and the hand that the following verses describe. The third is the demand that the proof supported: “So let the Children of Israel go with me,” or “fa-arsil ma‘iya Banee Isra’eel,” the command to release the enslaved Israelites, the first political demand of the mission.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the order of the three claims, for it is the order of every prophetic confrontation with power. Moses began with the fidelity to the truth, then the proof of the truth, and then the demand that the truth implied, for the release of the oppressed is not a separate request but the direct consequence of the lordship of God: if Pharaoh is not the lord, then his enslavement of the Children of Israel is a usurpation, and the demand for their release is the demand of the true Lord for His servants. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the unity of the prophetic message: the call to the worship of God and the call to the liberation of the oppressed are one call, for the Lord of all worlds is the Lord of justice, and the messenger of the Lord of all worlds is the messenger of liberation. The demand “let the Children of Israel go” is the political form of the theological claim, and the two cannot be separated.\n\nThe verse also introduces the theme that will run through the entire story: the liberation of the enslaved. The Children of Israel had been reduced to bondage under Pharaoh, their sons slaughtered and their daughters degraded, and the mission of Moses was, from the first word, their liberation, for the God who sent him is the God who hears the cry of the oppressed. Ibn Kathir notes that the story of Moses is, among its many lessons, the Quran’s great account of the struggle against tyranny, and that the demand “let the Children of Israel go” is the first declaration of that struggle. For the listener, the verse is the statement of the prophetic mission in its fullness: the truth spoken, the proof offered, and the liberation demanded — the three notes that sound through every confrontation between the messenger of God and the tyrant of the age.",
  "106": "“Pharaoh said, ‘If you have come with a sign, then bring it if what you say is true’” records Pharaoh’s challenge, and the commentators note the tone and the intent of the demand. Pharaoh did not deny the message outright, and he did not accept it; he demanded the proof, saying “If you have come with a sign, then bring it if what you say is true,” or “in kunta ji’ta bi-ayatin fa’ti biha in kunta mina as-sadiqeen.” Ibn Kathir explains the scene: Pharaoh, seated in the majesty of his court, surrounded by his chiefs, answered the declaration of Moses with the demand of the skeptic, the challenge that the messenger should produce the sign that would prove his claim, and the demand was delivered in the tone of the tyrant who is certain that no proof will come. The word “sadiqeen,” the truthful, is the key: Pharaoh demanded that Moses prove his truthfulness, and the demand was itself the opening that would lead to the destruction of the tyrant, for the sign that Moses would bring would leave no room for denial.\n\nThe commentators note the wisdom of the divine arrangement in this exchange. Pharaoh asked for a sign, and God had already given Moses the signs, so that the tyrant’s own demand became the occasion for the display of the divine power that he would then deny. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the pattern of the confrontation between truth and tyranny: the tyrant demands proof, the proof is given, and the tyrant, unable to answer it, retreats into the accusation that the proof itself is sorcery, for the heart that has decided to reject will not be moved by any sign. The challenge of Pharaoh is the first step of the pattern that the following verses unfold, and the listener is made to watch the demand and its answer with the knowledge of how the tyrant will respond.\n\nThe verse also reveals something of Pharaoh’s character. The demand “bring it if you are truthful” is the language of the one who has already resolved not to believe, for the demand is not the request of the seeker but the challenge of the opponent, and the tyrant who asks for proof as a challenge will not accept the proof when it comes. Ibn Kathir notes that this is the constant feature of the rejecters in the Quran: they ask for signs not to be convinced but to have something to reject, and the signs that come to them become, in their hands, the instruments of their own condemnation. For the listener, the verse is the first exchange of the confrontation: the message declared, the proof demanded, and the stage set for the sign that would come — the staff that would become a serpent before the eyes of the tyrant.",
  "107": "“So Moses threw down his staff and—behold!—it became a real snake” answers Pharaoh’s demand with the first of the signs, and the commentators describe the miracle and its witnesses. Moses cast down the staff that he carried, and the staff became a serpent, a living snake, before the eyes of Pharaoh and his court. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari relate the fuller descriptions from the traditions: the staff became a great serpent, a visible, moving creature that filled the onlookers with terror, and the word the Quran uses — “thu‘ban,” a great serpent or snake — conveys the magnitude of the transformation, for this was not a trick of the eye but a real creature, a living sign of the power that had been given to Moses. Al-Sa’di observes the precision of the verse: the staff became a “real snake,” and the emphasis on its reality is the answer to the accusation, which the next verses record, that Moses was a magician, for the magician deceives the eye while the sign of Moses transformed the substance.\n\nThe commentators note the significance of the staff as the instrument of the sign. The staff was the ordinary tool of the shepherd, the thing that Moses carried in his hand for the daily work of tending the flocks, and the choice of the ordinary as the vessel of the extraordinary is the Quran’s constant lesson: the power of God is not limited to the grand and the rare, and the humblest of things, in the hand of the one whom God has chosen, becomes the instrument of the greatest of signs. Ibn Kathir relates that Moses had been given the staff at the burning bush, and that it had already served as the sign of his mission, and that its transformation before Pharaoh was the first public display of the power that the staff carried. The staff that became a serpent was, at the same time, the symbol of the message itself: the ordinary word, in the mouth of the prophet, becomes the living truth that no power can withstand.\n\nThe verse also marks the beginning of the contest that the following verses describe. The sign of the staff was the challenge that Pharaoh could not ignore, and his response — to summon the magicians — is the acknowledgment, in his own way, that the sign was real, for the tyrant does not summon his sorcerers against a mere illusion. Al-Sa’di notes that the verse is the first blow of the confrontation, and that the serpent of Moses is the first of the signs that would multiply until the sea closed over Pharaoh and his host. For the listener, the verse is the first of the signs of Moses: the staff cast down, the serpent risen, and the tyrant confronted with the reality that his own demand had summoned.",
  "108": "“Then he drew his hand out of his collar and it was shining white for all to see” follows the first sign with the second, and the commentators describe the miracle and its meaning. Moses placed his hand within his garment and drew it forth, and the hand shone with a white radiance, visible to all who watched, a light that was not the light of leprosy but the light of the divine sign. Ibn Kathir explains the distinction that the commentators stress: the whiteness of the hand was not the whiteness of disease, for the hand was whole and healthy, but a radiance that shone from it, a brightness that dazzled the onlookers, and the Quran elsewhere describes it as a light “without harm.” Al-Sa’di observes that the two signs — the staff and the hand — were the two proofs that God had given Moses at the burning bush, and that their repetition before Pharaoh was the public confirmation of the private revelation, the same power displayed in the wilderness and in the court.\n\nThe commentators note the pairing of the two signs and its wisdom. The staff became a serpent — a sign of power and terror, the transformation of the dead into the living — and the hand shone white — a sign of purity and light, the illumination of the flesh — and the two together encompassed the two dimensions of the divine proof: the power that transforms and the light that guides. Ibn Kathir relates that the hand of Moses, when he drew it forth, shone so brightly that it lit the place, and that the sign was so unmistakable that even the court of Pharaoh was silenced by it for a moment. The two signs were the credentials of the messenger, the proofs that he spoke in the name of the One who alone could transform the staff and illuminate the hand.\n\nThe verse also functions within the narrative as the completion of the evidence before the summoning of the magicians. Moses had been asked for a sign, and he had produced two, and the two were of such a nature that no human art could counterfeit them, and yet the response of Pharaoh, as the next verses show, was to attribute them to sorcery and to summon the magicians of Egypt to match them. Al-Sa’di notes that the verse thus sets the stage for the great contest: the signs of God displayed, the accusation of sorcery leveled, and the magicians summoned to the confrontation that would end in their own conversion. For the listener, the verse is the second of the signs: the hand that shone white is the companion of the staff that became the serpent, and the two together are the proof that the messenger of the Lord of all worlds had come with the truth.",
  "109": "“The chiefs of Pharaoh’s people said, ‘He is indeed a skilled magician’” records the first reaction of Pharaoh’s court to the signs, and the commentators note the evasion that the accusation reveals. The chiefs, having witnessed the staff become a serpent and the hand shine white, did not acknowledge the truth of the signs but attributed them to sorcery: “He is indeed a skilled magician,” or “inna hatha la-sahirun ‘aleem.” Ibn Kathir explains the meaning of the accusation: the word “sahir” — magician — and the word “‘aleem” — skilled, knowledgeable — together declared that Moses was a master of the arts of illusion, a man whose command of sorcery was so complete that he could deceive the eyes of the court, and the accusation was the only answer that the rejecters could give to the signs they could not deny. The signs were real, and the court could not say they had not seen them, and so they said that what they had seen was magic — the oldest of the world’s evasions, the denial of the divine by the naming of it as the demonic.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the psychology of the accusation. The chiefs could not answer the argument of the signs, and so they changed the subject from the truth to its classification, calling the miracle magic in order to avoid calling it a proof, for the one who admits that the signs are from God has admitted the message, and the one who calls them magic has postponed, for a little, the admission that he cannot make. Al-Sa’di observes that the accusation of sorcery is the last refuge of the rejecter in every age: the truth that cannot be refuted is called a trick, the light that cannot be extinguished is called a deception, and the messenger whose signs cannot be matched is called a magician. The verse thus teaches the believer to recognize the accusation of “magic” for what it is — the confession of the accuser that he has no answer but the lie.\n\nThe verse also reveals the class that led the rejection. The accusation came from the “mala’,” the chiefs, the notables of Egypt, whose authority and privilege the message of Moses threatened, and the commentators note the pattern that the surah has established: the rejection of the prophets is led, in every story, by the powerful. Ibn Kathir notes that the chiefs of Pharaoh are the same “mala’” that rejected every prophet before, and that their accusation of Moses is the accusation of Noah, Hud, Salih, and Shu‘ayb in a new form — the accusation that the truth is sorcery and the prophet a magician. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the evasion: the signs were seen, the accusation was made, and the truth, which the accusation could not touch, moved on to its triumph over the very sorcerers that the chiefs would summon.",
  "110": "“who seeks to drive you from your land. So Pharaoh asked, ‘What do you propose?’” completes the accusation of the chiefs and records Pharaoh’s consultation, and the commentators note the political framing of the rejection. The chiefs had called Moses a skilled magician, and now they supplied the motive: “who seeks to drive you from your land,” or “yureedu an yukhrijakum min ardikum,” the claim that Moses’ message was not a religious call but a political conspiracy, a plot to expel the Egyptians from their land and to seize the power that they held. Ibn Kathir explains the significance of the accusation: the chiefs understood that the message of Moses — the declaration that the Lord of all worlds, not Pharaoh, is the sovereign — was a challenge to their entire order, and they framed the challenge in the political terms that would move Pharaoh, for the tyrant fears nothing so much as the loss of his power, and the accusation of the plot was the surest way to enlist him against the messenger. The word “ardikum,” your land, is the key: the chiefs spoke of the land as “theirs,” the property of the Egyptians, and the message of Moses was cast as the threat to that property.\n\nThe verse then records Pharaoh’s response: “So Pharaoh asked, ‘What do you propose?’” or “qaala fa-matha ta’muroon,” the tyrant turning to his council for the plan by which the threat would be met. Al-Sa’di observes the irony that the verse preserves: the man who claimed to be the lord of his people, the god who needed no counsel, consulted his chiefs for the strategy against a single messenger and his brother, and the consultation itself was the tacit confession that the message of Moses was a threat that the tyrant could not ignore. The commentators note the pattern: the rejecters, having called the truth sorcery and the messenger a conspirator, then gather their forces to oppose what they have already declared to be a mere illusion — the two accusations cannot both be true, and the haste with which the magicians were summoned betrays the fear that the accusation of sorcery was meant to hide.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer about the nature of the opposition to truth. The message of Moses was attacked not on its merits but on its consequences, and the attack was led by those whose position the message threatened, for the truth is never merely a matter of ideas but always a matter of power, and the powerful know, before the arguments are made, what the acceptance of the truth would cost them. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is placed here to show the Prophet Muhammad that the accusation of political conspiracy, which the Quraysh leveled against him, was the same accusation that the chiefs of Egypt leveled against Moses, and that the messenger of God has always been cast as the enemy of the established order by the very order he was sent to reform. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the political attack: the truth is called a conspiracy, the messenger a conspirator, and the tyrant consults his council — but the truth moves on, and the council’s plans will meet the power that no plan can withstand.",
  "111": "“They replied, ‘Let him and his brother wait and send mobilizers to all cities’” records the first part of the council’s plan, and the commentators explain its strategy. The chiefs advised Pharaoh to delay — “let him and his brother wait,” or “arjih wa akhahu,” the postponement of the confrontation — and to “send mobilizers to all cities,” or “wa arsil fi al-mada’ini hashireen,” the dispatch of agents throughout the land to gather the magicians. Ibn Kathir explains the reasoning: the council knew that Moses had come with signs that no ordinary man could match, and so they advised Pharaoh to assemble the greatest sorcerers of Egypt, the masters of the craft who could confront the signs of Moses with signs of their own, and the gathering required time, hence the delay. The strategy was the strategy of the powerful against the truth in every age: do not answer it at once, but assemble the experts, the authorities, the professionals of the age, and let them overwhelm the messenger with the weight of their collective skill. The word “hashireen,” the mobilizers, the gatherers, conveys the scale of the operation: the whole machinery of the state was to be set in motion to meet the challenge of two men.\n\nThe commentators note the confidence that the plan reveals, and the fear beneath it. The council proposed to match miracle with magic, and the proposal rested on the assumption that the signs of Moses were of the same order as the arts of their sorcerers, an assumption that the contest would shatter, for the magicians themselves would be the first to recognize, as the story will show, that what Moses brought was not of their craft. Al-Sa’di observes that the plan is the first step of the divine arrangement: the gathering of the magicians, intended as the defeat of Moses, became the occasion of their conversion, and the mobilizers who were sent to defeat the truth were the instruments by which the truth reached the hearts of the sorcerers. The verse thus teaches that the plans of the rejecters are themselves woven into the divine plan, and that the forces assembled against the truth are often the very means by which the truth is spread.\n\nThe verse also completes the picture of the confrontation as a clash of the state against the messenger. The full apparatus of Egypt — the court, the council, the mobilizers, and the magicians — was arrayed against Moses and Aaron, and the disparity of the forces is the measure of the lesson: the truth does not need the machinery of power, and the power that arrays itself against the truth will be turned, in the divine wisdom, to the service of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse prepares the great scene of the contest, the day of the festival when the magicians would confront Moses before the assembled people, and that the preparation of the rejecters was the preparation of their own defeat. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the gathering of power against truth: the delay, the mobilizers, and the assembled experts are the world’s answer to the messenger, and the world’s answer is about to meet the answer of God.",
  "112": "“to bring you every clever magician” is the purpose clause for the mobilizers sent to all cities, and the commentators note the word it uses and the assumption it carries. The chiefs advised Pharaoh to send the mobilizers “to bring you every clever magician,” or “ya’tooka bi-kulli sahirin ‘aleem,” the gathering of every sorcerer of skill, every master of the craft, from all the cities of Egypt, so that the confrontation with Moses might be met with the fullest display of the nation’s magical arts. Ibn Kathir explains that Egypt was, in that age, the centre of the magical arts, and that its sorcerers were renowned for the mastery of their illusions — the ropes and the staffs that seemed to move, the eyes that they deceived, the feats that the common people took for the power of their gods — and the plan of the council was to assemble the best of them against Moses. The word “‘aleem,” skilled, clever, is the measure of the gathering: not the ordinary practitioners but the masters, the ones whose skill was the pride of the craft.\n\nThe commentators note the irony that the verse preserves, and that the story will unfold. The magicians were summoned to defeat the sign of Moses by matching it, and the matching would fail, but not because the magicians were unskilled — the failure would come because the sign of Moses was not of the order of their skill at all. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse sets the terms of the contest that follows: the world’s greatest illusionists against the sign of God, and the outcome is certain from the first, for the illusion, however clever, cannot stand before the truth, and the craft, however masterful, cannot match the miracle. The gathering of “every clever magician” was thus the gathering of the witnesses who would, in the end, testify against the very art they had been summoned to defend.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the nature of the conflict between the truth and the falsehood that opposes it. The falsehood of Pharaoh summoned its greatest practitioners, its most skilled defenders, its most convincing performers, and the truth of Moses came with the staff and the word, and the disparity of the two is the lesson of the whole contest: the truth does not compete with the falsehood on the falsehood’s terms, but confronts it with the sign that no skill can counterfeit. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse prepares the day of the festival, the great public assembly at which the confrontation would take place, and that the assembly itself was the divine arrangement by which the truth would be displayed before the largest possible audience. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the summoned experts: the world gathers its cleverest to defeat the truth, and the cleverest, in the end, bow before it.",
  "113": "“The magicians came to Pharaoh, saying, ‘Shall we receive a suitable reward if we prevail?’” records the arrival of the magicians and their first concern, and the commentators note what the question reveals about them and about the contest. The magicians, gathered from the cities, came to Pharaoh, and before they agreed to the confrontation, they asked about their payment: “Shall we receive a suitable reward if we prevail?” or “a-inna lana la-ajran in kunna nahnu al-ghalibeen.” Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the magicians were professionals, men who practiced their craft for a living, and the summoning to the court was, for them, an engagement like any other, a commission for which they expected the reward, and their question was the question of the hired expert who wishes to fix his fee before he performs. The word “ghalibeen,” the prevailing, the victorious, is their confidence: they came to the contest certain of the outcome, for the art of the magician had never failed them, and the reward they asked for was the reward of the victory they did not doubt.\n\nThe commentators note the two things that the question reveals. First, the magicians were not believers in the cause for which they were summoned; they were mercenaries, hired for a fee, and their loyalty was to the payment, not to the gods of Egypt or to the throne of Pharaoh, and this very detachment would later open their hearts, as the story will show, to the truth that their craft could not match. Second, the question reveals the confidence of the falsehood: the magicians asked for the reward “if we prevail,” and the condition was, in their minds, a formality, for the prevalence of the craft was assumed, and the assumption was the measure of their blindness to the nature of the sign they were about to confront. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the first step of the magicians’ journey: they came as hirelings, and they would leave as believers, and the question about the reward is the prelude to the confession that would cost them everything.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer about the world’s champions of falsehood. The magicians were the best of their craft, and yet their first thought was the fee, for the falsehood that is hired for a price is a falsehood that will desert its employer when the truth appears, and the champion who fights for reward will not die for the lie. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse prepares the great reversal of the story: the men who came asking for the reward of Pharaoh would, before the day ended, declare their belief in the Lord of Moses and Aaron, and count the wrath of Pharaoh as nothing beside the truth they had seen. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the hired champions: the world’s defenders of falsehood fight for the fee, and the fee cannot hold them when the truth confronts them.",
  "114": "“He replied, ‘Yes, and you will certainly be among those closest to me’” records Pharaoh’s answer to the magicians, and the commentators note the promise and the bribe it contained. Pharaoh answered the question about the reward with a double assurance: “Yes,” or “na‘am,” the confirmation of the payment, and “you will certainly be among those closest to me,” or “wa innakum la-mina al-muqarrabin,” the promise of nearness to the throne, the elevation of the magicians to the inner circle of the court, beyond the mere fee that they had asked. Ibn Kathir explains the offer: Pharaoh sought to bind the magicians to his cause not only with money but with honour, promising them the status of the court’s closest favourites if they prevailed, for the tyrant knows that the hearts of the ambitious are won by the promise of nearness as much as by the promise of gold, and the offer of “the closest to me” was the highest honour that Egypt could bestow. The word “muqarrabin,” those brought near, is the measure of the promise: the magicians were to be raised from the rank of hired performers to the rank of the companions of the king.\n\nThe commentators note the contrast that the verse sets up, and that the story will resolve. Pharaoh promised the magicians the nearness of his court, and the magicians would soon exchange that nearness for a nearer station, for the one who is brought near to God is nearer than the one who is brought near to the tyrant, and the story of the magicians is the story of the choice between the two nearnesses. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the world’s offer to its champions: the reward and the rank, the gold and the glory, and the offer is made to every soul that is summoned to fight for the falsehood, and the soul that accepts the offer has sold the nearness of God for the nearness of a throne that will fall. The magicians accepted the offer at first, as the next verses show, but their acceptance was about to be undone by the sight of the truth.\n\nThe verse also reveals the character of the tyrant in the promise he makes. Pharaoh promised the magicians the nearness of his court as if the nearness were his to give, and the promise was the expression of the same claim to sovereignty that the message of Moses had challenged, for the one who gives the nearness of the court claims the authority that belongs to the One who gives the nearness of Paradise. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is placed here to show the full scale of the confrontation: the tyrant offers his nearness, and the Lord of all worlds offers His, and the story of the magicians is the story of the two offers and the choice between them. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the world’s bribe: the reward and the nearness are offered, and the believer is summoned to remember that the nearness that matters is the nearness that the tyrant cannot give and cannot take away.",
  "115": "“They asked, ‘O Moses! Will you cast, or shall we be the first to cast?’” opens the contest itself, and the commentators note the courtesy and the confidence of the question. The magicians, before the assembled people on the day of the festival, turned to Moses and offered him the choice of order: “Will you cast, or shall we be the first to cast?” or “a-tulqi am nakoonu nahnu al-mulqeen.” Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the contest was arranged as a public display, with the people gathered to witness the confrontation between the sorcerers of Egypt and the messenger of God, and the magicians, confident in their craft and generous in their confidence, offered Moses the honour of choosing whether he would cast first or whether they would, for the order of the casting was, in their eyes, a matter of no importance, since the outcome was certain. The question is the courtesy of the victor who offers his opponent the choice of the weapons, and the courtesy itself was the measure of their confidence.\n\nThe commentators note the detail of the question and its significance for the story. The magicians offered the choice, and Moses would accept, as the next verse shows, by commanding them to cast first, and the order of the casting would become the order of the proof: the magicians would cast their illusions first, and then the sign of Moses would swallow them, so that the people might see the full display of the falsehood before the full display of the truth. Al-Sa’di observes that the question is the Quran’s way of setting the scene for the great demonstration: the magicians, with their ropes and staffs, were given the first word, and the truth was given the last, and the last word of the truth is always the decisive one. The verse thus teaches that the falsehood is often given the first move, and the truth the second, and the second move of the truth is the end of the falsehood.\n\nThe verse also carries a subtle lesson about the manner of the messengers. Moses did not seize the advantage or demand the first cast, but accepted the arrangement that the magicians offered, for the messenger of God does not fear the display of the falsehood and does not hasten to silence it, but lets it show its full strength before the truth answers it. Ibn Kathir notes that the composure of Moses before the magicians is the composure of the one who knows that the truth needs no advantage, and that the falsehood, when it has done its worst, has only prepared the stage for the sign that will undo it. For the listener, the verse is the opening of the contest: the choice offered, the order to be decided, and the two sides arrayed — the craft of Egypt and the sign of God — before the eyes of the assembled people.",
  "116": "“Moses said, ‘You first.’ So when they did, they deceived the eyes of the people, stunned them, and produced a great display of magic” records the first half of the contest, and the commentators describe the display of the magicians and the phrase that exposes its nature. Moses commanded the magicians to cast first: “You first,” or “alqoo,” and they cast their ropes and their staffs, and the verse records the effect: “they deceived the eyes of the people, stunned them, and produced a great display of magic,” or “fa-lamma alqaw saharoo a‘yuna an-nasi wastarhaboohum wa ja’oo bi-sihrin ‘azeem.” Ibn Kathir explains the scene from the traditions: the magicians cast their ropes and staffs, and the people saw them as serpents, a great mass of moving shapes that filled the open ground, and the onlookers were struck with terror, for the display was the most powerful that the craft of Egypt had ever produced. The phrase “saharoo a‘yuna an-nas,” they bewitched the eyes of the people, is the key, and the commentators dwell on it: the magicians did not change the ropes into serpents but deceived the eyes, the “sihr” of illusion, the bewitching of the sight by which the ropes appeared to move, and the word distinguishes their craft, precisely, from the sign of Moses, for the magicians deceived the eye while Moses transformed the substance.\n\nThe commentators note the two effects that the verse names: the deception of the eyes and the terror of the hearts, and the two together are the complete work of the falsehood, which dazzles the sight and terrifies the soul, and which conquers, for a moment, through the very confusion it creates. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s description of the power of the falsehood at its height: the magicians had the people in their spell, the crowd was stunned, and the craft of Egypt stood at the summit of its achievement, and it was precisely at that summit that the sign of Moses would descend, for the falsehood is shown in its fullness before it is shown to be nothing. The word “‘azeem,” great, is applied to the magic with a deliberate irony that the following verses will expose: the “great display” of the magicians was about to meet a greatness before which it would vanish.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the nature of the illusion that the world calls power. The magicians deceived the eyes and stunned the people, and the world, watching, would have said that the power was theirs, and yet the verse, by naming their work “sihr,” the deception of the eyes, has already told the reader what the world could not see: the power of the falsehood is in the eye of the beholder, not in the thing beheld, and the truth that transforms the substance will undo the illusion that only bewitches the sight. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse prepares the great moment of the staff of Moses, and that the contrast between the “sihr” of the magicians and the sign of God is the contrast that the whole contest exists to teach. For the listener, the verse is the description of the falsehood at its summit: the eyes deceived, the crowd stunned, and the “great magic” displayed — and the truth about to answer.",
  "117": "“Then We inspired Moses, ‘Throw down your staff,’ and—behold!—it devoured the objects of their illusion” records the second half of the contest and its decisive blow, and the commentators describe the miracle and its effect. At the moment when the magicians’ display was at its height, God inspired Moses to cast his staff, and he cast it, and the staff became a great serpent that moved upon the ropes and staffs of the magicians and swallowed them all, so that nothing of their display remained. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller descriptions: the serpent of Moses opened its jaws and devoured the ropes and staffs one after another, gathering them into itself until the ground was cleared of every trace of the magicians’ work, and the people saw, with their own eyes, the swallowing of the illusion by the truth. Al-Sa’di observes the precision of the verse’s word: the staff “devoured the objects of their illusion,” or “talqafu ma ya’fikoon,” and the word “ya’fikoon” — what they fabricated, what they conjured — names the work of the magicians as illusion, so that the swallowing of the illusion by the truth is the statement, in visible form, of the relationship between the two.\n\nThe commentators note the complete reversal that the verse effects. The magicians had deceived the eyes and stunned the crowd with their “great magic,” and in a single movement the staff of Moses devoured it all, and the “greatness” of the magic was revealed, in an instant, as the nothingness that the serpent swallowed. Ibn Kathir explains that the swallowing of the ropes and staffs was the decisive proof, for the magicians themselves, who knew the secrets of their craft, saw that what Moses had done was not of their art, and the verse prepares the confession that the next verses record: the men who had deceived the eyes were the first to recognize the truth that their deception could not match. The contest between the craft of Egypt and the sign of God ended, in that moment, with the sign of God devouring the craft.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the eternal truth of the contest between the falsehood and the truth. The falsehood, at its height, dazzles and terrifies, and the truth, when it is cast, swallows the falsehood, and the swallowing is not gradual but instantaneous, for the truth has no need of a long struggle with the illusion that has no substance. Al-Sa’di observes that the staff of Moses is the symbol of the divine word against every falsehood: the truth, when it is thrown, devours the illusions of the age, and the believer who holds to the truth holds the staff that no magic can withstand. For the listener, the verse is the turning point of the contest: the staff cast, the serpent risen, and the illusion devoured — and the magicians, who saw it all, about to fall in prostration before the Lord of all worlds.",
  "118": "“So the truth prevailed and their illusions failed” states the verdict of the contest, and the commentators note the economy and the force of the sentence. The verse compresses the whole outcome into two clauses: “So the truth prevailed,” or “fa-waqa‘a al-haqqu,” the truth was established, the truth stood, and “their illusions failed,” or “wa-batala ma kanoo ya‘maloon,” their work was nullified, their deeds came to nothing. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning of the two verbs: “waqa‘a,” the truth fell into place, took its stand, and remained, while “batala,” the work of the magicians was rendered vain, emptied of all effect, and the contrast of the two verbs is the contrast of the two realities — the truth that stands and the falsehood that collapses. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s summary of every confrontation between the truth and the falsehood, for the outcome is always the same: the truth prevails, and the falsehood fails, and the failure of the falsehood is as certain as the standing of the truth.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the word “batala,” and its root. The root “batala” means to be futile, to be in vain, to come to nothing, and the verse declares that the entire work of the magicians — the ropes, the staffs, the deception, the terror, the “great magic” — was, in the end, “batil,” nothing, an illusion whose substance was swallowed and whose effect was erased. Ibn Kathir notes that this is the Quran’s constant verdict upon the falsehood: the works of the rejecters, however great they appear, are “batil,” and the day of the truth is the day of their nullification. The verse thus teaches the believer the certainty of the outcome: the struggle between the truth and the falsehood is not an open question, and the one who stands with the truth stands with what will prevail, while the one who stands with the falsehood stands with what will fail.\n\nThe verse also marks the moment at which the contest turned, and the commentators connect it to the conversion that follows. The truth having prevailed and the illusions having failed, the magicians — who knew the craft and could measure what they had seen — were the first to recognize the verdict, and the next verses record their prostration and their confession. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the hinge of the story: the display of the falsehood, the casting of the truth, the swallowing of the illusion, and now the verdict, and the verdict is the point at which the witnesses of the contest become the believers of the truth. For the listener, the verse is the statement of the eternal law: the truth prevails, and the falsehood fails, and the one who has seen the two meet cannot but recognize which is which.",
  "119": "“So Pharaoh and his people were defeated right there and put to shame” records the immediate effect of the verdict upon the rejecters, and the commentators note the completeness of the defeat. The verse states the outcome for Pharaoh and his party in two phrases: “were defeated right there,” or “fa-ghuliboo hunalika,” the overthrow on the very spot, in the presence of the assembled people, and “put to shame,” or “wanqalaboo saghireen,” the turning away in humiliation, the retreat of the defeated who are left small and disgraced. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the contest had been arranged by Pharaoh as a public triumph, the display by which the message of Moses would be crushed before the eyes of the nation, and the outcome was the exact reverse, for the triumph was Moses’, and Pharaoh and his chiefs stood defeated and humiliated before the very crowd they had assembled to witness their victory. The word “saghireen,” the humiliated, the belittled, is the measure of the reversal: the tyrant who had claimed to be the lord most high was reduced, in that moment, to the smallness of the defeated.\n\nThe commentators note the irony that the verse preserves and that the story will deepen. Pharaoh had summoned the magicians to defeat Moses, and the magicians had become the believers; he had gathered the people to witness the humiliation of the messenger, and the people had witnessed the humiliation of the tyrant; and the defeat “right there,” in the open, was the first of the defeats that would culminate in the sea. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the lesson of every public confrontation between the truth and the falsehood: the falsehood that arranges the display of its power is arranging the display of its own defeat, and the tyrant who stages the triumph of the falsehood stages the triumph of the truth. The shame of Pharaoh before the people is the first installment of the ruin that the story will complete.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the persistence of the rejecters despite the defeat. Pharaoh was defeated and shamed, and yet he did not believe, and the story that follows is the story of his hardening, for the defeat that should have awakened him only deepened his resolve, and the shame of the public overthrow fed the pride that would not bend. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse marks the point at which the rejection of Pharaoh became final, for the one who is defeated by the truth and still refuses has chosen the path that no further sign will reverse. For the listener, the verse is the record of the defeat of the falsehood and the warning of its persistence: the truth prevailed, the falsehood was shamed, and the shamed falsehood, refusing to learn, moved on to its ruin.",
  "120": "“And the magicians fell down, prostrating” captures the most unexpected outcome of the contest, and the commentators present it as one of the great reversals of the Quran. The magicians — the men who had been summoned to defeat Moses, who had asked for the reward of Pharaoh, and who had displayed their craft before the people — fell to the ground in prostration, “kharroo sujjadan,” the prostration of the face to the earth, the posture of the worshipper before his Lord. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the magicians, who knew the secrets of their craft better than any, recognized in the staff of Moses something that was not of their art, and the recognition struck them with such force that they fell in prostration, for the one who had seen the truth with the eyes of the expert could not but bow before it. The commentators note the significance of the prostration: it was the first act of the magicians as believers, the surrender of the body before the surrender of the tongue, and the public confession of the truth in the very place where they had been summoned to deny it.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the depth of the conversion that the prostration reveals. The magicians did not merely acknowledge that Moses had won; they prostrated, the act of worship that the falsehood of Egypt reserved for its gods, and their prostration declared, in the most public possible way, that the Lord of Moses was their Lord, and that the craft in which they had lived was, as they would say, the illusion that the truth had devoured. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the proof of the power of the truth over the honest heart: the magicians were not simple folk but the masters of deception, and their very expertise became the means of their certainty, for the one who knows the illusion best is the first to recognize the reality. The prostration of the magicians is the testimony of the experts, the witness of those who could not be deceived because they knew the art of deceiving.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the swiftness with which the truth can conquer the prepared heart. The magicians came to the contest as enemies, and they left it as believers, and the interval between the two states was the casting of a staff, for the truth, when it is seen, does not require a long argument but takes the heart at once. Ibn Kathir notes that the conversion of the magicians is one of the Quran’s great examples of the openness of the sincere soul: the men who were honest about what they had seen were the men who believed, and their prostration is the model of the response that the truth demands. For the listener, the verse is the great reversal of the contest: the summoned enemies became the prostrating believers, and the craft of Egypt bowed, in the open, before the Lord of Moses.",
  "121": "“They declared, ‘We now believe in the Lord of all worlds—’” records the first words of the magicians after their prostration, and the commentators note the title they chose. The magicians, rising from their prostration, declared their faith: “We now believe in the Lord of all worlds,” or “qaloo amanna bi-rabbi al-‘alameen.” Ibn Kathir explains the significance of the title: “the Lord of all worlds,” “rabb al-‘alameen,” was the very title with which Moses had opened his address to Pharaoh, and the magicians, in their confession, repeated the title that the tyrant had rejected, so that the words that Pharaoh had refused to accept were now spoken, in his own court, by the men he had summoned to refute them. The phrase “al-‘alameen,” all the worlds, is the declaration of the universality of the Lord, and the magicians’ use of it was the public acceptance of the claim that Pharaoh had called a conspiracy, the confession that the Lord of Moses was indeed the Lord of all.\n\nThe commentators note the boldness of the confession and its contrast with the magicians’ earlier state. The men who had asked Pharaoh for the reward of victory now declared their belief in the Lord of all worlds, and the declaration was made in the presence of the tyrant himself, in the assembly that had been gathered to witness their triumph, and the boldness of the confession is the measure of the certainty that produced it, for the one who has seen the truth does not calculate the cost of declaring it. Al-Sa’di observes that the confession of the magicians is the model of the believer’s confession: the truth, once seen, is declared, and the declaration is made without counting the consequences, for the consequences are in the hand of the Lord who is being declared. The word “amanna,” we believe, is the word that the whole contest had been moving toward, and the magicians spoke it in the face of the power that would, as the next verses show, threaten them with death.\n\nThe verse also marks the completeness of the reversal that the story has accomplished. The magicians had come to defeat the message of Moses, and they became its first public converts; the men who had been hired to deny the Lord of all worlds became the ones who declared Him; and the victory of Pharaoh, which the contest had been arranged to secure, became the victory of the truth over the very instruments of its opposition. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the beginning of the magicians’ testimony, which the next verses complete, and that their confession is preserved as the witness of the experts who could not be deceived. For the listener, the verse is the first word of the converted: “we believe in the Lord of all worlds” — the confession that the tyrant could not bear and the truth could not be denied.",
  "122": "“‘the Lord of Moses and Aaron’” specifies the Lord of all worlds just confessed, making the confession personal and historical, and the commentators note the precision of the addition. The magicians, having declared their belief in the Lord of all worlds, added the specification: “the Lord of Moses and Aaron,” or “rabbi Moosa wa Haroon,” and the addition fixed the meaning of the confession, for the “Lord of all worlds” is not an abstract principle but the God who sent Moses and Aaron, the God of the signs that had just been displayed, and the confession of the magicians was not a general theism but the specific faith in the message that the two brothers had brought. Ibn Kathir explains that the magicians named Moses and Aaron precisely because the message had reached them through the two brothers, and the naming of the messengers was the acknowledgment of the message, the confession that the Lord of all worlds had indeed sent the messengers whose signs they had witnessed.\n\nThe commentators note the significance of the specification for the confrontation with Pharaoh. The magicians did not merely say “we believe in God”; they said “we believe in the Lord of Moses and Aaron,” and the naming of the two brothers was a direct repudiation of Pharaoh, who had asked, as the Quran records elsewhere, “who is the Lord of the worlds?” and had declared himself the lord most high. Al-Sa’di observes that the confession of the magicians was thus the complete answer to the claim of Pharaoh: the Lord of all worlds is the Lord of Moses and Aaron, not the tyrant of Egypt, and the men who had been summoned to defend the claim of the tyrant ended by denying it in the most explicit terms. The specification was the courage of the confession made complete, for the magicians named the messengers whose Lord they had accepted and, in doing so, named the tyrant’s rival.\n\nThe verse also completes the magicians’ testimony, and the commentators connect it to the threat that follows. The confession “the Lord of Moses and Aaron” was the last word the magicians spoke before Pharaoh’s wrath descended, and the next verses record the threat of the tyrant and the answer of the believers, for the confession that named the messengers was the confession that would cost the confessors their lives, and they paid the price without flinching. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the transition from the confession to the trial, and that the magicians’ naming of Moses and Aaron is the proof that their faith was not a vague sentiment but a specific, costly, and personal commitment. For the listener, the verse is the completed confession: “we believe in the Lord of all worlds, the Lord of Moses and Aaron” — the faith that the tyrant could not tolerate and the believers could not abandon.",
  "123": "“Pharaoh threatened, ‘How dare you believe in him before I give you permission? This must be a plot you have all hatched in the city, to drive its people out. You will soon come to know’” records the wrath of Pharaoh and the accusation he invented, and the commentators note the desperation that the threat reveals. Pharaoh, confronted with the public conversion of his magicians, turned on them with fury: “How dare you believe in him before I give you permission?” or “a-amantum bihi qabla an athana lakum,” the outrage of the tyrant whose subjects had acted without his leave, and then the accusation that would justify the outrage: “This must be a plot you have all hatched in the city, to drive its people out,” or “inna hatha la-makrun makartumoohu fi al-madeenati li-tukhrijoo minha ahlaha.” Ibn Kathir explains the accusation: Pharaoh, unable to accept that the magicians had believed out of the evidence of the signs, invented the story that the whole contest had been a conspiracy, that Moses and the magicians had secretly colluded to stage the display and to use it to drive the Egyptians from their land, and the invention was the refuge of the tyrant who must explain away the truth he would not accept. The word “makr,” the plot, is the key: the falsehood, when it is defeated, always accuses the truth of conspiracy.\n\nThe commentators note the two elements of the threat: the claim of authority over belief and the accusation of conspiracy, and both are the marks of the tyrant. The first — “before I give you permission” — is the claim that faith itself is subject to the tyrant’s leave, the assertion that no one may believe except by his decree, and the claim is the logical end of the self-deification that the message of Moses had challenged. The second — the plot — is the attempt to reduce the truth to a scheme, to explain the miracle as a maneuver, and the attempt is the last resort of the mind that cannot admit the divine. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse exposes the complete bankruptcy of Pharaoh’s position: the signs had been displayed, the experts had believed, and the tyrant could only answer with the accusation of conspiracy and the threat of violence, for the one who has no argument left takes refuge in the lie and the lash.\n\nThe verse closes with the ominous promise that prefaces the punishment: “You will soon come to know,” or “fa-sawfa ta‘lamoon,” the threat that hangs over the magicians, the foretaste of the torture that the next verse names. Ibn Kathir notes that the threat is the tyrant’s answer to the truth he could not refute: the magicians had declared their belief, and Pharaoh answered with the promise of pain, and the two responses — the confession and the threat — are the two sides of the confrontation that would end, as the story will show, in the triumph of the confessors and the ruin of the tyrant. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the tyrant’s desperation: the truth is called a plot, the belief is made a crime, and the threat replaces the argument — but the confessors, as the next verses show, are not moved.",
  "124": "“‘I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, then crucify you all’” is Pharaoh’s full threat, and the commentators note the brutality it prescribes and the pattern it establishes. Pharaoh, having accused the magicians of conspiracy, now pronounced the sentence: “I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, then crucify you all,” or “la-uqatti‘anna aydiyakum wa arjulakum min khilafin thumma la-usallibannakum ajma‘een.” Ibn Kathir explains the punishment: the cutting off of the right hand and the left foot, or the reverse, the “crosswise” amputation that Pharaoh prescribed for those who defied his authority, followed by the crucifixion that displayed the punished to the public as a warning, and the threat was made against them all, “ajma‘een,” so that no one of the magicians would escape. The commentators note that this is the first crucifixion threatened in the Quran’s account, and that it reveals the character of the tyranny: the state that punishes the belief of its subjects with the mutilation of the body and the display of the corpse.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the contrast that the verse sets up, and that the next verses resolve. Pharaoh threatened the magicians with the severing of their hands and feet and the crucifixion of their bodies, and the magicians answered with the declaration that their return was to their Lord, for the tyrant could touch the body but not the soul, and the threat that was meant to terrify became the measure of the faith that could not be terrified. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the world’s ultimate threat to the believer, and that the story of the magicians is the Quran’s greatest lesson in the response to it: the believer who has seen the truth does not weigh the loss of the hands against the loss of the soul, and the threat of the tyrant is answered by the confidence of the one who knows where the true return lies. The threat of Pharaoh is the dark background against which the light of the magicians’ faith shines.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the reality of the price that the truth may demand. The magicians had asked, at the beginning, for the reward of Pharaoh, and the reward had become the sentence of death, and the verse records the sentence in full so that the listener may understand what the confession of the next verses cost. Ibn Kathir notes that the magicians’ acceptance of the sentence is the proof of the sincerity of their faith, for the men who had come for the reward stayed for the truth, and the threat that would have scattered the mercenary only confirmed the believer. For the listener, the verse is the tyrant’s full threat: the amputation and the crucifixion pronounced, and the confessors standing before it — about to answer with the words that no torture could silence.",
  "125": "“They responded, ‘Surely to our Lord we will all return’” records the calm answer of the magicians, and the commentators present it as one of the great declarations of faith in the Quran. The magicians, hearing the sentence of Pharaoh, answered not with fear or pleading but with the statement of their certainty: “Surely to our Lord we will all return,” or “inna ila rabbina munqaliboon.” Ibn Kathir explains the meaning of the answer: the magicians declared that the return of all things is to God, and that the death with which Pharaoh threatened them was not an end but a passage, and that the one who knows his return to his Lord does not fear the tyrant who can only hasten it. The word “munqaliboon,” those who return, is the key: the magicians had turned from Pharaoh to the Lord of Moses and Aaron, and their return to the Lord was the completion of the turn, so that the threat of death became, in their eyes, the shortening of the road to the return they now desired. The answer is the calm of the certainty that no threat can shake.\n\nThe commentators note the complete reversal that the answer reveals. The magicians had entered the court as the servants of Pharaoh, and they stood before him as the servants of God, and the sentence of death, which was meant to break them, found them already beyond its reach, for the soul that has returned to its Lord has no fear of the body’s return to the earth. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s teaching on the fearlessness of the believer: the one who knows that the return is to God is freed from the fear of every lesser return, and the tyrant who threatens the body has no power over the soul that has already been given to its Lord. The calm of the magicians before the threat is the calm of every believer who has made the same return, and the verse holds it up as the model of the response to persecution.\n\nThe verse also marks the magicians’ second confession, and the commentators note its progress. Their first confession had been “we believe in the Lord of all worlds, the Lord of Moses and Aaron,” and their second is “surely to our Lord we will all return,” and the two together are the complete faith: the declaration of the Lord and the certainty of the return, the belief in the message and the trust in the end. Ibn Kathir notes that the magicians, in their answer to Pharaoh, did not plead for their lives but affirmed their faith, and that the affirmation in the face of death is the highest testimony of the truth they had seen. For the listener, the verse is the believer’s answer to the threat: “surely to our Lord we will all return” — the calm that no tyrant can disturb and no torture can silence.",
  "126": "“Your rage towards us is only because we believed in the signs of our Lord when they came to us. Our Lord! Pour upon us patience and let us die as Muslims” completes the magicians’ reply, and the commentators present it as the full statement of their faith and their prayer. The first sentence is the diagnosis of the tyrant’s wrath: “Your rage towards us is only because we believed in the signs of our Lord when they came to us,” or “wa ma tanqimu minna illa an amanna bi-ayati rabbina lamma ja’atna.” Ibn Kathir explains the argument: the magicians declared that Pharaoh’s anger had no cause except their belief, that the only crime for which they were being threatened was the acceptance of the signs of God, and the declaration exposed the nakedness of the tyrant’s position — the punishment was not for a plot or a conspiracy but for the faith, and the faith alone was the offense. The word “tanqimu,” your rage, your resentment, is the key: the magicians stripped the accusation of the plot away and laid bare the true ground of the persecution, the resentment of the truth itself.\n\nThe second sentence is the prayer that seals their faith: “Our Lord! Pour upon us patience and let us die as Muslims,” or “rabbana afrigh ‘alayna sabran wa tawaffana muslimeen.” The commentators dwell on the prayer, for it is one of the great supplications of the Quran. The first request — “pour upon us patience” — uses the verb “afrigh,” the pouring of a vessel emptied out, and asks for a flood of patience, the patience to endure the amputation and the crucifixion without wavering, and the second request — “let us die as Muslims” — asks for the death of the submitters, the death upon the faith they had just declared, the end of the believers in the Lord of all worlds. Al-Sa’di observes that the prayer is the complete response of the believer to persecution: the patience to endure and the death upon the faith, and the two requests gather the whole of the believer’s hope in the hour of trial. The word “muslimeen,” Muslims, the submitters, is itself the statement of their identity: the magicians asked to die as those who had submitted to the Lord, and the prayer is the seal of their submission.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the two things to ask in the hour of trial: the patience to bear it and the constancy to the end, and the magicians’ prayer is the model of both, for the believer under persecution asks not for the removal of the trial but for the steadfastness within it and the good end beyond it. Ibn Kathir notes that the prayer of the magicians was answered, as the traditions relate, for they died as Muslims, and their death is the witness of the faith that no threat could break. For the listener, the verse is the completed answer of the confessors: the rage diagnosed, the patience requested, and the death upon the faith desired — the full measure of the believer’s response to the tyrant’s threat.",
  "127": "“The chiefs of Pharaoh’s people protested, ‘Are you going to leave Moses and his people free to spread corruption in the land and abandon you and your gods?’ He replied, ‘We will kill their sons and spare their women. We will certainly dominate them’” records the council’s advice and Pharaoh’s decree, and the commentators note the return of the persecution and its motive. The chiefs, seeing the defeat of the magicians and the conversion of their own experts, turned to Pharaoh with the protest: “Are you going to leave Moses and his people free to spread corruption in the land and abandon you and your gods?” or “a-tatharu Moosa wa qawmahu li-yufsidoo fi al-ardi wa yatharaka wa alihataka.” Ibn Kathir explains the protest: the chiefs saw that the message of Moses, left unchecked, would spread through Egypt and draw the people away from the worship of Pharaoh and his gods, and they pressed the tyrant to act, framing the message as corruption and the abandonment of the gods as the ruin of the state. The word “yufsidoo,” to spread corruption, is the same word that the Quran applies to the rejecters, and the verse preserves the irony of the chiefs’ accusation, for the true corruption was the tyranny they defended.\n\nPharaoh’s answer is the return to the slaughter: “We will kill their sons and spare their women. We will certainly dominate them,” or “sa-nuqattilu abna’ahum wa nastahyee nisa’ahum wa inna fawqahum qahiroon.” Ibn Kathir relates that this was the resumption of the policy of the slaughter of the newborn males of the Israelites, the genocide that had preceded the birth of Moses and that Pharaoh now renewed as his answer to the message, for the tyrant who could not answer the argument returned to the weapon of the massacre. The phrase “we will certainly dominate them,” “fawqahum qahiroon,” the claim of the overpowering might, is the boast of the tyrant, and the commentators note the irony that the verse will expose: the one who boasted of his dominance would drown, and the people he sought to extinguish would inherit the land. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse reveals the full character of the tyranny: the slaughter of the children and the enslavement of the women, and the boast of the dominance that the sea would swallow.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the constancy of the tyrant’s method. Pharaoh had seen the signs and the conversion, and his response was not reflection but renewed violence, for the tyrant who has chosen his path answers the truth with the massacre, and the massacre is the confession of his fear. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse prepares the full account of the plagues and the deliverance that follow, and that the persecution of the believers is the prelude to their liberation, for the oppression that reaches its peak is the oppression whose end is near. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the tyrant’s persistence: the slaughter renewed, the dominance boasted, and the ruin approaching — for the boast of the tyrant is the prelude to the drowning of the tyrant.",
  "128": "“Moses reassured his people, ‘Seek Allah’s help and be patient. Indeed, the earth belongs to Allah, and He grants it to whom He wills of His servants. The end is for the righteous’” records Moses’ answer to the persecution, and the commentators present it as the message of hope to the oppressed in every age. Faced with the renewed slaughter and the boast of Pharaoh, Moses turned to his people with the reassurance that the verse preserves: “Seek Allah’s help and be patient,” or “ista‘eenoo billahi wasbiroo,” the two commands that are the believer’s entire resource under oppression — the seeking of the divine help and the patience to endure until it comes. Ibn Kathir explains the pairing: the believer under persecution has no power of his own, and so he turns to the help of God, and the help of God is awaited with the patience that the command requires, for the two — the help and the patience — are inseparable, and the one who seeks the help without the patience has not yet understood the promise.\n\nThe second part of the reassurance is the theological ground of the hope: “Indeed, the earth belongs to Allah, and He grants it to whom He wills of His servants. The end is for the righteous,” or “inna al-arda lillahi yoorithuha man yasha’u min ‘ibadihi wal-‘aqibatu lil-muttaqeen.” Ibn Kathir and the commentators dwell on this sentence, for it is the Quran’s answer to the boast of the tyrant: the earth is not Pharaoh’s but God’s, and the inheritance of the earth is granted by the divine will to whom He chooses among His servants, and the final outcome belongs to the righteous, the “muttaqeen,” not to the oppressors. Al-Sa’di observes that the sentence is the complete theology of the believer’s hope under tyranny: the tyrant’s dominance is temporary, the earth is the Lord’s, and the end is for the God-fearing, so that the believer who is oppressed today is the heir of the promise tomorrow, and the patience that the verse commands is the waiting for the end that is already decreed.\n\nThe verse also carries the promise that the story of the Exodus would fulfill. Moses declared that the earth belongs to God and that the end is for the righteous, and the story that follows is the fulfillment of the declaration: the tyrant drowned, the oppressed delivered, and the land that had been the seat of the tyranny inherited by the very people whom the tyrant had sought to extinguish. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the charter of the oppressed in every age, and that the believer who reads it knows that the boast of the tyrant is not the last word, and that the last word belongs to the righteous. For the listener, the verse is the message of hope: seek the help of God, be patient, and know that the earth is His and the end is for the God-fearing — the assurance that no oppression is final and no tyrant is eternal.",
  "129": "“They complained, ‘We have always been oppressed—before and after you came to us with the message.’ He replied, ‘Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land, and then He will see how you behave’” records the complaint of the Israelites and the answer of Moses, and the commentators note the impatience that the complaint reveals. The people of Israel, crushed by the renewed persecution, complained to Moses: “We have always been oppressed—before and after you came to us with the message,” or “oothina min qabli an ta’tiyana wa min ba‘di ma ji’tana.” Ibn Kathir explains the complaint: the people had suffered under Pharaoh before the coming of Moses, and their suffering had not ended but deepened with his coming, for the message had provoked the tyrant to the renewed slaughter, and the people, in their despair, voiced the impatience of the oppressed who had hoped for a quicker deliverance. The complaint is the human cry of the one who has endured long and seen no relief, and the Quran preserves it without censure, for the prophets understood the weakness of the people they were sent to save.\n\nMoses’ answer is the answer of the prophet to the impatience of his people: “Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land, and then He will see how you behave,” or “‘asa rabbukum an yuhlika ‘aduwwakum wa yastakhlifakum fi al-ardi fa-yanzura kayfa ta‘maloon.” The commentators dwell on the word “‘asa,” perhaps, for it carries the hope of the promise and the condition of its fulfillment: the destruction of the enemy and the succession in the land were not certainties to be presumed but hopes to be awaited, and the waiting required the patience that the people had failed to show. Al-Sa’di observes that the answer contains the full pattern of the divine dealing: the destruction of the oppressor, the inheritance of the land, and the test that follows the inheritance, for the people who are made successors are then watched to see how they behave, and the succession itself is a trial as much as a gift. The phrase “He will see how you behave” is the warning within the promise: the deliverance is the beginning of the test, not its end.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the two-sided nature of the divine promise. The promise of deliverance is real, and the destruction of the oppressor is certain, but the promise is not a blank assurance, for the delivered are then tested, and the people who inherit the land are watched in their stewardship of it. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse anticipates the history of the Israelites after the Exodus — the deliverance followed by the trial of the wilderness and the test of the land — and that the believer is taught to receive the promise with hope and with fear, for the end of the oppression is the beginning of the reckoning. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the waiting: the impatience of the oppressed, the hope of the prophet, and the warning that the succession in the land is a trust that the Lord will watch.",
  "130": "“Indeed, We afflicted Pharaoh’s people with famine and shortage of crops so they might take heed” records the first of the plagues, and the commentators explain its nature and its purpose. Before the final destruction, God sent upon the people of Egypt the afflictions that the verse names: the famine, “as-sineen,” the years of scarcity, and the shortage of crops, “wa naqsin mina ath-thamarat,” the failure of the fruits and the harvests, so that the land that had been the granary of the region was struck with want. Ibn Kathir explains the purpose clause: “so they might take heed,” or “la‘allahum yadhakkaroon,” the affliction was not mere punishment but a call, a means by which the people might remember, for the hardship that follows the heedlessness is the mercy that seeks to awaken it. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the divine method of the warning: before the destruction, the reminder, and the reminder often comes in the form of the affliction that the heart, in its ease, had ignored. The famine of Egypt was the first of the warnings that would culminate in the sea.\n\nThe commentators note the sequence of the plagues that the following verses describe, and the wisdom of their order. The afflictions began with the famine and the shortage of crops — the blows against the prosperity of Egypt — and then progressed to the signs that the verses will name: the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs, and the blood, each a distinct blow against the pride of the tyrant, and each followed by the plea of the people and the broken promise of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir relates that the plagues were the signs that Moses had been given, and that they descended upon Egypt one after another, so that the land that had defied the Lord of all worlds was shown, sign by sign, the power that it had denied. The famine was the first of the signs, and its purpose — “so they might take heed” — is the purpose of them all.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the affliction as a mercy. The famine and the shortage were hard, and the people suffered, and yet the verse names their purpose as the awakening of remembrance, for the Lord who afflicts is the Lord who seeks the return, and the hardship that would have been the ruin of the heedless was, in the divine intent, the mercy of the reminder. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the answer to those who ask why the wicked are afflicted before their final punishment: the affliction is the last mercy, the final call before the door is closed, and the one who takes heed in the famine is the one who is spared the flood. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the warning: the famine came so that they might remember, and the memory that the famine failed to awaken was the loss that the sea would complete.",
  "131": "“In times of prosperity, they said, ‘This is what we deserve,’ but in adversity, they blamed Moses and those with him. Surely their fate is with Allah, but most of them do not know” records the response of the Egyptians to the alternation of the signs, and the commentators note the two attitudes the verse exposes. The verse describes the oscillation of the people between the good and the bad: “In times of prosperity, they said, ‘This is what we deserve,’” or “fa-itha ja’athumu al-hasanatu qaloo lana hathihi,” the claim of the good times as their due, the arrogance of the prosperous who attribute the blessing to their own merit and forget the Giver; “but in adversity, they blamed Moses and those with him,” or “wa in tusibhum sayyi’atun yattayyaroo bi-Moosa wa man ma‘ahu,” the superstitious blaming of the messenger and his followers for the hardship, the charge that the presence of Moses had brought the misfortune. Ibn Kathir explains the psychology: the people of Egypt, like the heedless of every age, took the good as their entitlement and the bad as the fault of the messenger, and the two responses are the two faces of the single refusal to recognize the hand of God in both.\n\nThe verse then corrects the superstition with the truth: “Surely their fate is with Allah, but most of them do not know,” or “ala innama ta’iruhum ‘inda Allahi walakinna aktharahum la ya‘lamoon.” The word “ta’ir,” the omen, the fate, the augury, is the key: the verse declares that the true omen, the true cause of their prosperity and their adversity, is with God alone, and that their blaming of Moses was the superstition of the ignorant, for the blessings and the afflictions both descend from the One whose decree they had denied. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the true attribution of events: the good and the bad are both from God, and the one who attributes the good to himself and the bad to others has inverted the order, for the believer says of the good “this is from my Lord” and of the bad “this is from my own soul,” while the heedless say the reverse. The verse is the Quran’s correction of the fatalism that blames the messenger for the misfortune that the rejecter has brought upon himself.\n\nThe verse also reveals the constancy of the pattern across the prophetic stories. The blaming of the prophet for the adversity is the oldest of the rejecters’ weapons, and the verse connects the Egyptians to every people that ever said “the misfortune is from you,” for the messenger is always made the scapegoat of the calamity that the rejection has invited. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is placed here to show the full picture of the Egyptian response: the good taken as the due, the bad blamed on the prophet, and the truth — that the fate is with God — unknown to the majority. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the attribution: the blessing is not the merit, the adversity is not the messenger, and the fate of all is with the One whom the heedless do not know.",
  "132": "“They said, ‘No matter what sign you may bring to deceive us, we will never believe in you’” records the hardening of the rejecters, and the commentators note the finality of the refusal. After the famine and the shortage, after the signs that had followed them, the people of Egypt declared their position in the most absolute terms: “No matter what sign you may bring to deceive us, we will never believe in you,” or “mahma ta’tina bihi min ayatin li-tasharana biha fa-ma nahnu laka bi-mu’mineen.” Ibn Kathir explains the declaration: the word “mahma,” whatever, no matter what, closed the door to every future sign, and the people announced that no proof, however clear, would move them, and the declaration is the self-sealing of the heart that has chosen its refusal, for the one who says “I will never believe” has made his disbelief an unbreakable vow. The word “li-tasharana,” to bewitch us, is the continued accusation of sorcery, the persistence of the label that the rejecters had fixed upon the signs, and the persistence is the proof of the hardening, for the heart that has called every light a deception has made itself incapable of seeing.\n\nThe commentators note the significance of the declaration for the story that follows. The people had said “we will never believe,” and the verse records the vow as the prelude to the plagues, for the one who has declared that no sign will move him has invited the full display of the signs, and the plagues that follow are the answer to the vow: the signs were sent, one after another, and the vow held, and the holding of the vow was the ruin of the vow-takers. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the terrible freedom of the human will: the heart is not forced to believe, and the one who swears his unbelief is given what he has sworn, and the signs that were sent to save become the witnesses against the one who refused them. The declaration “we will never believe” is the self-sentence of the rejecter, and the story that follows is the execution of the sentence.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the limit of the sign. The signs of God are sufficient for the one who seeks the truth, but they do not compel the one who has resolved to reject, and the people of Egypt, who had seen the staff and the hand and the famine, remained in their refusal because the refusal was the act of their will, not the failure of the evidence. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the Quran’s answer to the one who demands a sign as the condition of faith: the sign is given, and the heart that has already decided will not be moved by it, for the sign saves only the one who is willing to be saved. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the sealed heart: the vow of unbelief is the rejection of the light, and the light that is rejected becomes the darkness that the rejecter has chosen.",
  "133": "“So We plagued them with floods, locusts, lice, frogs, and blood—all as clear signs, but they remained arrogant, a sinful people” records the full sequence of the plagues, and the commentators describe each and note the stubbornness that met them. The verse lists the five signs that followed the famine: the flood, “at-toofan,” the inundation that covered the land; the locusts, “al-jarad,” the swarms that devoured the crops; the lice, “al-qummal,” the pests that afflicted the bodies; the frogs, “ad-dafadi‘,” the creatures that filled the houses and the food; and the blood, “ad-dam,” the water that turned to blood, so that the land of Egypt was struck with a chain of afflictions that the verse calls “clear signs,” “ayatin mufassalatin,” signs made plain and distinct, each one a separate and unmistakable demonstration of the divine power. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari relate the descriptions of each plague from the traditions, and the commentators note the cumulative force of the sequence: the famine had not moved them, and so the plagues came, one after another, each a distinct blow against the pride of the tyrant and the prosperity of his land.\n\nThe verse then records the response: “but they remained arrogant, a sinful people,” or “fastakbaroo wa kanoo qawman mujrimeen.” The word “istakbaroo,” they remained arrogant, is the verdict upon the whole sequence, and the word “mujrimeen,” the sinful, the criminal, is the description of the state that the arrogance had produced, for the people who had seen the floods and the locusts and the lice and the frogs and the blood still refused to bow, and their refusal was not the ignorance of the unwarned but the crime of the warned. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the measure of the hardening: the signs were clear and distinct, and the response was the same, and the sameness of the response is the proof that the disease was not in the evidence but in the heart, for the heart that has chosen arrogance will not be humbled by any sign.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the patience of the divine warning. The plagues were not sent in a single stroke but in a patient sequence, each sign given its time, each warning followed by the space for repentance, and the sequence itself is the mercy of the Lord who does not hasten the punishment but gives the sinner every opportunity to return. Ibn Kathir notes that the plagues are the fullest display of the divine forbearance in the story of Moses, and that the persistence of the Egyptians through them is the measure of their ruin, for the people who remained arrogant through the blood and the frogs had exhausted the mercy that had sought to save them. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the unheeded signs: the plagues came clear and distinct, and the arrogance remained, and the patience of the warning became the ground of the judgment that followed.",
  "134": "“When tormented, they pleaded, ‘O Moses! Pray to your Lord on our behalf, by virtue of the covenant He has made with you. If you remove the torment from us, we will certainly believe in you and let the Children of Israel go with you’” records the broken promise of the Egyptians, and the commentators note the pattern it begins. When the plagues descended, the people of Egypt turned to Moses with the plea that the verse preserves: “O Moses! Pray to your Lord on our behalf, by virtue of the covenant He has made with you,” or “ya Moosad‘u lana rabbaka bima ‘ahida ‘indaka,” the appeal to the messenger to intercede with his Lord, the acknowledgment, in the moment of distress, of the very covenant that they had denied. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the plagues brought the people to the point of desperation, and in their desperation they turned to Moses, promising that if the torment were removed, “we will certainly believe in you and let the Children of Israel go with you,” or “la’in kashafta ‘anna ar-rijza la-nu’minanna laka wa la-nursilanna ma‘aka Banee Isra’eel,” the double promise of faith and liberation. The commentators note the irony: the people who had said “we will never believe” now promised to believe, and the promise was the confession, wrung from them by the torment, of the truth they had denied.\n\nThe verse is the first instance of the pattern that the next verse completes, and the commentators describe it fully: the torment would come, the people would plead, Moses would pray, the torment would be removed, and the people would break their promise and return to their arrogance, and the cycle would repeat with each plague until the final judgment. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the nature of the repentance that is born of distress: the promise made in the torment is not the faith of the heart but the plea of the body, and the one who believes only under the lash will unbelieve when the lash is lifted, for the faith that is not rooted in the truth is rooted in the pain, and the pain passes. The verse is the warning against the repentance of the desperate, the promise that is broken the moment the danger is removed.\n\nThe verse also reveals the function of the messenger as the intercessor. The Egyptians, in their distress, knew that the torment was from the Lord of Moses and that the removal of the torment was in the prayer of Moses, and their plea was the acknowledgment, in the moment of need, of the very mediation they had scorned. Ibn Kathir notes that Moses prayed for them, as the verse implies, for the messenger is the mercy of his people even when they reject him, and the prayer of Moses for his tormentors is the model of the prophet’s compassion. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the broken promise: the plea of the desperate, the promise of the tormented, and the covenant invoked — and the breaking of the promise, which the next verse records, as the measure of the hearts that the torment could not change.",
  "135": "“But as soon as We removed the torment from them—until they met their inevitable fate—they broke their word” records the breaking of the promise, and the commentators note the exactness of the pattern and the phrase that qualifies it. The verse states the outcome of the plea: “as soon as We removed the torment from them,” or “fa-lamma kashafna ‘anhumu ar-rijza,” the lifting of the plague in answer to the prayer of Moses, “they broke their word,” or “itha hum yankuthoon,” the immediate violation of the promise, the return to the arrogance and the denial the moment the danger passed. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the torment was removed, and the promise was broken, and the cycle repeated itself with plague after plague, for the hearts of the Egyptians were not changed by the removal of the pain but only relieved by it, and the relief returned them to their former state. The word “yankuthoon,” they break, they violate, is the Quran’s term for the breaking of the covenant, and the verse applies it to the broken promise of the Egyptians, the covenant of the desperate that dissolved with the desperation.\n\nThe verse contains a phrase that the commentators dwell on: “until they met their inevitable fate,” or “ila ajalin hum balighoohu,” the term that they would reach, the appointed time of their destruction. The phrase is the key to the whole sequence, and Al-Sa’di explains its meaning: the removal of the torment was not the end of the affair but the postponement of the judgment, and the postponement was until the term that the people would inevitably reach — the term of their drowning in the sea, the final punishment that the plagues had foretold. The breaking of the promise, in other words, did not avert the fate but hastened it, for the people who were given the respite and broke their word were spending the last of their time, and the term that awaited them was certain. The phrase teaches that the delay of the punishment is not the cancellation of it, and that the respite granted to the sinner is the span until the inevitable fate, not the escape from it.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the repentance of convenience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own life. The Egyptian who promised under the plague and broke under the relief is the type of the soul that turns to God in the distress and turns away in the ease, and the Quran warns against this by the example, for the faith that is only for the storm is no faith at all. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the standing warning against the broken vow: the promise made in the torment and broken in the relief is the promise that the term will overtake, and the one who has broken his word to God has only the inevitable fate before him. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the broken promise: the torment lifted, the word broken, and the term approached — the pattern of the heedless whose repentance does not outlive the pain.",
  "136": "“So We inflicted punishment upon them, drowning them in the sea for denying Our signs and being heedless of them” records the final judgment upon Pharaoh and his host, and the commentators note the justice that the verse states. The verse declares the punishment and its two causes: “drowning them in the sea,” or “fa-aghraqnahum fi al-yammi,” the destruction of Pharaoh and his army in the waters of the sea, “for denying Our signs and being heedless of them,” or “bi-annahum kaththaboo bi-ayatina wa kanoo ‘anha ghafileen.” Ibn Kathir explains the two causes: the denial of the signs — the “takdhib,” the active rejection of the proofs that had been shown — and the heedlessness of them — the “ghaflah,” the turning away in negligence, the failure to reflect upon what had been witnessed — and the two together are the complete description of the rejecter, who denies with the tongue and turns away with the heart. The verse thus states the principle of the whole story: the drowning was not arbitrary but the recompense of the denial and the heedlessness, the harvest of the rejection that the plagues had failed to cure.\n\nThe commentators describe the scene of the drowning from the fuller accounts. Pharaoh, having at last allowed the Children of Israel to depart, pursued them with his host, and the sea, which had parted for Moses and his people, closed over Pharaoh and his army, and the tyrant who had boasted “we will certainly dominate them” was drowned in the very waters that had been the path of the deliverance. Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari relate that Pharaoh’s confession — “I believe in the One in whom the Children of Israel believe” — was uttered as the waters closed over him, and that it came too late, for the faith of the drowning is not the faith of the saved, and the verse’s statement of the two causes explains why the confession was not accepted. Al-Sa’di observes that the drowning of Pharaoh is the fulfillment of the promise that Moses had made: “perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy,” and the destruction was as complete as the promise, for the tyrant and his host perished in a single hour.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the end of the heedless, and the commentators connect it to the whole surah’s pattern. The five nations had been destroyed for their rejection, and now the greatest of the tyrants met the greatest of the destructions, and the drowning of Pharaoh is the culminating instance of the law that the surah has been teaching: the denial of the signs and the heedlessness of them are the two roads that lead to the ruin, and the sea that swallowed Pharaoh is the sea that awaits every heedless denier. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the verdict upon the tyranny of Egypt, and that the inheritance of the land by the oppressed, which the next verse records, is the reverse of the drowning — the two sides of the single judgment. For the listener, the verse is the judgment of the sea: the deniers drowned for their denial, and the heedless swallowed for their heedlessness, and the justice of the Lord of all worlds declared in the waters.",
  "137": "“And so We made the oppressed people successors of the eastern and western lands, which We had blessed. And the excellent word of your Lord was fulfilled for the Children of Israel for their patience. And We destroyed what Pharaoh and his people had made and what they had built” records the inheritance of the land and the fulfillment of the promise, and the commentators note the completeness of the reversal. The verse declares the outcome of the judgment: “We made the oppressed people successors of the eastern and western lands, which We had blessed,” or “wa awrathna al-qawma allatheena kanoo yustad‘afoona mashariqa al-ardi wa magharibaha allati barakna feeha.” Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the Children of Israel, the “oppressed,” the “mustad‘afoon” who had been enslaved and slaughtered under Pharaoh, were made the successors, the heirs, of the lands of Egypt and Syria and the blessed lands around them, the “eastern and western lands” that God had blessed, and the inheritance was the direct reversal of their former state — the slaves became the heirs, and the land of their bondage became the land of their dominion. The word “mustad‘afoon,” the oppressed, the weakened, is the key: the verse declares that the people whom Pharaoh had weakened were the people whom God had chosen to inherit.\n\nThe verse then states the cause of the inheritance: “And the excellent word of your Lord was fulfilled for the Children of Israel for their patience,” or “wa tammat kalimatu rabbika al-husna ‘ala Banee Isra’eela bima sabaroo.” The “excellent word,” the “kalimah al-husna,” is the promise that God had made to the Children of Israel through Moses — the promise of deliverance and succession — and the verse declares that the promise was fulfilled “for their patience,” the “sabr” of the people who had endured the oppression without losing faith. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the condition of every divine promise: the word of God is fulfilled for the patient, and the patience of the oppressed is the means by which the promise of the deliverance is realized, for the people who endured the slaughter and the slavery and kept their faith were the people whom the promise crowned with the inheritance. The patience of the Children of Israel is the bridge between the promise and its fulfillment.\n\nThe verse closes with the destruction of the works of the oppressor: “And We destroyed what Pharaoh and his people had made and what they had built,” or “wa dammarna ma kana yasna‘u Fir‘awnu wa qawmuhu wa ma kanoo ya‘rishoon.” The buildings, the monuments, the towers, and the works of Egypt — the pride of the tyrant and the achievement of his people — were destroyed, and the commentators note the completeness of the judgment: the tyrant was not only drowned but his works were erased, so that the one who had built for eternity was shown that his buildings were as perishable as his body. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the complete statement of the reversal: the oppressed inherited, the promise was fulfilled, and the works of the oppressor were destroyed, and the three together are the summary of the whole story of the Exodus. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the inheritance: the patience of the oppressed is crowned with the succession, the promise of the Lord is fulfilled in its time, and the works of the tyrant are erased by the hand that raised the slaves.",
  "138": "“We brought the Children of Israel across the sea and they came upon a people devoted to their idols. They said, ‘O Moses! Make for us a god just like their gods.’ He replied, ‘You are certainly an ignorant people’” records the first lapse of the delivered, and the commentators note its timing and its irony. The verse describes the scene after the crossing: the Children of Israel, saved from the sea and the tyrant, came upon a people who were devoted to their idols, “ya‘kufoona ‘ala asnamin lahum,” a people who clung to the worship of their statues, and the Israelites, seeing the devotion of the idolaters, said to Moses: “Make for us a god just like their gods,” or “ij‘al lana ilahan kama lahum alihah.” Ibn Kathir explains the irony that the verse preserves: the people who had just witnessed the drowning of Pharaoh and the parting of the sea, the people for whom the sea had opened and the manna had fallen, saw the idols of a pagan people and asked for a god like theirs, and the request was the measure of their spiritual immaturity, for the faith that had not taken root in their hearts was still the faith of the eye, ready to worship whatever it saw. The word “ya‘kufoona,” they were devoted, the clinging of the idolaters to their statues, is the description of the devotion that the Israelites envied.\n\nMoses’ answer is the rebuke of the prophet: “You are certainly an ignorant people,” or “innakum qawmun tajhaloon.” The word “jahl,” ignorance, is the key, and the commentators explain its force: the request for an idol after the signs of the sea was not a sin of knowledge but of ignorance, the failure of the mind to recognize the absurdity of what it asked, for the one who has seen the power of the Lord of all worlds and asks for a statue has not understood what he has seen. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse diagnoses the spiritual condition of the delivered: the crossing of the sea had moved their bodies but not their hearts, and the hearts that had lived under the shadow of Egypt’s idols still carried the image of the idol, so that the first pagan people they met became the occasion of their lapse. The verse is the first warning of the calf, the prelude to the great sin that the story will soon record.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the fragility of the faith that is not internalized. The Children of Israel had seen the greatest signs, and yet they asked for an idol, for the sign that is witnessed but not reflected upon is a sign that is lost, and the faith that is built on the witnessing of miracles without the rooting of the heart is a faith that the first temptation will overturn. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is placed here to prepare the story of the calf, and that the request for “a god like their gods” is the seed of the idolatry that would flower at the foot of the mountain. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the unrooted heart: the sea crossed, the idol desired, and the prophet’s rebuke — the warning that the delivered are not yet the devoted, and that the heart that seeks a god like the gods of the pagans has not yet known the God who parted the sea.",
  "139": "“What they follow is certainly doomed to destruction and their deeds are in vain” continues Moses’ rebuke, and the commentators note the argument it makes. Moses, having called his people ignorant, now explained why the worship of the idols was a ruin: “What they follow is certainly doomed to destruction,” or “inna ha’ula’i mutabbarun ma hum feehi,” the religion of the idolaters is doomed, the object of their devotion is destined for destruction, and “their deeds are in vain,” or “wa batilun ma kanoo ya‘maloon,” the works of the idolaters are “batil,” futile, empty, of no effect. Ibn Kathir explains the argument: Moses directed his people’s attention from the apparent devotion of the idolaters to the reality of their religion, for the idols that they worshipped would be destroyed, and the worship that they offered was in vain, and the people who envied the devotion of the idolaters were envying a ruin. The word “batil,” vain, empty, is the same word that the Quran uses for the works of the rejecters in every age, and the verse applies it to the religion of the idolaters, so that the people of Israel might see what their request for an idol would lead them to.\n\nThe commentators note the completeness of the rebuke. Moses answered the request for an idol with two arguments: the request itself was ignorance, and the object of the request was ruin, and the two arguments together left the people no ground, for the one who asks for a god like the gods of a doomed people has asked for a share in their doom. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the true measure of the false religions: the apparent devotion of the idolaters, which had impressed the people of Israel, was in reality a doomed enterprise and a vain labour, and the believer must see through the appearance of the false to the ruin that it is. The verse is the Quran’s answer to every envy of the false: what they follow is doomed, and what they do is in vain, and the one who envies them envies the falling.\n\nThe verse also prepares the contrast that the next verse completes, and the commentators note its place in the argument. Moses had said that the religion of the idolaters was doomed, and the next verse will ask the question that follows: shall I seek for you a god other than Allah, while He has honoured you above all the worlds? Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the first half of the rebuke, and that the two halves together are the complete answer to the request for the idol: the false is doomed, and the true has honoured them, and the choice between the two is the choice that the story of the calf will tragically reverse. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the doomed falsehood: the idols are destined for destruction, the worship of them is in vain, and the people who covet the religion of the doomed have coveted their end.",
  "140": "“He added, ‘Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah, while He has honoured you above all the worlds?’” completes Moses’ rebuke, and the commentators note the argument and its irony. Moses asked his people the question that exposed the absurdity of their request: “Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah, while He has honoured you above all the worlds?” or “a-ghayra Allahi abgheekum ilahan wa huwa faddalakum ‘ala al-‘alameen.” Ibn Kathir explains the argument: the people who had been honoured by God above all the peoples of their age — delivered from Pharaoh, fed with the manna, guided by the prophets — were asking for a god other than the One who had honoured them, and the question placed the honour and the request side by side, so that the absurdity of the request was made plain, for the one who has been honoured by God above all the worlds has no need of the gods of the unhonoured. The word “faddalakum,” He honoured you, He preferred you, is the key: the favour of God was the answer to the request, and the people who forgot the favour were the people who asked for the idol.\n\nThe commentators note the historical truth that the verse states. The Children of Israel were, in their age, the people of the prophets and the scripture, the people whom God had preferred over the worlds of their time through the signs and the guidance that He had given them, and the preference was the grace that the request for an idol insulted, for the one who has been preferred by the Lord of all worlds and asks for a lesser god has despised the preference. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the true source of honour: the honour of a people is not in their numbers or their strength but in the favour of God, and the people who forget the favour and seek the gods of others have abandoned the very source of their elevation. The verse is the warning against the ingratitude that takes the honour for granted and the idol for the answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the foreshadowing of the calf, and the commentators note its place in the story. Moses asked “shall I seek for you a god other than Allah,” and the people, within a short time, would make for themselves the golden calf, and the question of Moses would be answered by the deed of the people, for the request that had been rebuked became the sin that was committed. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the standing warning of the story: the people who had been honoured above the worlds were the people who worshipped the calf, and the fall from the honour to the idol is the fall that the Quran holds up as the warning of every people that forgets its Lord. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the forgotten honour: the question that exposed the absurdity, the favour that the request insulted, and the irony that the request would become the sin — for the honoured above the worlds were about to bow to the work of their own hands.",
  "141": "“And remember when We rescued you from the people of Pharaoh, who afflicted you with dreadful torment, slaughtering your sons and sparing your women. That was a great test from your Lord” recalls the deliverance from Egypt as the ground of the rebuke, and the commentators note the full content of the reminder. The verse returns the people of Israel to the memory of their bondage and their rescue: “We rescued you from the people of Pharaoh, who afflicted you with dreadful torment, slaughtering your sons and sparing your women,” or “idh anjaynakum min ali Fir‘awna yasoomoonakum soo’a al-‘athabi yuqattiloona abna’akum wa yastahyoona nisa’akum.” Ibn Kathir explains the reminder: the people who now asked for an idol had been delivered from the tyrant who slaughtered their sons and enslaved their women, and the memory of the deliverance was the answer to the request, for the God who had rescued them from the slaughter was the God whose worship they were about to abandon, and the rescue was the favour that the idol would betray. The verse is the third of the rebukes, and the three together — the ignorance, the doomed falsehood, and the forgotten deliverance — form the complete answer to the request for the idol.\n\nThe verse closes with the verdict upon the affliction: “That was a great test from your Lord,” or “wa fi thalikum bala’un min rabbikum ‘azeem.” The word “bala’,” the test, the trial, is the key, and the commentators explain its meaning: the affliction of Egypt was a test of the patience and the faith of the people, and the deliverance from it was the mercy that the test was meant to produce, so that the people who had endured the test and received the deliverance were the people who owed the gratitude that the idol would deny. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer to read the trials of the past as the ground of the gratitude of the present: the deliverance from the tyrant is the proof of the Lord, and the memory of the deliverance is the shield against the idol, for the heart that remembers the rescue will not bow to the statue. The verse is the reminder of the favour, and the reminder is the antidote to the request.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sequence of the three rebukes, and the commentators note its structure. Moses answered the request for the idol with three arguments: the ignorance of the requesters, the doom of the false gods, and the forgotten deliverance, and the three together are the complete education of the delivered people, the teaching that was meant to root the faith that the signs had begun. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the conclusion of the rebuke, and that the story then moves to the appointment of Moses at the mountain, where the root that the rebukes had tried to plant would be tested and, tragically, fail. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the remembered rescue: the slaughter recalled, the deliverance named, and the test declared — the memory that should have been the shield, and the forgetting of which would be the fall.",
  "142": "“We appointed for Moses thirty nights then added another ten—completing his Lord’s term of forty nights. Moses said to his brother Aaron, ‘Take my place among my people, act rightly, and do not follow the way of the corruptors’” records the appointment at the mountain and the charge to Aaron, and the commentators note the detail of the term and the content of the charge. The verse states the appointment: “We appointed for Moses thirty nights then added another ten—completing his Lord’s term of forty nights,” or “wa wa‘adna Moosa thalatheena laylatan wa atmamnaha bi-‘ashrin fa-tamma meeqatu rabbihi arba‘eena laylah.” Ibn Kathir explains the number: the appointment was for thirty nights, and the term was completed to forty by the addition of ten, the forty nights that Moses spent in communion with his Lord at the mountain, a period of fasting and devotion during which he would receive the Torah, and the commentators note the sacredness of the number forty in the tradition, the span of the preparation and the trial. The word “meeqat,” the appointed term, is the key: the appointment was the fixed time of the divine meeting, and the addition of the ten was the completion of the term that the Lord had set.\n\nThe verse then records the charge that Moses gave to his brother before departing: “Take my place among my people, act rightly, and do not follow the way of the corruptors,” or “ukhlufni fi qawmi wa aslih wa la tattabi‘ sabeela al-mufsideen.” Ibn Kathir explains the charge: Moses left Aaron as his deputy among the people, commanding him to act rightly, “aslih,” the doing of good and the setting of things aright, and to avoid the way of the corruptors, “al-mufsideen,” the path of those who spread corruption, and the charge was the complete instruction of the deputy, the command of the one who would be tested by the absence of his brother. Al-Sa’di observes that the charge reveals the anxiety of Moses, for he knew the weakness of his people, and the command to Aaron — act rightly, and do not follow the corruptors — was the warning of the very failure that the people would commit, the failure that the next verses record. The charge is the prelude to the calf, the warning that the people, left without Moses, would not heed.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the duty of the deputy and the nature of the trial of absence. Aaron was left to act rightly and to avoid the corruptors, and the trial of the people was the trial of the absence, the test of whether the faith they had received could survive the departure of the one who had brought it, and the test, as the story will show, failed, for the people who could not bear the absence of Moses fell to the calf. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the transition of the story from the deliverance to the great test of the mountain, and that the forty nights of the appointment were the span in which the people, left to themselves, would show what was in their hearts. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the appointment and the charge: the term set, the deputy charged, and the absence begun — the prelude to the test that the people would fail.",
  "143": "“When Moses came at the appointed time and his Lord spoke to him, he asked, ‘My Lord! Reveal Yourself to me so that I may see You.’ He replied, ‘You cannot see Me! But look at the mountain: if it remains firm in its place, then you will see Me.’ When his Lord appeared to the mountain, He crushed it to dust, and Moses fell unconscious. When he recovered, he declared, ‘Glory be to You! I turn to You in repentance, and I am the first of the believers’” records the supreme encounter of Moses with his Lord, and the commentators present it as one of the most exalted passages of the Quran. The verse describes the meeting: Moses came at the appointed time, and his Lord spoke to him, and in the intimacy of the divine speech Moses asked for what no creature can bear: “My Lord! Reveal Yourself to me so that I may see You,” or “rabbi arinee anzur ilayka.” Ibn Kathir explains the request: Moses, in the longing of the one who has been honoured with the speech of God, asked for the vision, the sight of the divine, and the request was the expression of the highest aspiration of the human soul, the desire to see the One whom it has known through His word. The commentators differ on whether the request was for Moses himself or on behalf of the people who had demanded to see God, but the verse itself records the answer that the request received.\n\nThe answer is the divine refusal and the demonstration that explains it: “You cannot see Me! But look at the mountain: if it remains firm in its place, then you will see Me,” or “lan taranee walakin inzur ila al-jabali fa-in istaqarra makanahu fa-sawfa taranee.” God declared that the vision was impossible for the creature in this life, and gave the sign that would prove the impossibility: the mountain, the firmest of created things, would be the test, and if it could bear the divine manifestation, then Moses would see. Then came the manifestation: “When his Lord appeared to the mountain, He crushed it to dust,” or “fa-lamma tajalla rabbuhu lil-jabali ja‘alahu dakkan,” the mountain, at the appearance of the divine, was ground to dust, and Moses fell unconscious, “sa‘iqa,” struck down by the overwhelming of the sight. Al-Sa’di observes the lesson of the mountain: if the firm mountain could not bear the manifestation, how could the human frame, which is weaker than the mountain, bear the vision of the One whose appearance crushes the mountain to dust?\n\nThe verse closes with the recovery and the confession: “When he recovered, he declared, ‘Glory be to You! I turn to You in repentance, and I am the first of the believers,’” or “subhanaka tubtu ilayka wa ana awwalu al-mu’mineen.” Moses, restored to consciousness, glorified his Lord, declared his repentance for the request that had exceeded the bounds of the creature, and affirmed his faith as the first of the believers, and the commentators note the completeness of the lesson: the prophet who had asked for the vision learned, through the mountain, the limit of the creature, and his confession is the model of the believer’s humility before the divine majesty. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the summit of the story of Moses, the moment at which the human aspiration for the vision met the divine limit, and the mountain, ground to dust, became the eternal proof that the vision of God in this life is beyond the capacity of the created. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the mountain: the aspiration of Moses, the crushing of the mountain, and the confession of the prophet — the teaching that the glory of God is too great for the eye, and that the believer’s portion is the speech, not the sight, the word, not the vision.",
  "144": "“Allah said, ‘O Moses! I have chosen you above all people with My messages and My speech. So take what I have given you and be grateful’” records the honour that God conferred upon Moses, and the commentators note the two gifts that the verse names. God addressed Moses with the declaration of his election: “I have chosen you above all people with My messages and My speech,” or “inni istafaytuka ‘ala an-nasi bi-risalatee wa bi-kalamee.” Ibn Kathir explains the two gifts: the messages, “risalat,” the revelation that Moses was charged to convey, and the speech, “kalam,” the direct address of God to Moses, the honour of hearing the divine word without the mediation of an angel, and the commentators note that the direct speech of God to Moses is the station that earned him the title “Kalim Allah,” the one to whom God spoke, a distinction that the verse declares above all the people of his age. Al-Sa’di observes that the election of Moses was not a favour of blood but a gift of grace, and that the two gifts — the message and the speech — are the two highest honours that a creature can receive, the receiving of the divine word and the bearing of it to the people.\n\nThe verse then states the response that the election demanded: “So take what I have given you and be grateful,” or “fakhuth ma ataytuka wa kun mina ash-shakireen.” The command is twofold: the taking of the gift, the acceptance of the revelation and the message, and the gratitude, “shukr,” the thankfulness that the gift deserves, and the commentators note that the two are inseparable, for the one who takes the gift without the gratitude has not truly received it, and the gratitude of the messenger is the obedience that carries the message to its destination. Ibn Kathir explains that the command to gratitude is the command to the whole of Moses’ subsequent life, for the prophet who has been honoured with the speech of God is the prophet whose every act must be the expression of the gratitude for that honour, and the verse sets the gratitude as the measure of the election. The election and the gratitude are the two sides of the relationship between God and His honoured servant.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the station of the prophets, and the commentators connect it to the Prophet Muhammad’s own station. Moses was chosen above the people of his age with the message and the speech, and the honour was specific to his time, for the prophets are the chosen of their ages, and the station of the Prophet Muhammad is the seal of the stations, the messenger whose message abrogated the messages and whose community is the witness over the communities. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the statement of the election of Moses, and that the believer reads in it the principle of all election: the honour of God is a gift, and the gift is received with gratitude, and the gratitude is the obedience that the gift commands. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the honoured servant: the election declared, the gratitude commanded, and the station of the one who spoke with his Lord held up as the model of the receiving of the divine favour.",
  "145": "“We wrote for him on the Tablets the fundamentals of everything; commandments and explanations for all things. ‘Hold firmly to them and command your people to take the best of them. I will soon show you the home of the rebellious’” records the giving of the Tablets, and the commentators describe their content and their command. The verse states the writing: “We wrote for him on the Tablets the fundamentals of everything; commandments and explanations for all things,” or “wa katabna lahu fi al-alwahi min kulli shay’in maw‘izatan wa tafseelan li-kulli shay’.” Ibn Kathir explains the Tablets: the “alwah,” the tablets upon which God wrote for Moses the law, containing the “maw‘izah,” the admonition, the moral and spiritual instruction, and the “tafseel,” the detailed explanation, the laws and rulings that the people would need, and the commentators note the comprehensiveness of the phrase “for all things” — the Tablets contained the fundamentals of the religion, the commandments of the law, and the explanations of the way, the complete guidance for the community of Moses. The giving of the Tablets was the culmination of the forty nights, the delivery of the scripture that the people had been promised.\n\nThe verse then records the command that accompanied the Tablets: “‘Hold firmly to them and command your people to take the best of them. I will soon show you the home of the rebellious,’” or “fakhuth-ha bi-quwwatin wa’mur qawmaka ya’khuthoo bi-ahsaniha sa-ureekum dara al-fasiqeen.” The threefold command is the charge of the scripture: the firm holding, “bi-quwwah,” the taking of the law with strength and seriousness; the commanding of the people to take the best of it, “ahsaniha,” the pursuit of the highest and the most excellent of its teachings; and the warning that closes the command, “I will soon show you the home of the rebellious,” the promise that the fate of the “fasiqeen,” the rebellious, the corruptors, would be shown, the warning that the scripture is a trust and the breach of it a ruin. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the complete relationship to the scripture: the firmness of the taking, the excellence of the application, and the fear of the end that awaits the rebellious, the three elements that make the possession of the Book a blessing rather than a burden.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the scripture as the guide, and the commentators connect it to the Quran itself. The Tablets of Moses were the complete guidance for his people, and the Quran is the complete guidance for the community of Muhammad, and the command “hold firmly to them” is the command that the believer hears in every age, for the scripture is not a book to be shelved but a law to be held and an instruction to be followed. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the giving of the Torah in its essential summary, and that the believer reads in it the principle of every revelation: the Book contains the fundamentals and the details, and the taking of it is the condition of the guidance, and the breach of it is the road of the rebellious. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the Tablets: the law written, the command given, and the warning declared — the scripture delivered to the one who would hold it, and the home of the rebellious shown to those who would not.",
  "146": "“I will turn away from My signs those who act unjustly with arrogance in the land. And even if they see every sign, they will not believe in it. And if they see the path of guidance, they will not take it; but if they see the path of error, they will take it. That is because they denied Our signs and were heedless of them” records the divine principle that governs the hardening of the rejecters, and the commentators present it as one of the Quran’s most searching statements on the sealed heart. The verse states the decree: “I will turn away from My signs those who act unjustly with arrogance in the land,” or “sa-asrifu ‘an ayatiya allatheena yatakabbaroona fi al-ardi bi-ghayri al-haqq.” Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: those who act with arrogance in the land, wronging others and exalting themselves without right, are turned away from the signs of God, and the turning away is not an arbitrary act but the consequence of their own arrogance, for the heart that has made itself great is the heart that cannot see the signs, and the divine turning away is the confirmation of the turning away that the heart has already chosen. The word “yatakabbaroona,” they act arrogantly, is the key: the arrogance is the disease, and the turning away from the signs is its symptom and its punishment.\n\nThe verse then describes the state of the turned-away in three clauses of terrible precision: “even if they see every sign, they will not believe in it,” or “wa in yaraw kulla ayatin la yu’minoo biha,” the sight of the signs without the belief in them, for the eye that is turned away sees without perceiving; “if they see the path of guidance, they will not take it,” or “wa in yaraw sabeela ar-rushdi la yattakhithoohu sabeelan,” the refusal of the path of rightness when it appears; “but if they see the path of error, they will take it,” or “wa in yaraw sabeela al-ghayyi yattakhithoohu sabeelan,” the eager taking of the path of error, the preference of the crooked over the straight. Al-Sa’di observes that the three clauses are the complete portrait of the hardened heart: the signs seen and denied, the guidance shown and refused, the error shown and embraced, and the three together reveal the inversion that arrogance works, the turning of the soul from the light to the dark.\n\nThe verse closes with the cause of it all: “That is because they denied Our signs and were heedless of them,” or “thalika bi-annahum kaththaboo bi-ayatina wa kanoo ‘anha ghafileen.” The denial and the heedlessness are the two roots, and the verse states them as the explanation of the state it has described, for the one who denied the signs and was heedless of them has earned the turning away that the verse declares. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the Quran’s warning against the arrogance that hardens, and that the believer is taught to guard against the first steps of the disease, for the arrogance that begins as a seed ends as the sealed heart that cannot see the signs. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the turned-away: the arrogance, the turning, the denial, and the heedlessness — the chain that leads from the pride of the heart to the blindness of the soul.",
  "147": "“The deeds of those who deny Our signs and the meeting with Allah in the Hereafter will be in vain. Will they be rewarded except for what they used to do?” records the verdict upon the works of the deniers, and the commentators note the principle it states. The verse declares the nullity of the deeds: “The deeds of those who deny Our signs and the meeting with Allah in the Hereafter will be in vain,” or “wallatheena kaththaboo bi-ayatina wa liqa’i al-akhirati habitat a‘maluhum.” Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the works of the one who denies the signs and denies the meeting with God in the Hereafter are “habitat,” nullified, wasted, of no weight, for the deed that is not done for the sake of the meeting with God is a deed without a destination, and the one who denies the Hereafter has emptied his own works of their reward, since the reward was to be given at the meeting he denied. The word “habitat,” they are in vain, they collapse, is the same word that the Quran uses for the works of the rejecters throughout, and the verse applies it to the deeds of the deniers, so that the listener understands that the denial of the Hereafter is the cancellation of the value of every act.\n\nThe verse closes with the question that states the justice of the verdict: “Will they be rewarded except for what they used to do?” or “hal yujzawna illa ma kanoo ya‘maloon.” The question is rhetorical, and the answer is the principle of the divine recompense: the reward is always for what was done, and the one whose deeds were done in the denial of the Hereafter has earned the reward of the denial, for the recompense is not arbitrary but the exact measure of the deed. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the connection between the intention and the reward: the deed is weighed by its destination, and the work that is not aimed at the meeting with God is a work that misses the mark, and the one who denies the meeting has denied himself the reward of his own labour. The verse is the Quran’s statement of the economy of the Hereafter: the deed without the belief in the meeting is the seed sown on the rock, and the harvest of the rock is nothing.\n\nThe verse also carries the warning that runs through the whole passage, and the commentators connect it to the Tablets that Moses had received. The Tablets had taught the people the way and warned them of the home of the rebellious, and the verse states the fate of the one who denies the signs and the meeting, so that the community of Moses might know, from the first, the end of the path that the calf-worshippers would take. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the verdict upon the deniers, and that the believer reads in it the principle that governs all deeds: the worth of the work is the worth of the belief that produced it, and the work that denies its Lord is a work that will find, at the meeting it denied, only the measure of what it used to do. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the vain deed: the denial of the meeting is the nullification of the work, and the reward of the denier is only the recompense of what he did.",
  "148": "“In the absence of Moses, his people made from their golden jewellery an idol of a calf that made a lowing sound. Did they not see that it could not speak to them nor guide them to any path? Yet they took it as a god and were wrongdoers” records the great sin of the calf, and the commentators describe its making and its absurdity. The verse states the deed and its occasion: “In the absence of Moses, his people made from their golden jewellery an idol of a calf that made a lowing sound,” or “wattakhatha qawmu Moosa min ba‘dihi min huliyyihim ‘ijlan jasadan lahu khuwar.” Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari relate the fuller story: during the forty nights of Moses’ absence, the Samiri, a man among the Israelites, gathered the gold and the jewellery that the people had carried out of Egypt, melted it down, and fashioned from it the statue of a calf, a hollow image through which the wind passed so that it emitted a lowing sound, the “khuwar,” the bellowing of a living calf, and the people, seeing the statue and hearing its sound, took it as their god and worshipped it. The word “jasad,” a body, a lifeless form, is the key: the verse calls the calf a “jasad,” a mere body, even as it records the lowing, so that the listener is made to see the absurdity — a body of gold, made by their own hands, that lowed like a calf and could not speak.\n\nThe verse then asks the question that exposes the absurdity: “Did they not see that it could not speak to them nor guide them to any path?” or “a-lam yaraw annahu la yukallimuhum wa la yahdeehim sabeelan.” The calf could not answer their questions, could not guide them to any way, could not benefit or harm, and the question places the fact before their eyes, for the god that cannot speak and cannot guide is no god at all, and the one who worships it has worshipped a thing less than himself. Al-Sa’di observes that the question is the Quran’s refutation of the idol in a sentence: the test of the deity is its speech and its guidance, and the calf, which had neither, was disqualified by its own silence and its own blindness. The verse closes with the verdict: “Yet they took it as a god and were wrongdoers,” or “ittakhathoohu wa kanoo zalimeen,” the taking of the calf as a god and the wronging of their own souls, for the worship of the speechless and the blind is the supreme displacement of the worship from its object.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the psychology of the fall that it records. The people of Israel had seen the sea part and the tyrant drown, and yet, within forty days of the departure of their prophet, they bowed to a calf of their own making, and the verse preserves the fall as the warning of every age: the faith that is attached to the presence of the guide and not rooted in the heart will fall at the first absence, and the idol of the calf is the symbol of every substitute that the unrooted heart invents when the guide is gone. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the central warning of the story of Moses, and that the calf is the Quran’s great emblem of the idolatry that lurks in the heart of every people that has not internalized the faith it professes. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the calf: the gold melted, the body fashioned, the question asked, and the wronging committed — the fall of the delivered to the work of their own hands.",
  "149": "“Later, when they were filled with remorse and realized they had gone astray, they cried, ‘If our Lord does not have mercy on us and forgive us, we will certainly be among the losers’” records the remorse of the calf-worshippers, and the commentators note the timing and the incompleteness of the repentance. The verse describes the state that followed the sin: “Later, when they were filled with remorse and realized they had gone astray,” or “wa lamma suqita fi aydeehim wa ra’aw annahum qad dalloo,” the idiom “suqita fi aydeehim,” literally “it was dropped into their hands,” conveying the sudden falling of regret upon them, the moment when the truth of what they had done broke upon their minds, and the realization that they had gone astray, “dalloo,” that the worship of the calf was the error. Ibn Kathir explains the timing: the remorse came when Moses returned, or when the truth of the calf’s silence became undeniable, and the remorse of the people was the sorrow of the one who has seen, too late, the wrongness of his deed. The commentators note that the remorse is the beginning of the repentance, but that the verse does not yet record the full return, for the remorse of the wrongdoer is not the repentance of the one who turns.\n\nThe verse then records their cry: “If our Lord does not have mercy on us and forgive us, we will certainly be among the losers,” or “la’in lam yarhamna rabbuna wa yaghfir lana la-nakoonanna mina al-khasireen.” The cry is the acknowledgment of the need, the confession that without the mercy and the forgiveness of God they would be among the losers, and the commentators note its affinity with the prayer of Adam, the “we have wronged ourselves” of the first repentance, for the people of Israel, in their remorse, approached the words that Adam had been taught. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the first step of every return: the recognition of the loss and the turning of the heart to the mercy of the Lord, and the cry of the calf-worshippers is the cry of every soul that has seen its error and hopes for the mercy that it does not deserve. The word “khasireen,” the losers, is the measure of their fear: the people knew that the worship of the calf had placed them among the losers, and their cry was the plea to be lifted from the loss by the mercy of the One they had wronged.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the incomplete repentance, and the commentators note the difference between the remorse of the calf-worshippers and the repentance that God accepts. The people felt the regret and recognized the error, and their cry was the plea for mercy, and the story that follows records the intercession of Moses and the acceptance of God, so that the verse is the beginning of the return, the remorse that precedes the forgiveness. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the turning point of the story of the calf, the moment at which the fall began to reverse, and that the believer learns from it the value of the remorse that leads to the plea, for the heart that can say “if our Lord does not have mercy on us, we will be among the losers” has begun the journey back. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the remorse: the regret dropped into their hands, the error realized, and the cry for mercy — the first step of the return from the calf to the Lord.",
  "150": "“Upon Moses’ return to his people, totally furious and sorrowful, he said, ‘What an evil thing you have committed in my absence! Did you seek to hasten your Lord’s command?’ He threw down the Tablets, seized his brother by the hair, and pulled him toward him. Aaron pleaded, ‘Son of my mother! The people overpowered me and nearly killed me. So do not let my enemies rejoice over me, nor count me among the wrongdoing people’” records the return of Moses and the confrontation with his brother, and the commentators describe the scene in full. Moses returned from the mountain with the Tablets, and the verse states his state: “totally furious and sorrowful,” or “ghadbana asifan,” the anger at the sin of the people and the sorrow at their fall, the two emotions of the prophet who has been betrayed by those he loves. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: Moses, who had been told by his Lord of the calf, descended to find his people worshipping the idol, and his first words were the rebuke: “What an evil thing you have committed in my absence! Did you seek to hasten your Lord’s command?” or “bi’sama khalaftumooni min ba‘di a-‘ajiltum amra rabbikum,” the question that charged the people with the impatience that had led them to the idol, as if they had tried to hasten the return or the command by their own device. The word “bi’sama,” what an evil thing, is the measure of the sin, and the question is the rebuke of the prophet whose people had failed him in his absence.\n\nThe verse then records the act of Moses’ grief: “He threw down the Tablets, seized his brother by the hair, and pulled him toward him,” or “wa alqa al-alwaha wa akhatha bi-ra’si akheehi yajurruhu ilayhi,” the casting down of the Tablets in the anguish of the moment, and the seizing of Aaron by the hair, the act of a brother whose grief had overflowed into the rough grasp of the one he had left in charge. Al-Sa’di observes that the throwing of the Tablets and the seizing of Aaron were the acts of a prophet in the extremity of his sorrow, not the calculated deeds of a calm mind, and the Quran preserves them as the human face of the prophet, the anger of the man who loved his people and found them fallen. The commentators note that the Tablets were not broken, as the next verses imply, and that the seizing of Aaron was the moment before the explanation that would restore the brother.\n\nThe verse closes with Aaron’s plea: “Son of my mother! The people overpowered me and nearly killed me. So do not let my enemies rejoice over me, nor count me among the wrongdoing people,” or “yabna umma inna al-qawma istad‘afoonee wa kadoo yaqtuloonanee fa-la tushmit biya al-a‘da’a wa la taj‘alnee ma‘a al-qawmi az-zalimeen.” Ibn Kathir explains the plea: Aaron addressed Moses as “son of my mother,” the appeal to the bond of the same mother, and explained that the people had overpowered him, that he had tried to restrain them and they had nearly killed him, and that Moses should not let the enemies rejoice over him nor count him among the wrongdoers, for he had not shared in the sin. The plea is the vindication of Aaron, and the commentators note that Moses, hearing it, understood, and the anger turned from his brother to the people and to the Samiri, the instigator of the sin. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the return and the vindication: the fury of the prophet, the explanation of the brother, and the beginning of the reckoning with the people and the instigator — the scene of the grief and the justice that the sin of the calf demanded.",
  "151": "“Moses prayed, ‘My Lord! Forgive me and my brother! And admit us into Your mercy. You are the Most Merciful of the merciful’” records the prayer of Moses after the confrontation, and the commentators note the two requests it joins. Moses, having heard the plea of Aaron and turned from the anger of the moment, raised his hands to his Lord with the prayer that the verse preserves: “My Lord! Forgive me and my brother! And admit us into Your mercy,” or “rabbi ighfir lee wa li-akhee wa adkhilna fi rahmatika.” Ibn Kathir explains the prayer: Moses asked forgiveness for himself and for Aaron, and the commentators differ on the sin for which Moses sought forgiveness — some say for the throwing of the Tablets and the seizing of Aaron, the excess of the moment of grief; others say for the haste in descending to the people before the completion of the command; and still others say the prayer was the humility of the prophet who asks forgiveness as the constant state of the servant, even when no specific sin is known, and the difference of the reports is itself the lesson, for the prophet who is nearest to God is the prophet who asks forgiveness most. The second request is the mercy: “admit us into Your mercy,” the entry into the encompassing mercy of God, the shelter that the forgiveness opens, and the prayer closes with the name that crowns it: “You are the Most Merciful of the merciful,” or “wa anta arhamu ar-rahimeen.”\n\nThe commentators note the completeness of the prayer as a model. The prayer joins the two requests that every believer needs — the forgiveness of the past and the mercy of the future — and it closes with the praise of the Most Merciful, so that the request and the praise are one, and the believer learns from Moses the manner of the supplication that seeks the mercy of the Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that the prayer of Moses is the expression of the prophet’s humility, for the man who had spoken with his Lord and received the Tablets still stood before his Lord as the needy servant, asking the forgiveness and the mercy that no station could make him independent of, and the humility of the prayer is the measure of the station. The verse thus teaches that the greatest of the servants are the greatest of the seekers, and that the forgiveness and the mercy of God are the constant need of every soul.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the brotherhood of the prophets, and the commentators note the inclusion of Aaron. Moses did not ask forgiveness for himself alone but for himself and his brother, and the inclusion is the love of the prophet for his brother and the unity of their mission, for the two had been sent together, and the prayer that sought the mercy of God for both is the prayer of the partnership that the mission had forged. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the prayer of Moses after the trial of the calf, and that the believer reads in it the complete response to the trial: the forgiveness sought, the mercy requested, and the Lord praised as the Most Merciful of the merciful. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the prayer of the prophet: the forgiveness of the two brothers, the entry into the mercy, and the praise of the Most Merciful — the supplication of the one who has passed through the trial and seeks the shelter of his Lord.",
  "152": "“Those who worshipped the calf will certainly be afflicted with Allah’s wrath as well as disgrace in the life of this world. This is how We recompense the fabricators” records the punishment of the calf-worshippers, and the commentators note the two parts of the sentence. The verse declares the punishment: “Those who worshipped the calf will certainly be afflicted with Allah’s wrath as well as disgrace in the life of this world,” or “inna allatheena ittakhathoo al-‘ijla sa-yanaluhum ghadabun min rabbihim wa thillatun fi al-hayati ad-dunya.” Ibn Kathir explains the two punishments: the wrath, “ghadab,” the anger of God that awaited the calf-worshippers in the Hereafter, and the disgrace, “thillah,” the humiliation in this world, and the commentators connect the worldly disgrace to the command that the calf-worshippers be executed by their own people, the communal punishment that the story elsewhere records, and to the humiliation that the sin itself brought upon the people. The two punishments are the recompense of the sin in both worlds, the wrath that is the share of the Hereafter and the disgrace that is the share of this life.\n\nThe verse closes with the principle that governs the recompense: “This is how We recompense the fabricators,” or “wa kathalika najzi al-muftareen.” The word “muftareen,” the fabricators, the inventors, is the key, and the commentators explain its application: the worship of the calf was a fabrication, the invention of a god that was no god, the forging of a religion that had no reality, and the recompense of the fabricators is the wrath and the disgrace, for the one who fabricates a god has fabricated a lie against the Lord, and the lie is requited with the anger of the one whom it defamed. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the general principle of the divine recompense: the punishment of the sin is the likeness of the sin, and the one who fabricated the false god is punished with the wrath and the disgrace that the fabrication deserved, and the principle applies to every fabrication, for the one who invents a lie against God earns the recompense of the inventors. The verse is the warning against the fabrication, and the statement of the justice that meets it.\n\nThe verse also completes the account of the calf’s punishment, and the commentators note its place in the story. The sin had been committed, the remorse had been felt, the prayer of Moses had been offered, and the verse states the punishment that the sin required, so that the listener understands that the mercy of God, which the next verses will record, did not cancel the justice, and that the calf-worshippers, even as they were forgiven, bore the wrath and the disgrace that the sin had earned. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the statement of the justice, and that the believer reads in it the seriousness of the sin of idolatry, the fabrication that the wrath and the disgrace requite. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the recompense: the wrath and the disgrace for the fabricators, and the principle that the inventor of the false god is requited with the anger and the humiliation that the invention deserves.",
  "153": "“But those who commit evil, then repent and become true believers, your Lord will certainly be All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” opens the door of mercy after the statement of the punishment, and the commentators note the balance that the verse completes. The verse states the promise: “those who commit evil, then repent and become true believers, your Lord will certainly be All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “wallatheena ‘amiloo as-sayyi’ati thumma taboo min ba‘diha wa amanoo inna rabbaka min ba‘diha la-ghafoorun raheem.” Ibn Kathir explains the conditions: the evil done, “as-sayyi’at,” the sins of whatever kind, and then the repentance, “tawbah,” the turning from the sin, and the faith, “iman,” the return to the belief that the sin had betrayed, and the verse promises that the Lord, after all of it, is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. The word “thumma,” then, is the key: the sequence of the evil, then the repentance, then the faith, and the sequence is the path of the return, for the forgiveness follows the turning, and the mercy follows the faith. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s great statement of the open door: no sin, however grave, closes the way to the mercy of God, provided the sinner turns and believes, and the promise of the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful stands over every fall as the hope of every return.\n\nThe commentators note the placement of the verse, and its function in the balance of the story. The previous verse had stated the wrath and the disgrace of the calf-worshippers, and the verse now states the mercy that awaits the one who repents, so that the two together are the complete picture of the divine dealing: the justice that punishes the persistence and the mercy that receives the return, and the believer is taught that the wrath of God is for the one who does not turn, while the mercy of God is for the one who does. Ibn Kathir explains that the verse was understood by the scholars to encompass every sin, including the sin of the calf and the sins greater than it, and that the condition of the promise is the sincerity of the repentance and the truth of the faith, for the forgiveness of God is not a license to sin but a shelter for the one who has sinned and turned. The verse is the answer to the despair of the sinner, and the summons to the return.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two divine names that seal it, and the commentators dwell on their pairing. “Ghafoor,” the All-Forgiving, the One who covers the sin and erases it, and “Raheem,” the Most Merciful, the One whose mercy follows the forgiveness and completes it, and the two names together are the promise that the repentant sinner is received, not merely tolerated, and that the return to God is the return to the mercy that no sin can exhaust. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is one of the Quran’s greatest consolations, and that the believer who has fallen, like the calf-worshippers, is summoned by it to rise, for the Lord to whom the return is made is the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the open door: the evil done, the repentance made, the faith restored, and the mercy promised — the path of the return that no sin can close.",
  "154": "“When Moses’ anger subsided, he took up the Tablets whose text contained guidance and mercy for those who fear their Lord” records the restoration of Moses and the description of the Tablets, and the commentators note the two qualities that the verse names. The verse states the return of Moses to composure: “When Moses’ anger subsided,” or “wa lamma sakata ‘an Moosa al-ghadab,” literally “when the anger fell silent from Moses,” the calming of the fury that the sin of the calf had provoked, and then the act that followed: “he took up the Tablets,” or “akhatha al-alwaha,” the taking up of the Tablets that he had thrown down in his anguish, so that the scripture was restored to the hand of the prophet. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the anger of Moses, having been spent in the rebuke and the prayer, subsided, and the prophet, restored to his composure, took up the Tablets again, and the commentators note the significance of the act, for the Tablets had not been broken, and the scripture that the sin had seemed to jeopardize was preserved, and the preservation of the Tablets is the symbol of the preservation of the guidance despite the fall of the people.\n\nThe verse then describes the content of the Tablets: “whose text contained guidance and mercy for those who fear their Lord,” or “fi nuskhatiha hudan wa rahmatun lillatheena hum li-rabbihim yarhaboon.” The two qualities — the guidance, “huda,” and the mercy, “rahmah” — are the two gifts of the scripture, and the commentators note the audience that the verse names: the guidance and the mercy are for “those who fear their Lord,” the “yarhaboon,” the reverent, the ones whose hearts stand in awe of their Lord, for the scripture benefits only the heart that receives it with reverence, and the guidance of the Tablets was the guidance of the God-fearing, not of the heedless who had worshipped the calf. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the true function of the scripture: it is guidance for the seeker and mercy for the reverent, and the one who opens it without the fear of the Lord finds in it only words, while the one who opens it with the reverence of the heart finds in it the light. The verse is the description of the Book, and the description is the condition of its benefit.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prophet’s restoration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own life. Moses, in the extremity of his anger, had thrown down the Tablets, and in the calm that followed he took them up again, and the lesson is that the anger of the moment must not be allowed to destroy the guidance of the Lord, and that the believer who has been swept by the fury of the trial must return, when the fury subsides, to the Book that the fury had set aside. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the restoration of Moses after the trial, and that the believer reads in it the discipline of the heart: the anger that is not allowed to govern, the scripture that is taken up again, and the guidance and the mercy that await the reverent. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the restoration: the anger subsided, the Tablets taken, and the guidance and the mercy described — the prophet restored to his Lord, and the Book restored to the prophet.",
  "155": "“Moses chose seventy men from among his people for Our appointment and, when they were seized by the earthquake, he prayed, ‘My Lord! Had You willed, You could have destroyed them and me before. Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done? It is only Your trial by which You lead astray whom You will and guide whom You will. You are our Guardian, so forgive us and have mercy on us. You are the best of forgivers’” records the appointment of the seventy and the prayer of Moses, and the commentators describe the scene and unfold the prayer. The verse states the selection: “Moses chose seventy men from among his people for Our appointment,” or “wakhtara Moosa qawmahu sab‘eena rajulan li-meeqatina,” the choosing of seventy elders to accompany him to the mountain, and the commentators explain the occasion: the people had demanded to see God, or the appointment was for the taking of the covenant, and the seventy were selected as the representatives of the people to witness the meeting. Then the event: “when they were seized by the earthquake,” or “fa-lamma akhathathumu ar-rajfah,” the quaking that struck the seventy, and the commentators differ on whether the earthquake was the punishment of their demand to see God or a warning that preceded it, but the verse records that the seventy were seized, and Moses fell to prayer. Ibn Kathir explains that the earthquake was the consequence of the audacity of the demand, and that the seventy fell, and Moses, in his grief, prayed for them.\n\nThe prayer of Moses is one of the longest and richest in the Quran, and the commentators unfold its parts. The first is the plea of the prophet’s grief: “My Lord! Had You willed, You could have destroyed them and me before. Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done?” or “rabbi law shi’ta ahlaktahum min qablu wa iyyaya a-tuhlikuna bima fa‘ala as-sufaha’u minna,” the appeal to the divine mercy, the plea that the destruction of all for the sin of the foolish is not the way of the just Lord, and the commentators note the tenderness of the plea, for Moses interceded for his people even as he acknowledged their folly. The second is the confession of the divine will: “It is only Your trial by which You lead astray whom You will and guide whom You will,” or “in hiya illa fitnatuka tudillu biha man tasha’u wa tahdee man tasha’,” the acknowledgment that the trial is from God and that the guidance and the misguidance are in His hand, and the commentators explain that this is the confession of the servant who knows that all is from his Lord. The third is the plea that closes: “You are our Guardian, so forgive us and have mercy on us. You are the best of forgivers,” or “anta waliyyuna faghfir lana warhamna wa anta khayru al-ghafireen,” the appeal to the guardianship of God, the request for the forgiveness and the mercy, and the praise of the best of forgivers.\n\nAl-Sa’di observes that the prayer of Moses is the model of the intercession of the prophet, the plea of the leader who stands between his people and the judgment and asks the mercy of the Lord for their folly, and that the verse teaches the believer the two truths of the divine dealing: the trial that tests and the mercy that receives the plea. Ibn Kathir notes that the prayer was answered, for God restored the seventy, and that the believer reads in the verse the complete relationship of the servant to the Lord — the grief of the fall, the confession of the will, and the plea for the mercy. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the intercession: the seventy seized, the prophet praying, and the prayer that gathered the plea, the confession, and the praise — the supplication of the leader whose people had stumbled, and whose Lord is the best of forgivers.",
  "156": "“‘Ordain for us what is good in this life and the next. Indeed, we have turned to You in repentance.’ Allah replied, ‘I inflict My punishment on whom I will, but My mercy encompasses everything. I will ordain it for those who are mindful, give zakah, and believe in Our signs’” continues the prayer of Moses and records the divine answer, and the commentators present the verse as one of the Quran’s greatest statements of the mercy of God. The first part is the conclusion of Moses’ prayer: “Ordain for us what is good in this life and the next,” or “waktub lana fi hathihi ad-dunya hasanatan wa fi al-akhirati,” the request for the good in both worlds, the “hasanah” of this life and the “hasanah” of the next, and the commentators note that this is the complete prayer of the believer, the seeking of the good in the two abodes, for the believer does not despise the good of this world nor forget the good of the next, but asks both from the Lord who gives both. The second part is the confession: “Indeed, we have turned to You in repentance,” or “inna hudna ilayka,” the declaration of the return, the “hudna,” the turning of the people to their Lord, and the confession is the condition of the request, for the one who asks the good must first return to the Giver of the good.\n\nThe divine answer is the heart of the verse: “I inflict My punishment on whom I will, but My mercy encompasses everything. I will ordain it for those who are mindful, give zakah, and believe in Our signs,” or “‘athabee useebu bihi man asha’u wa rahmatee wasi‘at kulla shay’in fa-sa-aktubuha lillatheena yattaqoona wa yu’toona az-zakata wallatheena hum bi-ayatina yu’minoon.” Ibn Kathir explains the answer with the care that its weight deserves: God declared that the punishment is inflicted on whom He wills, but the mercy, the divine mercy, encompasses everything, the “wasi‘at kulla shay’,” the mercy that is vaster than all things, and then God narrowed the ordaining of the mercy to those who fulfill the three conditions: the mindfulness, “taqwa,” the fear of God; the zakah, the giving of the due; and the belief in the signs, “iman,” the faith in the revelations. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the balance of the divine attributes: the punishment is real, but the mercy is wider, and the mercy is ordained for the mindful, the giving, and the believing, so that the believer is taught that the mercy that encompasses everything is received by the one who meets its conditions, and that the conditions are the three that the verse names.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer the fullness of the divine mercy, and the commentators dwell on the phrase “My mercy encompasses everything.” The mercy of God is not a narrow stream but an ocean that surrounds all things, and the verse declares its vastness before it declares its conditions, so that the listener is first assured of the breadth of the mercy and then taught the way to receive it, and the order of the verse is itself the lesson: the mercy is wider than every sin, and the one who despairs of it has not known its breadth. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the summit of the passage, the statement of the mercy that overarches the punishment, and that the believer reads in it the hope that no fall can extinguish, for the mercy that encompasses everything is the mercy that receives the returning. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the encompassing mercy: the prayer of Moses answered, the mercy declared vaster than all, and the conditions named — the mindfulness, the giving, and the faith — the three keys to the mercy that surrounds all things.",
  "157": "“˹They are˺ the ones who follow the Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, whose description they find in their Torah and the Gospel. He commands them to do good and forbids them from evil, permits for them what is lawful and forbids to them what is impure, and relieves them from their burdens and the shackles that bound them. ˹Only˺ those who believe in him, honour and support him, and follow the light sent down to him will be successful” defines more fully those for whom mercy was just ordained as shunning evil, paying zakat, and believing. The verse identifies them as followers of the Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, or “al-rasool al-nabiyy al-ummi,” the Prophet Muhammad, unlettered in the sense not taught by human writing, a sign that his knowledge comes from Him who knows from Allah what others do not. The description, “whose description they find in their Torah and the Gospel,” or “allathee yajidoonahu maktooban indahum fit-Tawrati wal-Injeel,” recalls those to whom We gave the Scripture recognize him as they recognize their children, confirming that the People of the Book know the blessed, detailed Book’s truth, removing the excuse that Scriptures were only for two groups before us and we were unaware.\n\nHis mission is then characterized in five actions: commanding good, or “ma‘roof,” forbidding evil, or “munkar,” permitting tayyibat, lawful good things, and forbidding khaba’ith, impure things, the same tayyibat earlier asked “Who has forbidden adornments and tayyibat Allah brought forth?” and the same khaba’ith forbidden as open and secret indecencies, sin, and waste. The final freeing, “relieves them from burdens and shackles,” or “wa yada‘u anhum israhum wa al-aghlala,” refers to the heavy yokes imposed by earlier laws, like the stringent restrictions on the Children of Israel, and the self-imposed burdens of fabricated cattle taboos and expulsion threats, all lifted by the mercy that encompasses everything.\n\nSuccess is then conditioned on four responses: believing in him, honouring and supporting him, and following the light sent down to him, or “al-noor allathee unzila ma‘ahu,” the Quran explained with knowledge as guide and mercy. These four replace the arrogant turning away from signs and choosing crooked paths; they are fertile soil that produces abundantly.\n\nA common misunderstanding separates the Prophet from the Tablets’ fundamentals. The verse shows his light confirms those fundamentals and makes them easily bearable, taking the best of them, so the hasanah written for this world and the next becomes attainable. For the inheritor of Scripture who indulges in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven,” the way to true forgiveness is to follow the unlettered Prophet whose description was already written, whose burdens’ removal is mercy ordained for the truly mindful.",
  "158": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘O humanity! I am Allah’s Messenger to you all. To Him ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him. He gives life and causes death.’ So believe in Allah and His Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, who believes in Allah and His revelations. And follow him, so you may be ˹rightly˺ guided” universalizes the previous description of the unlettered Prophet after defining his followers. The command “Say, O Prophet, O humanity! I am Allah’s Messenger to you all,” or “qul ya ayyuha al-nasu inni rasoolu Allahi ilaikum jamee‘an,” declares the scope long prepared: the Book sent down to warn the Mother of Cities and around it, the blessed Book reminder to whole worlds, the mercy ordained for the peoples after Moses, all culminating in a messenger to all, not to one town like Midian or to one people like ’Ad. This universality answers the earlier excuse “Scriptures were only for two groups before us.” Now a messenger to all stands with a light sent down to him.\n\nThe creed that follows, “To Him alone belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. There is no god worthy of worship except Him. He gives life and causes death,” or “allathee lahu mulku al-samawati wal-ard la ilaha illa huwa yuhyee wa yumeet,” is the same kingdom earlier said to belong to Him whose creation and command are His, who created heavens and earth in six Days, established on the Throne, makes night and day overlap, and subjects sun, moon, and stars. Giving life and causing death ties directly to winds producing every fruit and raising the dead, and to the sea that drowned Pharaoh and saved the oppressed. This is the Lord whose throne is above Pharaoh’s, whose earth is inherited by whom He chooses.\n\nThe call that follows completes the circle: “So believe in Allah and His Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, who believes in Allah and His revelations. And follow him, so you may be rightly guided,” or “fa-aminoo billahi wa rasoolihi al-nabiyyi al-ummiyyi allathee yu’minu billahi wa kalimatihi wa ittabi‘oohu la‘allakum tahtadoon.” Belief is in both the Sender and the sent, and following, or “ittiba‘,” the same following earlier commanded for what was sent down without taking other guardians, is the path that is perfectly straight made clear to the mindful, where the best clothing is righteousness and where hasanah is written for this world and the next.\n\nA common error limits the Prophet to tribal scope. The verse makes him humanity’s messenger, whose light relieves burdens and whose shackles’ removal proves mercy encompassing everything, inviting every successor who inherits land to choose the fertile guidance that produces abundantly over the infertile that hardly produces, and to be among those on the heights greeted with peace because they believed and did good within capacity, to be told “Enter Paradise, no fear for you.”",
  "159": "“There are some among the people of Moses who guide with the truth and establish justice accordingly” provides a balanced note after the universal call to follow the unlettered Prophet, showing that among the former community not all were lost to calf worship and rebellion. The previous verses traced the Children of Israel from rescue, through demand for a god, calf invention, remorse, wrath and disgrace, then repentance and mercy, Tablets with guidance, the seventy’s earthquake and prayer for hasanah, and the promise to follow the unlettered Prophet whose light relieves burdens. Now the Quran acknowledges, “There are some among the people of Moses who guide with the truth and establish justice accordingly,” or “wa min qawmi Moosa ummatun yahdoona bil-haqqi wa bihi ya‘diloona.” “Ummah” means a community, a group, not merely individuals, and “yahdoona bil-haqq,” guide with truth, is the same truth whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds. “Ya‘diloona” establish justice, or “adl,” accordingly, is the same justice commanded as uprightness, or qist, and as giving full measure and weighing with justice.\n\nThis commendation mirrors earlier commendations of the lowly who believed among Thamud and of the magicians who believed after seeing the devouring staff, showing that within every people there is fertile soil producing abundantly while other soil hardly produces. It prepares for the next details where twelve tribes, each with community, are guided to springs and shaded with clouds and given manna and quails, and where some among them guide with truth while others change words and break Sabbaths, illustrating the varied lessons for the thankful versus the heedless.\n\nThe verse also links to the broader theme of succession: those who take over land after former residents include both righteous and less so, dispersed through land and tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they return. Recognizing a truth-guiding, justice-establishing community among the People of Moses prevents generalization and shows that the noble Word fulfilled for those who patiently endured extends to individuals who remain true to covenant while most were found rebellious.\n\nA common misunderstanding paints an entire community as uniformly condemned after the calf. The verse corrects that: among the same people there remains an ummah that guides by the Book that was explained with knowledge, a guide and mercy for those who believe, holding firmly to the best of the Tablets and abiding firmly by Scripture and establishing prayer where reward is never discounted, whose example the new universal community is invited to follow by following the unlettered Prophet.",
  "160": "“We divided them into twelve tribes—each as a community. And We revealed to Moses, when his people asked for water, ‘Strike the rock with your staff.’ Then twelve springs gushed out. Each tribe knew its drinking place. We shaded them with clouds and sent down to them manna and quails, ˹saying˺, ‘Eat from the good things We have provided for you.’ They ˹certainly˺ did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves” details Allah’s meticulous care for the twelve communities. The phrase “We divided them into twelve tribes—each as a community,” or “wa qatta‘nahum ithnatai ‘ashrata asbatan umaman,” refers to the descendants of the twelve sons of Jacob, and the commentators explain that God did not merely multiply them but organized them, assigning each tribe its identity, its lineage, and its place, so that the nation was a federation of twelve self-contained communities rather than a shapeless mass. Ibn Kathir notes that this organization was itself a mercy, for the division into tribes preserved the lines of kinship and the order of the community, and it prepared the way for the division of the water that the verse describes.\n\nThe verse then recalls the miracle of the springs: “And We revealed to Moses, when his people asked for water, ‘Strike the rock with your staff.’ Then twelve springs gushed out. Each tribe knew its drinking place,” or “wa awhaina ila Moosa ith istasqahu qawmuhu ani idrib bi-‘asaka al-hajar, fa-nbajasat minhu ithnata ‘ashrata ‘ainan, qad ‘alima kullu unasin mashrabahum.” Ibn Kathir and al-Tabari describe the scene: in the waterless desert, the people cried out for water, and God commanded Moses to strike the rock, and from the single stone twelve springs burst forth, one for each tribe, so that no tribe encroached upon the water of another and no dispute arose over the source. The pairing of the twelve tribes with the twelve springs is the verse’s teaching: the divine provision is matched to the divine organization, and the same Lord who divided the people into tribes divided the water into springs, so that the social order and the material provision were both the gifts of the one Giver. Al-Sa’di observes that the arrangement eliminated the root of the tribal conflict that had plagued the people, for the man who knows his own drinking place has no cause to seize the water of his brother.\n\nThe verse then completes the recollection of the wilderness provision: “We shaded them with clouds and sent down to them manna and quails, ˹saying˺, ‘Eat from the good things We have provided for you,’” or “wa zallalna ‘alayhim al-ghamama wa anzalna ‘alayhim al-manna was-salwa, kuloo min tayyibati ma razaqnakum.” The shade of the cloud, the manna that fell like dew, and the quails that descended in the evening were the three continuous miracles of the desert, and the commentators note that the verse gathers them here to place the entire history of the provision before the people, for the God who divided the tribes and split the rock was the God who shaded them and fed them, and the recollection of the favours is the indictment of the ingratitude that followed. The verse closes with the principle that the Quran repeats: “They ˹certainly˺ did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves,” or “wa ma zalamoona wa lakin kanoo anfusahum yazlimoon,” the declaration that the ingratitude of the people harmed no one but themselves, for God is beyond all harm, and every complaint and every rebellion is an arrow that turns upon the one who fires it. For the listener, the verse is the complete picture of the divine care and the human response: the tribes organized, the springs divided, the shade and the food provided, and the wronging of the self that answered the favour.",
  "161": "“And ˹remember˺ when it was said to them, ‘Enter this city ˹of Jerusalem˺ and eat from wherever you please. Say, ‘Absolve us,’ and enter the gate with humility. We will forgive your sins, ˹and˺ We will multiply the reward for the good-doers’” recalls the command to enter the Holy Land and the conditions of the entry, and the commentators identify the “city” as Jerusalem or Jericho, the gateway to the land that had been promised to the people. The command, “Enter this city and eat from wherever you please,” or “udkhuloo hathihi al-qaryata wa kuloo minha haythu shi’tum,” carried with it the promise of total provision — the land would be theirs, and its abundance open to them without restriction — and the commentators note that the command was the fulfillment of the promise that Moses had made, the entry into the land that God had destined for them. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the people, after the years of wandering, stood at the threshold of the promised land, and the command to enter was the command that would have crowned their journey, and the conditions that accompanied it were the test of their humility.\n\nThe conditions are then stated: “Say, ‘Absolve us,’ and enter the gate with humility,” or “wa qooloo hittatun wadkhuloo al-baba sujjadan.” The word “hittah” is the prayer of forgiveness, the plea “Lord, absolve us, remove our burdens,” and the posture “sujjadan” is the bowing of humility, the entering of the gate with lowered heads as servants rather than as conquerors. The commentators dwell on the pairing of the word and the posture: the tongue was to speak the prayer of forgiveness while the body assumed the posture of submission, and the two together were the liturgy of humility, the ritual designed to keep the people’s hearts anchored in gratitude at the very moment of their triumph. Al-Sa’di observes that the command taught the people the etiquette of victory: the conqueror of the world enters with pride, but the servant of God enters with the prayer for forgiveness, for the triumph is a gift, and the gift is received on the knees of the heart.\n\nThe verse closes with the promise that the humble entry would purchase: “We will forgive your sins, ˹and˺ We will multiply the reward for the good-doers,” or “naghfir lakum khatayakum wa sa-nazeedu al-muhsineen.” The two rewards — the forgiveness of the past and the multiplication for the good-doers — are the measure of the generosity of the command, for a single act of humble entry, performed as commanded, would have bought the erasure of the sins and the increase of the reward, so light was the burden and so immense the compensation. Ibn Kathir notes the contrast that the next verse records: the people who were offered this mercy distorted the command, and the distortion of the word of forgiveness was the turning of the mercy into the punishment. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the humble entry: the city commanded, the provision promised, and the word and the posture prescribed — the test of the heart at the threshold of the blessing, and the mercy that awaited the humble.",
  "162": "“But the wrongdoers among them changed the words they were commanded to say. So We sent down a punishment from the heavens upon them for their wrongdoing” records the distortion of the command and its consequence, and the commentators describe the mockery that the verse condemns. The command had been to say “hittah” — “Absolve us” — and to enter the gate bowing, and the wrongdoers among the people changed the word and the posture: instead of the prayer of forgiveness, they said “hintah,” a grain of wheat, asking mockingly for the food of the land, and instead of entering with lowered heads, they dragged themselves in on their buttocks, as the hadith in the two Sahihs records, so that the ritual of humility became a scene of ridicule. Ibn Kathir explains the significance of the distortion: the word “hittah” expressed the essence of the command, the seeking of the forgiveness of God, and the substitution of “hintah” replaced the spiritual with the material, the prayer with a demand, and the posture of the servant with the posture of the scoffer, so that the mockery struck at the heart of what the command had meant. The word “zalamoo,” the wrongdoers, is the verdict upon them, for the distortion of the divine word is the wronging of the self that speaks it.\n\nThe consequence is stated with the word that the Quran uses for the punishment of the mockers: “So We sent down a punishment from the heavens upon them for their wrongdoing,” or “fa-anzalna ‘ala allatheena zalamoo rijzan mina as-sama’i bima kanoo yafsuqoon.” The “rijz,” the plague or the torment, descended from the heavens, and the commentators relate that it struck the mockers and destroyed a great number of them in a single stroke, so that the city that they had been commanded to enter in humility became the place of their punishment. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the gravity of the mockery of the sacred: the distortion of a single word, the substitution of “hittah” for “hintah,” was enough to draw down the punishment of the heavens, for the mockery of the command is the mockery of the One who commanded, and the one who ridicules the rituals of his Lord has declared, by the ridicule, his rebellion. The verse is the warning against the trivialization of the divine word, and the statement of the justice that meets it.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the manner of obedience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The command of God is not fulfilled by the mere performance of the outward act but by the spirit in which the act is performed, and the people who entered the gate and spoke the word — but in mockery and distortion — fulfilled the letter and destroyed the spirit, and the punishment that descended upon them was the recompense of the spirit that they had killed. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the warning against the hypocrisy of the tongue, the obedience that is performed with the limbs while the heart mocks, and that the believer is summoned to the sincerity that speaks the word of God as God would have it spoken. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the distorted word: the prayer changed to a demand, the humility changed to a mockery, and the punishment that fell upon the mockers — the justice of the heavens for the ridicule of the earth.",
  "163": "“Ask them ˹O Prophet˺ about ˹the people of˺ the town which was by the sea, who broke the Sabbath. During the Sabbath, ˹abundant˺ fish would come to them clearly visible, but on other days the fish were never seen. In this way We tested them for their rebelliousness” opens the story of the Sabbath-breakers, and the commentators identify the town and describe the test. The verse commands the Prophet to ask the Jews of his time about “the town which was by the sea,” or “al-qaryati allati kanat hadirata al-bahr,” a coastal town of the Children of Israel whose people were commanded to observe the Sabbath, the day set apart for worship and rest, and the commentators name it as Aylah, or a town on the shore of the Red Sea, though the lesson does not depend on the name. Ibn Kathir explains the command: the question was addressed to the Jews of Medina, who knew the story of their ancestors, and the asking was the reminder of a history that they had not learned from, for the town by the sea was the monument of the Sabbath-breaking that the verse recalls.\n\nThe verse then describes the test: “During the Sabbath, ˹abundant˺ fish would come to them clearly visible, but on other days the fish were never seen,” or “ith ya’deena fi sabtihim ith ta’teehim heetanuhum yawma sabtihim shurra‘an wa yawma la yasbitoona la ta’teehim.” The fish, the commentators relate, would throng to the surface on the Sabbath in great visible schools, offering themselves as if by appointment, and on the other six days they would disappear into the depths, so that the people were tempted, on the very day that God had reserved for Himself, by the abundance that their livelihood depended upon. The verse names the purpose of the arrangement: “In this way We tested them for their rebelliousness,” or “kathalika nabloohum bima kanoo yafsuqoon,” the test of the fish was the test of their obedience, and the commentators note the nature of the trial: it was not a test of scarcity but of plenty, the test of the heart that is offered easy gain in the hour that God has claimed, and the test exposed the “fisq,” the rebelliousness, that the people had kept hidden. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the nature of the divine test: the trial is often the abundance, not the deprivation, and the fish that came on the Sabbath were the bait that the rebelliousness of the people was waiting to seize.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sacred time, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own Sabbath — the Friday and the prayer and the hours of worship that God has set apart. The time that God has reserved is the time of the test, and the one who honours the sacred hour when the gain is offered is the one whose obedience has been proven, while the one who seizes the gain in the hour of God has seized the forbidden and failed the test. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the introduction to the story of the Sabbath-breakers, and that the question “ask them about the town” is the summons to remember, for the story of the fish is the story of every heart that is tested by the abundance that the hour of worship forbids. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the test of plenty: the fish offered on the Sabbath, the town tempted by its livelihood, and the rebelliousness that the test exposed — the trial of the heart at the boundary of the sacred.",
  "164": "“When some of ˹the righteous among˺ them questioned ˹their fellow Sabbath-keepers˺, ‘Why do you ˹bother to˺ warn those ˹Sabbath-breakers˺ who will either be destroyed or severely punished by Allah?’ They replied, ‘Just to be free from your Lord’s blame, and so perhaps they may abstain’” records the division within the town and the two groups that it produced, and the commentators explain the scene. The town, tested by the fish, split into three factions: the Sabbath-breakers who seized the fish through their tricks; the silent who did not break the Sabbath but did not speak against those who did; and the warners who kept the Sabbath and admonished the breakers. The verse records the exchange between the silent and the warners: the silent asked the warners, “Why do you bother to warn those who will either be destroyed or severely punished by Allah?” or “lima ta‘izoona qawman Allahu muhlikuhum aw mu‘aththibuhum ‘athaban shadeedan,” the question of the resigned, who saw that the breakers were doomed and asked what the point of the warning was, since the doomed would not be saved by it. Ibn Kathir explains the question: the silent had given up on the breakers, and their resignation was itself a position, the position of the one who sees the evil and does nothing, and their question to the warners was the rationalization of their own silence.\n\nThe answer of the warners is the reply that the Quran preserves as the model of the believer’s duty: “Just to be free from your Lord’s blame, and so perhaps they may abstain,” or “ma‘thiratan ila rabbikum wa la‘allahum yattaqoon.” The two reasons are the complete answer to the question: the warning is first for the discharge of the duty, the clearing of the warner’s own account before his Lord, the “ma‘thirah” that leaves no blame upon the one who has spoken; and second, for the hope of the abstention, the “la‘alla” that the warning might yet turn the warned, for the door of return is never closed while the word of warning is spoken. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two motives of the call to good: the discharge of the duty to God, which is the warner’s own concern, and the hope of the reform of the sinner, which is the warner’s love, and the two together answer every argument of the resigned, for the one who warns is never wasting his breath, since the warning itself is the discharge of his trust and the seed of the return. The verse is the Quran’s answer to the despair that says “why bother,” and the model of the steadfast minority that warns even when the warning seems hopeless.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the three groups, and the commentators note the fate of each. The breakers were destroyed, the silent were spared or included in the destruction according to the differing reports, and the warners were rescued, as the next verse records, and the three fates are the teaching of the verse: the one who commits the evil, the one who watches it in silence, and the one who warns against it, and the believer is summoned to the third, for the warning is the duty and the hope, and the silence is the resignation that the verse’s question exposes. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the heart of the story of the Sabbath, and that the question “why do you warn” and the answer “to be free from blame and so perhaps they may abstain” are the permanent dialogue of the believer with the despair that would silence him. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the warning: the resigned who questioned, the warners who answered, and the two reasons that make the warning the duty of every believer — the clearing of the account and the hope of the return.",
  "165": "“When they ignored the warning they were given, We rescued those who used to warn against evil and overtook the wrongdoers with a dreadful punishment for their rebelliousness” records the outcome of the division, and the commentators note the justice of the verdict. The verse states the two fates that the story had been building toward: the rescue of the warners and the punishment of the wrongdoers. “When they ignored the warning they were given,” or “fa-lamma nasoo ma thukkiroo bihi,” the forgetting and the ignoring of the reminder, the persistence of the breakers in their rebellion despite the admonition of the warners, and then the two verdicts: “We rescued those who used to warn against evil,” or “anjaina allatheena yanhawna ‘ani as-soo’i,” the salvation of the warners, the ones who had spoken against the evil, and “overtook the wrongdoers with a dreadful punishment,” or “wa akhathna allatheena zalamoo bi-‘athabin ba’eesin bima kanoo yafsuqoon,” the seizing of the wrongdoers with the terrible punishment, the “ba’ees,” the severe and bitter torment, for their rebelliousness. Ibn Kathir explains the detail: the warners were saved, and the commentators differ on the silent — whether they were included in the rescue or in the punishment — but the verse is clear on the two certain fates: the warner against evil is saved, and the wrongdoer is seized.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the principle that the verse teaches. The salvation is tied to the warning, for the one who warns against the evil has separated himself from the evil and discharged his duty, and the punishment is tied to the wrongdoing, for the one who committed the evil and persisted in it has earned the seizing that the verse records. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s statement of the law of the community: when a people persists in the sin and ignores the warning, the punishment descends upon the wrongdoers while the warners are spared, and the sparing of the warners is the proof that the one who speaks against the evil is not counted among the evil, even when he dwells in their midst. The verse is the comfort of the minority that stands for the truth in a corrupt society, and the assurance that the standing is not in vain.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the heedlessness that the word “nasoo” names, and the commentators note its meaning. The root “nasiya” means to forget, and the verse says that the people “forgot” the reminder, not that they were ignorant of it, for the forgetting of the reminder is the deliberate neglect of what has been known, and the punishment of the forgetters is the recompense of the neglect that they chose. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the conclusion of the story of the Sabbath, and that the rescue of the warners and the punishment of the wrongdoers are the two lessons of the whole account: the warning is the duty and the safety, and the persistence in the sin is the rebellion and the ruin. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two fates: the reminder ignored, the warners rescued, and the wrongdoers overtaken — the justice of the Lord who saves the one who speaks and seizes the one who persists.",
  "166": "“But when they stubbornly persisted in violation, We said to them, ‘Be disgraced apes!’” records the transformation of the Sabbath-breakers, and the commentators note the two stages that the verse implies. The verse states the first stage: “when they stubbornly persisted in violation,” or “fa-lamma ‘ataw ‘an ma nuhu ‘anhu,” the arrogance and the transgression of the people who, having been warned and having ignored the warning, persisted in their violation, refusing the command and the reminder alike, and the word “‘ataw” — they were arrogant, they exceeded — is the measure of their state, for the persistence of the Sabbath-breakers was not the weakness of the tempted but the arrogance of the defiant. Ibn Kathir explains the progression: the people had been tested by the fish, warned by the warners, and they had ignored the warning, and now they persisted in the violation, and the persistence was the final step before the punishment, for the one who persists in the sin after the warning has exhausted the mercy that the warning offered. The verse then records the punishment: “We said to them, ‘Be disgraced apes!’” or “qulna lahum koonoo qiradatan khasi’een,” the command that transformed them, and the commentators relate that the transformation was the punishment of their persistence.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the nature of the transformation, and the two interpretations that the scholars have preserved. The majority, including Ibn Abbas and Mujahid, held that the transformation was literal and physical, that the Sabbath-breakers were turned into apes in their bodies, their forms changed as their hearts had been changed, and the transformation was the visible sign of the inward fall, a punishment that the whole community could witness. Others understood the transformation as moral and spiritual, the stripping of the human dignity so that the people became, in their character and their conduct, like apes, aping and imitating without the reason that distinguishes the human. Ibn Kathir notes that both interpretations teach the same truth: the violation of the divine command degrades the violator, and the one who abandons the obedience that makes him human falls to the level of the beast, whether the fall is in the body or in the soul. Al-Sa’di observes that the word “khasi’een,” the disgraced, the humiliated, is the key to the punishment: the people who had been arrogant in their violation were reduced to the disgrace that the arrogance deserved, and the arrogance and the disgrace are the two ends of the single fall.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the persistence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The Sabbath-breakers had been given the warning and the time, and their persistence, not their initial sin, was the cause of the punishment, for the one who sins and repents is forgiven, while the one who sins and persists invites the transformation that the verse records. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the warning against the arrogance of the persistent sinner, and that the believer is taught to hasten the repentance, for the delay of the return is the approach of the fall. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the transformation: the persistence in the violation, the command of the disgrace, and the fall to the apes — the warning that the one who will not return from the sin may be changed, in body or in soul, into the image of the sin he loved.",
  "167": "“And ˹remember, O Prophet,˺ when your Lord declared that He would send against them others who would make them suffer terribly until the Day of Judgment. Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” records the divine declaration concerning the persistent, and the commentators note its content and its balance. The verse states the declaration: “when your Lord declared that He would send against them others who would make them suffer terribly until the Day of Judgment,” or “wa ith ta’aththana rabbuka la-yab‘athanna ‘alayhim ila yawmi al-qiyamati man yasoomuhum soo’a al-‘athab,” the promise that the Lord would set over the persistent among the Children of Israel, generation after generation, oppressors who would inflict upon them the worst of torments, the “soo’ al-‘athab,” until the Day of Judgment. Ibn Kathir explains the declaration: the punishment of the transformation was not the end of the matter, and the persistent among the people were given over, in the divine decree, to the successive oppressions of the nations that would rule over them, the humiliation and the affliction that would follow them through the ages, and the declaration was the warning of the long chastisement that the persistence had earned. The word “ta’aththana,” He declared, He proclaimed, conveys the formality of the decree, the solemn announcement of the punishment.\n\nThe verse then states the two attributes that balance the declaration: “Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “inna rabbaka la-saree‘u al-‘iqabi wa innahu la-ghafoorun raheem.” The swiftness of the punishment is affirmed, and the forgiveness and the mercy are affirmed beside it, and the commentators dwell on the pairing, for the verse teaches the complete picture of the divine dealing: the punishment is swift for the one who persists, and the mercy is certain for the one who returns, and the two attributes are not in conflict but in harmony, each exercised upon the soul that its state deserves. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s constant balance: the fear of the swift punishment and the hope of the encompassing mercy are held together, and the believer lives between the two, hastening the return out of the fear and trusting the mercy out of the hope. The name “Ghafoor,” the All-Forgiving, and “Raheem,” the Most Merciful, are placed at the end, so that the verse closes on the note of mercy, for the mercy of God is the final word even after the declaration of the punishment.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the long chastisement, and the commentators connect it to the history of the Children of Israel. The declaration that God would send against them the oppressors until the Day of Judgment was fulfilled, as the commentators note, in the successive dominations of the nations over the people, and the fulfillment is the proof of the truth of the declaration, for the persistence of the people in their rejection drew upon them the afflictions of the ages. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the warning to every community that persists in the rejection of its Lord, and that the believer reads in it the two roads: the road of the persistence that invites the swift punishment and the long chastisement, and the road of the return that meets the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the declaration: the oppressors promised, the swift punishment affirmed, and the mercy declared beside it — the balance of the fear and the hope that the believer holds until the Judgment.",
  "168": "“We dispersed them through the land in groups—some were righteous, others were less so. We tested them with prosperity and adversity, so perhaps they would return ˹to the Right Path˺” records the dispersal of the people and the test that followed, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the dispersal: “We dispersed them through the land in groups,” or “wa qatta‘nahum fi al-ardi umaman,” the scattering of the Children of Israel among the nations, so that they ceased to be a single gathered community and became scattered groups throughout the earth, and the commentators explain that the dispersal was itself part of the divine decree, the fragmentation of the people who had failed to preserve their unity in the obedience, so that the nation that had been gathered by the favour was scattered by the neglect. Ibn Kathir notes that the dispersal is the historical fact that the verse states, and that the groups into which the people were divided were themselves of two kinds, as the verse declares.\n\nThe second part is the description of the two kinds: “some were righteous, others were less so,” or “minhum as-salihoona wa minhum doona thalik,” the righteous among them, the “salihoon,” who held to the scripture and the obedience, and those below that, the “doona thalik,” the ones who fell short of the righteousness, and the commentators note the honesty of the description, for the verse does not condemn the people as a whole but distinguishes the righteous from the less-than-righteous, and the distinction is the justice of the divine account, which weighs each group by its own deeds. The verse then states the test: “We tested them with prosperity and adversity, so perhaps they would return,” or “wa balawnahum bil-hasanati was-sayyi’ati la‘allahum yarji‘oon,” the alternation of the good and the bad, the prosperity that tests the gratitude and the adversity that tests the patience, and the purpose of the test is the return, the “rujoo‘” to the Right Path, for the alternation of the states is the divine method of awakening the heart that the ease had lulled. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the meaning of the changing conditions of life: the good and the bad are both tests, and the purpose of both is the return, and the one who reads the prosperity and the adversity as the signs of the Lord will be led by them back to the Lord, while the one who reads them as the accidents of fortune will wander in them without return.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two responses to the test, and the commentators connect it to the whole history of the people. The righteous among the dispersed met the prosperity and the adversity with the gratitude and the patience that the test demanded, and the less-than-righteous met them with the heedlessness and the complaint that the next verses record, and the two responses are the two harvests of the single seed. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the summary of the condition of the dispersed, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of his own life: the good and the bad that alternate upon him are the tests of his Lord, and the return that the tests are meant to produce is the measure of his response. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the dispersal and the test: the people scattered, the two kinds distinguished, and the prosperity and the adversity alternated — the trial of the dispersed, and the return that the trial invites.",
  "169": "“Then they were succeeded by other generations who inherited the Scripture. They indulged in unlawful gains, claiming, ‘We will be forgiven ˹after all˺.’ And if similar gain came their way, they would seize it. Was a covenant not taken from them in the Scripture that they would not say anything about Allah except the truth? And they studied what is in it. The Home of the Hereafter is better for the mindful. Will you not then understand?” records the corruption of the successors, and the commentators note the two diseases that the verse diagnoses. The verse describes the generations that followed: “Then they were succeeded by other generations who inherited the Scripture,” or “fa-khalafa min ba‘dihim khalfun warithoo al-kitaba,” the successors who inherited the Torah, the generations that came after the prophets and received the Book without the effort of those who had earned it, and the inheritance became the occasion of their corruption, for the one who receives the scripture without the struggle often receives it without the reverence. Ibn Kathir explains the first disease: “They indulged in unlawful gains, claiming, ‘We will be forgiven,’” or “ya’khuthoona ‘arada hatha al-adna wa yaqooloona sa-yughfaru lana,” the taking of the “arad,” the paltry goods of this world, the bribes and the unlawful gains, and the claiming of the forgiveness that would cancel them, the presumption that the mercy of God would cover the sin they had no intention of leaving, and the presumption is the first disease, the trading of the scripture for the bribe and the hope of the pardon without the repentance.\n\nThe second disease follows: “And if similar gain came their way, they would seize it,” or “wa in ya’tihim ‘aradun mithluhu ya’khuthoohu,” the persistence in the sin, the seizing of the same unlawful gain whenever it presented itself, so that the claim of the forgiveness was not the remorse of the one who had fallen but the license of the one who intended to fall again, and the two diseases together are the complete corruption of the heart that uses the promise of the mercy as the permission of the sin. The verse then confronts them with the covenant: “Was a covenant not taken from them in the Scripture that they would not say anything about Allah except the truth? And they studied what is in it,” or “a-lam yu’khath ‘alayhim meethaqu al-kitabi an la yaqooloo ‘ala Allahi illa al-haqqa wa darasoo ma feehi,” the covenant that bound them to speak nothing of God but the truth, and the study of the scripture that should have taught them the truth, and the verse asks the question that exposes the betrayal: they had the covenant and they had studied the Book, and still they said of God what they knew to be false, the claim that the forgiveness would cover the sin they had chosen.\n\nThe verse closes with the contrast that corrects the corruption: “The Home of the Hereafter is better for the mindful. Will you not then understand?” or “wad-daru al-akhiratu khayrun lillatheena yattaqoona afala ta‘qiloon,” the declaration that the Home of the Hereafter is better for the mindful, the “muttaqeen,” and the question that summons the intellect, for the one who sells the Hereafter for the bribe has not understood the trade, and the one who truly understands will know that the paltry gain is not worth the eternal Home. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s diagnosis of the corruption of the religious class, the scholars who inherited the Book and sold it for the world, and the warning against the presumption of the forgiveness that the corruption breeds. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the corrupted inheritance: the scripture inherited, the gains seized, the forgiveness presumed, and the covenant and the study that made the presumption a lie — the warning that the claim of the mercy is not the license of the sin, and that the Home of the Hereafter is for the mindful alone.",
  "170": "“As for those who firmly abide by the Scripture and establish prayer—surely We never discount the reward of those acting righteously” records the contrast to the corrupt successors, and the commentators note the two qualities that the verse praises. The verse turns from the corruption of the previous verse to the remnant that held to the Book: “As for those who firmly abide by the Scripture,” or “wallatheena yumassikoona bil-kitabi,” the ones who hold fast to the scripture, the “mussakoon,” the word conveying the grip of the one who will not let go, the firm holding of the Book in the face of the temptations that had corrupted the others, and “and establish prayer,” or “wa aqamoo as-salata,” the establishment of the prayer, the second quality that the verse names, for the prayer is the proof of the holding, the outward act that confirms the inward grip, and the two qualities together are the complete portrait of the righteous remnant. Ibn Kathir explains the phrase “yumassikoona,” the firm abiding: the word is intensive, conveying not the casual keeping of the Book but the clinging to it with the whole strength, the holding that no bribe and no temptation could loosen, and the praise of the verse is for the steadfastness that the holding required.\n\nThe verse then states the promise: “surely We never discount the reward of those acting righteously,” or “inna la nudee‘u ajra al-musliheen,” the assurance that the reward of the righteous is never lost, the “ajr” of the “musliheen,” the ones who set things aright, is preserved in full, and the commentators note the form of the promise: the verse does not specify the reward but guarantees its preservation, for the certainty of the preservation is the point, and the believer who holds to the Book and establishes the prayer can be sure that his labour is not wasted. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the comfort of the righteous minority in every age: the corrupt may seize the gains of the world, but their gains are the “arad” that perishes, while the reward of the ones who hold to the Book is preserved with the One who never discounts it, and the difference between the two is the difference between the temporary and the eternal. The verse is the answer to the previous verse’s warning, the statement of the reward that the holding to the Book earns.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two qualities, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s own life. The holding to the Book and the establishment of the prayer are the two pillars of the righteous life, the inward grip on the revelation and the outward observance of the worship, and the one who combines the two is the “muslih,” the one who sets things aright, whose reward the verse promises. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the description of the righteous among the dispersed, and that the believer reads in it the standard of his own steadfastness: the holding to the Book in the face of the world’s temptations and the prayer that the holding produces, and the reward that the Lord preserves. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the firm abiding: the Book held, the prayer established, and the reward preserved — the portrait of the righteous remnant and the promise that their labour is not in vain.",
  "171": "“And ˹remember˺ when We raised the mountain over them as if it were a cloud and they thought it would fall on them. ˹We said,˺ ‘Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful ˹of Allah˺’” recalls the covenant at Sinai, and the commentators describe the scene and the command. The verse returns to the moment of the covenant: “when We raised the mountain over them as if it were a cloud,” or “wa ith nataqna al-jabala fawqahum ka-annahu zullatun,” the lifting of the mountain above the people, and the commentators explain that the mountain of Sinai was raised over their heads like a canopy, a shadow suspended above them, and “they thought it would fall on them,” or “wa zannoo annahu waqi‘un bihim,” the people, seeing the mountain above them, believed that it was about to fall upon them, and the fear of the falling mountain was the atmosphere in which the covenant was taken. Ibn Kathir describes the scene: the people had hesitated to accept the Torah, and God raised the mountain over them and commanded them to take the covenant, and the raising of the mountain was the display of the seriousness of the command, the visible proof that the covenant was not a matter of choice but of binding obligation, taken in the shadow of the mountain that hung above them.\n\nThe command that was given in that moment is then stated: “Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful,” or “khuthoo ma ataynakum bi-quwwatin wathkuroo ma feehi la‘allakum tattaqoon.” The two commands — the firm holding, “bi-quwwah,” the taking of the scripture with strength and seriousness, and the remembrance, “wathkuroo ma feehi,” the keeping of its teachings in the mind and the heart — are the complete relationship to the Book, and the purpose of both is stated at the end: “so perhaps you will become mindful,” the “taqwa” that is the goal of every command, the protective consciousness of God that the scripture was sent to cultivate. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the manner of receiving the revelation: the Book is not to be taken lightly but with the strength of the resolve, and its teachings are not to be shelved but remembered and practiced, and the two together produce the mindfulness that is the fruit of the whole covenant.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear and the love, and the commentators note the balance that the scene teaches. The mountain raised above them was the fear, the visible weight of the command, and the promise of the mindfulness was the love, the end for which the command was given, and the covenant of Sinai joined the two, for the believer serves the Lord out of the fear of His majesty and the love of His mercy, and the mountain and the promise are the two faces of the single covenant. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the conclusion of the long history of the Children of Israel in this passage, and that the believer reads in it the permanent command: to hold the scripture firmly, to remember its teachings, and to seek the mindfulness that the scripture was sent to produce. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the covenant: the mountain raised, the fear felt, and the command given — the firm holding and the remembrance, and the mindfulness that the covenant sought.",
  "172": "“And ˹remember˺ when your Lord brought forth from the loins of the children of Adam their descendants and had them testify regarding themselves. ˹Allah asked,˺ ‘Am I not your Lord?’ They replied, ‘Yes, You are! We testify.’ ˹He cautioned,˺ ‘Now you have no right to say on Judgment Day, ‘We were not aware of this’” moves from the mountain covenant with the Children of Israel to the primordial covenant of all humanity, rooting every later covenant in an original testimony. The previous verse showed the mountain raised like a cloud to make successors hold firmly so perhaps they will become mindful. This verse goes deeper to the loins, or “min zuhoorihim,” from the backs of the children of Adam, bringing forth every descendant, a scene of all future humanity gathered as seeds before being placed as successors on earth where winds usher mercy and tests alternate. The question, “Am I not your Lord?” or “a-lastu bi-Rabbikum,” asks for acknowledgment of lordship that was earlier described as the Lord who creates in six Days, subjects sun, moon, and stars, sends provision, and alone deserves worship, the same Lord whose Word is perfected.\n\nThe answer, “Yes, You are! We testify,” or “qaloo bala shahidna,” is the innate recognition that later generations suppress when they take other guardians, claim Allah commanded shame, or demand a god like the idol devotees’ gods. The caution that follows explains the purpose of this testimony: “Now you have no right to say on Judgment Day, ‘We were not aware of this,’” or “an taqooloo yawma al-qiyamati inna kunna an hatha ghafileen.” Heedlessness, or “ghafleen,” was the excuse offered by those who said Scriptures were only for two groups before us and we were unaware of their teachings. This primordial bearing of witness removes that excuse universally, establishing that fitrah, innate knowledge of the Lord, was sown before any society’s term.\n\nThe verse links to the next, which anticipates the other excuse, “It was our forefathers who associated and we followed,” showing both personal unawareness and ancestral imitation are nullified by this testimony. A common misunderstanding thinks covenant is only historical for the Israelites under the mountain. This verse universalizes it: every child of Adam testified, so every successor who inherits Scripture and indulges in unlawful gains claiming “We will be forgiven” cannot claim unawareness, nor can those turned away from signs for arrogance claim they never knew the Right Path. The signs varied for the thankful are reminders, not first introductions, calling the heart back to its first “Yes, You are our Lord,” before the Day fulfilment leaves only self-ruin and vanished fabrications.",
  "173": "“Nor say, ‘It was our forefathers who had associated others ˹with Allah in worship˺ and we, as their descendants, followed in their footsteps. Will you then destroy us for the falsehood they invented?’” continues the cautions attached to the primordial testimony. The previous verse removed the excuse “We were not aware” by recalling the testimony “Am I not your Lord? Yes, we testify.” This verse removes the second excuse: ancestral responsibility. The imagined plea, “It was our forefathers who had associated and we followed in their footsteps,” or “innama ashraka aba’una min qablu wa kunna thurriyyatan min ba‘dihim,” is the classic justification used by ’Ad, Thamud, Midian, and Mecca when told to abandon what forefathers worshipped. They asked “Have you come to make us abandon what our forefathers used to worship?” and claimed forefathers’ cycles explain affliction and prosperity. Here the Quran preempts that line before it can be offered on the Day the barrier stands and scales weigh.\n\nThe question, “Will you then destroy us for the falsehood they invented?” or “a-fa-tuhlikuna bima fa‘ala al-mubtiloon,” suggests that if ancestors innovated the divide of crops for associates, the calf’s lowing, or the Sabbath trickery, the descendants who merely inherited it are victims. The covenant and the varied signs answer that each generation is made successors to see what it will do, tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they will return, and given a Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy. Inheritance without examination is itself a choice, as earlier those who took faith as play were deluded, not compelled.\n\nThe verse connects to the next principle, “This is how We make our signs clear, so perhaps they will return,” showing that signs are clarified precisely to prevent blind following. The mubtileen, falsifiers, are those who invent, and the followers who persist become inventors themselves when they repeat the invention without truth, as with the town by the sea that invented Sabbath fishing tricks.\n\nA common error today is to excuse current practice solely because family or culture did it. The primordial covenant teaches that each soul testified independently, so following forefathers in shirk does not shift burden; as earlier “No one will reap except what they sow. No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another,” and ranks are according to deeds, yet alliance of wrongdoers as allies for misdeeds shows followers still earn their own share. The call is to be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, not among those who, well-versed in teachings yet indulging in lowly gain, claim “We will be forgiven” while repeating forefathers’ falsehood.",
  "174": "“This is how We make our signs clear, so perhaps they will return ˹to the Right Path˺” follows the two removed excuses for ignorance and ancestral imitation, giving the purpose of the primordial covenant and all the narrated stories from Noah to the Sabbath town. The previous verses detailed the testimony “Am I not your Lord?” and the warnings not to claim unawareness or forefather association as excuse. This verse states the divine method: “This is how We make our signs clear,” or “wa kathalika nufassilu al-ayat,” the same phrase used for the fertile-infertile parable, for the varied lessons for the thankful, and for the Book explained with knowledge. “Nufassilu” means We detail, distinguish, elaborate from different angles, as with winds, staff, springs, and Tablets, so the Way of the wicked becomes distinct and the straight path is made clear to the mindful.\n\nThe hopeful purpose, “so perhaps they will return to the Right Path,” or “wa la‘allahum yarji‘oon,” repeats the “perhaps they will return” that motivated testing with prosperity and adversity after dispersion, the “perhaps they may abstain” of the warner’s excuse, and the “so perhaps you will become mindful” under the raised mountain. “Raja‘a” here means to turn back, to revert to the original testimony “Yes, You are our Lord,” after wandering like the disoriented by devils in the wilderness whom companions call “Come to us.”\n\nThis verse bridges the universal covenant and the upcoming individual story of the one given signs who abandoned them and was seized by Satan, showing that clear signs alone do not guarantee return; the infertile soil that clings to this life and follows desires must also choose to be among the thankful.\n\nA common misunderstanding is to expect signs to be hidden or overly mystical. The Quran says signs are made clear, varied, and detailed, from the mountain over heads to the calf’s inability to speak to the Springs gushing for twelve tribes, leaving excuses void. The next story of the dog-like denier who abandoned signs after being given them will illustrate that even a single soul given many signs can deviate if he chooses this life, confirming that perhaps they will return is not passive waiting but active invitation to reflect on stories of the past, as the following verse commands to narrate stories so perhaps they will reflect, keeping the barrier’s hope of “Enter Paradise, no fear for you” still attainable before the home of the rebellious is finally shown.",
  "175": "“And relate to them ˹O Prophet˺ the story of the one to whom We gave Our signs, but he abandoned them, so Satan took hold of him, and he became a deviant” shifts from collective covenants to an individual test, and the commentators identify the man and explain the meaning of the story. The command, “And relate to them the story,” or “wa utlu ‘alayhim naba’a allathee ataynahu ayatina,” is the summons to recite the account of a single figure, and the commentators differ on his identity: the dominant view, held by Ibn Abbas and many of the successors, identifies him as Bal‘am ibn Ba‘ura, a learned man of the Children of Israel who had been given the knowledge of the divine signs, and whose name has become proverbial for the scholar who sold his knowledge for the world. Ibn Kathir explains the identification: Bal‘am had been given the greatest of the divine names, or the knowledge of the scripture, and his supplication was answered, and then he abandoned the signs and inclined to the side of Pharaoh and the enemies of Moses, and the story of his fall is the warning that the verse commands the Prophet to relate. The word “ataynahu ayatina,” We gave him Our signs, is the key: the man had received the signs, the knowledge of the truth, and his fall was not from ignorance but from the abandonment of what he had known.\n\nThe verse then records the fall: “but he abandoned them, so Satan took hold of him, and he became a deviant,” or “fa-insalakha minha fa-atba‘ahu ash-shaytanu fa-kana mina al-ghaween.” The word “insalakha,” to slough off, to slip out of, is the commentators’ focus, for it conveys the image of the snake that sheds its skin, the complete stripping away of the signs that had clothed the man, and the abandonment was not a gradual forgetting but a deliberate casting off of the knowledge that had been his. Then the consequence: “Satan took hold of him,” the “atba‘ahu,” the following of the man by Satan, the seizing of the abandoned soul by the enemy who had been waiting, and “he became a deviant,” the “ghaween,” the one who has gone astray, the fall of the scholar to the ranks of the deviants. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the law of the abandonment: the signs that are cast off leave the soul naked before the enemy, and the scholar who abandons his knowledge is seized by the very Satan that the knowledge was meant to repel, for the knowledge that is not held is the knowledge that is lost. The verse is the warning against the scholar’s fall, and the summons to the holding of the signs that the previous verse commanded.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the individual test, and the commentators connect it to the collective history that the passage has traced. The communities had fallen through the abandonment of the covenant, and the verse now shows the fall of the individual, the man who had the signs and cast them off, and the two falls are one, for the abandonment of the truth is the same disease whether it strikes the community or the soul. Ibn Kathir notes that the story of Bal‘am is related to the Prophet as a warning to the scholars of his own time and of every time, and that the believer reads in it the danger of the knowledge that is not lived. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the abandoned signs: the knowledge given, the signs cast off, and the seizing of Satan — the fall of the one who knew and did not hold, and the warning that the knowledge of the truth is no protection for the one who abandons it.",
  "176": "“If We had willed, We would have elevated him with Our signs, but he clung to this life—following his evil desires. His example is that of a dog: if you chase it away, it pants, and if you leave it, it ˹still˺ pants. This is the example of the people who deny Our signs. So narrate ˹to them˺ stories ˹so perhaps they will reflect˺” records the parable of the abandoned scholar, and the commentators explain both its first and its second parts. The first part states the contrast: “If We had willed, We would have elevated him with Our signs, but he clung to this life—following his evil desires,” or “wa law shi’na la-rafa‘nahu biha wa lakinnahu akhlada ila al-ardi wattaba‘a hawahu.” Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the signs that the man had been given were enough, had he held to them, to elevate him to the highest stations, and the elevation was withheld not by any lack in the signs but by the choice of the man, who “akhlada ila al-ard,” clung to the earth, inclined to the low ground of the worldly life, and “ittaba‘a hawahu,” followed his own desire, and the two phrases are the complete description of the fall: the clinging to the world and the following of the desire, the two roots from which the abandonment of the signs grew. The elevation and the clinging are the two choices, and the man chose the earth.\n\nThe second part is the parable: “His example is that of a dog: if you chase it away, it pants, and if you leave it, it still pants,” or “fa-mathaluhu kamathali al-kalbi in tahmil ‘alayhi yalhath aw tatruk-hu yalhath.” The commentators dwell on the image, for it is one of the Quran’s most striking similes: the dog that pants whether it is driven or left alone, its tongue hanging out in every state, is the image of the soul that has sunk to the level of the base desire, and the parable teaches that the man who abandoned the signs for the desire has become like the panting dog, restless and low in every condition, for the desire that he followed has made him pant after the world whether the world comes to him or departs from him. Al-Sa’di observes the depth of the simile: the scholar who had the signs and cast them off for the earth is lowered, in the parable, below the station of the human to the state of the animal, and the panting of the dog is the picture of the soul that the desire has mastered, panting after the world in the chase and in the rest alike. The verse then applies the parable: “This is the example of the people who deny Our signs,” the likening of the deniers to the panting dog, and the command to narrate the stories, “so perhaps they will reflect,” the summons to the reflection that the parable invites.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the desire, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own heart. The man of the signs fell not through the absence of the knowledge but through the presence of the desire, and the parable of the dog is the warning against the “hawa,” the desire that drags the soul down from the elevation of the knowledge to the panting of the beast, and the believer is taught that the knowledge that is not guarded by the fear of God is the knowledge that the desire will steal. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the completion of the story of Bal‘am, and that the parable of the dog is the Quran’s most vivid warning against the scholar who follows his desire, for the one who knows and still sinks to the desire has sunk lower than the one who never knew. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the parable: the elevation refused, the earth clung to, and the desire followed — and the dog that pants in every state, the image of the soul that the desire has made its own.",
  "177": "“What an evil example of those who deny Our signs! They ˹only˺ wronged their own souls” closes the parable with its verdict, and the commentators note the two parts of the sentence. The first is the exclamation of the evil: “What an evil example of those who deny Our signs!” or “sa’a mathalan al-qawmu allatheena kaththaboo bi-ayatina,” the declaration that the example of the deniers — the dog that pants — is an evil example, the worst of likenesses, and the word “sa’a,” how evil, is the Quran’s verdict upon the state that the parable had described, for the soul that denies the signs and sinks to the desire has sunk to a state whose very likeness is the panting dog, and the exclamation is the measure of the fall. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the verse does not merely state that the example is apt but that it is evil, for the likeness of the denier to the dog is the likeness of the human to the base, and the evil of the likeness is the evil of the state that produced it, the denial of the signs that reduced the denier to the level of the beast.\n\nThe second part states the principle that the whole passage has taught: “They ˹only˺ wronged their own souls,” or “wa anfusahum kanoo yazlimoon,” the declaration that the denial of the signs is the wronging of the self, for the denier harms no one but himself, and the commentators connect the sentence to the refrain that runs through the surah — the repeated teaching that the wrongdoer wrongs only his own soul, for God is beyond all harm, and the denial of the signs is the self-inflicted wound that the denier carries. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the summary of the story of Bal‘am and of the whole passage: the one who is given the signs and denies them, who knows the truth and follows the desire, has wronged no one but himself, and the evil example that the verse pronounces is the example of the self-wronging that the denial of the signs is. The verse is the closing verdict, and the lesson of the individual test is complete.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the self-wronging, and the commentators dwell on its meaning for the believer. The wronging of the self is the displacement of the soul from its station, the fall of the one who was made for the elevation to the level of the panting dog, and the verse teaches that the denial of the signs is not a harm done to God but a harm done to the denier, for the signs that are denied are the lights that were given for the guidance of the soul, and the one who extinguishes them has extinguished his own way. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the conclusion of the story of the abandoned scholar, and that the believer reads in it the warning and the hope: the warning against the abandonment of the signs, and the hope that the one who holds to them will not be among those who wrong their own souls. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the evil example: the likeness of the dog, the verdict of the evil, and the wronging of the self — the closing of the story of the one who knew and fell, and the summons to the one who knows to hold.",
  "178": "“Whoever Allah guides is truly guided. And whoever He leaves to stray, they are the ˹true˺ losers” states the principle behind elevation with signs versus clinging to earth and panting like a dog, directly after calling the deniers’ example evil and self-wronging. The previous verses showed that one given signs who abandons them is not elevated and becomes deviant. This verse abstracts the law: “Whoever Allah guides is truly guided,” or “man yahdi Allahu fa-huwa al-muhtadi.” “Yahdi” is the same guidance praised as opening the chest to Islam, as the straight path perfectly straight, as the light that lets the dead walk among people, and as the guide and mercy in the Tablets for those who stand in awe. True guidance is the one from Allah, not from taking devils as masters thinking one is guided, nor from following the crooked path that seems appealing.\n\nThe counterpart, “And whoever He leaves to stray, they are the true losers,” or “wa man yudlil fa-ula’ika humu al-khasiroon,” uses the same “khasiroon” for losers who feel secure from Allah’s planning, who denied the meeting until the Hour surprised them, and who were wiped as if never there. To be left to stray, or “yudlil,” is not arbitrary; the previous verse showed the straying follows denying signs and wronging own souls, and the wider context shows those who act arrogantly without right are turned away from signs even if they see every sign. The sealing that makes successors unable to hear is the leaving to stray.\n\nThis verse balances earlier statements that If We had willed We would have elevated him, that Allah knows best where to place His message, and that He has guided some while others are destined to stray because they took devils as allies, all showing that will and earnings intertwine: Allah guides the thankful fertile soil that produces abundantly and leaves the unthankful that hardly produces after refusing at first.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this as fatalism. The Quran pairs it with constant invitation to seek guidance: follow what was sent down without taking other guardians, call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, remember favours as successors, hold firmly to Scripture, and narrate stories so perhaps they will reflect. The “true losers” are not those who lacked a chance but those who, like the dog panting for more gain and the heir who claims “We will be forgiven” while seizing again, chose clinging and desire over elevation, thereby confirming the law that deeds become vain and gates remain closed until an impossibility.",
  "179": "“Indeed, We have destined many jinn and humans for Hell. They have hearts they do not understand with, eyes they do not see with, and ears they do not hear with. They are like cattle. In fact, they are even less guided! Such ˹people˺ are ˹entirely˺ heedless” expands the losers just mentioned into a vivid anatomy of heedlessness, showing why guidance and straying are truly distinguishable. The opening, “Indeed, We have destined many jinn and humans for Hell,” or “wa laqad thara’na li-Jahannama katheeran mina al-jinni wal-ins,” uses “thara’na,” to create, scatter, destine, as earlier “We have made for every prophet enemies—devilish humans and jinn.” The destiny is not without cause; the description that follows gives the reason: hearts, eyes, and ears given for understanding, seeing, and hearing are left unused for the purpose they were created, like the fertile land that produces hardly anything despite rain.\n\nThe three disabilities are then listed: “They have hearts they do not understand with,” or “lahum quloobun la yafqahoona biha,” they do not comprehend signs varied for the thankful; “eyes they do not see with,” or “wa lahum a‘yunun la yubsiroona biha,” they do not see the mountain raised like a cloud, the sea split, the she-camel left to graze; “and ears they do not hear with,” or “wa lahum athanun la yasma‘oona biha,” they do not hear the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the “Am I not your Lord?” testimony, or the warners who said “perhaps you will return.” The same triad appeared for the heedless whose punishment comes at night while asleep or day while at play.\n\nThe similitude, “They are like cattle. In fact, they are even less guided!” or “ula’ika ka-al-an‘am bal hum adall,” says they are like grazing livestock that follow pasture without reflection, but even less guided because cattle at least follow instinct beneficially, while these reject the best clothing of righteousness, forbid adorning for worship, and change “Hittah” at the gate. This is the same ranking as “evil example” of dog panting for desire.\n\nThe seal, “Such people are entirely heedless,” or “ula’ika humu al-ghafiloon,” is the heedlessness for which Pharaoh’s people were drowned after denying and being heedless of signs. A common error thinks Hell’s destiny is mere predestination. The verse shows the destined share the same unused faculties; destiny follows the heedless use of hearts, eyes, and ears that were turned away from signs for arrogance, while the path remains to be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, or among those who guide with truth and justice, opening the chest to Islam before the weighing that will be just.",
  "180": "“Allah has the Most Beautiful Names. So call upon Him by them, and keep away from those who abuse His Names. They will be punished for what they used to do” turns from the anatomy of heedlessness to the remedy of remembrance, directly after saying many jinn and humans destined for Hell are like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless. The previous verses diagnosed hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, like the dog panting and the evil example of deniers. Now the Quran offers the names that give those faculties work: “Allah has the Most Beautiful Names,” or “wa lillahi al-asma’u al-husna.” “Al-husna” means most beautiful, most excellent, surpassing all, like the names Ar-Rahman, Ar-Raheem, Al-Hakeem, Al-Khabeer, Al-Wali, the Best Forgiver, the Best of Judges, the Lord of all worlds, the Blessed, the Most Merciful of the merciful, all of which have varied through this surah as winds, springs, and Tablets.\n\nThe command, “So call upon Him by them,” or “fa-id‘oohu biha,” echoes “Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly” and “Call upon Him with hope and fear,” but now with specified means: by the husna names. Calling by Al-Ghafoor when seeking forgiveness after calf remorse, by Ar-Raheem when seeking hasanah in both lives, by Al-Hadi when chest is tight as if climbing into the sky, makes supplication precise and humble, opposite to abusing names.\n\nThe prohibition, “and keep away from those who abuse His Names,” or “wa tharoo allatheena yulhidoona fi asma’ihi,” means to leave those who deviate, distort, incline away in names, as earlier “do not sit with those who ridicule Our revelations” and “leave them and their deceit.” Abuse includes naming idols with divine names, assigning sons and daughters out of ignorance, claiming “the Merciful has a child,” or inventing names without sultan, like the fabricated shares for associates. Such abuse was the theological root of demanding a god like theirs after the sea.\n\nThe warning, “They will be punished for what they used to do,” ties to ranks according to deeds and the home of the rebellious shown, showing that distorting the most beautiful names is not trivial wordplay but rebellious fisq for which dreadful punishment overtook Sabbath violators and for which hearts are sealed.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats names as mere poetry. The verse shows they are tools for guidance: by them the fertile land produces, by abuse the infertile hardly produces. For the dispersed successors who claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains, calling by husna names with hope and fear while abandoning abusers keeps the firm hold on Scripture and establishes prayer where reward is never discounted, before the appointed term.",
  "181": "“And among those We created is a group that guides with the truth and establishes justice accordingly” offers the counterpart to those who deny and those destined for Hell as cattle, showing that not all are heedless. The previous verse taught to call upon Allah by His most beautiful names and to keep away from those who abuse them, whose punishment awaits. This verse then says, “And among those We created is a group that guides with the truth and establishes justice accordingly,” or “wa mimman khalaqna ummatun yahdoona bil-haqqi wa bihi ya‘diloona.” This repeats verbatim the earlier praise of a community among the people of Moses that guides with truth and establishes justice, now universalized to among those We created, indicating that in every era there exists an ummah that holds to the straight path, takes the best of the Tablets, firmly abides by Scripture, and establishes prayer where reward is never discounted.\n\n“Yahdoona bil-haqq,” guides with truth, echoes the earlier “truth prevailed and illusions failed” when the staff devoured great magic, and “ya‘diloona” accordingly means they judge, balance, give just measure as Shu‘ayb commanded, not defrauding people, not spreading corruption after order. This group is the fertile land that produces abundantly by its Lord’s leave, the thankful for whom lessons are varied, the mindful for whom the Home of the Hereafter is far better, the heaviness on the scale that will be successful.\n\nThe verse links directly to promises of mercy ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, and who follow the unlettered Prophet whose light relieves burdens. Such a group does not feel secure from Allah’s planning as losers do, but lives with hope and fear calling humbly and secretly, remembering the mountain over them and the primordial “Am I not your Lord?”\n\nA common error despairs that guidance is extinct after widespread calf worship, Sabbath breaking, and dispersal. The verse asserts a guiding ummah persists, like the Heights’ hopeful entry and the magicians’ prostration, so the narrative of stories is told so perhaps they will reflect, and is not told to induce despair. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing gain, the alternative is to join this ummah that guides with the truth of the Book explained with knowledge and establishes justice accordingly, keeping the barrier’s peace and the rivers’ flow within reach before the term that cannot be delayed, and before the hour that only the Lord knows arrives by surprise.",
  "182": "“As for those who deny Our signs, We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend” returns to the fate of deniers after affirming that a truth-guiding, justice-establishing community persists. The previous verse praised that ummah; this verse shows the opposite trajectory. The phrase “As for those who deny Our signs,” or “wa allatheena kathaboo bi-ayatina,” is the same deniers who rejected the mountain covenant, the she-camel, the Springs, and the Book, who deny Our signs and the meeting so deeds are vain, the wicked whose end was shown, the arrogant turned away from signs even if they see every sign. The recompense, “We will gradually draw them to destruction,” or “sa-nastadrijuhum,” means We will lead step by step, by degrees, like the wind-driven clouds that seem gentle before the flood. “Istidraj” is to draw gradually by giving respite that feels like blessing, as earlier “We changed adversity to prosperity until they flourished and said forefathers also had cycles, so We seized them by surprise while unaware,” the same pattern where adversity was meant to humble but prosperity after it was misread as “This is what we deserve.”\n\nThe qualifier, “in ways they cannot comprehend,” or “min haithu la ya‘lamoon,” stresses subtlety; the draw is from where they do not perceive, as the winds usher mercy invisibly before heavy clouds, and as creation and command belong to Him whose planning only losers feel secure from. This mirrors the dispersion test with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return: prosperity itself can be the gradual draw if not met with thankfulness.\n\nThe verse links to the earlier “We afflicted them with suffering so perhaps they would be humbled, then changed to prosperity,” and to the successor who seizes lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven” and would seize again if it came, showing the istidraj of repeated gain without immediate penalty.\n\nA common misunderstanding mistakes continued prosperity after denying as approval. The Quran calls it istidraj, a slow leading to the home of the rebellious that will soon be shown and to the fire whose bed and cover await the arrogant. For those who call with hope and fear, the antidote is to remain among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, where reward is never discounted, and to vary lessons thankfully so that prosperity becomes opening to Islam rather than tightening of the chest as if climbing into the sky, before the gradual drawing ends in being overtaken by surprise.",
  "183": "“I ˹only˺ delay their end for a while, but My planning is flawless” continues the explanation of gradual drawing, clarifying that the delay is intentional and the plan is perfect. The previous verse said as for those who deny Our signs, We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend. Now Allah says, “I only delay their end for a while,” or “wa umlee lahum,” I give them rope, respite, extension, as earlier Pharaoh’s people had famine then removal until a term they must reach then broke, and as Iblis was given delay until the appointed Day. The word “umlee” means to extend, to grant time, the same respite that earlier seemed like prosperity until they flourished and were seized while unaware.\n\nThe second clause, “but My planning is flawless,” or “inna kaidi mateen,” uses “kaid,” planning, scheme, stratagem, the same term used for Allah’s planning that none feel secure from except losers, and for the scheming of the wicked whose large numbers and arrogance are of no use. “Mateen” means firm, strong, unbreakable, not flimsy like the deceivers’ elegant words that are devoured. The contrast is deliberate: their makr is decorated speech that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter and then fails, while His kaid is the encompassing knowledge that was never absent, that knows what is in land and sea, that writes not a leaf falls without a record, that makes the weighing just.\n\nThis verse links to the earlier “We will gradually draw them” and to “Had the people of those societies been faithful We would have opened blessings from heaven and earth, but they disbelieved so We seized them.” The delay is not abandonment but flawless arrangement where each community’s appointed term is kept, where the fertile and infertile are both given rain before harvest is assessed, and where the Book explained with knowledge remains as guide and mercy for those who believe.\n\nA common error treats delay as weakness or forgetfulness. The Quran says delay is mateen, firm, with purpose: to let the warners warned so as to be excused, to let perhaps they may abstain remain possible, and to let the guiding ummah that establishes justice continue. For the living, the lesson is to use the umlah, respite, not as license to claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing again, but as time to hold firmly to the Scripture raised over like a cloud, to observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful, before the flawless plan reaches its term that can neither be delayed nor advanced.",
  "184": "“Have they not ever given it a thought? Their fellow man is not insane. He is only sent with a clear warning” turns the critique from the deniers’ being gradually drawn to their accusation against the warner himself, as the previous verses described the flawless plan that delays the wicked. The question, “Have they not ever given it a thought?” or “awa lam yatafakkaroo,” asks about thinking, or “tafakkur,” the reflecting that stories are narrated so perhaps they will reflect, and that fertile production requires thankful variation. The subject of thought is the messenger: “Their fellow man is not insane,” or “ma bi-sahibihim min jinnah.” “Sahib” means companion, fellow, one among them, like Moses as brother, Hud as brother, the unlettered Prophet from among yourselves, not a stranger. “Jinnah” is madness, possession by jinn, the same slur the chiefs used when they called Hud a fool and Noah misguided and the Meccan chiefs called Muhammad poet and magician. The Quran says their companion is not mad; his behaviour shows the opposite of madness: he conveys Lord’s messages with sincere advice, knows from Allah what they do not, commands uprightness and sincere devotion, and asks no reward.\n\nThe correction, “He is only sent with a clear warning,” or “in huwa illa natheerun mubeen,” defines the role as “natheer,” warner, the same role given to every prophet and to the Book sent down to warn the disbelievers and remind the believers. “Mubeen” clear, manifest, as the staff was a real snake, the hand shining white, the she-camel not to be harmed, the gate’s “Hittah” to be said humbly. The warner’s clarity is the sign that arrogance turns away from even if they see every sign, preferring the crooked path.\n\nThis verse links to the next invitation to reflect on wonders of heavens and earth and that perhaps their end is near, showing madness accusation is a substitute for that reflection, as earlier the disbelieving chiefs saw the messenger as misguided while themselves blind.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates intensity of warning with insanity. The Quran shows the warner’s intensity is mercy, like winds ushering rain, and his companionship is the most intimate proof he seeks their good. For successors who inherited Scripture and say “We will be forgiven” while taking gain, diagnosing the warner as insane is the crooked path that keeps hearts sealed; diagnosing him as clear warner opens the chest to Islam, where calling humbly and secretly with hope and fear keeps mercy close to good-doers before the hour that only the Lord knows arrives by surprise.",
  "185": "“Have they ever reflected on the wonders of the heavens and the earth, and everything Allah has created, and that perhaps their end is near? So what message after this ˹Quran˺ would they believe in?” expands the invitation to think from the warner to the world, after asking whether their fellow is insane and affirming he is a clear warner. The question, “Have they ever reflected on the wonders of the heavens and the earth,” or “awa lam yanzuroo fi malakooti al-samawati wal-ardi,” uses “malakoot,” the kingdom, dominion behind heavens and earth shown to Abraham so he would be certain, the same heavens and earth created in six Days, Throne, overlapping night and day, subjected sun, moon, and stars, winds with heavy clouds producing every fruit. This reflection is the fertile production that earlier hardly produced in the infertile; it is the looking that earlier communities refused when they worshipped a lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides.\n\nThe second object, “and everything Allah has created,” generalizes beyond sky to every leaf whose fall is written and every spring among twelve for each tribe, encompassing the Book explained with knowledge as well. The third reflection, “and that perhaps their end is near?” or “wa an asa an yakoona qad iqtaraba ajaluhum,” uses “ajal,” the appointed term for each community that when it comes cannot be delayed or advanced, that seized societies at night while asleep or day while at play, that made Midian as if never there, that drowned Pharaoh after famine and plagues. The term’s nearness is uncertain to humans but certain to the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who was never absent, making perhaps near a call to urgency, like “You will soon know who will fare best in the end.”\n\nThe closing challenge, “So what message after this Quran would they believe in?” or “fa-bi-ayyi hadeethin ba‘dahu yu’minoonoon,” says if the Quran that is the Fundamentals of everything explained, that holds the best of the Tablets, that is the light relieving burdens, does not move them, what other hadeeth, discourse, will? This echoes “This is how We make our signs clear so perhaps they will return” and “Have they not reflected on wonders,” showing reflection is the antidote to sport.\n\nA common illusion is that wonders are obvious and will always be there. The verse joins wonders above, around, and the nearness of end to provoke mindful urgency before the barrier and the weighing, inviting the heedless who are like cattle, even less guided, to use hearts to understand, eyes to see, ears to hear, before the Gradual drawing while unaware reaches its term.",
  "186": "“Whoever Allah allows to stray, none can guide, leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance” gives the immutable law after two reflection questions about the warner’s sanity and the heavens and earth’s wonders with the near term. The previous verse asked what message after the Quran they would believe, showing that without reflection even wonders do not guide. This verse states the outcome: “Whoever Allah allows to stray, none can guide,” or “man yudlil Allahu fa-la hadiya lahu.” The same law appeared as “Whoever Allah guides is truly guided, and whoever He leaves to stray are the true losers,” and as “We will gradually draw those who deny Our signs.” To be left to stray, or “yudlil,” is to be abandoned to the crooked path chosen when one acts arrogantly without right, is turned away from signs even if seeing every sign, sees the right path and does not take it but sees the crooked and follows it, denies and is heedless, as the rebellious whose home will be shown.\n\nThe consequence, “leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance,” or “wa yatharuhum fi tughyanihim ya‘mahoona,” uses “tughyan,” exceeding bounds, transgression, like spreading corruption after order, taking the calf as god, breaking the Sabbath with fish tricks, claiming “We will be forgiven” while seizing. “Ya‘mahoona” is to wander blindly, to be confused, like the disoriented by devils in the wilderness whom companions call “Come to us,” and like those whose hearts and eyes are turned as they refused to believe at first, leaving them to wander. This wandering is the opposite of the guided ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice, and of those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer.\n\nThis verse links the gradual drawing and flawless planning to personal responsibility: the drawing is of those who deny signs and whose hearts, eyes, and ears do not understand, see, or hear, so the straying is both divine leaving and self-chosen.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this as arbitrary. The Quran’s sequence shows the leaving follows the chain: denying signs and meeting, making deeds vain, acting arrogantly without right, preferring crooked, being heedless. For the successor who still has the Most Beautiful Names to call upon and the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the remedy is to recall the primordial “Am I not your Lord? Yes, we testify,” to hold firmly to the Scripture under the raised mountain, and to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, lest the wander become permanent and the dog’s panting become the final example.",
  "187": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding the Hour, ‘When will it be?’ Say, ‘That knowledge is only with my Lord. He alone will reveal it when the time comes. It is too tremendous for the heavens and the earth and will only take you by surprise.’ They ask you as if you had full knowledge of it. Say, ‘That knowledge is only with Allah, but most people do not know’” addresses the same fixation on timing that earlier communities used to challenge prophets, now focused on the Hour itself. The previous verse warned that whoever Allah allows to stray will wander blindly in defiance after being gradually drawn. Now the question about the Hour, or “as-Sa‘ah,” is quoted, “When will it be?” or “ayyana mursaha,” when will it anchor, when will it occur. This was the same “When will the Hour be if you are truthful?” asked to hasten punishment, like Pharaoh’s “Bring what you threaten” and the Meccan “Bring the torment” and the modern “If this Quran is truth let the sky fall.”\n\nThe commanded answer repeats the earlier creed that signs are only with Allah and that creation and command belong to Him. Twice it is said, “That knowledge is only with my Lord” and “That knowledge is only with Allah,” or “innama ilmuha inda Rabbi” and “innama ilmuha inda Allah.” The hour’s knowledge is one of the keys of the unseen, the same unseen whose keys are with Him alone as sunset to dawn is His to split. “He alone will reveal it when the time comes,” or “la yujalliha li-waqtiha illa huwa,” means He alone will manifest it at its time, like the mountain levelled when He appears, not a prophet’s estimate. The reason it cannot be known is then given: “It is too tremendous for the heavens and the earth and will only take you by surprise,” or “thaqulat fi al-samawati wal-ard la ta’teekum illa baghtah.” “Thaqulat” heavy, tremendous, too weighty for heavens and earth to bear easily; if its weight is heavy on creation, asking for its date as amusement is treating the tremendous as play, the same taking of faith as amusement for which the Fire’s water is forbidden. “Illa baghtah” only by surprise echoes every term that came at night while asleep or day while at play, and the surprise seizure while unaware after prosperity.\n\nThe Quran notes their false impression: “They ask you as if you had full knowledge of it,” or “ka-annaka hafiyyun anha,” as if you are eager, well-informed, intimate with its news, expecting the warner to be a soothsayer. The reply again, “That knowledge is only with Allah, but most people do not know,” mirrors “most of them did not know” when they blamed omens on Moses, and “most people do not know” after the Hour’s heaviness, showing ignorance is the root of the question itself.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats the Hour’s unknown timing as reason to postpone preparation. The verse teaches the opposite: because it is heavy and surprising, the near term, perhaps your term is near, should keep you reflecting on heavens and earth and calling humbly with hope and fear, before the gradual drawing while unaware ends in wandering.",
  "188": "“Say, ‘I have no power to benefit or protect myself, except by the Will of Allah. If I had known the unknown, I would have benefited myself enormously, and no harm would have ever touched me. I am only a warner and deliverer of good news for those who believe’” follows the Hour’s unknown timing with a personal disclaimer that defines prophethood. The previous verse said the Hour’s knowledge is only with the Lord and is too tremendous for heavens and earth. This verse shows the warner’s own limits before that tremendousness. The opening, “I have no power to benefit or protect myself, except by the Will of Allah,” or “qul la amliku li-nafsi naf‘an wa la darran illa ma sha’a Allah,” mirrors Adam’s parents’ dependence, the magicians’ return to the Lord, and Moses’ prayer for hasanah, all acknowledging that the One whose creation and command are His alone grants benefit and harm. A warner who cannot benefit himself without Allah’s will certainly cannot advance or delay the Hour for others.\n\nThe counterfactual, “If I had known the unknown, I would have benefited myself enormously, and no harm would have ever touched me,” or “wa law kuntu a‘lamu al-ghayba la-istakthartu min al-khair wa ma massaniya al-soo’,” clarifies the keys of the unseen are with Him alone. “Ghaib” is the unseen, the same unseen whose keys no one knows except Him, the Hour, the provision of children, the tomorrow’s rain. If the Prophet knew it, he would have amassed every good and avoided every harm, but his life shows both: victory at Badr and wound at Uhud, proving humanity. This is the same humanness as eating after the staff-snake and needing the mountain’s awe.\n\nThe role definition that follows, “I am only a warner and deliverer of good news for those who believe,” or “in ana illa natheerun wa basheerun li-qawmin yu’minoonoon,” repeats the messenger limits given since Noah’s “I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds, conveying messages and giving sincere advice.” Warmer, or “natheer,” and bringer of good news, or “basheer,” are the two faces of the Book sent down to warn and remind, of the winds that usher mercy and the plagues that are clear signs.\n\nA common error expects a prophet to be angelic controller of fate or economist of the unseen who can time the Hour. The verse corrects that expectation, aligning with “Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly, He does not like transgressors” and “He has the Most Beautiful Names, so call upon Him by them.” The warner’s humanity makes his warning more merciful, not less, inviting listeners to reflect on wonders and nearness of term, to follow the unlettered Prophet who believes in Allah and His words, and to prepare with prayer and firm abiding before harm touches.",
  "189": "“He is the One Who created you from a single soul, then from it made its spouse so he may find comfort in her. After he had been united with her, she carried a light burden that developed gradually. When it grew heavy, they prayed to Allah, their Lord, ‘If you grant us good offspring, we will certainly be grateful’” returns to the origin of humanity after defining the warner’s limits. The previous verses left the Hour as unknown and the warner as human; now the Quran roots humanity in a single soul and in pairhood, showing the Lord whose Hour is unknown is the same Lord who creates and gives life. The phrase “He is the One Who created you from a single soul, then from it made its spouse so he may find comfort in her,” or “huwa allathee khalaqakum min nafsin wahidatin wa ja‘ala minha zawjaha li-yaskuna ilaiha,” echoes “We have established you on earth” and “created you then shaped you” and “from a single soul, then assigned a place to live,” emphasizing unity of origin that removes the ground for arrogance that said “I am better than he is from fire, him from clay.” The spouse, or “zawj,” made from the same soul, finds “sakan,” tranquility, covering, as clothing covers nakedness and night is made for rest.\n\nThe next scene is intimate: “After he had been united with her, she carried a light burden that developed gradually,” or “fa-lamma taghashshaha hamalat hamlan khafeefan fa-marrat bihi.” “Taghashsha” covering as a garment, “hamlan khafeefan,” light burden, early pregnancy not yet heavy, that passes, or “marrat bihi,” she continues, moves lightly. “When it grew heavy, they prayed to Allah, their Lord, ‘If you grant us good offspring, we will certainly be grateful,’” or “fa-lamma athqalat da‘awaa Allaha Rabbahuma la-in ataitana salihan la-nakoonanna min al-shakireen.” “Athqalat,” heavy, near term, both prayed together, the same joint prayer Adam and his wife prayed after the tree, “If You do not forgive and have mercy we will be losers.” “Salehan” here means upright, sound, righteous child, body and faith, and “shakireen,” grateful, the fertile soil that produces abundantly and for whom lessons are varied.\n\nThis origin story links to the next verse where, when good offspring is granted, some associate false gods in what He gave, forgetting the prayer. It also links to the earlier mountain covenant and the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” where all testified “Yes, we testify,” showing gratitude was the natural vow before birth. A common misunderstanding treats family creation as secular. The Quran frames pair and pregnancy as signs among wonders of heavens and earth to reflect upon, whose near term may be the child’s own term, inviting thankfulness that dresses for worship and eats without waste, rather than later association that makes deeds vain like the dog’s panting and the calf’s lowing that neither speaks nor guides.",
  "190": "“But when He granted their descendants good offspring, they associated false gods in what He has given them. Exalted is Allah above what they associate ˹with Him˺!” continues the pair’s story from a grateful prayer to a subsequent association, showing how quickly the fertile vow can turn infertile. The previous verse described the light burden growing heavy and the joint supplication “If You grant us good offspring we will be certainly grateful,” a vow made in the vulnerability of pregnancy that mirrors the call “He alone you call upon humbly openly and secretly, If You rescue us from this we will be ever grateful” at sea. Now the outcome, “But when He granted their descendants good offspring, they associated false gods in what He has given them,” or “fa-lamma atahuma salihan ja‘ala lahu shurakaa’a fima atahuma,” records that after the gift of a sound, righteous child, some among their descendants, or broader humanity, “ja‘ala lahu shurakaa’,” make for Him partners in what He gave, naming children with names that serve others, dedicating them to others, or believing others granted the child, like the she-camel made a partner’s share unfairly or the calf taken as god after the sea. The phrase “in what He has given them” emphasizes the very gift prayed for becomes the occasion for ungratefulness, as earlier “Is it not clear to those who take over land” and “We tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return” showed prosperity itself is a test.\n\nThe exclamation, “Exalted is Allah above what they associate with Him!” or “fa-ta‘ala Allahu amma yushrikoon,” is the same glorification uttered after they associated jinn though He created them and falsely attributed sons and daughters out of ignorance, and after “Exalted is He above what they claim.” “Ta‘ala” high, far above, like the Throne established after creation in six Days, the One who makes night and day overlap and subjects sun, moon, stars, whose creation and command belong to Him alone.\n\nThis verse also warns against the common practice of naming children “servant of” other than Allah or attributing a healthy child to a shrine, a spirit, or a star, the same attribution without sultan that invented cattle taboos and mountain carvings. The remedy taught throughout is to remain among those who call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, who firm abide by Scripture and establish prayer, and who remember “We have certainly brought them a Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy,” so gratitude for good offspring remains gratitude to the One who created the single soul and its spouse for tranquility, not to a partner whose idols will soon be shown to have no feet to walk, hands to hold, eyes to see, or ears to hear.",
  "191": "“Do they associate ˹with Allah˺ those ˹idols˺ which cannot create anything, but are in fact created” begins the systematic dismantling of the association just condemned as exalted above. After saying when good offspring was granted they associated in what He gave and that Allah is exalted above that, the Quran now asks about the nature of the associates. The question, “Do they associate those idols which cannot create anything, but are in fact created,” or “a-yushrikoona ma la yakhluqu shay’an wa hum yukhlaqoon,” contrasts inability to create, “ma la yakhluqu shay’an,” anything at all, not a leaf, not a spring among twelve, not a wind that ushers mercy, with being themselves created, “wa hum yukhlaqoon,” fashioned by human hands, carved from stone, cast from golden jewellery into a lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides. The One who created you from a single soul and made earth in six Days and subjected celestial bodies and sends heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit is the Creator, “who created the heavens and earth in six Days” and “who created all things,” while idols are the inverts, possessed, needing creation.\n\nThis contrast echoes the earlier “How could He have children when He has no mate? He created all things and has perfect knowledge” and “That is Allah your Lord, Creator of all things, so worship Him alone, and He is Maintainer of everything,” and directly answers the successor who demanded “Make for us a god like theirs,” showing that like theirs means like the created, not like the Creator.\n\nThe rhetorical “Do they associate?” implies astonishment, as earlier “Do you find it astonishing that a reminder comes through one of your own,” that the mind should find association astonishing given the evidence of winds and fertile land. The next verses will continue, they cannot help them or even themselves, cannot respond, are created beings like yourselves, have no feet, hands, eyes, ears, and if called for guidance cannot hear and appear to face you but do not see.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats idols as metaphorical only. The Quran treats any thing taken as partner that cannot create yet is created, whether wood, stone, gold calf, or desire followed, as falling under this critique. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains, the reminder is that the gain cannot create, the idol cannot create, only the Lord whose mercy encompasses everything and whose punishment is swift can create and command, so worship belongs to Him alone before the Hour that is too tremendous for heavens and earth takes by surprise.",
  "192": "“which cannot help them, or even help themselves?” continues the audit of the associates that cannot create and are themselves created. The previous verse established creative impotence; this verse establishes helplessness. The phrase “which cannot help them, or even help themselves,” or “wa la yastatee‘oona lahum nasran wa la anfusahum yansuroon,” says the idols are double-helpless: they cannot grant “nasr,” help, victory, rescue, to those who call upon them as guardians besides Him, nor can they provide it to themselves when harmed. The lowing calf could not speak or guide; the idol devotees’ gods after the sea could not provide; the associate-gods for whom crops were divided could not share or withhold; the fabricated shares did not help when famine, blood, or earthquake came. The same helplessness was seen when the staff devoured illusions, when the sea drowned Pharaoh while his gods remained silent, and when the Fire’s residents begged for water that the disbelievers’ provision could not give.\n\nThis double inability contrasts with the Lord who rescues from the darkest times on land and at sea, who rescues from every distress, who sends winds, who gives twelve springs, shade, manna, and quails, who writes on Tablets guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe, and who is the Guardian forgiving and most merciful to those who repent after evil. He helps them and Himself needs no help, as earlier He provides for all and is not in need of provision.\n\nThe verse also mirrors the earlier test of the Heights where tyrants’ large numbers and arrogance are of no use; idol helpers are of no use either, because the creation belongs to Him alone and the command belongs to Him. Their inability to help themselves proves they cannot help others; a helper who cannot self-preserve cannot preserve followers on the Day the weighing will be just and the barrier stands.\n\nA common error imagines that at least idols provide psychological comfort. The Quran shows comfort without help is self-deception, like the dog’s panting that persists whether chased or left, and like garments that neither cover nor adorn. The proper Protector is then named two verses later, “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book, for He alone protects the righteous,” the same Protector to call upon humbly and secretly with hope and fear, whose mercy is close to good-doers and whose most beautiful names are to be called upon while keeping away from those who abuse them.",
  "193": "“And if you ˹idolaters˺ call upon them for guidance, they cannot respond to you. It is all the same whether you call them or remain silent” deepens the helplessness audit from creating and helping to hearing and responding. The previous verses established the associates cannot create and cannot help themselves or others; this verse adds they cannot hear and answer. The condition, “And if you call upon them for guidance,” or “wa in tad‘oohum ila al-huda la yattabi‘ookum,” imagines idolaters sincerely seeking huda, the guidance that is the Book explained with knowledge, the straight path made clear to the mindful, the Tablets’ fundamentals and the light of the unlettered Prophet. The result, “they cannot respond to you,” or “la yattabi‘ookum,” they will not follow you, will not answer, just as earlier the calf could not speak or guide, and as later “Those idols you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves, so call upon them and see if they will answer” challenges. The idols lack the hearing that was just praised as “He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing” and the “We were never absent” that makes a full account with sure knowledge, so they cannot be the guardians besides Him warned against.\n\nThe equivalence, “It is all the same whether you call them or remain silent,” or “sawaa’un alaikum a-da‘awtumoo hum am antum samitoon,” shows futility: calling with humble secret hope and calling not at all produce identical nothing, unlike calling upon your Lord humbly and secretly where mercy is close to good-doers and winds usher rain. The same equivalence was seen when the magicians’ great display stunned people but did not guide; stun without guidance is calling without answer.\n\nThis verse links directly to the later “If you call them to guidance, they cannot hear, and you may see them facing towards you but they cannot see,” extending deafness to blindness, and to the challenge “Do they have feet to walk with? hands to hold? eyes to see? ears to hear?” where the idols’ lack of organs is mocked.\n\nA common misunderstanding assigns subtle agency to idols, thinking silence is still presence. The Quran says silence is absence, and presence that cannot guide is not presence at all. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing gain and the student of the calf that lows, the call is to redirect calling to the One whose creation and command are His, blessed Lord of all worlds, who gives life and causes death and who, through the unlettered Prophet, relieves burdens and shackles, so that following him becomes the following that is rightly guided, rather than calling upon the deaf.",
  "194": "“Those ˹idols˺ you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves. So call upon them and see if they will answer you, if your claims are true!” issues the practical challenge after establishing that associates cannot create, cannot help, and cannot respond. The verse states humanity: “Those idols you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves,” or “inna allatheena tad‘oona min dooni Allahi ibadun amthalukum.” “Ibad,” servants, beings like you, as earlier you were created from a single soul, established then shaped, given hearts, eyes, and ears, and as earlier the jinn were created and the mountain raised like a cloud, all are servants, not lords. Idols are servants doubly: they are created by humans and they are created by the Creator who created the humans who made them, the same chain that makes the calf a jasad with lowing and the Sabbath fish a test.\n\nThe word “amthalukum,” like you, removes the aura of mysterious superiority that makes the straight seem crooked in the eyes of those who take devils as masters thinking they are guided. The idol is not a hidden power watching from where you cannot see them; the devils do that, but the idol itself is a servant like you, standing among the twelve tribes who received springs but did not wrong Us but wronged themselves.\n\nThe challenge, “So call upon them and see if they will answer you, if your claims are true!” or “fa-id‘oohum fa-l-yastajeebu lakum in kuntum sadiqeen,” mirrors Moses’ challenge to Pharaoh, “If you have come with a sign bring it if you are truthful,” and Hud’s “Remember He made you successors,” and Shu‘ayb’s “give just measure.” It is an empirical test parallel to “See what was the end of the wicked,” “See how We vary lessons,” and “Have they reflected on the kingdom of heavens and earth.” If invocation is true, let the servant answer, as the All-Hearing answers the humble secret caller and as the winds answer the command to drive heavy clouds.\n\nThe verse prepares the limb-mockery next: feet, hands, eyes, ears, and the Prophet’s counter-challenge “Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay—my Protector is Allah Who revealed the Book.” A common error treats idols as necessary mediators to the Lord of all worlds. The verse says mediators who are servants like you cannot replace the Lord whose beautiful names are to be called upon directly, whose mercy is close to good-doers, and whose guidance via the unlettered Prophet relieves burdens. Testing the call now, while the term remains, shows silence before the silence of the Fire where residents cry for water and are refused.",
  "195": "“Do they have feet to walk with? Or hands to hold with? Or eyes to see with? Or ears to hear with? Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay!’” continues the empirical challenge, now dissecting the idols’ lack of faculties that were earlier said to be unused in many jinn and humans destined for Hell. After saying idols are servants like you, so call and see if they answer if you are truthful, the Quran asks four anatomical questions: “Do they have feet to walk with? Or hands to hold with? Or eyes to see with? Or ears to hear with?” or “a-lahum arjulun yamshoona biha am lahum aidin yabtishoona biha am lahum a‘yunun yubsiroona biha am lahum athanun yasma‘oona biha.” Each question expects “No” in the truest sense. They may have carved feet, hands, eyes, ears, but none function: feet do not walk to help, hands do not grasp to withhold or give, eyes do not see the mountain raised like a cloud or the sea split, ears do not hear the humble secret call with hope and fear. This mirrors the earlier anatomy of heedlessness, hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, who are like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless, and contrasts with the Lord who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing, Most Subtle, All-Aware, never absent, whose knowledge encompasses everything and who hears the magicians’ prayer for perseverance.\n\nThe second half is the Prophet’s defiance: “Say, O Prophet, Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay!” or “qul id‘oo shuraka’akum thumma keedooni fa-la tunziroon.” This is the same defiance Hud showed, “Do you dispute regarding names without sultan? Then wait, I too am waiting,” and Shu‘ayb’s “in Allah we trust, our Lord, judge between us,” and Noah’s Ark against the flood, all trusting creation-and-command belong to Him. The challenge invites all shuraka, partners, to plot, or “keed,” scheme, the word for the flawless planning whose mateen belongs only to Allah, showing their kid is nothing.\n\nThe verse’s placement before “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who revealed this Book, for He alone protects the righteous” explains the defiance’s basis: the Prophet is not protected by numbers and arrogance that are of no use, but by the Wali who protects the righteous, whose mercy is close to good-doers.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats these questions as mockery without purpose. The Quran’s purpose is diagnostic: idols are servants with decorative limbs, not living guardians, so taking them as guardians besides Him violates “Follow what was sent down and do not take other guardians,” and keeps the scale light. The living are invited to choose feet that walk to worship properly adorned, hands that give just measure, eyes that reflect on heavens and earth, ears that listen to the Quran attentively and silently so mercy may be shown.",
  "196": "“Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book. For He ˹alone˺ protects the righteous” gives the answer to the defiance just issued, “Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay.” After inviting all idols to scheme and proving they lack feet, hands, eyes, ears, the Prophet declares his true reliance. The phrase “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book,” or “inna waliyyi Allahu allathee nazzala al-kitaba,” names the Wali, the protecting ally, guardian, the same Wali whose allies are not devils for those who disbelieve, the same Wali who was best forgiver and Guardian for Moses’ seventy, the same Wali whom no one should take besides Him. His qualification, “Who has revealed this Book,” ties protection to revelation, the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the Tablets’ fundamentals, the light sent down with the unlettered Prophet that relieves burdens. The Book is not an abstract text but the cord of protection, as the staff and the sea were protection for Moses.\n\nThe principle that follows, “For He alone protects the righteous,” or “wa huwa yatawalla al-saliheen,” generalizes. “Saliheen,” righteous, are those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, who guide with truth and establish justice, who give just measure, do not spread corruption, whose hearts understand, eyes see, ears hear, unlike cattle. Protection, or “yatawalla,” is to take as wali, to befriend, to manage affairs, the opposite of the dispersing of the heedless through land and sealing of arrogant hearts.\n\nThis declaration contrasts with the immediate context: the idolaters’ gods cannot protect them or themselves, cannot answer, are servants like you, have no functional limbs, while the Prophet’s Wali, who created you from a single soul, created heavens and earth in six Days, sends winds with heavy clouds producing every fruit, and afflicts and relieves, does protect, as He saved Noah in the Ark, Hud and Salih by mercy, Lot’s family except the doomed, Shu‘ayb’s saved, and the magicians who fell prostrate.\n\nA common error seeks protection in numbers, walls, or status, the same large numbers and arrogance said to be of no use. The verse re-centers protection on the One whose most beautiful names are to be called upon and whose knowledge encompasses everything, the same Lord who declared He would send against the heedless others who would make them suffer until the Day, yet is swift in punishment but certainly all-forgiving, most merciful to the righteous He protects when they call humbly and secretly with hope and fear.",
  "197": "“But those ˹false gods˺ you call besides Him can neither help you nor even themselves’” restates the helplessness audit after the declaration that the Protector is Allah who revealed the Book and protects the righteous. The same sentence appeared earlier as “which cannot help them, or even help themselves,” but now it is placed after the Prophet’s defiance, making the contrast between the two protectors explicit. The phrase “But those false gods you call besides Him can neither help you nor even themselves,” or “wa allatheena tad‘oona min doonihi la yastatee‘oona nasrakum wa la anfusahum yansuroon,” uses “dawna,” besides, indicating a guardian taken instead of Him, the very guardians the Book warned not to take. “Nasr,” help, is the same help Moses told his people to seek from Allah and be patient, whose earth belongs to Him and outcome to the righteous. The false gods provide neither help to callers nor to themselves, unlike the winds that usher mercy and the rain that produces every fruit and the Springs that gush for twelve tribes.\n\nThe repetition is not redundancy but emphasis, closing the circle that began with “Do they associate those idols which cannot create anything but are in fact created,” then “cannot help,” then “if you call for guidance they cannot respond,” then “servants like you, call and see if they answer,” then the limb questions, then the challenge to conspire, then the protector declaration, now the summary. Each repetition adds a layer: creation, self-help, hearing, answering, limbs, conspiracy, guardianship, now the final seal that the helpers are helpless.\n\nThe verse also subtly answers the reminder to the Children of Israel “Make for us a god like their gods,” showing that like theirs means like the helpless, while their rescue from dreadful torment, the split sea, and the twelve springs already demonstrated who truly helps.\n\nA common misunderstanding persists in thinking that at least idols do no harm, so calling them is harmless. The Quran shows calling them is harmful because it makes deeds vain, turns away from signs even if every sign is seen, and leads to the home of the rebellious being shown, to the weighing that is just where light scales doom selves, and to gates not opening. The alternative remains to call upon Him by His most beautiful names and to be among the group that guides with truth and establishes justice, whose Protector, who revealed the Book, is already close to good-doers who call with hope and fear and who will be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer.",
  "198": "“If you ˹idolaters˺ call them to guidance, they cannot hear. And you ˹O Prophet˺ may see them facing towards you, but they cannot see” returns to the sensory failure of idols, now applied to guidance specifically, after summarizing their double helplessness. The phrase “If you call them to guidance, they cannot hear,” or “wa in tad‘oohum ila al-huda la yasma‘oo,” repeats the earlier “if you call them to guidance they cannot respond,” but with “yasma‘oo,” hear, the faculty most needed for “Listen to the Quran attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy,” which will be commanded two verses later. Idols lack the ears that earlier were said many jinn and humans possess but do not hear with, becoming like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless. They cannot hear because they were not given hearing that functions, just as they have no feet to walk, hands to hold, eyes to see, ears to hear.\n\nThe second sentence adds a visual illusion: “And you may see them facing towards you, but they cannot see,” or “wa tarahum yanzuroona ilaika wa hum la yubsiroon.” “Yanzuroona,” looking, facing, gives the appearance of attention, like the magicians’ eyes-deceiving great magic that stunned, or the fish appearing abundantly on Sabbath to test. The idols seem to look, facing the worshipper, carved to appear watchful, yet “la yubsiroon,” they do not see, as earlier “no vision can encompass Him but He encompasses all vision,” the idols encompass nothing. The worshipper feels seen, but the gaze is vacant, the same vacant gaze that led the town by the sea to think tricks would hide Sabbath violation while He is never absent.\n\nThis double failure—deaf to guidance and blind though facing—explains why those turned away from signs for arrogance even if they see every sign will not believe, and why if they see the right path they will not take it but if they see a crooked path they will follow it, as earlier described. The human faculties of the worshippers themselves are also dulled by mirroring: calling to the deaf makes the caller deaf to the Quran’s recitation, a warning that leads directly to “When the Quran is recited, listen attentively and be silent.”\n\nA common error assumes that a deity’s image facing the devotee implies care. The Quran strips that illusion, recalling the calf that neither speaks nor guides despite being fashioned from jewellery, and recalling the winds that do hear the command to drive heavy clouds. The cure is to turn calling to the Most Beautiful Names of the One who does hear and who does see, whose mercy is close, and to remember that the righteous He protects are those who hear the Quran and see the wonders of heavens and earth and that perhaps their term is near, so what message after this Quran would they believe if not this.",
  "199": "“Be gracious, enjoin what is right, and turn away from those who act ignorantly” gives the prophetic ethic after the long exposure of idol helplessness, the protector declaration, and the deaf-blind diagnosis. The command is threefold, each part balancing the previous warnings not to take other guardians and not to spread corruption. First, “Be gracious,” or “khuth al-afwa,” take the easy, pardon what is within capacity, be lenient with people’s faults, as earlier “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford” and “hold firmly to the Scripture which We have given you” with strength but without harshness. “Afwa” is what is readily given without strain, the same ease that the Tayyibat are for enjoyment in this world without burden. Graciousness is the opposite of Pharaoh’s cutting hands and feet and crucifying, and of the chiefs’ “We will kill their sons” domination.\n\nSecond, “enjoin what is right,” or “wa’mur bil-‘urf,” command the known good, the ma‘roof that the unlettered Prophet commands and the evil he forbids, the uprightness and sincere devotion earlier commanded, the just measure, the covering of nakedness with best clothing, the remembrance under the raised mountain. “‘Urf” is what is recognized as good by sound fitrah and revelation combined, the guidance that varies for the thankful and produces abundantly, not the elegant deception that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter.\n\nThird, “and turn away from those who act ignorantly,” or “wa a‘rid an al-jahileen,” is the same turning away commanded for those who ridicule revelations unless they change topics, for leaving them and their deceit, for not sitting with mockers, and for Moses’ “you are the best of those who judge.” Ignorant here are not merely uninformed but those who act on ignorance, like those who demanded a god like theirs, who made the calf, who changed “Hittah” and who abused the most beautiful names.\n\nTogether the three create a community like the ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice among those We created, and the group among the People of Moses that does so. A common misunderstanding pits graciousness against commanding right, as if one must be harsh to enjoin. The verse joins them: graciousness makes enjoining attractive, turning away from the ignorant keeps graciousness from being exploited by those turned away from signs who even if they see every sign will not believe. This balance keeps the caller humbly, secretly hopeful yet fearful, while the winds still usher mercy and the term nears, so that when the Quran is recited two verses later, listening attentively and in silence is the natural response rather than the heedless panting of the dog that clings to earth.",
  "200": "“If you are tempted by Satan, then seek refuge with Allah. Surely He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” follows the ethic of graciousness, enjoining right, and turning away from the ignorant with the necessary spiritual protection for that ethic. The previous verse set a demanding balance: to be gracious and to command good while turning from the ignorant who ridicule, change words, and abuse beautiful names. This verse anticipates the inner push that makes that balance wobble. The phrase “If you are tempted by Satan,” or “wa imma yanzaghannaka mina al-shaytani nazghun,” describes a prick, a touch, an incitement, the same nazgh that made forgetfulness about sitting with mockers and that drives the ambush on the Straight Path from front, back, right, and left. Satan’s soldiers who watch from where you cannot see them use such pricks to turn graciousness into resentment or enjoining into harshness.\n\nThe remedy, “then seek refuge with Allah,” or “fa-ista‘ith billah,” is to take shelter, like taking Allah as Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, and like calling upon Him humbly and secretly with hope and fear. Seeking refuge is the practical form of remembering, or “tathakkur,” that fertile land needs to produce abundantly. The reason, “Surely He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” or “innahu Samee‘un Aleem,” repeats the pairing that after “To Him belongs whatever is in day and night and He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” and that guarantees no vision encompasses Him but He encompasses all vision, that We were never absent, and that the most beautiful names belong to Him. He hears the prick’s whisper and the refuge’s utterance, and He knows the temper that would make the caller transgress and thereby become unliked, since He does not like transgressors.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next, where those mindful, when Satan whispers, remember then they start to see clearly, while devils plunge their associates deeper sparing no effort. The heedless are like cattle, even less guided, while the mindful use refuge to restore sight.\n\nA common error believes willpower alone resists the ignorant’s provocation. The Quran teaches refuge is needed, a return to the Guardian who encompasses, to the Lord who subjects winds and writes not a leaf falls without record. Seeking it keeps the heart among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, rather than among those turned away from signs for arrogance who see the crooked path and follow it. In daily practice, before responding to an ignorant act, the gracious, commanding, turning caller whispers “I seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” and the ambush loses its foothold.",
  "201": "“Indeed, when Satan whispers to those mindful ˹of Allah˺, they remember ˹their Lord˺ then they start to see ˹things˺ clearly” describes the successful use of refuge just commanded, contrasting the heedless who are like cattle with the mindful whose hearts understand. The previous verse warned if Satan tempts you seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing. This verse shows what happens when those who are mindful, or “muttaqoon,” the wearers of the best clothing of righteousness, the hopeful-fearful callers whose mercy is close, the thankful fertile soil, are touched. The phrase “when Satan whispers to those mindful, they remember,” or “inna allatheena ittaqaw itha massahum ta’ifun mina al-shaytani tathakkaroo,” uses “massahum ta’if,” a touch, a visitation, a circling, the lightest contact, not possession, showing even the mindful are touched by the ambush from four sides. “Tathakkaroo” they remember, the same remembrance commanded under the raised mountain, “observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful,” and after the winds producing every fruit so perhaps you will be mindful, and after the primordial testimony so perhaps they will return.\n\nThe result, “then they start to see things clearly,” or “fa-itha hum mubsiroon,” they become seeing, insightful, with “basirah,” inner sight, like the insight, or “basa’ir,” from your Lord that came as the Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy. Clear sight is the opposite of the hearts, eyes, ears that do not understand, see, or hear, and of the idols that face you but do not see and are called to guidance but cannot hear. The mindful’s memory restores the faculty the cattle-like lack.\n\nThis verse links the ethic of being gracious, enjoining right, turning from the ignorant, with the practical need for refuge. The ambush that aims to make the gracious turn harsh and the commander turn transgressing is broken by a remembered Name, one of the Most Beautiful Names they are taught to call upon, like Al-Hafiz, the Guardian.\n\nA common misunderstanding expects the mindful to be never touched. The Quran says they are touched, but they remember. Another error expects remembrance without sight. The verse shows remembrance leads to sight, the same sight that makes the Heights people recognize by marks and that makes the magicians fall prostrate. For successors who inherited Scripture and are tempted to seize lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven,” remembering the covenant not to say about Allah except truth restores sight before the gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend reaches its flawless plan, keeping the path perfectly straight visible while the crooked remains enticing but now seen clearly.",
  "202": "“But the devils persistently plunge their ˹human˺ associates deeper into wickedness, sparing no effort” gives the counterpart to the mindful remembering and then seeing clearly, showing what happens without that remembrance. The previous verse described the mindful touched by a visitation who remember and become insightful. This verse describes the associates of devils: “But the devils persistently plunge their human associates deeper into wickedness, sparing no effort,” or “wa ikhwanuhum yamuddoonahum fil-ghayyi thumma la yuqsiron.” “Ikhwanuhum,” their brothers, associates, those who took devils as masters instead of Allah thinking they are guided. “Yamuddoonahum fil-ghayyi,” they extend them in error, in the crooked path, or “ghayy,” the same ghayy that those turned away from signs choose when they see the right path they will not take but the crooked they will follow. “Thumma la yuqsiron,” then they do not fall short, they spare no effort, they do not tire, matching the relentless ambush that promised to come from front, back, right, left to make most ungrateful and the whispering with elegant words that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter to be pleased and persistent.\n\nThe contrast is stark: the mindful are touched lightly then remember and see, the associates are extended in ghayy without curtailment, plunged deeper step by step, as earlier those who deny Our signs are gradually drawn in ways they cannot comprehend and delayed with flawless planning until the appointed term. The devils’ effort is the engine of istidraj, the gradual drawing that feels like ease while being descent, like the Sabbath fish appearing abundantly on the forbidden day to test rebelliousness, like the lowly gain that if it came again they would seize.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats association with devils as dramatic possession. The Quran shows it as persistent, unsparing extension into ghayy, ordinary choices: not giving just measure, lying in ambush on every road, changing “Hittah,” breaking Sabbath, seizing Scripture’s price. Each extension is a choice that invites another, until the heart that could have been turned toward signs for arrogance after seeing every sign still does not believe.\n\nThe remedy remains the previous verse’s pair: refuge with the All-Hearing, All-Knowing and the gracious, commanding, turning ethic that keeps the land from corruption after order and keeps calls humble, hopeful, and fearing. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven,” the warning is that devils do not spare effort in extending the easy excuse into deeper wickedness, until the dreadful punishment for rebelliousness and the disgrace of apes becomes the home of the rebellious shown, while the firm holders of Scripture and establishers of prayer, guided by truth and justice, remain the group that guides and is saved.",
  "203": "“If you ˹O Prophet˺ do not bring them a sign ˹which they demanded˺, they ask, ‘Why do you not make it yourself?’ Say, ‘I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord. This ˹Quran˺ is an insight from your Lord—a guide and a mercy for those who believe’” addresses the demand for signs that has run from Pharaoh’s “Bring it if you are truthful” through the Sabbath tribe’s testing and the gradually drawn deniers. The condition, “If you do not bring them a sign which they demanded,” or “wa itha lam ta’tihim bi-ayatin,” imagines the prophet pausing between signs, as Moses waited with thirty plus ten nights and as winds pause before heavy clouds. The deniers’ retort, “Why do you not make it yourself?” or “law la ijtabaitaha,” why did you not invent it, select it, bring it on your own, assumes revelation is self-authored, like the golden calf’s lowing fashioned from jewellery or the shares divided for associates by claim. It mirrors the chiefs calling Moses a skilled magician who could invent, and the mockers saying “You have studied previous scriptures.”\n\nThe reply defines the prophet’s office: “I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord,” or “qul innama attabi‘u ma yooha ilayya min Rabbi.” This repeats “I only follow what is revealed to me” from earlier, “we only follow what is revealed,” the same following commanded to the community “Follow what was sent down to you from your Lord and do not take other guardians,” the same holding firmly to the Tablets with strength, and the same abiding firmly by Scripture. Following, or “ittiba‘,” replaces invention, or “ijtabaa,” the root of their question, showing the prophet is not an inventor of signs but a follower, the humble secret caller whose staff and hand were given, not fabricated.\n\nThe description of the Quran that follows elevates it above any demanded spectacle: “This Quran is an insight from your Lord—a guide and a mercy for those who believe,” or “hatha basa’iru min Rabbikum wa hudan wa rahmah li-qawmin yu’minoonoon.” “Basa’ir,” insights, is plural of inner sight, the same insight that came as Book explained with knowledge. Guide and mercy are the pair that makes the Book blessed, that the Tablets contained for those who stand in awe, that defines the group that guides with truth, and that makes the fertile land produce abundantly.\n\nA common error deems the Quran insufficient without an extra demanded sign. The verse says the Quran itself is the sign, the insight, sufficient for eyes that refused even though they saw every sign but were turned away for arrogance. The next verses will command listening to it attentively and silently to be shown mercy, remembering inwardly morning and evening with humility not heedlessness, and joining angels who are not too proud to worship and prostrate, rather than persisting in asking why a sign is not self-made.",
  "204": "“When the Quran is recited, listen to it attentively and be silent, so you may be shown mercy” gives the communal ethic for the insight just described as guide and mercy. The previous verse said this Quran is insight from your Lord, a guide and mercy for those who believe, and that the prophet only follows what is revealed, not inventing signs on demand. This verse tells the audience how to receive that insight. The command, “When the Quran is recited, listen to it attentively and be silent,” or “wa itha quri’a al-Qur’anu fa-istami‘oo lahu wa ansitoo,” uses two verbs: “istama‘a” to listen with intent, giving ear, and “ansata” to be silent, to hush, to still. Together they mean not merely hearing sound while devils plunge associates deeper, nor hearing like cattle with ears they do not hear with, but active, quiet, focused listening, as earlier those who intend to understand, see, or hear use hearts, eyes, ears, and as the mindful remember and then see clearly.\n\nThe purpose, “so you may be shown mercy,” or “la‘allakum turhamoon,” is the same “perhaps you will be shown mercy” promised for following the blessed Book and being mindful that winds produce every fruit similarly to raising the dead. Mercy close to good-doers was promised to those who call humbly with hope and fear and do not spread corruption, and now it is promised to those who listen and be silent before the recitation that is insight, guide, and mercy. This ties to the later “Remember your Lord inwardly with humility morning and evening and do not be among the heedless,” showing outward silence and inward remembrance are paired.\n\nThe verse also corrects the demand “Why do you not make a sign yourself?” By listening attentively, the demanded sign is already present: the Quran’s recitation itself is the sign whose wonders of heavens and earth were earlier said to be heedlessly ignored. The silent, attentive listening defeats the ambush from four sides that aims to make most ungrateful, because gratitude begins with hearing.\n\nA common misunderstanding reserves listening for a mosque. The Quran’s recitation occurs in many places; wherever its insight is recited, the same ethic applies: give it ear, make silence, so mercy may descend, as earlier “We have brought them a Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy for those who believe” requires reception. For the righteous community that guides with truth and establishes justice and for those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, this listening is the daily fertile reception that makes the land produce abundantly, before the heedless who do not hear are left wandering blindly in defiance.",
  "205": "“Remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence and in a moderate tone of voice, both morning and evening. And do not be one of the heedless” details the personal counterpart to communal listening. The previous verse commanded when the Quran is recited listen attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy, an outward, collective discipline. This verse gives the inward, constant discipline: “Remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence and in a moderate tone of voice, both morning and evening,” or “wa uthkur Rabbaka fi nafsika tadarru‘an wa kheefatan wa doona al-jahri mina al-qawli bil-ghuduwwi wal-asaal.” “Fi nafsika,” within yourself, inwardly, is the same inwardness praised as best clothing of righteousness and as secret calling that keeps mercy close. “Tadarru‘an,” humbly, lowly, and “kheefatan,” fearfully, reverently, repeat the humble secret calling with hope and fear, opposite to the arrogance for which gates do not open. “Doona al-jahri,” without loudness, moderate, balanced, like the winds not always as flood, suggests voice between silent heart and shouting show, as earlier not transgressing when calling, and as earlier not making faith as play.\n\nThe time, “both morning and evening,” or “bil-ghuduwwi wal-asaal,” is the same morning and evening of those who invoke their Lord seeking His Face, whose scales become heavy, and of the heights’ prayer. The two ends of the day bookend remembrance, as day and night overlap swiftly by His command and He belongs to whatever is in day and night and is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\n\nThe warning, “And do not be one of the heedless,” or “wa la takun min al-ghafileen,” names the heedless, or “ghafileen,” the cattle-like destined for Hell, hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, who are turned away from signs for arrogance and who even if they see every sign will not believe, whose gathering is like the Sabbath town that forgot what they were reminded. Heedlessness is the soil that hardly produces despite winds and Springs.\n\nA common error imagines remembrance only in loud public display. The Quran commends inward, humble, moderate remembrance as the fertile practice that remembers the primordial “Yes, we testify” and the mountain raised like a cloud, keeping the covenant not to say about Allah except truth. Another error defers remembrance to later, but morning and evening mark now, before the near term that perhaps is near and before the Hour that takes by surprise, so perhaps they will return and so stories are narrated to reflect. The next verse shows the model for such remembrance: angels nearest to your Lord not too proud to worship, glorifying and prostrating, whom the mindful join with attentive silence and inward humility.",
  "206": "“Surely those ˹angels˺ nearest to your Lord are not too proud to worship Him. They glorify Him. And to Him they prostrate” closes the chapter with the highest example of the remembrance just commanded. The previous verse taught to remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence in a moderate tone morning and evening and not be among the heedless who are like cattle. This verse shows who does remember without heedlessness: “Surely those angels nearest to your Lord are not too proud to worship Him,” or “inna allatheena inda Rabbika la yastakbiroona an ibadatihi.” The phrase “inda Rabbika,” near your Lord, denotes nearness, the Heights’ nearness hoped for, the Home of Peace with the Guardian because of what they used to do, while “la yastakbiroona,” not too proud, directly answers the arrogance for which gates of heaven are not opened, for which the wicked are turned away from signs even if they see every sign, and for which Iblis refused to prostrate for Adam saying “I am better.” The angels, though near, remain humble, opposite to Pharaoh’s “We will completely dominate” and the chiefs’ swearing humble believers would never receive mercy.\n\nTheir activity is then described: “They glorify Him. And to Him they prostrate,” or “wa yusabbihoonahu wa lahu yasjudoon.” To glorify, or “tasbeeh,” is to declare flawless, exalted above what they associate, as after good offspring were associated and after jinn were associated though He created them, and as Moses cried “Glory be to You” after the mountain crumbled. Prostration, or “sujood,” is the same bowing the magicians fell into, the same sijdah commanded at the city gate with “Hittah,” the same humility those who firmly abide manifest. The angels’ prostration mirrors the believer’s listening and inward remembrance, showing worship is continuous between heavens and earth, day and night, as creation belongs to Him.\n\nThis final verse connects to the opening of the surah where the Book was sent down so you may warn and remind and not let anxiety tighten the chest, and to the principle that every community has a term and the weighing will be just: the angels’ constant glorification prefigures the rivers flowing and bitterness removed for the righteous.\n\nA common misunderstanding imagines nearness to Allah breeds complacency. The angels show nearness increases worship and glorification, not decreases. For the successor who inherited the blessed eastern and western lands, who was given springs, shade, manna, and Tablets to hold firmly, the mountain’s shadow still calls, the primordial “Yes” still testifies, and the Quran’s insight still recites to be heard in silence. The surah that began with Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad ends with prostration, inviting the attentive, grateful, mindful hearer to join the nearest to the Lord, not among those who claim “We will be forgiven” while persisting, but among those who guide with truth and establish justice, whose reward the righteous never lose."
};
